  '<h2>Importance of Language Use</h2>
<h3> NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Improves Communication</b> → helps express ideas clearly in speaking and writing</li>
<li><b>Helps in Exams</b> → improves performance in essays, comprehension, and grammar questions</li>
<li><b>Builds Professionalism</b> → develops formal writing skills used in work and official communication</li>
<li><b>Enhances Confidence</b> → makes it easier to express thoughts without confusion</li>
<li><b>Improves Clarity</b> → reduces misunderstandings in communication</li>
</ul>
<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Good writing skills help people communicate clearly, succeed academically, and act professionally.
</pre>
<h3> EXAMPLE</h3>
<pre>
Good language → clear message understood easily
Bad language → confusing message and misunderstanding
</pre>
<h3> BREAKDOWN</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Good language</b> → correct grammar, clear vocabulary, and proper structure</li>
<li><b>Bad language</b> → unclear sentences, errors, and poor organization</li>
</ul>
<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li> Ignoring grammar rules</li>
<li> Writing without structure</li>
<li> Using unclear or informal language in formal situations</li>
</ul>
',
  '[{"q":"Why language use important?","steps":["Step 1: communication","Step 2: clarity"],"ans":"Improves communication","why":"importance"},{"q":"What does good language do?","steps":["Step 1: clear message","Step 2: understanding"],"ans":"Ensures clarity","why":"effect"},{"q":"Why students learn language use?","steps":["Step 1: exams","Step 2: writing skills"],"ans":"For effective communication","why":"purpose"}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> physical -> Earth structure
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'physical',
  'Earth structure',
  '<h2>Earth Structure (Detailed)</h2>
<h2> Structure of the Earth (Advanced Mastery Notes – Exam Structured)</h2>
<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
The Earth is made up of concentric layers with different compositions, densities, temperatures, and physical states, ranging from the crust to the inner core.
</p>
<h3> EARTH LAYERS EXPLAINED</h3>
<h4> 1. CRUST</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Thin outermost layer of the Earth</li>
  <li>Broken into tectonic plates</li>
  <li>Two types: Continental (SIAL) and Oceanic (SIMA)</li>
  <li>Site of human life and surface processes</li>
</ul>
<h4> 2. MANTLE</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Thickest layer of the Earth</li>
  <li>Made of hot semi-molten rock (magma)</li>
  <li>Contains convection currents that drive plate movement</li>
</ul>
<h4> 3. OUTER CORE</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Liquid layer composed of iron and nickel</li>
  <li>Movement of liquid metal generates Earth''s magnetic field</li>
</ul>
<h4> 4. INNER CORE</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Solid iron and nickel sphere</li>
  <li>Extremely hot but solid due to intense pressure</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> TRANSITIONAL BOUNDARIES</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Moho Discontinuity:</b> boundary between crust and mantle</li>
  <li><b>Asthenosphere:</b> soft upper mantle where tectonic plates move</li>
  <li><b>Core–Mantle Boundary:</b> separation between mantle and outer core</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> EARTH MATERIAL COMPOSITION</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Crust:</b> SIAL (Silicon + Aluminium), SIMA (Silicon + Magnesium)</li>
  <li><b>Mantle:</b> Silicate rocks rich in iron and magnesium</li>
  <li><b>Core:</b> NIFE (Nickel + Iron)</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> KEY GEOGRAPHICAL PRINCIPLES</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Density increases with depth</li>
  <li>Temperature increases with depth (geothermal gradient)</li>
  <li>Pressure increases towards the Earth''s center</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> EARTH CROSS-SECTION (STRUCTURE DIAGRAM)</h3>

<pre>
             EARTH STRUCTURE

         CRUST (solid, thin layer)
        ┌──────────────────────────────┐
        │ Continental crust (SIAL)     │
        │ Oceanic crust (SIMA)         │
        │ Density: 2.7 – 3.0 g/cm³     │
        ├──────────────────────────────┤

         MANTLE (semi-solid / plastic)
        │ Magnesium + Iron silicates   │
        │ Semi-molten rock (magma)     │
        │ Density: 3.3 – 5.5 g/cm³     │
        │ Convection currents present   │
        ├──────────────────────────────┤

         OUTER CORE (liquid)
        │ Iron + Nickel (NIFE)         │
        │ Density: 9.9 – 12.2 g/cm³    │
        │ Generates Earth''s magnetic field
        ├──────────────────────────────┤

         INNER CORE (solid)
        │ Iron + Nickel (solid state)  │
        │ Density: 12.8 – 13.1 g/cm³   │
        │ Extremely high temperature   │
        │ Solid due to immense pressure│
        └──────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> COMMON EXAM MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Confusing mantle with crust composition</li>
  <li>Saying inner core is liquid (incorrect)</li>
  <li>Mixing up SIAL and SIMA</li>
  <li>Ignoring discontinuities in explanations</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> FINAL MASTER IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Earth Structure = layered system of increasing density
Crust → life & tectonics  
Mantle → movement engine (convection)  
Outer core → magnetic field  
Inner core → solid energy center  
System = dynamic planet, not static rock
</pre>
  ',
  '[{"type":"written","q":"What are the two types of crust?","ans":"Sial and Sima","sol":"Continental crust is Sial while oceanic crust is Sima.","mark":"2 correct types"},{"type":"written","q":"Why is the inner core solid even though it is very hot?","ans":"High pressure","sol":"Extreme pressure keeps iron and nickel solid despite high temperatures.","mark":"Pressure explanation"},{"type":"written","q":"What materials make up the core?","ans":"Iron and Nickel","sol":"The core is mainly composed of nickel and iron (NIFE).","mark":"Correct materials"}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> physical -> Plate tectonics
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'physical',
  'Plate tectonics',
  '<h2>Plate Tectonics (Advanced)</h2>
<h2> Plate Tectonics & Continental Drift (Advanced Mastery Notes – Exam Structured)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
The Earth’s lithosphere is broken into large rigid plates that float and move slowly over the semi-molten asthenosphere, causing earthquakes, volcanoes, and the formation of major landforms.
</p>
<h3> BIG IDEA (ANALOGY MODEL)</h3>
<pre>
 Lithosphere plates = floating logs
 Asthenosphere = slow-moving semi-molten "sea"
              
← drifting slowly drifting →
</pre>
<h4> Meaning</h4>
<p>
Tectonic plates move very slowly, driven by heat and convection currents inside the Earth.
</p>
<hr>
<h3> CONTINENTAL DRIFT (PANGAEA THEORY)</h3>
<h4> BEFORE (Pangaea)</h4>
<pre>
         
  All continents joined as one supercontinent
</pre>
<h4> AFTER BREAKUP</h4>
<pre>
Laurasia (North)        Gondwanaland (South)
North America           Africa      South America
Europe                  India       Antarctica
Asia                    Australia
</pre>
<h4> Panthalassa Ocean</h4>
<p>
A vast ancient ocean that surrounded Pangaea before it split apart.
</p>
<hr>
<h3> TYPES OF PLATE BOUNDARIES</h3>
<h4>1. DIVERGENT BOUNDARY (← →)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Plates move apart</li>
  <li>Magma rises and forms new crust</li>
  <li>Creates mid-ocean ridges</li>
</ul>
<pre>
←   |   →
    crack
  magma rises
</pre>
<h4>Example:</h4>
<p>Mid-Atlantic Ridge</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. CONVERGENT BOUNDARY (→ ←)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Plates collide</li>
  <li>One plate may sink (subduction)</li>
  <li>Forms mountains, volcanoes, and trenches</li>
</ul>
<pre>
→   /   ←
    /   mountain / volcano formation
</pre>
<h4>Example:</h4>
<p>Himalayas (collision of Indian and Eurasian plates)</p>
<hr>
<h4>3. TRANSFORM BOUNDARY (⇆)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Plates slide past each other</li>
  <li>Creates strong friction</li>
  <li>Causes earthquakes</li>
</ul>
<pre>
↑
|  plates slide
↓
</pre
<h4>Example:</h4>
<p>San Andreas Fault (USA)</p>
<hr>
<h3> GLOBAL PLATE SYSTEM (MAJOR PLATES)</h3>
<pre>
 Pacific Plate → surrounds Pacific Ocean
    Ring of Fire (volcanoes + earthquakes)

 Eurasian Plate → Europe + Asia

 African Plate → Africa + Rift Valley

 Indo-Australian Plate → India + Australia

 South American Plate
 North American Plate
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> RING OF FIRE (IMPORTANT ZONE)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Located around Pacific Plate</li>
  <li>High volcanic activity</li>
  <li>Frequent earthquakes</li>
  <li>Includes Japan, Indonesia, Chile, Alaska</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> EFFECTS OF PLATE MOVEMENT</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Earthquakes (sudden crustal shaking)</li>
  <li>Volcano formation</li>
  <li>Mountain building (Himalayas)</li>
  <li>Ocean trenches (deep sea features)</li>
  <li>Continental drift over millions of years</li>
</ul>
<h3> COMMON EXAM MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Confusing magma (inside Earth) with lava (on surface)</li>
  <li>Mixing boundary types and their effects</li>
  <li>Not linking movement to landform creation</li>
  <li>Ignoring diagrams in explanations</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> KEY INSIGHT</h3>
<p>
Continents are still moving today at a rate of a few centimeters per year due to tectonic plate motion.
</p>
<hr>
<h3> FINAL MASTER IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Plate Tectonics = Earth’s moving crust system
Divergent → creation of crust  
Convergent → destruction + mountains  
Transform → earthquakes  
Result = dynamic Earth surface
</pre>
  ',
  '[{"type":"written","q":"What was Pangaea?","ans":"A supercontinent","sol":"Pangaea was a single giant landmass before continents split.","mark":"Correct"},{"type":"written","q":"Name the ocean that surrounded Pangaea.","ans":"Panthalassa","sol":"Panthalassa was the global ocean around Pangaea.","mark":"Correct"},{"type":"written","q":"What is the Ring of Fire?","ans":"Volcanic zone around Pacific Plate","sol":"It is a zone of earthquakes and volcanoes around the Pacific plate.","mark":"Correct"},{"type":"written","q":"What causes plates to move?","ans":"Convection currents in mantle","sol":"Heat in the mantle creates currents that move tectonic plates.","mark":"Correct"}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> physical -> Volcanoes & earthquakes
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'physical',
  'Volcanoes & earthquakes',
  '<h2>Volcanoes & Earthquakes (Advanced System)</h2>
 <h2> Volcanoes & Earthquakes (Advanced Mastery Notes – Exam Structured)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Volcanoes and earthquakes are natural hazards caused by movement and interaction of tectonic plates, leading to pressure build-up and release of energy within the Earth’s crust.
</p>
<h3> VOLCANIC PROCESS (STEP-BY-STEP)</h3>
<pre>
1. Magma forms in the mantle due to intense heat
2. Pressure builds up beneath the crust
3. Cracks (vents) form in the Earth''s crust
4. Magma rises through the vent
5. Eruption occurs (lava, ash, gases released)
6. Lava cools and solidifies → new landforms
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> VOLCANIC STRUCTURE (DIAGRAM EXPLANATION)</h3>
<div style="text-align:center;margin:1rem 0;">
<svg viewBox="0 0 340 300" width="340" height="300" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:12px;background:#1a1a2e;">
  <!-- Ash Cloud -->
  <ellipse cx="170" cy="32" rx="40" ry="18" fill="#666" opacity="0.7"/>
  <ellipse cx="145" cy="28" rx="22" ry="13" fill="#888" opacity="0.6"/>
  <ellipse cx="195" cy="28" rx="22" ry="13" fill="#777" opacity="0.6"/>
  <text x="170" y="16" fill="#ccc" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle" font-family="sans-serif">Ash Cloud</text>

  <!-- Lava Ejection -->
  <line x1="170" y1="88" x2="155" y2="55" stroke="#ff6b1a" stroke-width="3" stroke-dasharray="4,2"/>
  <line x1="170" y1="88" x2="185" y2="50" stroke="#ff6b1a" stroke-width="3" stroke-dasharray="4,2"/>

  <!-- Volcano body -->
  <polygon points="170,88 60,240 280,240" fill="#5a3e28"/>
  <!-- Crater opening -->
  <ellipse cx="170" cy="90" rx="22" ry="8" fill="#c0392b"/>

  <!-- Vent (main conduit) -->
  <rect x="162" y="90" width="16" height="100" fill="#8b0000" opacity="0.8"/>

  <!-- Magma chamber -->
  <ellipse cx="170" cy="210" rx="55" ry="28" fill="#ff4500"/>
  <ellipse cx="170" cy="210" rx="40" ry="19" fill="#ff6b1a" opacity="0.7"/>

  <!-- Lava flows on sides -->
  <path d="M148,130 Q100,180 80,240" stroke="#ff4500" stroke-width="6" fill="none" stroke-linecap="round"/>
  <path d="M192,130 Q240,180 260,240" stroke="#ff4500" stroke-width="6" fill="none" stroke-linecap="round"/>

  <!-- Ground -->
  <rect x="50" y="240" width="240" height="12" fill="#4a3728" rx="4"/>

  <!-- Labels -->
  <text x="208" y="94" fill="#ffcc00" font-size="10" font-family="sans-serif" font-weight="bold">← Crater</text>
  <text x="208" y="148" fill="#ffa07a" font-size="10" font-family="sans-serif">← Vent</text>
  <text x="155" y="216" fill="#fff" font-size="10" font-family="sans-serif" text-anchor="middle">Magma Chamber</text>
  <text x="170" y="6" fill="#aaa" font-size="11" font-family="sans-serif" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold">VOLCANO CROSS-SECTION</text>
</svg>
</div>

<hr>
<h3> TYPES OF LAVA</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Acidic Lava:</b> Thick, slow-moving, high silica content → explosive eruptions</li>
  <li><b>Basic Lava:</b> Thin, fast-flowing, low silica content → less explosive eruptions</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> TYPES OF VOLCANOES</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Shield Volcano:</b> Gentle slopes, basic lava, quiet eruptions</li>
  <li><b>Composite Volcano:</b> Steep sides, alternating layers, explosive eruptions</li>
  <li><b>Cinder Cone:</b> Small, steep, short-lived eruptions</li>
</ul>
<hr>

<h3> INTERNAL IGNEOUS FEATURES</h3>

<h4>1. Laccolith</h4>
<div style="text-align:center;margin:1rem 0;">
<svg viewBox="0 0 300 150" width="300" height="150" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:8px;background:#1a1a2e;">
  <!-- Surrounding rock layers -->
  <rect x="20" y="20" width="260" height="110" fill="#3e3832"/>
  <rect x="20" y="55" width="260" height="35" fill="#5a4d41"/>
  <rect x="20" y="90" width="260" height="40" fill="#7f7267"/>
  
  <!-- Curved/domed layers (forced upward by Laccolith) -->
  <path d="M 20,55 L 80,55 Q 150,20 220,55 L 280,55" stroke="#3e3832" stroke-width="35" fill="none"/>
  <path d="M 20,90 L 80,90 Q 150,50 220,90 L 280,90" stroke="#5a4d41" stroke-width="35" fill="none"/>
  
  <!-- Laccolith Magma Dome -->
  <path d="M 80,105 Q 150,55 220,105 Z" fill="#ff4500" stroke="#ff6b1a" stroke-width="2"/>
  <!-- Feeder dyke -->
  <rect x="145" y="105" width="10" height="25" fill="#ff4500"/>
  
  <!-- Labels -->
  <text x="150" y="85" fill="#fff" font-size="10" font-family="sans-serif" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold">LACCOLITH (Dome)</text>
  <text x="150" y="125" fill="#ffcc00" font-size="8" font-family="sans-serif" text-anchor="middle">Feeder Conduit</text>
</svg>
</div>

<h4>2. Lopolith</h4>
<div style="text-align:center;margin:1rem 0;">
<svg viewBox="0 0 300 150" width="300" height="150" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:8px;background:#1a1a2e;">
  <!-- Surrounding rock layers -->
  <rect x="20" y="20" width="260" height="110" fill="#3e3832"/>
  <rect x="20" y="55" width="260" height="35" fill="#5a4d41"/>
  <rect x="20" y="90" width="260" height="40" fill="#7f7267"/>
  
  <!-- Saucer-shaped bent layers (Lopolith weight sinking) -->
  <path d="M 20,55 L 60,55 Q 150,90 240,55 L 280,55" stroke="#3e3832" stroke-width="35" fill="none"/>
  <path d="M 20,90 L 60,90 Q 150,125 240,90 L 280,90" stroke="#5a4d41" stroke-width="35" fill="none"/>
  
  <!-- Lopolith Magma Basin -->
  <path d="M 60,70 Q 150,110 240,70 Q 150,90 60,70 Z" fill="#ff4500" stroke="#ff6b1a" stroke-width="2"/>
  <!-- Feeder dyke -->
  <rect x="145" y="95" width="10" height="35" fill="#ff4500"/>
  
  <!-- Labels -->
  <text x="150" y="70" fill="#fff" font-size="10" font-family="sans-serif" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold">LOPOLITH (Basin)</text>
</svg>
</div>

<h4>3. Batholith</h4>
<div style="text-align:center;margin:1rem 0;">
<svg viewBox="0 0 300 150" width="300" height="150" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:8px;background:#1a1a2e;">
  <!-- Surrounding rock layers -->
  <rect x="20" y="20" width="260" height="110" fill="#5a4d41"/>
  
  <!-- Batholith giant chamber -->
  <path d="M 40,130 Q 50,60 100,50 Q 150,30 200,60 Q 250,70 260,130 Z" fill="#ff4500" stroke="#ff6b1a" stroke-width="2"/>
  
  <!-- Labels -->
  <text x="150" y="90" fill="#fff" font-size="11" font-family="sans-serif" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold">BATHOLITH</text>
  <text x="150" y="105" fill="#fff" font-size="8" font-family="sans-serif" text-anchor="middle">(Large Intrusive Mass)</text>
</svg>
</div>

<h4>4. Dyke</h4>
<div style="text-align:center;margin:1rem 0;">
<svg viewBox="0 0 300 150" width="300" height="150" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:8px;background:#1a1a2e;">
  <!-- Surrounding rock layers -->
  <rect x="20" y="20" width="260" height="30" fill="#3e3832"/>
  <rect x="20" y="50" width="260" height="35" fill="#5a4d41"/>
  <rect x="20" y="85" width="260" height="45" fill="#7f7267"/>
  
  <!-- Vertical Dyke cutting across -->
  <rect x="140" y="20" width="20" height="110" fill="#ff4500" stroke="#ff6b1a" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  
  <!-- Labels -->
  <text x="170" y="75" fill="#fff" font-size="10" font-family="sans-serif" font-weight="bold">DYKE (Vertical)</text>
</svg>
</div>

<h4>5. Sill</h4>
<div style="text-align:center;margin:1rem 0;">
<svg viewBox="0 0 300 150" width="300" height="150" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:8px;background:#1a1a2e;">
  <!-- Surrounding rock layers -->
  <rect x="20" y="20" width="260" height="40" fill="#3e3832"/>
  <rect x="20" y="60" width="260" height="70" fill="#7f7267"/>
  
  <!-- Horizontal Sill between layers -->
  <rect x="20" y="60" width="260" height="15" fill="#ff4500" stroke="#ff6b1a" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  
  <!-- Labels -->
  <text x="150" y="72" fill="#fff" font-size="10" font-family="sans-serif" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold">SILL (Horizontal)</text>
</svg>
</div>
<hr>
<h3> GEYSERS & HOT SPRINGS</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Hot Springs:</b> Heated groundwater emerging gently at the surface</li>
  <li><b>Geysers:</b> Intermittent explosive eruptions of hot water and steam</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> RELATIONSHIP: VOLCANOES & EARTHQUAKES</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Earthquakes create cracks allowing magma to rise → volcano formation</li>
  <li>Volcanic pressure can trigger earthquakes during eruptions</li>
  <li>Both are caused by tectonic plate movement and internal Earth energy</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> EFFECTS OF VOLCANIC ACTIVITY</h3>
<h4> Positive Effects</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Fertile soils for agriculture</li>
  <li>Geothermal energy production</li>
  <li>Mineral deposits (gold, copper, sulfur)</li>
  <li>Tourism attraction sites</li>
  <li>New land formation</li>
  <li>Scientific research opportunities</li>
  <li>Building materials (basalt, pumice)</li>
  <li>Hot springs for recreation</li>
  <li>Forest growth on volcanic soils</li>
  <li>Natural landscape beauty</li>
</ul>

<h4> Negative Effects</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Loss of human life</li>
  <li>Destruction of settlements</li>
  <li>Crop failure</li>
  <li>Air pollution from ash and gases</li>
  <li>Climate cooling after major eruptions</li>
  <li>Displacement of populations</li>
  <li>Infrastructure destruction</li>
  <li>Lava fires and burning of land</li>
  <li>Water contamination</li>
  <li>Economic losses</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> EFFECTS OF EARTHQUAKES</h3>

<h4> Positive Effects</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Formation of new landforms (faults, rift valleys)</li>
  <li>Exposure of mineral resources</li>
  <li>Creation of lakes due to faulting</li>
  <li>Improved building codes and technology</li>
  <li>Scientific understanding of Earth structure</li>
  <li>Access to geothermal energy</li>
  <li>Mountain building in some regions</li>
  <li>Improved disaster preparedness systems</li>
  <li>Revealing underground water channels</li>
  <li>Geological research opportunities</li>
</ul>

<h4> Negative Effects</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Loss of lives</li>
  <li>Collapse of buildings</li>
  <li>Road and bridge destruction</li>
  <li>Tsunamis (if undersea)</li>
  <li>Fire outbreaks from ruptured gas lines</li>
  <li>Economic disruption</li>
  <li>Homelessness</li>
  <li>Disruption of services (water, electricity)</li>
  <li>Psychological trauma</li>
  <li>Long-term rebuilding costs</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> RELIEF BARRIER EFFECT (LINK TO TECTONICS)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Mountains block moist winds → windward side receives heavy rainfall</li>
  <li>Leeward side becomes dry (rain shadow effect)</li>
  <li>Rugged volcanic terrain makes transport and settlement difficult</li>
</ul>
<h3> COMMON EXAM MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Confusing magma (inside Earth) with lava (on surface)</li>
  <li>Mixing volcanic and earthquake effects</li>
  <li>Listing points without explanation</li>
  <li>Ignoring diagrams in answers</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> FINAL MASTER IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Volcanoes + Earthquakes = Tectonic Energy Release System
Cause: Plate movement  
Process: Pressure build-up → release  
Result: Landforms + hazards + benefits
</pre>
  ',
  '[{"type":"written","q":"What is magma?","ans":"Molten rock below Earth''s surface","sol":"Magma is molten rock inside the Earth before eruption.","mark":"Correct"},{"type":"written","q":"Difference between acidic and basic lava?","ans":"Acidic is thick and explosive; basic is thin and fluid","sol":"Acidic lava flows slowly and erupts violently, basic lava flows easily.","mark":"Correct"},{"type":"written","q":"Name one positive effect of volcanoes.","ans":"Fertile soil","sol":"Volcanic ash improves soil fertility for farming.","mark":"Correct"},{"type":"written","q":"What is a dyke?","ans":"Vertical igneous intrusion","sol":"A dyke is magma that cuts vertically across rock layers.","mark":"Correct"}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> physical -> Mountains & rivers
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'physical',
  'Mountains & rivers',
  '<h2>Mountains & Rivers (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>
  <h3> 1. Mountains</h3>
  <p>Mountains are large landforms that rise high above the surrounding terrain, usually forming a peak.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Fold Mountains:</b> Formed by the folding of rock layers (e.g., Himalayas, Alps, Andes).</li>
    <li><b>Block Mountains (Horsts):</b> Formed by faulting where blocks of crust are pushed up (e.g., Ruwenzori, Vosges).</li>
    <li><b>Volcanic Mountains:</b> Formed by the accumulation of volcanic material (e.g., Mt. Kilimanjaro, Mt. Fuji).</li>
    <li><b>Residual Mountains:</b> Formed by the erosion of surrounding areas, leaving a resistant core (e.g., Mt. Kenya).</li>
  </ul>
  <hr>
  <h3> 2. Rivers</h3>
  <p>A river is a natural flowing stream of water moving from a source to a mouth.</p>
  <h4> Upper Course (Youthful Stage)</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>Dominant process: Vertical erosion.</li>
    <li>Features: V-shaped valleys, waterfalls, rapids, gorges, interlocking spurs.</li>
  </ul>
  <h4> Middle Course (Mature Stage)</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>Dominant process: Lateral erosion and transportation.</li>
    <li>Features: Wide valleys, meanders, oxbow lakes.</li>
  </ul>
  <h4> Lower Course (Old Stage)</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>Dominant process: Deposition.</li>
    <li>Features: Floodplains, levees, deltas, estuaries.</li>
  </ul>
  <hr>
  <h3> Common Exam Mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Confusing the stages of a river and their dominant processes.</li>
    <li>Misidentifying the type of mountain formation.</li>
  </ul>
  ',
  '[{"q":"Name two types of mountains. (2 marks)","ans":"Fold mountains and volcanic mountains.","hint":"Think how they are formed.","reason":"Basic classification."},{"q":"What is the dominant process in the upper course of a river? (1 mark)","ans":"Vertical erosion.","hint":"Think downward cutting.","reason":"Process identification."},{"q":"How is an oxbow lake formed? (3 marks)","ans":"A meander loop is cut off from the main river channel due to continuous erosion and deposition.","hint":"Think meander changes.","reason":"Formation process."},{"q":"Give an example of a fold mountain. (1 mark)","ans":"Himalayas (or Andes/Alps).","hint":"Think major world ranges.","reason":"Identification."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> physical -> Climate zones
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'physical',
  'Climate zones',
  '<h2>Climate Zones</h2>
 <h2> Climate Zones & Global Atmospheric Systems </h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Climate zones are regions of the Earth with similar long-term weather patterns, controlled by latitude, global atmospheric circulation, and physical geographical factors.
</p>
<h3> GLOBAL HEAT BELTS & CLIMATE ZONES</h3>
<pre>
        90°N    Polar Zone (Very cold, low sun angle)
        66.5°N Arctic Circle
        30°N    Subtropical Desert Zone (High pressure)
        23.5°N Tropic of Cancer
         0°     Equatorial Zone (High temperature & rainfall)
        23.5°S Tropic of Capricorn
        30°S    Subtropical Desert Zone
        66.5°S Antarctic Circle
        90°S    Polar Zone
</pre>

<h4> Key Idea</h4>
<p>
Climate zones are arranged mainly according to latitude because the amount of solar energy received varies from Equator to poles.
</p>
<hr>
<h3> INSOLATION (SOLAR ENERGY)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Equator receives direct rays → high temperature</li>
  <li>Poles receive slanting rays → low temperature</li>
  <li>Unequal heating drives global wind systems</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> GLOBAL PRESSURE & WIND BELTS</h3>

<pre>
0°     ↑ Rising warm air → LOW pressure → rain
30°    ↓ Sinking air → HIGH pressure → deserts
60°    ↑ Rising air → LOW pressure → rainfall
90°    ↓ Cold sinking air → HIGH pressure → dry

Winds:
→ Trade Winds (0°–30°)
→ Westerlies (30°–60°)
→ Polar Easterlies (60°–90°)
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> MAJOR CLIMATE ZONES</h3>
<h4> 1. Equatorial Climate</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Hot and wet throughout the year</li>
  <li>Low pressure zone (ITCZ)</li>
  <li>Daily convectional rainfall</li>
  <li>Dense rainforest vegetation</li>
</ul>
<h4> 2. Tropical Savannah Climate</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Distinct wet and dry seasons</li>
  <li>Controlled by ITCZ movement</li>
  <li>Grasslands with scattered trees</li>
</ul>
<h4> 3. Desert Climate</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Very low rainfall</li>
  <li>High pressure at 30° latitudes</li>
  <li>Large diurnal temperature range</li>
  <li>Clear skies and intense heat</li>
</ul>
<h4> 4. Temperate Climate</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Moderate temperatures</li>
  <li>Four distinct seasons</li>
  <li>Westerly winds influence weather</li>
  <li>Mixed forests and grasslands</li>
</ul>
<h4> 5. Polar Climate</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Extremely cold conditions</li>
  <li>Very low precipitation (polar desert)</li>
  <li>Short, cold summers</li>
  <li>Tundra vegetation</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> TYPES OF RAINFALL</h3>
<h4>1. Convectional Rainfall</h4>
<pre>
 heats ground
↑ warm air rises
 condensation
 heavy rainfall
</pre>
<h4>2. Relief (Orographic) Rainfall</h4>
<pre>
Windward side (wet)     Leeward side (dry)
                           
     /\
    /  \  mountain
   /    \
</pre>

<h4>3. Frontal Rainfall</h4>
<pre>
Warm air rises over cold air → condensation → rain
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> FACTORS AFFECTING CLIMATE</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Latitude:</b> Controls temperature (Equator hot, poles cold)</li>
  <li><b>Altitude:</b> Higher places are colder</li>
  <li><b>Distance from sea:</b> Coastal areas have moderate climate</li>
  <li><b>Ocean currents:</b> Warm = hot & wet, Cold = cool & dry</li>
  <li><b>Relief:</b> Mountains influence rainfall distribution</li>
  <li><b>Winds:</b> Move heat and moisture globally</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> OCEAN CURRENTS EFFECTS</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Warm currents increase temperature and rainfall</li>
  <li>Cold currents reduce rainfall and can create deserts</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> MICROCLIMATE</h3>
<p>
A microclimate is a small area with climate conditions different from surrounding regions, influenced by vegetation, buildings, and water bodies.
</p>
<hr>
<h3> CLIMATE CHANGE LINK</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Rising global temperatures shift climate zones</li>
  <li>Changes rainfall distribution patterns</li>
  <li>Increases extreme weather events (droughts, floods)</li>
</ul>
<h3> COMMON EXAM MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Confusing weather with climate</li>
  <li>Mixing wind belts with pressure belts incorrectly</li>
  <li>Forgetting latitude influence on climate</li>
  <li>Listing rainfall types without explanation</li>
</ul>
<h3> FINAL MASTER IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Climate = long-term weather patterns
Controlled by:
 Latitude (energy)
 Winds (movement)
 Oceans (moderation)
 Relief (barriers)
System = global energy + circulation balance
</pre>
  ',
  '[{"type":"written","q":"What is ITCZ?","ans":"Intertropical Convergence Zone","sol":"It is a low-pressure zone where trade winds meet causing rainfall.","mark":"Correct"},{"type":"written","q":"Why is the equator hotter than the poles?","ans":"Direct sunlight","sol":"Sun rays are direct at equator but slanted at poles.","mark":"Correct"},{"type":"written","q":"Name two types of rainfall.","ans":"Relief and convectional","sol":"Rainfall types include convectional, relief, and frontal.","mark":"Any two"},{"type":"written","q":"What causes deserts at 30° latitude?","ans":"High pressure descending air","sol":"Dry sinking air prevents rainfall.","mark":"Correct concept"}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> human -> Population distribution
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'human',
  'Population distribution',
  '<h2>Population Distribution (Advanced)</h2>
<h2> Population Distribution (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>
<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Population distribution refers to the way people are spread across the Earth’s surface, which can be uneven due to physical, economic, and social factors.
</p>
<h3> WORLD POPULATION DISTRIBUTION PATTERN</h3>
<pre>
HIGH POPULATION AREAS:
- Asia (China , India )
- Europe
- Southeast Asia

LOW POPULATION AREAS:
-  Deserts (Sahara, Arabian Desert)
-  Polar regions (Arctic, Antarctica)
-  High mountain regions (Himalayas)
-  Dense tropical forests (Amazon Basin)
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> EXPLANATION OF PATTERNS</h3>
<h4> High Population Areas</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Favourable climate (moderate temperatures)</li>
  <li>Fertile soils for agriculture</li>
  <li>Good transport and industries</li>
  <li>High job availability and urban growth</li>
</ul>
<h4> Low Population Areas</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Extreme climates (too hot or too cold)</li>
  <li>Poor soils and vegetation</li>
  <li>Limited water supply</li>
  <li>Difficult terrain (mountains, forests)</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> FACTORS AFFECTING POPULATION DISTRIBUTION</h3>
<h4> Physical Factors</h4>
<ul>
  <li><b>Climate:</b> People prefer moderate climates, avoid extremes</li>
  <li><b>Relief:</b> Flat land supports farming and construction</li>
  <li><b>Soil fertility:</b> Fertile soils support agriculture and settlement</li>
  <li><b>Water availability:</b> Rivers and lakes attract settlements</li>
</ul>
<h4> Human & Economic Factors</h4>
<ul>
  <li><b>Resources:</b> Minerals and energy sources attract industries</li>
  <li><b>Employment:</b> Job opportunities increase population concentration</li>
  <li><b>Transport:</b> Good infrastructure supports settlement growth</li>
  <li><b>Urbanisation:</b> Cities attract people from rural areas</li>
</ul>
<h3> COMMON EXAM MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Confusing distribution with population growth</li>
  <li>Listing factors without explanation</li>
  <li>Ignoring human factors (only writing physical factors)</li>
  <li>Giving examples without linking to reasons</li>
</ul>
<h3> FINAL MASTER IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Population distribution = uneven spread of people
High density → good climate + jobs + fertile land  
Low density → extreme climate + poor resources + harsh terrain  
Human + Physical factors = final pattern
</pre>
  ',
  '[{"q":"What is population distribution? (2 marks)","ans":"It refers to how people are spread across the Earth’s surface.","hint":"Think how people are spread.","reason":"Tests understanding of the basic definition."},{"q":"Name two densely populated regions in the world. (2 marks)","ans":"Asia and Europe.","hint":"Think Asia and Europe.","reason":"Tests knowledge of global population patterns."},{"q":"State two sparsely populated areas. (2 marks)","ans":"Deserts and polar regions.","hint":"Think extreme environments.","reason":"Checks understanding of low population areas."},{"q":"State three factors affecting population distribution. (3 marks)","ans":"Climate, relief, and water supply.","hint":"Think climate, water, land.","reason":"Tests ability to identify influencing factors."},{"q":"Why do people prefer settling in areas with fertile soil? (2 marks)","ans":"Because fertile soil supports agriculture and food production.","hint":"Think farming.","reason":"Tests understanding of human-environment interaction."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> human -> Urbanisation
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'human',
  'Urbanisation',
  '<h2>Urbanisation (Advanced)</h2>
  <h2> Urbanisation (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>
<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Urbanisation is the increase in the proportion of people living in towns and cities, caused by rural–urban migration and natural population growth within urban areas.
</p>
<h3> PROCESS OF URBANISATION</h3>
<pre>
Rural Area  →  Migration  →  Urban Growth

               ‍           
 (farm life)   (movement)   (city expansion)
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> CAUSES OF URBANISATION</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Employment opportunities:</b> Jobs in industries, trade, and services</li>
  <li><b>Education:</b> Better schools, colleges, and universities in towns</li>
  <li><b>Healthcare:</b> Improved hospitals and medical services</li>
  <li><b>Industrial growth:</b> Factories attract workers to urban areas</li>
  <li><b>Rural push factors:</b> Poverty, unemployment, drought, insecurity</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> PROBLEMS OF URBANISATION</h3>
<h4> Social & Economic Problems</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Overcrowding and housing shortages</li>
  <li>Unemployment and informal jobs</li>
  <li>Growth of slums and informal settlements</li>
</ul>
<h4> Environmental Problems</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Air, water, and noise pollution</li>
  <li>Improper waste disposal</li>
  <li>Traffic congestion</li>
</ul>
<h4> Service Pressure</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Overstretched schools and hospitals</li>
  <li>Inadequate water and sanitation systems</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> BENEFITS OF URBANISATION</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Economic growth:</b> More businesses and industries develop</li>
  <li><b>Employment:</b> Creation of jobs in different sectors</li>
  <li><b>Infrastructure development:</b> Roads, housing, electricity, transport</li>
  <li><b>Improved services:</b> Better access to education and healthcare</li>
  <li><b>Innovation:</b> Growth of technology and modern industries</li>
</ul>
<h3> COMMON EXAM MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Confusing urbanisation with urban growth only</li>
  <li>Mixing causes and effects</li>
  <li>Not explaining answers fully in structured questions</li>
  <li>Ignoring environmental impacts</li>
</ul>
<h3> FINAL MASTER IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Urbanisation = Rural → Urban shift
Causes: jobs + services + migration  
Effects: growth + problems + development pressure  
Balance = development vs sustainability
</pre>
',
  '[{"q":"What is urbanisation? (2 marks)","ans":"Urbanisation is the increase in the proportion of people living in towns and cities.","hint":"Think movement to towns and cities.","reason":"Tests understanding of the basic definition of urbanisation."},{"q":"State two causes of urbanisation. (2 marks)","ans":"Job opportunities and better education facilities.","hint":"Think jobs and services.","reason":"Tests knowledge of factors attracting people to urban areas."},{"q":"State two problems caused by urbanisation. (2 marks)","ans":"Overcrowding and growth of slums.","hint":"Think overcrowding and slums.","reason":"Tests understanding of negative effects of urban growth."},{"q":"State two benefits of urbanisation. (2 marks)","ans":"Economic growth and improved infrastructure.","hint":"Think development and services.","reason":"Tests understanding of positive impacts of urbanisation."},{"q":"Why does urbanisation lead to pressure on services? (3 marks)","ans":"Because rapid population growth increases demand for housing, schools, hospitals, and other services beyond what cities can supply.","hint":"Think population increase in cities.","reason":"Tests higher-order understanding of cause-effect relationships."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> human -> Migration
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'human',
  'Migration',
  '<h2>Migration (Advanced)</h2>
<h2> Migration (Push & Pull Factors) — Advanced Mastery Notes</h2>
<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Migration is the movement of people from one place to another, either permanently or temporarily, for various reasons such as work, safety, or better living conditions.
</p>
<h3> PUSH & PULL FACTORS</h3>
<pre>
PUSH FACTORS (Force people to leave)      PULL FACTORS (Attract people)

- Unemployment                          - Job opportunities
- Drought / famine                      - Better living conditions
- Conflict / insecurity                 - Better education
- Poor services (health, water)         - Better healthcare
- Overpopulation                        - Security and stability
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> DEEP UNDERSTANDING</h3>

<h4> Push Factors</h4>
<p>
These are negative conditions in the place of origin that force people to leave.
</p>
<h4> Pull Factors</h4>
<p>
These are positive conditions in the destination that attract people.
</p>
<hr>
<h3> EFFECTS OF MIGRATION</h3>

<h4> Effects on Destination (Receiving Area)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Population increase in urban areas</li>
  <li>Improved labour supply</li>
  <li>Economic growth due to increased workforce</li>
  <li>Pressure on housing, schools, and healthcare</li>
  <li>Urban congestion and slum development</li>
</ul>

<h4> Effects on Origin (Sending Area)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Reduction in population</li>
  <li>Loss of skilled workers (brain drain)</li>
  <li>Reduced pressure on local resources</li>
  <li>Decline in economic productivity</li>
</ul>
<h3> COMMON EXAM MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Confusing push and pull factors</li>
  <li>Not explaining effects fully</li>
  <li>Mixing migration causes with effects</li>
  <li>Giving examples without explanation</li>
</ul>
<h3> FINAL MASTER IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Migration = Movement of people
Push → force to leave  
Pull → attract to move  
Effects = changes in population, economy, and services
</pre>
',
  '[{"q":"What is migration? (2 marks)","ans":"Migration is the movement of people from one place to another either permanently or temporarily.","hint":"Think movement of people between places.","reason":"Tests understanding of the basic definition of migration."},{"q":"Name two types of migration. (2 marks)","ans":"Internal migration and international migration.","hint":"Think within country and between countries.","reason":"Tests knowledge of classification of migration."},{"q":"State two push factors of migration. (2 marks)","ans":"Unemployment and drought.","hint":"Think reasons people leave areas.","reason":"Tests understanding of factors forcing people to leave."},{"q":"State two pull factors of migration. (2 marks)","ans":"Job opportunities and better education.","hint":"Think reasons people are attracted to areas.","reason":"Tests understanding of factors attracting people."},{"q":"State two effects of migration. (2 marks)","ans":"Population increase in urban areas and brain drain in rural areas.","hint":"Think cities and rural areas.","reason":"Tests ability to explain consequences of migration."},{"q":"Why does migration lead to brain drain? (3 marks)","ans":"Because skilled and educated people leave rural or home areas to seek better opportunities elsewhere, reducing skilled labour in the origin area.","hint":"Think skilled people leaving.","reason":"Tests higher-order understanding of economic impact."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> human -> Settlement
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'human',
  'Settlement',
  '<h2>Settlement (Advanced)</h2>
<h2> Settlement (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>
<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Settlement refers to places where people live. These can range from small rural villages to large urban cities, depending on population size, function, and development.
</p>
<h3> TYPES OF SETTLEMENT</h3>
<h4>1. Rural Settlement</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Found in countryside areas</li>
  <li>Low population density</li>
  <li>Main activities: farming, fishing, livestock keeping</li>
  <li>Examples: villages, scattered homesteads, farms</li>
</ul>

<h4>2. Urban Settlement</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Found in towns and cities</li>
  <li>High population density</li>
  <li>Main activities: industry, trade, services</li>
  <li>Examples: Nairobi, Mombasa, Kisumu</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> SETTLEMENT PATTERNS</h3>
<pre>
Nucleated:   ●●●●● (clustered around a central point)
Dispersed:   ●   ●     ●     ● (scattered settlements)
Linear:      ●●●●● (along roads, rivers, or valleys)
</pre>
<h4> Explanation</h4>
<ul>
  <li><b>Nucleated:</b> Houses grouped together, often around water, roads, or markets</li>
  <li><b>Dispersed:</b> Houses spread far apart, common in farming areas</li>
  <li><b>Linear:</b> Houses arranged in a line along physical or human features</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> FACTORS INFLUENCING SETTLEMENT</h3>

<ul>
  <li><b>Water Supply:</b> Settlements are found near rivers, lakes, or boreholes</li>
  <li><b>Relief:</b> Flat or gently sloping land is preferred for construction and farming</li>
  <li><b>Soil Fertility:</b> Fertile soils attract farming communities</li>
  <li><b>Transport & Communication:</b> Roads and railways encourage settlement growth</li>
  <li><b>Security:</b> People avoid areas with conflict or danger</li>
  <li><b>Employment:</b> Jobs attract people to towns and cities</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<p>
People settle in areas where conditions support survival, safety, and economic opportunities.
</p>
<h3> COMMON EXAM MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Confusing rural and urban settlements</li>
  <li>Mixing settlement patterns (linear vs nucleated)</li>
  <li>Not explaining answers fully in structured questions</li>
  <li>Ignoring physical and human factors separately</li>
</ul>
<h3> FINAL MASTER IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Settlement = Where people live
Patterns:
Nucleated → clustered  
Dispersed → scattered  
Linear → along features  
Choice of settlement = survival + resources + safety + jobs
</pre>',
  '[{"q":"What is settlement? (2 marks)","ans":"Settlement refers to places where people live such as villages, towns, and cities.","hint":"Think places where people live.","reason":"Tests understanding of the basic definition of settlement."},{"q":"Name two types of settlement. (2 marks)","ans":"Rural settlement and urban settlement.","hint":"Think rural and urban.","reason":"Tests knowledge of classification of settlements."},{"q":"Name three settlement patterns. (3 marks)","ans":"Nucleated, linear, and dispersed.","hint":"Think cluster, line, scattered.","reason":"Tests ability to identify settlement patterns."},{"q":"State two factors influencing settlement. (2 marks)","ans":"Availability of water and transport routes.","hint":"Think water and transport.","reason":"Tests understanding of settlement location factors."},{"q":"Why do people prefer settling in areas with flat land? (2 marks)","ans":"Because flat land is easier for construction and farming activities.","hint":"Think building and farming.","reason":"Tests understanding of physical factors affecting settlement."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> human -> Economic activities
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'human',
  'Economic activities',
  '<h2>Economic Activities (Advanced)</h2>
<h2>Economic Activities (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>
<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Economic activities are the ways in which people earn a living through the production, distribution, and consumption of goods and services in an economy.
</p>
<h3> TYPES OF ECONOMIC ACTIVITIES</h3>
<h4>1. Primary Activities</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Involve extraction of raw materials from nature</li>
  <li>Examples: farming, fishing, mining, forestry</li>
  <li>Depend directly on natural resources</li>
</ul>
<h4>2. Secondary Activities</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Involve processing raw materials into finished products</li>
  <li>Examples: manufacturing, construction, factories</li>
  <li>Add value to raw materials</li>
</ul>
<h4>3. Tertiary Activities</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Involve provision of services</li>
  <li>Examples: transport, banking, trade, tourism</li>
  <li>Support primary and secondary sectors</li>
</ul>
<h4>4. Quaternary Activities</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Knowledge-based services</li>
  <li>Examples: ICT, research, education, innovation</li>
  <li>Focus on information and intellectual work</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> FLOW OF ECONOMIC ACTIVITIES</h3>

<pre>
Raw Materials → Processing → Services → Consumer

                               
   Primary        Secondary     Tertiary
</pre>

<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Each stage adds value before goods reach the consumer.</p>

<hr>

<h3> FACTORS AFFECTING ECONOMIC ACTIVITIES</h3>

<ul>
  <li><b>Natural Resources:</b> Availability of raw materials like minerals and fertile land</li>
  <li><b>Capital:</b> Money needed to start and run businesses</li>
  <li><b>Technology:</b> Machines and skills that improve productivity</li>
  <li><b>Market:</b> Demand for goods and services</li>
  <li><b>Transport & Infrastructure:</b> Roads, railways, and communication systems</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> IMPORTANCE OF ECONOMIC ACTIVITIES</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Creates employment opportunities</li>
  <li>Generates income for individuals and governments</li>
  <li>Promotes industrial and national development</li>
  <li>Improves living standards</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> COMMON EXAM MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Confusing tertiary and quaternary activities</li>
  <li>Thinking primary activities involve manufacturing</li>
  <li>Not linking factors to real-life examples</li>
  <li>Failing to explain answers fully in structured questions</li>
</ul>
',
  '[{"q":"What are economic activities? (2 marks)","ans":"Economic activities are ways in which people earn a living through production, distribution, and consumption of goods and services.","hint":"Think ways people earn a living.","reason":"Tests understanding of the basic definition."},{"q":"Name two primary economic activities. (2 marks)","ans":"Farming and mining.","hint":"Think extraction of resources.","reason":"Tests knowledge of primary sector activities."},{"q":"Name two tertiary economic activities. (2 marks)","ans":"Transport and banking.","hint":"Think services.","reason":"Tests understanding of service-based activities."},{"q":"State two factors affecting economic activities. (2 marks)","ans":"Availability of resources and transport.","hint":"Think resources and transport.","reason":"Tests understanding of influencing factors."},{"q":"Why is capital important in economic activities? (2 marks)","ans":"Capital is needed to start and run businesses and purchase equipment.","hint":"Think money and investment.","reason":"Tests understanding of economic support systems."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> mapwork -> Latitude & longitude
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'mapwork',
  'Latitude & longitude',
  '<h2>Latitude & Longitude</h2>
<h2> Latitude & Longitude (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>
<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Latitude and longitude are imaginary lines used to locate places on the Earth’s surface using a coordinate system.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4> Key Idea</h4>
<ul>
  <li><b>Latitude:</b> Measures distance north or south of the Equator</li>
  <li><b>Longitude:</b> Measures distance east or west of the Greenwich Meridian</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> Direction System</h3>
<pre>
   Latitude → horizontal lines (East-West direction)
   Longitude → vertical lines (North-South direction)
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> Important Reference Lines</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>0° Latitude:</b> Equator → divides Earth into Northern and Southern Hemispheres</li>
  <li><b>0° Longitude:</b> Greenwich Meridian → divides Earth into Eastern and Western Hemispheres</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> Key Characteristics</h3>
<h4>Latitude (Parallels)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Run east to west</li>
  <li>Never meet (parallel lines)</li>
  <li>Measured in degrees from 0° to 90°</li>
</ul>
<h4>Longitude (Meridians)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Run north to south</li>
  <li>Meet at the poles</li>
  <li>Measured in degrees from 0° to 180°</li>
</ul>
  ',
  '[{"q":"In which direction do lines of latitude run? (1 mark)","ans":"East–West.","hint":"They run horizontally around the Earth.","reason":"Tests understanding of the orientation of latitude lines."},{"q":"In which direction do lines of longitude run? (1 mark)","ans":"North–South.","hint":"They run from pole to pole.","reason":"Ensures distinction between longitude and latitude."},{"q":"What is 0° latitude? (1 mark)","ans":"The Equator.","hint":"It divides the Earth into two equal halves.","reason":"Checks knowledge of key reference lines."},{"q":"What is 0° longitude? (1 mark)","ans":"The Prime Meridian (Greenwich).","hint":"It passes through Greenwich.","reason":"Tests understanding of global reference systems."},{"q":"What is the main purpose of latitude and longitude? (2 marks)","ans":"To determine the exact location of places on the Earth''s surface.","hint":"Think about locating positions on a map.","reason":"Moves beyond recall into application."},{"q":"What is the maximum latitude? (1 mark)","ans":"90°.","hint":"At the North and South Poles.","reason":"Tests boundary knowledge of latitude."},{"q":"What is the maximum longitude? (1 mark)","ans":"180°.","hint":"Halfway around the Earth.","reason":"Ensures understanding of Earth''s full longitudinal extent."},{"q":"What does latitude measure? (2 marks)","ans":"Distance north or south of the Equator in degrees.","hint":"Measured from 0° at the Equator.","reason":"Reinforces directional measurement."},{"q":"What does longitude measure? (2 marks)","ans":"Distance east or west of the Prime Meridian in degrees.","hint":"Measured from 0° at Greenwich.","reason":"Complements understanding of longitude."},{"q":"State one use of latitude and longitude. (1 mark)","ans":"They are used for navigation and locating places.","hint":"Think maps, GPS, and travel.","reason":"Tests practical application of the concept."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> mapwork -> Topographical maps
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'mapwork',
  'Topographical maps',
  '<h2>Topographical Maps</h2>
<h2> Contour Lines & Relief (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Contour lines are imaginary lines drawn on a map that join points of equal height (altitude) above sea level.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4> Key Concepts</h4>
<ul>
  <li><b>Contour Line:</b> Line joining points of equal height</li>
  <li><b>Contour Interval:</b> Vertical difference between successive contour lines</li>
  <li><b>Relief:</b> The variation in elevation of land</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Important Rule:</b> Contour lines never cross each other.</p>
<hr>
<h3> Interpreting Contours</h3>
<ul>
  <li>
    <b>Close Contours:</b> Indicate a <b>steep slope</b>  
     Land rises quickly over a short distance
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Wide/Spaced Contours:</b> Indicate a <b>gentle slope</b>  
     Land rises slowly over a long distance
  </li>
</ul>
<p>
 This represents a <b>hill</b> because heights increase towards the center (300 → 400 → 500).
</p>
<p>
 The spacing between lines shows slope:
<ul>
  <li>If lines were closer → steep hill</li>
  <li>If lines were wider → gentle hill</li>
</ul>
</p>
<hr>
<h3> Common Landforms from Contours</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Hill:</b> Circular contours with increasing height inward</li>
  <li><b>Valley:</b> V-shaped contours pointing uphill</li>
  <li><b>Ridge:</b> Long narrow high land</li>
  <li><b>Plateau:</b> Flat elevated land with widely spaced contours on top</li>
</ul>
  ',
  '[{"q":"What are contour lines? (2 marks)","ans":"Lines drawn on a map joining points of equal height above sea level.","hint":"They connect points with the same elevation.","reason":"Tests understanding of the basic concept of contours."},{"q":"What do close contour lines indicate? (2 marks)","ans":"A steep slope.","hint":"Height changes rapidly over a short distance.","reason":"Checks interpretation of slope from contour spacing."},{"q":"What do widely spaced contour lines indicate? (2 marks)","ans":"A gentle slope.","hint":"Height changes slowly over a long distance.","reason":"Ensures understanding of gradual elevation change."},{"q":"What does a V-shape in contour lines indicate? (2 marks)","ans":"A river valley.","hint":"The V-shape points upstream.","reason":"Tests recognition of landforms from contour patterns."},{"q":"What do closed contour lines indicate? (2 marks)","ans":"A hill or mountain.","hint":"Height increases towards the center.","reason":"Checks understanding of elevation patterns."},{"q":"What is a contour interval? (2 marks)","ans":"The vertical difference in height between successive contour lines.","hint":"Difference between one contour line and the next.","reason":"Tests knowledge of measurement in topographic maps."},{"q":"Define relief. (2 marks)","ans":"The variation in elevation or height of the land surface.","hint":"Think differences in height.","reason":"Ensures understanding of terrain variation."},{"q":"What do maps show? (2 marks)","ans":"Maps show physical features such as landforms, rivers, and other geographical features.","hint":"Think natural features of the land.","reason":"Reinforces the purpose of maps."},{"q":"State one use of contour maps. (2 marks)","ans":"They are used in planning and engineering projects such as road construction.","hint":"Think construction and land use.","reason":"Tests application of contour knowledge in real life."},{"q":"In which direction do rivers flow on a contour map? (2 marks)","ans":"From higher elevation to lower elevation (downhill).","hint":"Water flows downhill.","reason":"Tests understanding of drainage patterns and elevation."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> mapwork -> Scale & symbols
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'mapwork',
  'Scale & symbols',
  '<h2>Scale & Symbols (Advanced)</h2>
<h2> Map Scale &  Map Symbols (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Map scale shows the relationship between distance on a map and the actual distance on the ground, while map symbols are signs used to represent real-world features on a map.
</p>
<h3> TYPES OF MAP SCALE</h3>
<h4>1. Ratio Scale (Representative Fraction)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Written as 1:50,000, 1:25,000, etc.</li>
  <li>Means 1 unit on map represents many units on ground</li>
  <li>Used in topographic maps</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Example:</b> 1:50,000 means 1 cm on map = 50,000 cm on ground</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Statement Scale (Verbal Scale)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Written in words (e.g., 1 cm represents 1 km)</li>
  <li>Easy to understand but less precise for calculations</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Example:</b> 1 cm = 1 km</p>
<hr>
<h4>3. Linear Scale (Bar Scale)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Shown as a drawn line divided into equal parts</li>
  <li>Used to measure distances directly on maps</li>
  <li>Remains accurate even if map is resized</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> MAP SYMBOLS</h3>
<h4> Colour Symbols (VERY IMPORTANT)</h4>
<ul>
  <li><b>Blue:</b> Water features (rivers, lakes, oceans)</li>
  <li><b>Green:</b> Vegetation (forests, grasslands)</li>
  <li><b>Black:</b> Human-made features (roads, buildings, railways)</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> Symbol Interpretation</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Symbols represent real features in a simplified form</li>
  <li>They allow large areas to be shown on small maps</li>
  <li>All map users understand standard symbols globally</li>
</ul>
<h4>Example  (Statement Scale Conversion)</h4>
<p><b>Question:</b> Convert 1 cm = 1 km into ratio scale.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p>
<pre>
1 km = 100,000 cm
So:
1 cm : 100,000 cm
= 1:100,000
</pre>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 1:100,000</p>
<hr>
<h3> COMMON EXAM MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Confusing statement scale with ratio scale</li>
  <li>Forgetting to convert km into cm in calculations</li>
  <li>Assuming all green areas are forests only</li>
  <li>Mixing up symbols and colours</li>
</ul>
  ',
  '[{"q":"What is a map scale? (2 marks)","ans":"A map scale is the ratio between a distance on a map and the corresponding distance on the ground.","hint":"It compares map distance to real-world distance.","reason":"Tests understanding of the relationship between map and reality."},{"q":"What does a scale of 1:50,000 mean? (2 marks)","ans":"1 cm on the map represents 50,000 cm (or 500 m) on the ground.","hint":"Convert centimeters into meters.","reason":"Tests ability to interpret and convert scale units."},{"q":"What does the color blue represent on a map? (1 mark)","ans":"Water bodies such as rivers, lakes, and oceans.","hint":"Think of natural water features.","reason":"Checks knowledge of standard map color conventions."},{"q":"What does the color green represent on a map? (1 mark)","ans":"Vegetation such as forests or grasslands.","hint":"Think plants and natural cover.","reason":"Tests recognition of vegetation symbols."},{"q":"What does the color black represent on a map? (1 mark)","ans":"Man-made features such as buildings and roads.","hint":"Think human constructions.","reason":"Checks understanding of human features on maps."},{"q":"Name the three types of map scale. (3 marks)","ans":"Ratio (Representative Fraction), statement scale, and linear (bar) scale.","hint":"There are three main forms of expressing scale.","reason":"Tests recall and classification of scale types."},{"q":"What is a Representative Fraction (RF)? (2 marks)","ans":"A scale expressed as a ratio showing the relationship between map distance and ground distance (e.g., 1:50,000).","hint":"It is written as a ratio.","reason":"Ensures understanding of RF as a standard scale format."},{"q":"What is a large-scale map? (2 marks)","ans":"A map that shows a small area with a lot of detail.","hint":"Large scale = more detail, smaller area.","reason":"Tests conceptual understanding of scale size."},{"q":"What is a small-scale map? (2 marks)","ans":"A map that shows a large area with less detail.","hint":"Small scale = less detail, larger area.","reason":"Complements understanding of scale comparison."},{"q":"What is the use of symbols on a map? (2 marks)","ans":"Symbols are used to represent real-world features in a simplified way to make maps easier to read and understand.","hint":"They simplify complex features.","reason":"Tests understanding of map interpretation tools."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> mapwork -> Map reading
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'mapwork',
  'Map reading',
  '<h2>Map Reading (Advanced)</h2>
<h2> Map Reading & Interpretation (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>
<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Map reading is the skill of interpreting and analyzing information shown on a map, including symbols, directions, distances, relief, and grid references.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4> Importance of Map Reading</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Helps locate places accurately on Earth</li>
  <li>Used in navigation, planning, and geography exams</li>
  <li>Helps understand physical and human features of an area</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> Key Map Reading Skills</h3>
<h4>1. Distance Measurement</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Measured using map scale</li>
  <li>Converted into real ground distance (km or m)</li>
</ul>
<h4>2. Direction</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Uses compass directions (N, S, E, W)</li>
  <li>Also uses bearings (0°–360°)</li>
</ul>
<h4>3. Relief Interpretation</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Uses contour lines to show height</li>
  <li>Close contours = steep slope</li>
  <li>Wide contours = gentle slope</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> Grid Reference (VERY IMPORTANT)</h3>
<p>
A grid reference is a system used to locate exact positions on a map using numbered grid lines.
</p>
<pre>
Example: 123456 (6-figure grid reference)
First 3 digits = Eastings (horizontal)
Last 3 digits = Northings (vertical)
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> How to Read Grid References</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Eastings (X-axis):</b> Read from left to right</li>
  <li><b>Northings (Y-axis):</b> Read from bottom to top</li>
  <li><b>Always remember:</b> “Go in the door, then up the stairs”</li>
</ul>
<h3> Common Exam Mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Reversing Eastings and Northings</li>
  <li>Using 4-figure instead of 6-figure grid references when precision is required</li>
  <li>Misreading contour spacing</li>
  <li>Forgetting direction when interpreting maps</li>
</ul>
  ',
  '[{"q":"What is a grid reference? (2 marks)","ans":"A grid reference is a system used to locate a specific point on a map using a series of numbers, usually a six-figure reference.","hint":"It uses numbers to show exact position on a map.","reason":"Tests understanding of location systems in map reading."},{"q":"In grid referencing, which is written first: eastings or northings? (1 mark)","ans":"Eastings are written first, followed by northings.","hint":"Think ''along the corridor, then up the stairs.''","reason":"Checks correct procedure in writing grid references."},{"q":"How is distance measured on a map? (2 marks)","ans":"Distance is measured using the map scale and then converted into real ground distance.","hint":"Measure on map, then apply scale.","reason":"Tests practical application of scale in calculations."},{"q":"What does relief mean in geography? (2 marks)","ans":"Relief refers to the variation in elevation or height of the land surface.","hint":"Think differences in height.","reason":"Ensures understanding of terrain variation."},{"q":"Name three basic map reading skills. (3 marks)","ans":"Distance measurement, direction, and relief interpretation.","hint":"Think how far, which way, and how high.","reason":"Tests knowledge of essential map skills."},{"q":"What does ''interpret'' mean in map reading? (2 marks)","ans":"It means to understand and explain the features shown on a map.","hint":"Go beyond seeing—explain what it means.","reason":"Tests higher-order thinking in geography."},{"q":"What do maps show? (2 marks)","ans":"Maps show both physical and human features of an area.","hint":"Natural and man-made features.","reason":"Reinforces general purpose of maps."},{"q":"State one use of maps. (1 mark)","ans":"Maps are used for navigation and locating places.","hint":"Think finding direction or position.","reason":"Tests practical application of maps."},{"q":"How do symbols help in map reading? (2 marks)","ans":"Symbols represent real-world features in a simplified way, making maps easier to read and understand.","hint":"They simplify complex information.","reason":"Checks understanding of map interpretation tools."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> mapwork -> Compass direction
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'mapwork',
  'Compass direction',
  '<h2>16 Point Compass</h2>
<h2> Bearings (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>
<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Bearings are directions measured in degrees clockwise from North (0°), used to describe the position of one place from another.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4> Key Rules of Bearings</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Measured <b>clockwise</b> from North</li>
  <li>Always written in <b>three digits</b> (e.g., 045°, 120°, 300°)</li>
  <li>North = <b>000°</b> or <b>360°</b></li>
  <li>East = <b>090°</b></li>
  <li>South = <b>180°</b></li>
  <li>West = <b>270°</b></li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> Compass Diagram</h3>
<div style="text-align:center;margin:1rem 0;">
<svg viewBox="0 0 260 260" width="260" height="260" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:50%;background:#12122a;">
  <!-- Outer circle -->
  <circle cx="130" cy="130" r="118" fill="none" stroke="#334" stroke-width="2"/>
  <circle cx="130" cy="130" r="90" fill="none" stroke="#223" stroke-width="1"/>

  <!-- Cardinal spokes -->
  <line x1="130" y1="12" x2="130" y2="248" stroke="#445" stroke-width="1"/>
  <line x1="12" y1="130" x2="248" y2="130" stroke="#445" stroke-width="1"/>
  <!-- Diagonal spokes -->
  <line x1="47" y1="47" x2="213" y2="213" stroke="#334" stroke-width="1"/>
  <line x1="213" y1="47" x2="47" y2="213" stroke="#334" stroke-width="1"/>

  <!-- N pointer (red) -->
  <polygon points="130,20 123,130 137,130" fill="#e74c3c"/>
  <!-- S pointer (white) -->
  <polygon points="130,240 123,130 137,130" fill="#ecf0f1"/>
  <!-- E pointer -->
  <polygon points="240,130 130,123 130,137" fill="#ecf0f1"/>
  <!-- W pointer -->
  <polygon points="20,130 130,123 130,137" fill="#ecf0f1"/>

  <!-- Centre dot -->
  <circle cx="130" cy="130" r="6" fill="#f39c12"/>

  <!-- Cardinal labels -->
  <text x="130" y="12" fill="#e74c3c" font-size="16" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle" font-family="sans-serif">N</text>
  <text x="130" y="253" fill="#ecf0f1" font-size="16" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle" font-family="sans-serif">S</text>
  <text x="252" y="135" fill="#ecf0f1" font-size="16" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle" font-family="sans-serif">E</text>
  <text x="8" y="135" fill="#ecf0f1" font-size="16" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle" font-family="sans-serif">W</text>

  <!-- Intermediate labels -->
  <text x="206" y="55" fill="#3498db" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" font-family="sans-serif">NE</text>
  <text x="55" y="55" fill="#3498db" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" font-family="sans-serif">NW</text>
  <text x="206" y="215" fill="#3498db" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" font-family="sans-serif">SE</text>
  <text x="55" y="215" fill="#3498db" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" font-family="sans-serif">SW</text>

  <!-- Bearing degrees -->
  <text x="130" y="30" fill="#f39c12" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle" font-family="monospace">000°</text>
  <text x="230" y="134" fill="#f39c12" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle" font-family="monospace">090°</text>
  <text x="130" y="242" fill="#f39c12" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle" font-family="monospace">180°</text>
  <text x="30" y="134" fill="#f39c12" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle" font-family="monospace">270°</text>
</svg>
</div>

<hr>
<h3> Common Bearings Table</h3>
<ul>
  <li>North-East (NE) = 045°</li>
  <li>South-East (SE) = 135°</li>
  <li>South-West (SW) = 225°</li>
  <li>North-West (NW) = 315°</li>
</ul>
  ',
  '[{"q":"What is the bearing of North? (1 mark)","ans":"0° (or 360°).","hint":"It is the starting point for measuring bearings.","reason":"Tests understanding of the reference direction in bearings."},{"q":"What is the bearing of East? (1 mark)","ans":"90°.","hint":"Measured clockwise from North.","reason":"Checks knowledge of basic compass bearings."},{"q":"What is the bearing of South? (1 mark)","ans":"180°.","hint":"It is directly opposite North.","reason":"Tests understanding of opposite directions."},{"q":"What is the bearing of West? (1 mark)","ans":"270°.","hint":"Continue clockwise past South.","reason":"Ensures full understanding of the 360° system."},{"q":"How many compass points are there in a full compass? (1 mark)","ans":"16.","hint":"Includes main and intermediate directions.","reason":"Tests knowledge of the complete compass system."},{"q":"What is the bearing of North-East (NE)? (1 mark)","ans":"45°.","hint":"Halfway between North (0°) and East (90°).","reason":"Checks understanding of intermediate bearings."},{"q":"What is the bearing of South-West (SW)? (1 mark)","ans":"225°.","hint":"Halfway between South (180°) and West (270°).","reason":"Reinforces calculation of intermediate directions."},{"q":"How are bearings measured? (2 marks)","ans":"Bearings are measured in degrees clockwise from North.","hint":"Always start from North and move clockwise.","reason":"Tests correct method of measuring bearings."},{"q":"How many main (cardinal) directions are there? (1 mark)","ans":"4 (North, South, East, West).","hint":"The four primary directions.","reason":"Tests basic directional knowledge."},{"q":"How many intermediate directions are there? (1 mark)","ans":"4 (North-East, North-West, South-East, South-West).","hint":"They lie between the main directions.","reason":"Completes understanding of compass structure."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> mapwork -> Scale calculations
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'mapwork',
  'Scale calculations',
  '<h2>Scale Calculations</h2>
<h2> Map Scale (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Map scale is the relationship between distance on a map and the actual distance on the ground.
It shows how much the real world has been reduced to fit on a map.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4> Key Idea</h4>
<p>
Scale tells you how many units on the ground are represented by one unit on the map.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Large scale maps → more detail, small area</li>
  <li>Small scale maps → less detail, large area</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> Formula</h3>
<pre>
Scale = Map Distance : Ground Distance
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> Conversion Guide (VERY IMPORTANT)</h3>
<pre>
1 : 50,000
1 cm on map =
50,000 cm on ground
= 500 m
= 0.5 km
</pre>
<p><b>Key Skill:</b> Always convert cm → m → km when required.</p>
<h3> Worked Example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A road measures 6.5 cm on a map with scale 1:25,000. Find real distance.</p>
<h4>Solution:</h4>
<pre>
6.5 × 25,000 = 162,500 cm
162,500 cm ÷ 100 = 1625 m
1625 m ÷ 1000 = 1.625 km
</pre>
<p><b>Final Answer:</b> 1.625 km</p>
<hr>
<h3> Key Exam Steps (Memory Trick)</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>M</b>ultiply map distance by denominator</li>
  <li><b>C</b>onvert cm → m → km</li>
  <li><b>S</b>how full working clearly</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Mnemonic:</b> MCS = Multiply → Convert → Show working</p>
<hr>
<h3> Common Exam Mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Forgetting to convert cm into km</li>
  <li>Dividing instead of multiplying</li>
  <li>Skipping working steps (loses marks)</li>
  <li>Mixing up map and ground distance</li>
</ul>
',
  '[{"q":"What is a map scale? (2 marks)","ans":"It is the ratio between distance on a map and actual distance on the ground.","hint":"Think relationship between map and ground.","reason":"Tests understanding of the basic definition of scale."},{"q":"What does 1:50,000 mean? (2 marks)","ans":"1 cm on the map represents 50,000 cm (500 m) on the ground.","hint":"Convert map distance to real distance.","reason":"Tests interpretation of representative fraction scale."},{"q":"Calculate real distance if map distance is 6 cm and scale is 1:50,000. (3 marks)","ans":"6 × 50,000 = 300,000 cm = 3 km.","hint":"Multiply then convert units.","reason":"Tests ability to apply scale in calculations."},{"q":"Why is scale important in map reading? (2 marks)","ans":"It helps determine real distances between places accurately.","hint":"Think measurement and accuracy.","reason":"Tests understanding of practical application of scale."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> mapwork -> Bearing calculations
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'mapwork',
  'Bearing calculations',
  '<h2>Bearing Calculations</h2>
<h2> Bearings (Compass Direction & Calculation) — Advanced Mastery Notes</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
A bearing is the direction of one point from another, measured in degrees clockwise from North (000°).
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4> Key Principles of Bearings</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Always measured <b>clockwise from North</b></li>
  <li>North is always <b>000° (or 360°)</b></li>
  <li>All bearings are written in <b>three digits</b></li>
  <li>Used in navigation, maps, aviation, and surveying</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> Compass Diagram</h3>

<div style="text-align:center;margin:1rem 0;">
<svg viewBox="0 0 220 220" width="220" height="220" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:50%;background:#111124;box-shadow: 0 4px 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);">
  <!-- Axis -->
  <line x1="110" y1="15" x2="110" y2="205" stroke="#334" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <line x1="15" y1="110" x2="205" y2="110" stroke="#334" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  
  <!-- Clockwise Measurement Path Arrow -->
  <path d="M 110 40 A 70 70 0 1 1 55 160" fill="none" stroke="#f1c40f" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-dasharray="3,3"/>
  <polygon points="53,155 45,170 63,165" fill="#f1c40f"/>
  <text x="175" y="65" fill="#f1c40f" font-size="8" font-family="sans-serif" font-weight="bold">Clockwise</text>

  <!-- Pointers -->
  <circle cx="110" cy="110" r="4" fill="#e74c3c"/>

  <!-- Labels -->
  <text x="110" y="12" fill="#e74c3c" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle" font-family="sans-serif">N (000°)</text>
  <text x="215" y="114" fill="#ecf0f1" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" font-family="sans-serif">E (090°)</text>
  <text x="110" y="217" fill="#ecf0f1" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle" font-family="sans-serif">S (180°)</text>
  <text x="5" y="114" fill="#ecf0f1" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" font-family="sans-serif">W (270°)</text>
</svg>
</div>

<hr>

<h3> Key Rules (VERY IMPORTANT)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Always start measuring from North (000°)</li>
  <li>Move in a clockwise direction only</li>
  <li>Write answers as 3-digit numbers (e.g., 005°, 090°, 225°)</li>
  <li>Do not use compass words in final exam answers unless asked</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES (Exam Style)</h3>

<h4>Example 1 (Basic Bearing)</h4>
<p><b>Question:</b> Find the bearing of East from North.</p>

<p><b>Solution:</b></p>
<ul>
  <li>Step 1: Start at North = 000°</li>
  <li>Step 2: Move clockwise to East</li>
  <li>Step 3: East = 090°</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Answer:</b> 090°</p>

<p><b>Why:</b> East is exactly a quarter turn clockwise from North.</p>

<hr>

<h4>Example 2 (Intermediate Compass Bearing)</h4>
<p><b>Question:</b> Find the bearing of South-West (SW).</p>

<p><b>Solution:</b></p>
<ul>
  <li>Step 1: North = 000°</li>
  <li>Step 2: South = 180°</li>
  <li>Step 3: West = 270°</li>
  <li>Step 4: SW is halfway between 180° and 270°</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Calculation:</b></p>
<pre>
(180° + 270°) ÷ 2 = 225°
</pre>

<p><b>Answer:</b> 225°</p>

<hr>

<h4>Example 3 (Exam Application Question)</h4>
<p><b>Question:</b> A town lies directly South-East of a school. State its bearing.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> 135°</p>

<p><b>Explanation:</b> South-East lies halfway between 090° (East) and 180° (South).</p>

<hr>

<h4>Example 4 (Reverse Bearing – Common Exam Trick)</h4>
<p><b>Question:</b> The bearing of B from A is 060°. Find the bearing of A from B.</p>

<p><b>Solution:</b></p>
<ul>
  <li>Since 060° is less than 180°, add 180°</li>
</ul>

<pre>
060° + 180° = 240°
</pre>

<p><b>Answer:</b> 240°</p>

<hr>

<h3> QUICK REFERENCE TABLE</h3>

<ul>
  <li>North = 000°</li>
  <li>East = 090°</li>
  <li>South = 180°</li>
  <li>West = 270°</li>
  <li>North-East = 045°</li>
  <li>South-East = 135°</li>
  <li>South-West = 225°</li>
  <li>North-West = 315°</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> Common Exam Mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Measuring anticlockwise instead of clockwise</li>
  <li>Writing 90° instead of 090°</li>
  <li>Confusing compass direction with bearing</li>
  <li>Forgetting to start from North (000°)</li>
</ul>
  ',
  '[{"q":"What is bearing measured from? (1 mark)","ans":"North (000°).","hint":"Think starting direction.","reason":"Bearings always start from North in a clockwise direction."},{"q":"What is the bearing of South? (1 mark)","ans":"180°.","hint":"Opposite of North.","reason":"South lies directly opposite North on the compass."},{"q":"What is the bearing of North-East? (1 mark)","ans":"045°.","hint":"Between North and East.","reason":"NE is exactly halfway between 0° and 90°."},{"q":"State two rules of bearing measurement. (2 marks)","ans":"Measured clockwise from North; written in 3 digits.","hint":"Think direction and format.","reason":"Ensures standard navigation accuracy and consistency."},{"q":"What is the bearing of West? (1 mark)","ans":"270°.","hint":"Turn clockwise from North.","reason":"West lies three-quarters around the compass circle."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> mapwork -> Vertical Exaggeration
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'mapwork',
  'Vertical Exaggeration',
  '<h2>Vertical Exaggeration</h2>
<h2> Vertical Exaggeration (V.E) — Advanced Mastery Notes</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Vertical exaggeration is the number of times vertical scale has been enlarged compared to horizontal scale in a diagram (such as a cross section). It shows how much relief is “stretched” upward to make features easier to see.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4> Key Idea</h4>
<p>
Vertical exaggeration is used because natural landforms are often very flat compared to their horizontal distance. Without exaggeration, slopes and hills would look too flat to interpret clearly.
</p>
<p><b>Important:</b> A higher V.E means steeper-looking landforms on the diagram (not in real life).</p>
<hr>
<h3> Formula</h3>
<pre>
V.E = Horizontal Scale ÷ Vertical Scale
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> Step-by-Step Worked Example</h3>
<h4>Given:</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Horizontal scale = 1 cm : 1000 m</li>
  <li>Vertical scale = 1 cm : 100 m</li>
</ul>
<h4>Step 1: Convert to same units (ratio form)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>H.S = 1000</li>
  <li>V.S = 100</li>
</ul>
<h4>Step 2: Apply formula</h4>
<pre>
V.E = Horizontal Scale ÷ Vertical Scale
V.E = 1000 ÷ 100
</pre>
<h4>Step 3: Calculate</h4>
<pre>
V.E = 10
</pre>
<p><b>Final Answer:</b> Vertical exaggeration = 10 times</p>
<hr>
<h3> Interpretation of V.E Values</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>V.E = 1:</b> No exaggeration (realistic slope)</li>
  <li><b>V.E > 1:</b> Landforms appear steeper than reality</li>
  <li><b>High V.E (e.g. 10):</b> Very steep-looking relief</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> Exam Tips (Very Important)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Always convert scales into the same unit before dividing</li>
  <li>Ensure horizontal scale is divided by vertical scale (not reversed)</li>
  <li>Show working steps clearly for full marks</li>
  <li>Write final answer with units like “times”</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> Common Mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Reversing the formula (V.S ÷ H.S )</li>
  <li>Not converting units before calculation</li>
  <li>Forgetting to state final “times” value</li>
</ul>
  ',
  '[{"q":"What is vertical exaggeration? (2 marks)","ans":"It is the increase in vertical scale compared to horizontal scale in a diagram.","hint":"Think comparison of heights and distances.","reason":"Tests understanding of how landforms are visually represented."},{"q":"State the formula for vertical exaggeration. (1 mark)","ans":"V.E = Horizontal Scale ÷ Vertical Scale","hint":"Think division of scales.","reason":"Checks recall of correct formula."},{"q":"Calculate V.E if H.S = 2000 and V.S = 200. (2 marks)","ans":"2000 ÷ 200 = 10","hint":"Divide horizontal by vertical.","reason":"Tests ability to apply formula in calculations."},{"q":"Why is vertical exaggeration used in geography? (2 marks)","ans":"To make landforms like hills and valleys more visible and clearer.","hint":"Think visibility of landforms.","reason":"Tests understanding of practical application."},{"q":"What does a vertical exaggeration of 10 mean? (2 marks)","ans":"It means the vertical scale is shown 10 times larger than the horizontal scale.","hint":"Think multiplication effect on height.","reason":"Tests interpretation of results in scale analysis."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> mapwork -> Cross section drawing
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'mapwork',
  'Cross section drawing',
  '<h2>Cross Section Drawing</h2>
<h2> Cross Section (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
A cross section is a side view (profile) of the land surface that shows changes in relief along a line drawn on a map by using contour lines.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4> Key Idea</h4>
<p>
A cross section transforms a flat map into a vertical profile, allowing you to “see” hills, valleys, and slopes as if viewed from the side.
</p>

<p><b>Important:</b> It shows <b>height (vertical axis)</b> against <b>distance (horizontal axis)</b>.</p>

<hr>

<h3> Step-by-Step Construction Method</h3>

<ol>
  <li><b>Draw a section line</b> across the map where you want the profile.</li>
  <li><b>Mark intersections:</b> Identify where contour lines cross the line.</li>
  <li><b>Transfer to graph paper:</b> Copy distances accurately along the base line.</li>
  <li><b>Plot heights:</b> At each point, mark the correct contour height.</li>
  <li><b>Join smoothly:</b> Connect points using a smooth curved line (not straight segments).</li>
</ol>

<hr>
<h3> Worked Example (Exam Style)</h3>

<h4>Given Data:</h4>
<p>Contour values along a line: 100m → 200m → 300m → 200m → 100m</p>

<h4>Step-by-Step Solution:</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Step 1: Draw horizontal axis (distance)</li>
  <li>Step 2: Draw vertical axis (height in meters)</li>
  <li>Step 3: Plot points according to contour heights</li>
  <li>Step 4: Join points smoothly</li>
</ul>

<h4>Result (Shape Interpretation):</h4>

<div style="text-align:center;margin:1rem 0;">
<svg viewBox="0 0 340 180" width="340" height="180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:12px;background:#1e1e2f;box-shadow: 0 4px 15px rgba(0,0,0,0.35);">
  <!-- Grid Lines -->
  <line x1="50" y1="130" x2="300" y2="130" stroke="#2e2e3f" stroke-width="1"/>
  <line x1="50" y1="90" x2="300" y2="90" stroke="#2e2e3f" stroke-width="1"/>
  <line x1="50" y1="50" x2="300" y2="50" stroke="#2e2e3f" stroke-width="1"/>

  <!-- Y-Axis Labels (Elevation) -->
  <text x="40" y="134" fill="#aaa" font-size="8" text-anchor="end" font-family="monospace">100m</text>
  <text x="40" y="94" fill="#aaa" font-size="8" text-anchor="end" font-family="monospace">200m</text>
  <text x="40" y="54" fill="#aaa" font-size="8" text-anchor="end" font-family="monospace">300m</text>
  <text x="12" y="95" fill="#fff" font-size="9" font-family="sans-serif" transform="rotate(-90 12 95)" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold">Height (m)</text>

  <!-- X-Axis Labels (Distance) -->
  <text x="50" y="160" fill="#aaa" font-size="8" text-anchor="middle" font-family="monospace">0</text>
  <text x="112.5" y="160" fill="#aaa" font-size="8" text-anchor="middle" font-family="monospace">1</text>
  <text x="175" y="160" fill="#aaa" font-size="8" text-anchor="middle" font-family="monospace">2</text>
  <text x="237.5" y="160" fill="#aaa" font-size="8" text-anchor="middle" font-family="monospace">3</text>
  <text x="300" y="160" fill="#aaa" font-size="8" text-anchor="middle" font-family="monospace">4</text>
  <text x="175" y="174" fill="#fff" font-size="9" font-family="sans-serif" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold">Distance (km)</text>

  <!-- Axes lines -->
  <line x1="50" y1="30" x2="50" y2="145" stroke="#ccc" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <line x1="50" y1="145" x2="310" y2="145" stroke="#ccc" stroke-width="1.5"/>

  <!-- Plotted points -->
  <circle cx="50" cy="130" r="4" fill="#ff5722"/>
  <circle cx="112.5" cy="90" r="4" fill="#ff5722"/>
  <circle cx="175" cy="50" r="4" fill="#ff5722"/>
  <circle cx="237.5" cy="90" r="4" fill="#ff5722"/>
  <circle cx="300" cy="130" r="4" fill="#ff5722"/>

  <!-- Smooth Profile Curve -->
  <path d="M 50,130 C 80,110 90,90 112.5,90 C 135,90 150,50 175,50 C 200,50 215,90 237.5,90 C 260,90 270,110 300,130" fill="none" stroke="#2ecc71" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round"/>

  <!-- Title/Interpretation -->
  <text x="175" y="20" fill="#ffcc00" font-size="10" font-family="sans-serif" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold">PLOTTED CROSS-SECTION PROFILE (HILL)</text>
</svg>
</div>

<p><b>Interpretation:</b> This represents a hill because height increases to a peak then decreases.</p>
<hr>
<h3> How to Interpret Cross Sections</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Upward slope:</b> Land is rising</li>
  <li><b>Downward slope:</b> Land is falling</li>
  <li><b>Peak:</b> Highest point (hill or mountain)</li>
  <li><b>Dip/valley:</b> Low area between highlands</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> Exam Tips (Very Important)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Always label axes: <b>Height (m)</b> and <b>Distance (km or cm)</b></li>
  <li>Use a <b>smooth curve</b>, not straight broken lines</li>
  <li>Ensure correct contour values are transferred exactly</li>
  <li>Check spacing accuracy before plotting heights</li>
  <li>Use a light pencil for construction, darken final curve</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> Common Mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Joining points with straight lines instead of smooth curves</li>
  <li>Mixing up contour values</li>
  <li>Incorrect spacing on horizontal axis</li>
  <li>Forgetting to label axes</li>
</ul>
',
  '[{"q":"What is a cross section in geography? (2 marks)","ans":"A cross section is a side view of the land showing changes in height along a line on a map.","hint":"Think side view of land.","reason":"Tests understanding of relief representation."},{"q":"What is the first step in drawing a cross section? (1 mark)","ans":"Draw a straight line across the area of interest.","hint":"Think starting point on map.","reason":"Checks procedural knowledge."},{"q":"Why are contour values plotted on graph paper? (2 marks)","ans":"To accurately show changes in elevation along the section line.","hint":"Think accuracy of height representation.","reason":"Tests understanding of data transfer from map to graph."},{"q":"How should the final cross section be drawn? (2 marks)","ans":"It should be drawn using a smooth curved line connecting all plotted points.","hint":"Think shape of land surface.","reason":"Tests correct presentation of relief features."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> mapwork -> Map features
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'mapwork',
  'Map features',
  '<h2>Map Features</h2>
<h2> Map Symbols & Features (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Map symbols are signs, letters, and marks used on maps to represent real-world features such as landforms, buildings, and height points.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4> Why Map Symbols Are Important</h4>
<p>
Maps cannot show real objects in their actual size, so symbols are used to represent features in a simplified form.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>They save space on maps</li>
  <li>Make maps easier to read</li>
  <li>Standardized so all map users understand them</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> Common Map Symbols (Relief Features)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>
    <b>Δ Trigonometrical Station:</b> A precisely measured point used for surveying  
     Has exact height and location
  </li>

  <li>
    <b>BM (Benchmark):</b> A fixed point with a known official height  
     Used as reference for mapping and construction
  </li>

  <li>
    <b>● Spot Height:</b> An approximate height of a specific point  
     Less accurate than benchmarks
  </li>

  <li>
    <b>Contours:</b> Lines joining points of equal height  
     Used to show relief (hills, valleys, slopes)
  </li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> Diagram</h3>
<pre>
     Δ        BM        ●
  (Trig)   (Bench)   (Spot)

Contour lines → show elevation and relief
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> Key Differences (Very Important for Exams)</h3>
<table border="1" cellpadding="5">
<tr>
  <th>Feature</th>
  <th>Description</th>
  <th>Accuracy</th>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>Trigonometrical Station (Δ)</td>
  <td>Surveyed point used for precise mapping</td>
  <td>Very high accuracy</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>Benchmark (BM)</td>
  <td>Fixed official height reference point</td>
  <td>High accuracy</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>Spot Height (●)</td>
  <td>Estimated height of a location</td>
  <td>Approximate</td>
</tr>
</table>
',
  '[{"q":"What is a trigonometrical station? (2 marks)","ans":"A trigonometrical station is a fixed point on a map whose exact height has been accurately measured.","hint":"Think exact height point.","reason":"Tests knowledge of precise surveying symbols."},{"q":"What does BM stand for on a map? (1 mark)","ans":"Benchmark.","hint":"Fixed height reference.","reason":"Checks recognition of map symbols."},{"q":"What is a spot height? (2 marks)","ans":"A spot height is an approximate height of a point shown on a map.","hint":"Think approximate height.","reason":"Tests understanding of elevation representation."},{"q":"What do contour lines represent? (2 marks)","ans":"Contour lines represent points of equal height above sea level.","hint":"Think relief.","reason":"Reinforces understanding of relief mapping."},{"q":"State one difference between a benchmark and a spot height. (2 marks)","ans":"A benchmark is an exact fixed height point while a spot height is an approximate height.","hint":"Think accuracy.","reason":"Tests comparison and analytical skills."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> environment -> Pollution
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'environment',
  'Pollution',
  '<h2>Pollution (Advanced)</h2>
<h2> Pollution (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Pollution is the introduction of harmful substances or energy into the environment, causing damage to living organisms and natural systems.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4> Key Concept</h4>
<p>
Pollution affects <b>air, water, soil, and sound environments</b>, and can be caused by both human activities and natural processes. However, most modern pollution is due to human activities.
</p>
<hr>
<h3> Types of Pollution (Detailed Explanation)</h3>

<h4>1. Air Pollution</h4>
<p>
Contamination of the atmosphere by harmful gases and particles.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Examples:</b> Carbon dioxide (CO₂), sulfur dioxide (SO₂), smoke</li>
  <li><b>Main Sources:</b> Vehicles, factories, burning fuels</li>
  <li><b>Effects:</b> Respiratory diseases, global warming</li>
</ul>
<h4>2. Water Pollution</h4>
<p>
Contamination of water bodies such as rivers, lakes, and oceans.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Examples:</b> Sewage, oil spills, chemicals</li>
  <li><b>Main Sources:</b> Industrial waste, agricultural runoff</li>
  <li><b>Effects:</b> Death of aquatic life, unsafe drinking water</li>
</ul>
<h4>3. Soil (Land) Pollution</h4>
<p>
Degradation of land due to harmful substances.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Examples:</b> Pesticides, plastics, waste dumping</li>
  <li><b>Main Sources:</b> Agriculture, improper waste disposal</li>
  <li><b>Effects:</b> Reduced soil fertility, food contamination</li>
</ul>
<h4>4. Noise Pollution</h4>
<p>
Excessive or harmful levels of noise in the environment.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Examples:</b> Traffic, machinery, loud music</li>
  <li><b>Effects:</b> Hearing loss, stress, disturbance</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> Sources of Pollution (Expanded)</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Industries:</b> Release smoke, chemicals, and waste</li>
  <li><b>Vehicles:</b> Emit harmful gases like CO₂</li>
  <li><b>Agricultural Chemicals:</b> Fertilizers and pesticides pollute soil and water</li>
  <li><b>Urban Waste:</b> Garbage and sewage contaminate land and water</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> Effects of Pollution (Detailed)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>
    <b>Health Problems:</b> Diseases such as asthma and lung infections
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Global Warming:</b> Caused by greenhouse gases in air pollution
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Water Contamination:</b> Unsafe for drinking and aquatic life
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Loss of Biodiversity:</b> Plants and animals die or migrate
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Soil Degradation:</b> Reduced agricultural productivity
  </li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> Control and Prevention Measures</h3>
<ul>
  <li>
    <b>Reduce Emissions:</b> Use cleaner fuels and technologies
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Recycling:</b> Reuse materials to reduce waste
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Use Clean Energy:</b> Solar, wind, and hydro power
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Proper Waste Disposal:</b> Avoid dumping waste into the environment
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Legislation:</b> Enforce environmental laws and regulations
  </li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> Concept Diagram</h3>
<pre>
Human Activities → Release pollutants → Contaminate environment → Harm life
Air → Breathing problems  
Water → Unsafe drinking  
Soil → Poor crops  
Noise → Stress
</pre>
<hr>
  ',
  '[{"q":"What is pollution? (2 marks)","ans":"Pollution is the introduction of harmful substances into the environment, causing contamination and damage.","hint":"Think contamination of air, water, or land.","reason":"Tests understanding of the core definition of pollution."},{"q":"Name one type of pollution. (1 mark)","ans":"Air pollution.","hint":"Think of pollution affecting the atmosphere.","reason":"Checks ability to identify types of pollution."},{"q":"Give one source of pollution. (1 mark)","ans":"Factories (industrial emissions).","hint":"Think industries releasing waste or gases.","reason":"Tests knowledge of causes of pollution."},{"q":"State one effect of pollution. (1 mark)","ans":"It causes diseases and health problems.","hint":"Think impact on human health.","reason":"Checks understanding of consequences of pollution."},{"q":"Suggest one method of controlling pollution. (1 mark)","ans":"Recycling waste materials.","hint":"Think reducing or managing waste.","reason":"Tests application of solutions to environmental problems."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> environment -> Deforestation
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'environment',
  'Deforestation',
  '<h2>Deforestation (Advanced)</h2>
<h2> Deforestation (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Deforestation is the large-scale clearing or removal of trees from forests without adequate replacement, leading to permanent loss of forest cover.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4> Key Concept</h4>
<p>
Deforestation is not just cutting trees — it involves <b>permanent destruction of forest ecosystems</b>, affecting climate, soil, water cycles, and biodiversity.
</p>
<p><b>Important Distinction:</b></p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Afforestation:</b> Planting trees in areas that had no forests before</li>
  <li><b>Reforestation:</b> Replanting trees in areas where forests were previously cut down</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> Causes of Deforestation (Detailed Explanation)</h3>
<h4>1. Farming Expansion</h4>
<p>
Forests are cleared to create land for crops and livestock.
</p>
<p> Especially common in areas with increasing population.</p>
<h4>2. Logging</h4>
<p>
Cutting trees for timber, furniture, and paper production.
</p>
<p> Can be legal or illegal (illegal logging is a major issue).</p>
<h4>3. Urban Growth</h4>
<p>
Expansion of towns, roads, and infrastructure leads to clearing of forests.
</p>
<h4>4. Fuelwood Collection</h4>
<p>
Trees are cut for firewood and charcoal, especially in developing regions.
</p>
<hr>
<h3> Effects of Deforestation (Expanded)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>
    <b>Soil Erosion:</b> Tree roots hold soil together; without them, soil is washed away by rain
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Reduced Rainfall:</b> Trees help in the water cycle; fewer trees mean less rainfall
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Climate Change:</b> Trees absorb CO₂; cutting them increases greenhouse gases
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Loss of Wildlife:</b> Animals lose their natural habitats, leading to extinction
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Desertification:</b> Land becomes dry and unproductive over time
  </li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> Control and Prevention Measures</h3>
<ul>
  <li>
    <b>Afforestation:</b> Planting new forests in bare areas
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Reforestation:</b> Replacing trees that have been cut down
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Forest Laws:</b> Government policies to regulate tree cutting
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Agroforestry:</b> Combining farming with tree planting
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Alternative Energy:</b> Using gas or electricity instead of firewood
  </li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> Concept Diagram</h3>
<pre>
Forests removed → Soil exposed → Erosion increases → Land becomes infertile
Trees cut → Less CO₂ absorbed → More greenhouse gases → Climate change
</pre>
  ',
  '[{"q":"What is deforestation? (2 marks)","ans":"Deforestation is the large-scale removal or cutting down of trees and forests.","hint":"Think loss of forest cover.","reason":"Tests understanding of the core environmental concept."},{"q":"State one cause of deforestation. (1 mark)","ans":"Farming (clearing land for agriculture).","hint":"Think land being cleared for human use.","reason":"Checks knowledge of human activities causing deforestation."},{"q":"State one effect of deforestation. (1 mark)","ans":"Soil erosion.","hint":"Without trees, soil is exposed.","reason":"Tests understanding of environmental consequences."},{"q":"Suggest one solution to deforestation. (1 mark)","ans":"Afforestation (planting trees).","hint":"Think replacing cut trees.","reason":"Checks knowledge of environmental conservation methods."},{"q":"What is one environmental impact of deforestation? (2 marks)","ans":"It contributes to climate change by increasing carbon dioxide levels in the atmosphere.","hint":"Think global warming and weather changes.","reason":"Tests higher-level understanding of environmental systems."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> environment -> Climate change
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'environment',
  'Climate change',
  '<h2>Climate Change (Advanced)</h2>
<h2> Climate Change (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Climate change refers to the long-term alteration of global weather patterns and average temperatures, mainly caused by human activities such as burning fossil fuels and deforestation.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>

<h4> The Greenhouse Effect (Core Concept)</h4>
<p>
The greenhouse effect is the process by which greenhouse gases trap heat in the Earth''s atmosphere, preventing it from escaping into space.
</p>

<ul>
  <li>Sun’s energy reaches Earth</li>
  <li>Earth absorbs and re-emits heat</li>
  <li>Greenhouse gases trap some of this heat</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Without greenhouse gases, Earth would be too cold. But excess gases cause global warming.</p>

<hr>
<h3> Causes of Climate Change (Detailed Explanation)</h3>
<h4>1. Greenhouse Gases</h4>
<ul>
  <li><b>Carbon dioxide (CO₂):</b> Released from burning fuels</li>
  <li><b>Methane (CH₄):</b> From livestock and decomposition</li>
</ul>
<p> These gases trap heat and increase global temperatures.</p>
<h4>2. Burning Fossil Fuels</h4>
<p>
Coal, oil, and gas are burned in cars, factories, and power plants, releasing large amounts of CO₂.
</p>
<h4>3. Deforestation</h4>
<p>
Trees absorb CO₂. Cutting them reduces this absorption, increasing atmospheric carbon levels.
</p>
<h4>4. Industrial Emissions</h4>
<p>
Factories release pollutants and greenhouse gases into the atmosphere.
</p>
<hr>
<h3> Effects of Climate Change (Expanded)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>
    <b>Rising Global Temperatures:</b> Leads to heatwaves and extreme weather
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Melting Ice Caps:</b> Glaciers melt due to higher temperatures
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Sea Level Rise:</b> Caused by melting ice and thermal expansion of water
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Frequent Droughts and Floods:</b> Weather becomes unpredictable
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Ecosystem Disruption:</b> Animals and plants lose their natural habitats
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Food Insecurity:</b> Crop yields decrease due to poor weather conditions
  </li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> Solutions and Mitigation Measures</h3>
<ul>
  <li>
    <b>Renewable Energy:</b> Solar, wind, and hydro reduce reliance on fossil fuels
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Reducing Emissions:</b> Using cleaner technologies and transport systems
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Afforestation & Reforestation:</b> Planting trees to absorb CO₂
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Energy Conservation:</b> Turning off unused devices, using efficient appliances
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Sustainable Agriculture:</b> Reducing harmful farming practices
  </li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> Concept Diagram</h3>
<pre>
Sun → Earth absorbs heat → Heat re-radiated → 
Greenhouse gases trap heat → Temperature rises → Climate change
</pre>
',
  '[{"q":"What is climate change? (2 marks)","ans":"Climate change is the long-term alteration of global weather patterns mainly due to human activities.","hint":"Think long-term weather change.","reason":"Tests understanding of the scientific definition."},{"q":"State two causes of climate change. (2 marks)","ans":"Burning fossil fuels and deforestation.","hint":"Think human activities.","reason":"Tests knowledge of human impact on environment."},{"q":"State two effects of climate change. (2 marks)","ans":"Rising temperatures and sea level rise.","hint":"Think temperature and sea level.","reason":"Checks understanding of environmental consequences."},{"q":"State two solutions to climate change. (2 marks)","ans":"Use of renewable energy and afforestation.","hint":"Think clean energy and trees.","reason":"Tests ability to suggest mitigation strategies."},{"q":"How do greenhouse gases cause global warming? (3 marks)","ans":"They trap heat in the atmosphere, preventing it from escaping into space, which increases Earth’s temperature.","hint":"Think heat trapping effect.","reason":"Tests deeper understanding of the greenhouse effect."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> environment -> Natural hazards
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'environment',
  'Natural hazards',
  '<h2>Natural Hazards (Advanced)</h2>
<h2> Natural Hazards (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>
<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Natural hazards are extreme natural events or processes that pose a threat to human life, property, and the environment.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4> Key Concept: Hazard vs Disaster</h4>
<ul>
  <li><b>Hazard:</b> A potential threat (e.g., an earthquake)</li>
  <li><b>Disaster:</b> When the hazard actually causes severe damage and loss</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> A hazard becomes a disaster when it affects vulnerable people.</p>
<hr>
<h3> Types of Natural Hazards (With Explanation)</h3>
<h4>1. Earthquakes</h4>
<p>
Sudden shaking of the Earth''s crust caused by movement of tectonic plates.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Cause:</b> Plate movement along faults</li>
  <li><b>Effects:</b> Building collapse, deaths, landslides</li>
</ul>
<h4>2. Floods</h4>
<p>
Overflow of water onto normally dry land.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Cause:</b> Heavy rainfall, poor drainage, river overflow</li>
  <li><b>Effects:</b> Property damage, waterborne diseases</li>
</ul>
<h4>3. Droughts</h4>
<p>
Long periods of little or no rainfall.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Cause:</b> Climate change, lack of rain</li>
  <li><b>Effects:</b> Crop failure, famine, water shortage</li>
</ul>
<h4>4. Volcanoes</h4>
<p>
Eruption of molten rock (magma) from beneath the Earth''s surface.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Cause:</b> Pressure build-up in the Earth''s crust</li>
  <li><b>Effects:</b> Lava flows, ash clouds, destruction of settlements</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> Effects of Natural Hazards (Detailed)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>
    <b>Loss of Life:</b> People may die due to collapsing buildings, drowning, or starvation
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Destruction of Property:</b> Homes, roads, and infrastructure are damaged
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Displacement:</b> People are forced to leave their homes and become refugees
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Economic Loss:</b> Businesses and agriculture are affected
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Disease Outbreaks:</b> Especially after floods due to contaminated water
  </li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> Control and Mitigation Measures</h3>
<ul>
  <li>
    <b>Early Warning Systems:</b> Technology used to predict hazards (e.g., weather forecasts)
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Disaster Preparedness:</b> Training people on what to do during hazards
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Education:</b> Teaching communities about risks and safety measures
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Proper Planning:</b> Avoid building in flood-prone or earthquake zones
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Afforestation:</b> Reduces flooding and soil erosion
  </li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> Concept Diagram</h3>
<pre>
Hazard → Exposure → Vulnerability → Disaster
(If people are prepared, disaster impact reduces)
</pre>
<hr>
  ',
  '[{"q":"What is a hazard? (2 marks)","ans":"A hazard is anything that poses a danger or threat to life, property, or the environment.","hint":"Think danger or risk.","reason":"Tests understanding of the basic definition of hazards."},{"q":"Name one example of a natural hazard. (1 mark)","ans":"Flood.","hint":"Think excess water causing danger.","reason":"Checks ability to identify types of hazards."},{"q":"State one effect of hazards. (1 mark)","ans":"Loss of life.","hint":"Think impact on humans.","reason":"Tests understanding of consequences of hazards."},{"q":"Suggest one way of controlling hazards. (2 marks)","ans":"Early warning systems and preparedness measures.","hint":"Think prevention and readiness.","reason":"Checks knowledge of disaster management strategies."},{"q":"What is drought? (2 marks)","ans":"Drought is a long period of time with little or no rainfall.","hint":"Think lack of rain for a long time.","reason":"Tests understanding of climatic hazards."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> environment -> Sustainable development
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'environment',
  'Sustainable development',
  '<h2>Sustainable Development (Advanced)</h2>
<h2> Sustainable Development (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Sustainable development refers to development that meets the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. The Three Pillars of Sustainable Development</h4>
<ul>
  <li><b>Environmental Sustainability</b> – Protecting natural resources (forests, water, soil)</li>
  <li><b>Economic Sustainability</b> – Ensuring economic growth without destroying resources</li>
  <li><b>Social Sustainability</b> – Improving quality of life (education, health, equality)</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> True sustainability balances ALL three pillars. Ignoring one leads to long-term problems.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Core Principles Explained</h4>
<ul>
  <li><b>Resource Conservation:</b> Using resources wisely to avoid depletion</li>
  <li><b>Intergenerational Equity:</b> Future generations should have equal access to resources</li>
  <li><b>Pollution Control:</b> Reducing waste and harmful emissions</li>
  <li><b>Use of Renewable Resources:</b> Solar, wind, hydro instead of fossil fuels</li>
  <li><b>Biodiversity Protection:</b> Preserving plants and animals</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> Sustainable Practices (With Explanation)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>
    <b>Afforestation:</b> Planting trees to replace those cut down  
    <br> Helps control climate, prevent soil erosion, and increase rainfall
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Recycling:</b> Reusing materials like paper, plastic, and metal  
    <br> Reduces waste and saves raw materials
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Energy Conservation:</b> Using less energy (switching off appliances, efficient bulbs)  
    <br> Reduces pollution and saves resources
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Sustainable Farming:</b> Crop rotation, organic farming  
    <br> Maintains soil fertility and reduces chemical damage
  </li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> Unsustainable Practices (Important for Exams)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Deforestation without replacement</li>
  <li>Overfishing</li>
  <li>Burning fossil fuels excessively</li>
  <li>Improper waste disposal</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Exam Tip:</b> Questions often ask you to compare sustainable vs unsustainable practices.</p>
<hr>
<h3> Importance of Sustainable Development</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Prevents resource depletion</li>
  <li>Protects ecosystems and biodiversity</li>
  <li>Ensures long-term economic growth</li>
  <li>Improves quality of life</li>
  <li>Reduces climate change impacts</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> Concept Diagram</h3>

<div style="text-align:center;margin:1rem 0;">
<svg viewBox="0 0 300 240" width="300" height="240" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:12px;background:#131326;box-shadow: 0 4px 15px rgba(0,0,0,0.35);">
  <!-- Intersecting circles -->
  <!-- Environment (Green) -->
  <circle cx="150" cy="90" r="65" fill="#2ecc71" opacity="0.3" stroke="#2ecc71" stroke-width="2"/>
  <!-- Economy (Blue) -->
  <circle cx="105" cy="155" r="65" fill="#3498db" opacity="0.3" stroke="#3498db" stroke-width="2"/>
  <!-- Society (Purple) -->
  <circle cx="195" cy="155" r="65" fill="#9b59b6" opacity="0.3" stroke="#9b59b6" stroke-width="2"/>

  <!-- Center intersection glow (Sustainability) -->
  <path d="M 150,118 A 65,65 0 0,0 128,155 A 65,65 0 0,0 172,155 A 65,65 0 0,0 150,118 Z" fill="#f1c40f" opacity="0.75"/>

  <!-- Labels for circles -->
  <text x="150" y="55" fill="#2ecc71" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle" font-family="sans-serif">ENVIRONMENT</text>
  <text x="75" y="195" fill="#3498db" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle" font-family="sans-serif">ECONOMY</text>
  <text x="225" y="195" fill="#9b59b6" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle" font-family="sans-serif">SOCIETY</text>

  <!-- Labels for intersection (Viable, Equitable, Bearable, Sustainable) -->
  <text x="108" y="112" fill="#fff" font-size="8" text-anchor="middle" font-family="sans-serif" opacity="0.8">Viable</text>
  <text x="192" y="112" fill="#fff" font-size="8" text-anchor="middle" font-family="sans-serif" opacity="0.8">Bearable</text>
  <text x="150" y="185" fill="#fff" font-size="8" text-anchor="middle" font-family="sans-serif" opacity="0.8">Equitable</text>

  <!-- Central sustainability label -->
  <rect x="110" y="128" width="80" height="15" fill="#000" rx="3" opacity="0.85"/>
  <text x="150" y="139" fill="#f1c40f" font-size="8" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle" font-family="sans-serif">SUSTAINABLE</text>
</svg>
</div>
  ',
  '[{"q":"What is sustainable development? (2 marks)","ans":"Sustainable development is development that meets the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs.","hint":"Think using resources without destroying future supply.","reason":"Tests understanding of the standard global definition."},{"q":"State one principle of sustainable development. (1 mark)","ans":"Conservation of natural resources.","hint":"Think saving and protecting resources.","reason":"Checks knowledge of sustainability principles."},{"q":"Give one practice of sustainable development. (1 mark)","ans":"Recycling materials.","hint":"Think reusing waste to reduce consumption.","reason":"Tests application of sustainable practices."},{"q":"Why is sustainable development important? (2 marks)","ans":"It ensures that resources are protected for future generations.","hint":"Think long-term survival and resource protection.","reason":"Tests understanding of purpose and importance."},{"q":"Give one example of renewable energy used in sustainable development. (1 mark)","ans":"Solar energy (or wind energy).","hint":"Think natural energy sources that do not run out.","reason":"Checks knowledge of sustainable energy sources."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> oceans -> Introduction to Oceans and Seas
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'oceans',
  'Introduction to Oceans and Seas',
  '<h2> Introduction to Oceans and Seas (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>
<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Oceans are vast bodies of salt water that cover most of the Earth''s surface, while seas are smaller parts of oceans that are partly enclosed by land.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Meaning of Oceans</h4>
<p>
Oceans are the largest continuous bodies of salt water on Earth, covering about 71% of the Earth''s surface.
</p>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Oceans are Earth''s largest water bodies.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Meaning of Seas</h4>
<p>
Seas are smaller than oceans and are usually partially enclosed by land.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Connected to oceans</li>
  <li>Shallower than oceans</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Seas are extensions of oceans.</p>
<hr>
<h4>3. Major Oceans of the World</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Pacific Ocean (largest)</li>
  <li>Atlantic Ocean</li>
  <li>Indian Ocean</li>
  <li>Southern Ocean</li>
  <li>Arctic Ocean (smallest)</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4>4. Differences Between Oceans and Seas</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Oceans are larger and deeper</li>
  <li>Seas are smaller and shallower</li>
  <li>Seas are partly enclosed by land</li>
</ul>
  ',
  '[{"q":"Define oceans. (1 mark)","ans":"Large bodies of salt water covering most of the Earth''s surface.","hint":"Think global water body.","reason":"Basic definition recall."},{"q":"State one difference between oceans and seas. (1 mark)","ans":"Oceans are larger than seas.","hint":"Think size.","reason":"Comparison skill."},{"q":"Name the largest ocean. (1 mark)","ans":"Pacific Ocean.","hint":"Think biggest water body.","reason":"Identification skill."},{"q":"What are seas? (2 marks)","ans":"Smaller bodies of salt water partly enclosed by land.","hint":"Think coastal water.","reason":"Concept understanding."},{"q":"Why are seas shallower than oceans? (2 marks)","ans":"Because they are smaller and located near land areas.","hint":"Think geography position.","reason":"Explanation skill."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> oceans -> Distribution of Land and Water
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'oceans',
  'Distribution of Land and Water',
  '<h2> Distribution of Land and Water (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Distribution of land and water refers to how the Earth''s surface is divided between land masses (continents and islands) and water bodies (oceans and seas).
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>

<h4>1. Global Distribution</h4>
<ul>
  <li>About <b>71% of Earth</b> is covered by water</li>
  <li>About <b>29% of Earth</b> is land</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Earth is often called the <b>"Blue Planet"</b> because water dominates its surface.</p>

<hr>

<h4>2. Uneven Distribution</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Land is concentrated mainly in the <b>Northern Hemisphere</b></li>
  <li>Water dominates the <b>Southern Hemisphere</b></li>
  <li>Some areas have large continents, others vast oceans</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Insight:</b> This uneven distribution affects climate, population, and economic activities.</p>

<hr>

<h4>3. The Hemispheres</h4>

<ul>
  <li>
    <b>Land Hemisphere:</b>  
    Area with the greatest concentration of land (mainly Northern Hemisphere)
  </li>

  <li>
    <b>Water Hemisphere:</b>  
    Area with the greatest concentration of water (mainly Southern Hemisphere)
  </li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h4>4. Continents and Oceans Relationship</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Continents are surrounded by oceans</li>
  <li>Oceans separate and connect continents</li>
  <li>Coastal regions link land and water</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> Importance of Distribution</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Affects global climate patterns</li>
  <li>Influences human settlement</li>
  <li>Determines trade routes</li>
  <li>Impacts biodiversity and ecosystems</li>
</ul>
  ',
  '[{"q":"What percentage of Earth is covered by water? (1 mark)","ans":"71%.","hint":"More than half of Earth is water.","reason":"Tests basic factual knowledge."},{"q":"What percentage of Earth is land? (1 mark)","ans":"29%.","hint":"Less than one-third.","reason":"Complements water distribution knowledge."},{"q":"What is the land hemisphere? (2 marks)","ans":"The part of Earth with the greatest concentration of land.","hint":"Think where most continents are.","reason":"Tests understanding of hemispheres."},{"q":"Why is Earth called the Blue Planet? (2 marks)","ans":"Because most of its surface is covered by water.","hint":"Think color seen from space.","reason":"Tests application of knowledge."},{"q":"State one importance of land and water distribution. (1 mark)","ans":"It affects climate.","hint":"Think weather patterns.","reason":"Tests conceptual understanding."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> oceans -> Ocean Floor (Relief of the Ocean Basin)
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'oceans',
  'Ocean Floor (Relief of the Ocean Basin)',
  '<h2> Ocean Floor (Relief of the Ocean Basin — Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
The ocean floor (or ocean basin) refers to the physical features found beneath the ocean water, including plains, ridges, trenches, and slopes.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>

<h4>1. Structure of the Ocean Floor</h4>
<p>The ocean floor is not flat — it has varied features just like land.</p>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Think of the ocean floor as an "underwater landscape" with mountains, valleys, and plains.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Major Features Explained</h4>
<h4> (a) Continental Shelf</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Shallow, gently sloping area near the coast</li>
  <li>Rich in marine life and fishing grounds</li>
  <li>Important for oil and gas extraction</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Exam Tip:</b> Most human activities occur here.</p>
<hr>
<h4> (b) Continental Slope</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Steep slope connecting the shelf to deep ocean</li>
  <li>Marks the edge of the continent</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4> (c) Abyssal Plain</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Very deep and flat area</li>
  <li>Covers large parts of the ocean floor</li>
  <li>Least explored region</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4> (d) Ocean Trenches</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Very deep, narrow depressions</li>
  <li>Formed by tectonic plate movement</li>
  <li>Example: Mariana Trench</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Deepest parts of the ocean.</p>

<hr>

<h4> (e) Mid-Ocean Ridges</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Underwater mountain ranges</li>
  <li>Formed by volcanic activity</li>
  <li>New crust is created here</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h4> (f) Seamounts and Guyots</h4>
<ul>
  <li><b>Seamounts:</b> Underwater volcanic mountains</li>
  <li><b>Guyots:</b> Flat-topped seamounts</li>
</ul>
<hr>

<h3> OCEAN FLOOR CONCEPT DIAGRAM</h3>

<pre>
CONTINENT
   |
   |____ Continental Shelf (shallow, gently sloping)
                                 ____ Continental Slope (steep drop)
                                                         ________ Abyssal Plain (flat deep ocean)
                             ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
                                   /        / 
                                  /  ______/     ← Mid-Ocean Ridge (underwater mountains)
                                          |
                                          |
                                       Deep-Sea Trench
<h3> Importance of Ocean Floor Features</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Support marine ecosystems</li>
  <li>Provide resources (oil, gas, minerals)</li>
  <li>Help scientists understand Earth''s structure</li>
  <li>Influence ocean currents</li>
</ul>
  ',
  '[{"q":"What is the continental shelf? (2 marks)","ans":"A shallow, gently sloping part of the ocean floor near the coast.","hint":"Think shallow area near land.","reason":"Tests basic feature identification."},{"q":"Name one feature of the ocean floor. (1 mark)","ans":"Abyssal plain.","hint":"Think flat deep area.","reason":"Checks knowledge of features."},{"q":"Which is the deepest part of the ocean? (1 mark)","ans":"Ocean trench.","hint":"Think deepest depression.","reason":"Tests key concept."},{"q":"Why is the continental shelf important? (2 marks)","ans":"It supports fishing and contains oil and gas resources.","hint":"Think resources and food.","reason":"Application-based question."},{"q":"How are mid-ocean ridges formed? (2 marks)","ans":"They are formed when tectonic plates move apart and magma rises to create new crust.","hint":"Think volcano + plate movement.","reason":"Tests process understanding."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> oceans -> Ocean Water Properties
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'oceans',
  'Ocean Water Properties',
  '<h2> Ocean Water Properties (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Ocean water properties refer to the physical and chemical characteristics of seawater, including temperature, salinity, density, and color.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Temperature of Ocean Water</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Surface water is warmer than deep water</li>
  <li>Temperature decreases with depth</li>
  <li>Equatorial regions are warmer than polar regions</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Factors affecting temperature:</b></p>
<ul>
  <li>Latitude (distance from equator)</li>
  <li>Ocean currents</li>
  <li>Season</li>
  <li>Depth of water</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Sun heats the surface → deeper water remains cold.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Salinity (Salt Content)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Average salinity is about <b>35‰ (parts per thousand)</b></li>
  <li>Salt mainly comes from dissolved minerals from land</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Factors affecting salinity:</b></p>
<ul>
  <li>Evaporation (increases salinity)</li>
  <li>Rainfall (decreases salinity)</li>
  <li>River inflow (reduces salinity)</li>
  <li>Ice melting/freezing</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Insight:</b> Hot, dry areas → high salinity.</p>
<hr>
<h4>3. Density of Ocean Water</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Density = how heavy water is</li>
  <li>Cold and salty water is denser</li>
  <li>Warm and less salty water is lighter</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Importance:</b></p>
<ul>
  <li>Controls ocean currents</li>
  <li>Causes sinking and rising of water</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4>4. Color and Transparency</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Ocean appears blue due to reflection of sky</li>
  <li>Clear water allows light penetration</li>
  <li>Murky water contains sediments or plankton</li>
</ul>
<h3> Importance of Ocean Water Properties</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Influence ocean currents</li>
  <li>Affect marine life distribution</li>
  <li>Control global climate</li>
  <li>Determine water movement (circulation)</li>
</ul>

  ',
  '[{"q":"What is salinity? (2 marks)","ans":"The amount of salt dissolved in ocean water.","hint":"Think salt content.","reason":"Basic definition test."},{"q":"What is the average salinity of ocean water? (1 mark)","ans":"35‰.","hint":"Standard ocean value.","reason":"Factual recall."},{"q":"Why is deep ocean water cold? (2 marks)","ans":"Because it does not receive direct heat from the sun.","hint":"Think sunlight only reaches surface.","reason":"Conceptual understanding."},{"q":"How does evaporation affect salinity? (2 marks)","ans":"It increases salinity by removing water and leaving salts behind.","hint":"Water leaves, salt stays.","reason":"Cause-effect understanding."},{"q":"What happens to density when temperature increases? (1 mark)","ans":"Density decreases.","hint":"Warm water is lighter.","reason":"Relationship test."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> oceans -> Ocean Movements (Waves, Tides, Currents)
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'oceans',
  'Ocean Movements (Waves, Tides, Currents)',
  '<h2> Ocean Movements (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Ocean movements refer to the continuous motion of ocean water in the form of waves, tides, and currents.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Waves</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Waves are movements of water on the ocean surface</li>
  <li>Energy moves, but water particles mostly stay in place</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Causes of Waves:</b></p>
<ul>
  <li>Wind (main cause)</li>
  <li>Earthquakes (tsunamis)</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Types of Waves:</b></p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Constructive waves:</b> Build up beaches</li>
  <li><b>Destructive waves:</b> Erode coastlines</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Effects:</b></p>
<ul>
  <li>Coastal erosion</li>
  <li>Formation of beaches</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4>2. Tides</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Regular rise and fall of sea level</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Causes of Tides:</b></p>
<ul>
  <li>Gravitational pull of the moon (main cause)</li>
  <li>Gravitational pull of the sun</li>
  <li>Rotation of the Earth</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Types of Tides:</b></p>
<ul>
  <li><b>High Tide:</b> Water rises</li>
  <li><b>Low Tide:</b> Water falls</li>
  <li><b>Spring Tide:</b> Very high tides (moon + sun aligned)</li>
  <li><b>Neap Tide:</b> Lower tides (moon and sun at right angles)</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Importance:</b></p>
<ul>
  <li>Aids navigation</li>
  <li>Supports fishing activities</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4>3. Ocean Currents</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Large masses of moving ocean water</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Types:</b></p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Warm currents:</b> Move from equator</li>
  <li><b>Cold currents:</b> Move from polar regions</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Causes:</b></p>
<ul>
  <li>Wind patterns</li>
  <li>Temperature differences</li>
  <li>Earth''s rotation</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Effects:</b></p>
<ul>
  <li>Influence climate</li>
  <li>Affect rainfall patterns</li>
  <li>Support fishing (nutrient-rich waters)</li>
</ul>
<h3> Importance of Ocean Movements</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Regulate global climate</li>
  <li>Shape coastlines</li>
  <li>Support marine ecosystems</li>
  <li>Assist in navigation and trade</li>
</ul>
  ',
  '[{"q":"What are waves? (2 marks)","ans":"Movements of water on the ocean surface caused mainly by wind.","hint":"Think surface movement.","reason":"Tests basic definition."},{"q":"What causes tides? (2 marks)","ans":"The gravitational pull of the moon and the sun.","hint":"Think gravity.","reason":"Core concept."},{"q":"What is a current? (2 marks)","ans":"A large mass of moving ocean water.","hint":"Think flowing water.","reason":"Definition test."},{"q":"Name one type of tide. (1 mark)","ans":"Spring tide.","hint":"Think highest tides.","reason":"Recall question."},{"q":"State one effect of ocean currents. (1 mark)","ans":"They influence climate.","hint":"Think temperature changes.","reason":"Application understanding."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> oceans -> Ocean Currents (Advanced)
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'oceans',
  'Ocean Currents (Advanced)',
  '<h2> Ocean Currents (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Ocean currents are large, continuous movements of ocean water flowing in specific directions across the Earth''s oceans.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Types of Ocean Currents</h4>
<ul>
  <li>
    <b>Warm Currents:</b>  
    Flow from equatorial regions towards the poles  
     Example: Gulf Stream  
     Effect: Warm nearby coastal areas
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Cold Currents:</b>  
    Flow from polar regions towards the equator  
     Example: Canary Current  
     Effect: Cool nearby coastal areas
  </li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Warm = heat transfer outward, Cold = cooling influence.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Causes of Ocean Currents</h4>
<ul>
  <li><b>Wind:</b> Drives surface currents</li>
  <li><b>Temperature differences:</b> Cause density changes</li>
  <li><b>Salinity differences:</b> Affect density and movement</li>
  <li><b>Earth’s rotation (Coriolis effect):</b> Deflects currents</li>
  <li><b>Shape of continents:</b> Redirects flow</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4>3. Major Ocean Currents</h4>
<ul>
  <li><b>Gulf Stream</b> – Warm current in Atlantic Ocean</li>
  <li><b>Canary Current</b> – Cold current off northwest Africa</li>
  <li><b>Labrador Current</b> – Cold current from Arctic</li>
  <li><b>Kuroshio Current</b> – Warm current near Japan</li>
  <li><b>Benguela Current</b> – Cold current off southwest Africa</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4>4. Effects on Climate</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Warm currents increase temperature of nearby land</li>
  <li>Cold currents reduce temperature</li>
  <li>Currents influence rainfall patterns</li>
  <li>Can create deserts (e.g., coastal deserts)</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Example Insight:</b> Cold currents → dry conditions → desert formation.</p>
<hr>
<h4>5. Effects on Fishing</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Cold currents bring nutrients from deep water</li>
  <li>Support plankton growth</li>
  <li>Attract large fish populations</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Best fishing zones = where warm and cold currents meet.</p>
<hr>
<h4>6. Effects on Navigation</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Currents can speed up or slow down ships</li>
  <li>Affect shipping routes</li>
  <li>Influence fuel efficiency</li>
</ul>
  ',
  '[{"q":"What are ocean currents? (2 marks)","ans":"Large, continuous movements of ocean water flowing in specific directions.","hint":"Think flowing water over long distances.","reason":"Core definition."},{"q":"What is a warm current? (2 marks)","ans":"A current that flows from the equator towards the poles carrying warm water.","hint":"Think heat moving away from equator.","reason":"Tests classification."},{"q":"Name one cold current. (1 mark)","ans":"Canary Current.","hint":"Think Africa west coast.","reason":"Recall question."},{"q":"State one effect of ocean currents on climate. (1 mark)","ans":"They influence temperature.","hint":"Think warming or cooling.","reason":"Concept application."},{"q":"Why are fishing grounds found where currents meet? (2 marks)","ans":"Because nutrients are brought to the surface, supporting plankton and fish.","hint":"Think food chain.","reason":"Tests deeper understanding."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> oceans -> Coastal Features (Erosion and Deposition)
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'oceans',
  'Coastal Features (Erosion and Deposition)',
  '<h2> Coastal Features (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Coastal features are landforms found along the coast, formed mainly by the action of waves through processes such as erosion, transportation, and deposition.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Processes at the Coast</h4>
<ul>
  <li>
    <b>Erosion:</b> Wearing away of land by waves  
     Processes include hydraulic action, abrasion, attrition, and solution
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Transportation:</b> Movement of materials by waves  
     Includes longshore drift
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Deposition:</b> Laying down of materials when wave energy reduces
  </li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Strong waves = erosion, Weak waves = deposition.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Erosional Coastal Features</h4>
<ul>
  <li>
    <b>Cliffs:</b> Steep rock faces formed by wave erosion
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Caves:</b> Formed when waves erode weak parts of a cliff
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Arches:</b> Form when caves enlarge and break through a headland
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Stacks:</b> Isolated rock pillars formed when arches collapse
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Stumps:</b> Remains of eroded stacks
  </li>
</ul>
<p><b>Formation Sequence (IMPORTANT):</b></p>
<pre>
Crack → Cave → Arch → Stack → Stump
</pre>
<hr>
<h4>3. Depositional Coastal Features</h4>
<ul>
  <li>
    <b>Beaches:</b> Accumulation of sand and pebbles
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Spits:</b> Long narrow ridges of sand projecting into the sea
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Bars:</b> Ridges of sand closing off bays
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Lagoons:</b> Water bodies formed behind bars
  </li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4>4. Longshore Drift</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Movement of materials along the coast</li>
  <li>Caused by waves approaching at an angle</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Moves sand along the coastline → forms spits and beaches.</p>
<h3> Importance of Coastal Features</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Tourism (beaches)</li>
  <li>Fishing and settlement</li>
  <li>Natural protection against waves</li>
  <li>Harbor formation</li>
</ul>
  ',
  '[{"q":"What is coastal erosion? (2 marks)","ans":"The wearing away of land by wave action.","hint":"Think waves destroying land.","reason":"Tests core definition."},{"q":"What is a spit? (2 marks)","ans":"A long narrow ridge of sand formed by deposition along the coast.","hint":"Think sand stretching into sea.","reason":"Feature identification."},{"q":"Name one erosional feature. (1 mark)","ans":"Cliff.","hint":"Think steep rock.","reason":"Recall question."},{"q":"What is longshore drift? (2 marks)","ans":"Movement of materials along the coast by wave action.","hint":"Think sideways movement.","reason":"Process understanding."},{"q":"How is a stack formed? (2 marks)","ans":"When an arch collapses due to erosion, leaving a pillar of rock.","hint":"Think arch breaking.","reason":"Sequence understanding."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> oceans -> Importance and Disadvantages of Oceans
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'oceans',
  'Importance and Disadvantages of Oceans',
  '<h2> Importance and Disadvantages of Oceans (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Oceans are large bodies of salt water that play a critical role in supporting life, regulating climate, and enabling human activities, but they also pose certain risks and challenges.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Importance of Oceans</h4>
<h4> (a) Climate Regulation</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Oceans absorb and store heat</li>
  <li>Ocean currents distribute heat globally</li>
  <li>Help control temperature and rainfall patterns</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Oceans act as Earth''s temperature control system.</p>
<hr>
<h4> (b) Source of Food</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Provide fish and other marine resources</li>
  <li>Support livelihoods (fishing industry)</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4> (c) Transport and Trade</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Major sea routes connect continents</li>
  <li>Cheapest method of transporting bulky goods</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4> (d) Natural Resources</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Oil and natural gas</li>
  <li>Salt and minerals</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4> (e) Water Cycle</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Main source of evaporation</li>
  <li>Leads to rainfall formation</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4> (f) Tourism and Recreation</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Beaches attract tourists</li>
  <li>Activities: swimming, boating, surfing</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4> (g) Biodiversity</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Home to diverse marine life</li>
  <li>Supports ecosystems</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> 2. Disadvantages of Oceans</h3>
<h4> (a) Natural Hazards</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Storms and hurricanes</li>
  <li>Tsunamis</li>
  <li>Coastal flooding</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4> (b) Transport Risks</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Ship accidents and sinking</li>
  <li>Navigation challenges</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4> (c) Spread of Diseases</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Waterborne diseases can spread through coastal areas</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4> (d) Pollution Problems</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Oil spills damage marine life</li>
  <li>Plastic pollution harms ecosystems</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4> (e) Coastal Erosion</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Waves destroy land and property</li>
  <li>Threatens settlements</li>
</ul>

<hr>
<h4> (f) Overfishing</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Leads to depletion of fish stocks</li>
  <li>Disrupts marine ecosystems</li>
</ul>
  ',
  '[{"q":"State one importance of oceans. (1 mark)","ans":"They regulate climate.","hint":"Think temperature control.","reason":"Basic importance recall."},{"q":"Give one use of oceans. (1 mark)","ans":"Transport of goods.","hint":"Think trade routes.","reason":"Application knowledge."},{"q":"State one disadvantage of oceans. (1 mark)","ans":"They cause coastal erosion.","hint":"Think destruction of land.","reason":"Understanding risks."},{"q":"How do oceans help in rainfall formation? (2 marks)","ans":"They provide water through evaporation which forms clouds and leads to rainfall.","hint":"Think water cycle.","reason":"Process understanding."},{"q":"Why is ocean pollution harmful? (2 marks)","ans":"It damages marine life and disrupts ecosystems.","hint":"Think animals and environment.","reason":"Impact awareness."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> mining_and_minerals -> Introduction to Mining
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'mining_and_minerals',
  'Introduction to Mining',
  '<h2> Introduction to Mining (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Mining is the process of extracting valuable minerals and geological materials from the Earth’s crust for human use.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Meaning of Mining</h4>
<p>
Mining involves removing minerals such as gold, iron ore, coal, and copper from beneath or on the surface of the Earth.
</p>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Mining transforms natural underground resources into usable materials.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Importance of Mining</h4>
<h4> (a) Industrial Development</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Provides raw materials for factories</li>
  <li>Supports manufacturing industries</li>
</ul>
<h4> (b) Economic Growth</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Generates national income</li>
  <li>Earns foreign exchange through exports</li>
</ul>
<h4> (c) Employment Creation</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Provides jobs in extraction and processing</li>
</ul>
<h4> (d) Infrastructure Development</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Mining areas often develop roads and towns</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4>3. History of Mining</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Early humans used stone tools from surface mining</li>
  <li>Ancient civilizations mined gold, copper, and salt</li>
  <li>Industrial Revolution introduced mechanized mining</li>
  <li>Modern mining uses advanced machines and technology</li>
</ul>
  ',
  '[{"q":"Define mining. (1 mark)","ans":"Mining is the extraction of minerals from the Earth''s crust.","hint":"Think extraction from ground.","reason":"Basic definition recall."},{"q":"Give one importance of mining. (1 mark)","ans":"It provides raw materials for industries.","hint":"Think factories.","reason":"Application knowledge."},{"q":"Name one mineral obtained from mining. (1 mark)","ans":"Gold.","hint":"Precious metal.","reason":"Basic identification."},{"q":"Explain how mining contributes to employment. (2 marks)","ans":"Mining creates jobs in extraction, processing, and transport of minerals.","hint":"Think job creation process.","reason":"Understanding economic impact."},{"q":"Describe the importance of mining in economic development. (2 marks)","ans":"Mining generates income, earns foreign exchange, and supports industries.","hint":"Think national economy.","reason":"Higher-order understanding."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> mining_and_minerals -> Metallic Minerals
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'mining_and_minerals',
  'Metallic Minerals',
  '<h2> Metallic Minerals (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>
<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Metallic minerals are minerals that contain metal elements which can be extracted and used in industries after processing.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Meaning of Metallic Minerals</h4>
<p>
These are minerals that contain metals in their raw form. They are mined and processed to extract useful metals.
</p>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Metallic minerals are the main source of industrial metals.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Examples of Metallic Minerals</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Iron ore</li>
  <li>Copper</li>
  <li>Gold</li>
  <li>Bauxite (Aluminium ore)</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4>3. Types of Metallic Minerals</h4>
<h4> (a) Ferrous Minerals</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Contain iron</li>
  <li>Example: Iron ore</li>
  <li>Used in steel production</li>
</ul>
<h4> (b) Non-Ferrous Minerals</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Do not contain iron</li>
  <li>Examples: Gold, copper, aluminium</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4>4. Uses of Metallic Minerals</h4>

<h4> (a) Construction</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Iron is used in buildings and bridges</li>
</ul>
<h4> (b) Manufacturing</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Copper used in electrical wiring</li>
</ul>
<h4> (c) Wealth and Trade</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Gold is used as currency and jewelry</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4>5. Economic Importance</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Foreign exchange earnings</li>
  <li>Industrial development</li>
  <li>Job creation</li>
</ul>
  ',
  '[{"q":"Define metallic minerals. (1 mark)","ans":"Minerals that contain metals which can be extracted and used.","hint":"Think metal content.","reason":"Basic definition recall."},{"q":"Give two examples of metallic minerals. (2 marks)","ans":"Iron ore and gold.","hint":"Think common metals.","reason":"Identification skill."},{"q":"State one use of iron ore. (1 mark)","ans":"It is used to make steel.","hint":"Think construction material.","reason":"Application knowledge."},{"q":"Differentiate ferrous and non-ferrous minerals. (2 marks)","ans":"Ferrous minerals contain iron while non-ferrous minerals do not contain iron.","hint":"Think iron presence.","reason":"Comparison understanding."},{"q":"Explain the importance of metallic minerals in industry. (2 marks)","ans":"They provide raw materials for manufacturing and construction.","hint":"Think factories and buildings.","reason":"Economic application."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> mining_and_minerals -> Non-Metallic Minerals
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'mining_and_minerals',
  'Non-Metallic Minerals',
  '<h2> Non-Metallic Minerals (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Non-metallic minerals are minerals that do not contain metals and are mainly used in construction, chemicals, and agriculture.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Meaning</h4>
<p>
These minerals are non-metal bearing and are not used to extract metals but are used in raw form or processed in industries.
</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Examples</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Limestone</li>
  <li>Salt</li>
  <li>Gypsum</li>
  <li>Phosphates</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4>3. Uses</h4>
<h4> (a) Construction</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Limestone used in cement production</li>
</ul>
<h4> (b) Food Industry</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Salt used for food preservation</li>
</ul>
<h4> (c) Agriculture</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Phosphates used in fertilizers</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4>4. Importance</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Supports construction industry</li>
  <li>Improves agriculture productivity</li>
  <li>Supports chemical industries</li>
</ul>
  ',
  '[{"q":"Define non-metallic minerals. (1 mark)","ans":"Minerals that do not contain metals and are used in industries.","hint":"Think no metal content.","reason":"Basic definition recall."},{"q":"Name two non-metallic minerals. (2 marks)","ans":"Limestone and salt.","hint":"Think construction and food.","reason":"Identification skill."},{"q":"State one use of limestone. (1 mark)","ans":"It is used in cement production.","hint":"Think building materials.","reason":"Application knowledge."},{"q":"Why are phosphates important? (2 marks)","ans":"They are used in fertilizers to improve crop growth.","hint":"Think agriculture.","reason":"Agricultural importance."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> mining_and_minerals -> Energy Minerals
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'mining_and_minerals',
  'Energy Minerals',
  '<h2> Energy Minerals (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Energy minerals are natural resources that are mined or extracted and used to produce energy for domestic, industrial, and transport purposes.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Meaning of Energy Minerals</h4>
<p>
These are minerals that provide fuel or energy when processed or burned. They are essential for electricity generation and industrial production.
</p>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Energy minerals power modern society.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Examples of Energy Minerals</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Coal</li>
  <li>Petroleum (oil)</li>
  <li>Natural gas</li>
  <li>Uranium</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4>3. Types of Energy Minerals</h4>
<h4> (a) Fossil Fuels</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Coal</li>
  <li>Petroleum</li>
  <li>Natural gas</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Formation:</b> Formed from dead plants and animals over millions of years under heat and pressure.</p>
<hr>
<h4> (b) Nuclear Energy Minerals</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Uranium</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Use:</b> Used in nuclear power plants to generate electricity.</p>
<hr>
<h4>4. Uses of Energy Minerals</h4>
<h4> (a) Electricity Generation</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Coal and gas used in thermal power plants</li>
  <li>Uranium used in nuclear energy</li>
</ul>
<h4> (b) Transport</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Petroleum used in vehicles (petrol and diesel)</li>
</ul>
<h4> (c) Industry</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Provides energy for factories and machinery</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4>5. Importance of Energy Minerals</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Power industrial development</li>
  <li>Support transport systems</li>
  <li>Generate electricity for homes and cities</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4>6. Environmental Effects</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Air pollution from burning fossil fuels</li>
  <li>Global warming due to carbon emissions</li>
  <li>Land degradation from mining activities</li>
</ul>
  ',
  '[{"q":"Define energy minerals. (1 mark)","ans":"Minerals used to produce energy such as coal and petroleum.","hint":"Think fuel sources.","reason":"Basic definition recall."},{"q":"Give two examples of fossil fuels. (2 marks)","ans":"Coal and petroleum.","hint":"Think natural fuels.","reason":"Identification skill."},{"q":"State one use of petroleum. (1 mark)","ans":"It is used as fuel in vehicles.","hint":"Think transport energy.","reason":"Application knowledge."},{"q":"Differentiate fossil fuels and nuclear energy minerals. (2 marks)","ans":"Fossil fuels come from organic matter while nuclear minerals like uranium are used in nuclear reactions.","hint":"Think formation and use.","reason":"Comparison understanding."},{"q":"Explain one environmental effect of energy minerals. (2 marks)","ans":"Burning fossil fuels causes air pollution and global warming.","hint":"Think emissions.","reason":"Environmental awareness."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> mining_and_minerals -> Surface Mining
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'mining_and_minerals',
  'Surface Mining',
  '<h2> Surface Mining (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Surface mining is the method of extracting minerals that are located close to the Earth''s surface by removing soil and rock covering them.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Meaning of Surface Mining</h4>
<p>
Surface mining involves removing overburden (soil and rocks) to access minerals found near the Earth’s surface.
</p>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> It is used when minerals are shallow and easy to reach.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Types of Surface Mining</h4>
<h4> (a) Open-Cast Mining</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Large pit is dug to extract minerals</li>
  <li>Used for iron ore, copper, coal</li>
  <li>Layers of soil are removed step by step</li>
</ul>
<h4> (b) Quarrying</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Extraction of building materials like limestone and granite</li>
  <li>Usually done in hilly areas</li>
  <li>Materials are cut or blasted from rock faces</li>
</ul>
<h4> (c) Strip Mining</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Strips of soil are removed in long sections</li>
  <li>Common in flat areas</li>
  <li>Used for coal and some minerals</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4>3. Process of Surface Mining</h4>
<ol>
  <li>Vegetation and topsoil are removed</li>
  <li>Overburden (rock/soil) is cleared</li>
  <li>Mineral layer is exposed</li>
  <li>Minerals are extracted using machines or explosives</li>
  <li>Site may be reclaimed after mining</li>
</ol>
<hr>
<h4>4. Advantages of Surface Mining</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Cheaper than underground mining</li>
  <li>Safer for workers</li>
  <li>Allows easy access to minerals</li>
  <li>High production rate</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4>5. Disadvantages of Surface Mining</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Causes land destruction</li>
  <li>Deforestation</li>
  <li>Dust and air pollution</li>
  <li>Displacement of people</li>
</ul>
  ',
  '[{"q":"Define surface mining. (1 mark)","ans":"It is the extraction of minerals close to the Earth''s surface by removing soil and rock.","hint":"Think shallow minerals.","reason":"Basic definition recall."},{"q":"Name two types of surface mining. (2 marks)","ans":"Open-cast mining and quarrying.","hint":"Think pits and rocks.","reason":"Identification skill."},{"q":"State one advantage of surface mining. (1 mark)","ans":"It is cheaper than underground mining.","hint":"Think cost.","reason":"Application knowledge."},{"q":"Describe strip mining. (2 marks)","ans":"It is the removal of strips of soil to expose minerals beneath.","hint":"Think long strips of land.","reason":"Process understanding."},{"q":"Explain one environmental effect of surface mining. (2 marks)","ans":"It leads to land destruction and deforestation.","hint":"Think environment damage.","reason":"Environmental awareness."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> mining_and_minerals -> Underground Mining
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'mining_and_minerals',
  'Underground Mining',
  '<h2> Underground Mining (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>
<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Underground mining is the method of extracting minerals that are located deep beneath the Earth''s surface by digging tunnels, shafts, and passages.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Meaning of Underground Mining</h4>
<p>
This method is used when minerals are located too deep for surface mining. Workers dig tunnels and shafts to reach the mineral deposits.
</p>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> It is used for deep mineral deposits.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Types of Underground Mining</h4>
<h4> (a) Shaft Mining</h4>
<ul>
  <li>A vertical shaft is dug from the surface</li>
  <li>Horizontal tunnels are made to reach minerals</li>
  <li>Used for deep minerals like gold and coal</li>
</ul>
<h4> (b) Drift Mining</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Horizontal tunnels are dug into hillsides</li>
  <li>Used when mineral seams are exposed on slopes</li>
  <li>Common in coal mining</li>
</ul>
<h4> (c) Slope Mining</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Slanting tunnels are dug into the ground</li>
  <li>Follows the slope of the land</li>
  <li>Used when minerals are slightly deep</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4>3. Process of Underground Mining</h4>
<ol>
  <li>Surveying and locating mineral deposits</li>
  <li>Digging shafts or tunnels</li>
  <li>Installing support structures to prevent collapse</li>
  <li>Extracting minerals using machines or explosives</li>
  <li>Transporting minerals to the surface</li>
</ol>
<hr>
<h4>4. Advantages of Underground Mining</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Less surface damage compared to surface mining</li>
  <li>Allows extraction of deep minerals</li>
  <li>Less visual pollution on land surface</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4>5. Disadvantages of Underground Mining</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Very expensive to operate</li>
  <li>Dangers like tunnel collapse and accidents</li>
  <li>Hard working conditions (heat, darkness)</li>
  <li>Slow extraction process</li>
</ul>
  ',
  '[{"q":"Define underground mining. (1 mark)","ans":"It is the extraction of minerals from deep beneath the Earth''s surface using tunnels and shafts.","hint":"Think deep mining.","reason":"Basic definition recall."},{"q":"Name two types of underground mining. (2 marks)","ans":"Shaft mining and drift mining.","hint":"Think tunnels and slopes.","reason":"Identification skill."},{"q":"State one advantage of underground mining. (1 mark)","ans":"It causes less surface destruction.","hint":"Think land impact.","reason":"Application knowledge."},{"q":"Describe shaft mining. (2 marks)","ans":"A vertical shaft is dug and tunnels are made to reach deep minerals.","hint":"Think vertical digging.","reason":"Process understanding."},{"q":"Explain two challenges of underground mining. (2 marks)","ans":"It is expensive and dangerous due to possible tunnel collapse.","hint":"Think risk and cost.","reason":"Critical understanding."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> mining_and_minerals -> Factors Influencing Mining
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'mining_and_minerals',
  'Factors Influencing Mining',
  '<h2> Factors Influencing Mining (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>
<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Factors influencing mining are the physical, economic, and political conditions that determine whether mining will take place and how successful it will be.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Type of Mineral</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Valuable minerals like gold and diamonds attract more mining</li>
  <li>Low-value minerals may not be mined if extraction is costly</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> The value of a mineral determines mining intensity.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Depth of Deposit</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Shallow deposits are cheaper to mine (surface mining)</li>
  <li>Deep deposits require expensive underground mining</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> The deeper the mineral, the higher the cost of extraction.</p>
<hr>
<h4>3. Capital Availability</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Mining requires heavy investment in machinery and labor</li>
  <li>Countries with more capital develop advanced mining industries</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Without money, mining cannot be developed even if minerals exist.</p>
<hr>
<h4>4. Transport Infrastructure</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Good roads and railways make mining easier</li>
  <li>Poor transport increases cost of moving minerals</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Mining depends on efficient transport systems.</p>
<hr>
<h4>5. Government Policies</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Laws and taxes can encourage or discourage mining</li>
  <li>Stable governments attract foreign mining investors</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Political stability supports mining growth.</p>
  ',
  '[{"q":"Define factors influencing mining. (1 mark)","ans":"They are conditions that determine how and whether mining takes place.","hint":"Think mining conditions.","reason":"Basic definition recall."},{"q":"State two factors influencing mining. (2 marks)","ans":"Capital availability and transport infrastructure.","hint":"Think money and roads.","reason":"Identification skill."},{"q":"How does depth of minerals affect mining? (2 marks)","ans":"Deep minerals are more expensive to extract because they require underground mining.","hint":"Think surface vs deep.","reason":"Understanding cost impact."},{"q":"Why is capital important in mining? (2 marks)","ans":"It is needed to buy machinery, pay workers, and run mining operations.","hint":"Think investment needs.","reason":"Economic reasoning."},{"q":"Explain the role of government policies in mining. (2 marks)","ans":"They regulate mining activities and can encourage or discourage investment through laws and taxes.","hint":"Think rules and laws.","reason":"Political/economic understanding."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> mining_and_minerals -> Importance of Mining
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'mining_and_minerals',
  'Importance of Mining',
  '<h2> Importance of Mining (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
The importance of mining refers to the benefits that mining activities bring to individuals, industries, and the national economy.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Employment Creation</h4>
<p>
Mining provides direct and indirect jobs to people. Direct jobs include workers in mines, while indirect jobs include transporters, traders, and equipment suppliers.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Miners (extraction workers)</li>
  <li>Engineers and geologists</li>
  <li>Transport workers</li>
  <li>Security and administrative staff</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Mining reduces unemployment in mining regions.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Foreign Exchange Earnings</h4>
<p>
Countries earn foreign exchange by exporting minerals such as gold, diamonds, oil, and copper to other countries.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Exports generate national income</li>
  <li>Strengthens the country’s economy</li>
  <li>Helps balance trade deficits</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Mining increases a country’s global trade value.</p>
<hr>
<h4>3. Industrial Raw Materials</h4>
<p>
Mining provides essential raw materials for industries that produce goods and services.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Iron ore → steel production</li>
  <li>Bauxite → aluminium production</li>
  <li>Coal → energy for factories</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Without mining, most industries cannot function.</p>
<hr>
<h4>4. Infrastructure Development</h4>
<p>
Mining activities lead to the development of infrastructure such as roads, railways, and towns in mining areas.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Construction of roads for mineral transport</li>
  <li>Development of housing for workers</li>
  <li>Establishment of schools and hospitals</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Mining promotes regional development.</p>
  ',
  '[{"q":"State one importance of mining. (1 mark)","ans":"It creates employment opportunities.","hint":"Think jobs.","reason":"Basic recall."},{"q":"How does mining create foreign exchange? (2 marks)","ans":"By exporting minerals to other countries and earning foreign currency.","hint":"Think exports.","reason":"Trade understanding."},{"q":"Give one example of an industrial raw material. (1 mark)","ans":"Iron ore.","hint":"Think steel production.","reason":"Identification skill."},{"q":"Explain how mining supports infrastructure development. (2 marks)","ans":"It leads to construction of roads, railways, and settlements in mining areas.","hint":"Think development around mines.","reason":"Application understanding."},{"q":"Describe the importance of mining in economic development. (2 marks)","ans":"Mining creates jobs, earns foreign exchange, supplies raw materials, and supports infrastructure.","hint":"Think overall economy.","reason":"Higher-order synthesis."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> mining_and_minerals -> Environmental Effects of Mining
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'mining_and_minerals',
  'Environmental Effects of Mining',
  '<h2> Environmental Effects of Mining (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Environmental effects of mining refer to the negative impacts that mining activities have on the natural environment, ecosystems, and human settlements.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Land Degradation</h4>
<p>
Mining removes vegetation and topsoil, leaving land bare and unproductive.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Creates open pits and scars on the landscape</li>
  <li>Reduces agricultural productivity</li>
  <li>Destroys natural habitats</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Mining permanently alters the natural land surface.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Deforestation</h4>
<p>
Trees are cleared to access mineral deposits and build mining infrastructure.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Loss of forest cover</li>
  <li>Destruction of wildlife habitats</li>
  <li>Reduced oxygen production</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Mining contributes to forest destruction and biodiversity loss.</p>
<hr>
<h4>3. Water Pollution</h4>
<p>
Chemicals and waste from mining contaminate rivers, lakes, and groundwater.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Acid mine drainage</li>
  <li>Oil and chemical spills</li>
  <li>Death of aquatic life</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Polluted water affects both humans and ecosystems.</p>
<hr>
<h4>4. Air Pollution</h4>
<p>
Dust and gases released during mining and mineral processing pollute the air.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Dust from blasting and excavation</li>
  <li>Smoke from machinery and processing plants</li>
  <li>Contributes to respiratory diseases</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Mining activities reduce air quality in nearby areas.</p>
<hr>
<h4>5. Displacement of People</h4>
<p>
Communities are sometimes forced to relocate when mining projects expand.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Loss of homes and farmland</li>
  <li>Social disruption</li>
  <li>Loss of cultural heritage sites</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Mining can lead to social and economic displacement.</p>
  ',
  '[{"q":"State one environmental effect of mining. (1 mark)","ans":"Land degradation.","hint":"Think land damage.","reason":"Basic recall."},{"q":"How does mining cause water pollution? (2 marks)","ans":"Chemicals and waste from mining contaminate rivers and groundwater.","hint":"Think waste and rivers.","reason":"Process understanding."},{"q":"Give one effect of deforestation caused by mining. (1 mark)","ans":"Loss of wildlife habitats.","hint":"Think animals.","reason":"Impact recognition."},{"q":"Why does mining cause air pollution? (2 marks)","ans":"Because dust and gases from mining operations are released into the air.","hint":"Think dust and smoke.","reason":"Environmental process understanding."},{"q":"Explain how mining affects human settlements. (2 marks)","ans":"Mining can force people to relocate, causing loss of homes and farmland.","hint":"Think displacement.","reason":"Social impact understanding."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> mining_and_minerals -> Problems Facing Mining
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'mining_and_minerals',
  'Problems Facing Mining',
  '<h2> Problems Facing Mining</h2>
<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Problems facing mining are the challenges and difficulties that reduce efficiency, safety, and productivity in mining activities.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. High Cost of Equipment</h4>
<p>
Mining requires expensive machines, tools, and technology which many countries or companies cannot afford.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Heavy machinery is costly to buy and maintain</li>
  <li>Advanced technology is needed for deep mining</li>
  <li>Small companies struggle to compete</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Mining is capital-intensive and expensive to operate.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Poor Transport Systems</h4>
<p>
In many mining areas, roads and railways are underdeveloped, making it difficult to transport minerals.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Delays in moving minerals to markets</li>
  <li>High transportation costs</li>
  <li>Isolation of mining regions</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Without transport, mining loses economic value.</p>
<hr>
<h4>3. Accidents and Hazards</h4>
<p>
Mining is a dangerous activity with risks such as tunnel collapse, explosions, and machinery accidents.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Underground collapses</li>
  <li>Gas explosions in mines</li>
  <li>Injuries and deaths of workers</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Safety risks are high in mining environments.</p>
<hr>
<h4>4. Exhaustion of Minerals</h4>
<p>
Mineral deposits are finite and can be completely used up over time.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Some mines close after depletion</li>
  <li>Reduces long-term mining activities</li>
  <li>Affects local economies dependent on mining</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Minerals are non-renewable resources.</p>
  ',
  '[{"q":"State one problem facing mining. (1 mark)","ans":"High cost of equipment.","hint":"Think expensive machinery.","reason":"Basic recall."},{"q":"How does poor transport affect mining? (2 marks)","ans":"It delays movement of minerals and increases transport costs.","hint":"Think roads and delays.","reason":"Economic impact understanding."},{"q":"Give one danger of mining. (1 mark)","ans":"Tunnel collapse.","hint":"Think underground risk.","reason":"Safety awareness."},{"q":"Why is exhaustion of minerals a problem? (2 marks)","ans":"It leads to closure of mines and loss of jobs.","hint":"Think depletion.","reason":"Resource sustainability understanding."},{"q":"Explain two problems facing mining. (2 marks)","ans":"Mining is expensive due to equipment costs and dangerous due to accidents like explosions and collapses.","hint":"Think cost and safety.","reason":"Integrated understanding."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> agriculture -> Introduction to Agriculture
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'agriculture',
  'Introduction to Agriculture',
  '<h2> Introduction to Agriculture </h2>
<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Agriculture is the practice of cultivating crops and rearing livestock for human use such as food, clothing, and raw materials.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Meaning of Agriculture</h4>
<p>
Agriculture involves growing crops like maize, wheat, and rice, and raising animals like cattle, goats, and sheep for human needs.
</p>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Agriculture is the foundation of human survival.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Importance of Agriculture</h4>
<h4> (a) Food Supply</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Provides crops and livestock products</li>
  <li>Ensures food security for populations</li>
</ul>
<h4> (b) Raw Materials</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Supplies cotton, wool, sugarcane for industries</li>
</ul>
<h4> (c) Employment</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Creates jobs for farmers and workers</li>
</ul>
<h4> (d) Economic Growth</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Contributes to national income</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4>3. Branches of Agriculture</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Crop production (arable farming)</li>
  <li>Livestock production (animal husbandry)</li>
</ul>
  ',
  '[{"q":"Define agriculture. (1 mark)","ans":"It is the practice of growing crops and rearing animals for human use.","hint":"Think farming.","reason":"Basic definition recall."},{"q":"State one importance of agriculture. (1 mark)","ans":"It provides food for people.","hint":"Think survival.","reason":"Basic understanding."},{"q":"Name the two branches of agriculture. (2 marks)","ans":"Crop production and livestock production.","hint":"Think plants and animals.","reason":"Identification skill."},{"q":"How does agriculture create employment? (2 marks)","ans":"It provides jobs for farmers, farm workers, and traders.","hint":"Think jobs in farming.","reason":"Economic understanding."},{"q":"Explain the importance of agriculture in food supply. (2 marks)","ans":"It produces crops and animal products that feed the population.","hint":"Think food security.","reason":"Application understanding."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> agriculture -> Types of Agriculture
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'agriculture',
  'Types of Agriculture',
  '<h2> Types of Agriculture (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Types of agriculture refer to the different ways farming is carried out based on purpose, scale, and level of production.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Subsistence Agriculture</h4>
<p>
Subsistence agriculture is a type of farming where crops and livestock are produced mainly for family consumption rather than for sale.
</p>
<h4> Characteristics</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Small-scale production</li>
  <li>Low use of technology</li>
  <li>Family labor is commonly used</li>
  <li>Little or no surplus for sale</li>
</ul>
<h4> Examples</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Growing maize for home use</li>
  <li>Keeping a few goats or chickens</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Farming for survival, not profit.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Commercial Agriculture</h4>
<p>
Commercial agriculture is large-scale farming carried out mainly for sale and profit in local and international markets.
</p>
<h4> Characteristics</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Large-scale production</li>
  <li>High use of machinery and technology</li>
  <li>Use of hired labor</li>
  <li>Production for market sale</li>
</ul>
<h4> Examples</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Tea plantations</li>
  <li>Coffee farming</li>
  <li>Large wheat farms</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Farming for profit and trade.</p>
  ',
  '[{"q":"Define subsistence agriculture. (1 mark)","ans":"It is farming done mainly for family consumption.","hint":"Think home use.","reason":"Basic definition recall."},{"q":"State one feature of commercial agriculture. (1 mark)","ans":"It is large-scale farming.","hint":"Think size.","reason":"Identification skill."},{"q":"Give one example of subsistence farming. (1 mark)","ans":"Growing maize for home use.","hint":"Think food crops.","reason":"Application knowledge."},{"q":"Differentiate subsistence and commercial agriculture. (2 marks)","ans":"Subsistence farming is for family use while commercial farming is for sale and profit.","hint":"Think purpose.","reason":"Comparison understanding."},{"q":"Explain the importance of commercial agriculture. (2 marks)","ans":"It provides income and produces large quantities of food for markets.","hint":"Think trade and economy.","reason":"Economic understanding."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> agriculture -> Farming Systems
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'agriculture',
  'Farming Systems',
  '<h2> Farming Systems (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Farming systems refer to the different ways in which crops and livestock are organized and managed on a farm to achieve production.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Mixed Farming</h4>
<p>
Mixed farming is a system where both crop production and livestock rearing are carried out on the same farm.
</p>
<h4> Characteristics</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Crops and animals are kept together</li>
  <li>Wastes from animals used as manure</li>
  <li>Provides diversified income</li>
</ul>
<h4> Example</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Growing maize and keeping cattle on the same farm</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Integration of crops and livestock.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Dairy Farming</h4>
<p>
Dairy farming is the rearing of cattle for milk production and related products.
</p>
<h4> Characteristics</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Focus on milk production</li>
  <li>Requires high-quality breeds</li>
  <li>Often near urban markets</li>
</ul>
<h4> Products</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Milk</li>
  <li>Cheese</li>
  <li>Yogurt</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Milk production-based farming system.</p>
<hr>
<h4>3. Pastoralism (Livestock Farming)</h4>
<p>
Pastoralism is a farming system where people keep large herds of animals such as cattle, goats, and sheep, often moving in search of pasture and water.
</p>
<h4> Characteristics</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Nomadic or semi-nomadic lifestyle</li>
  <li>Large herds of animals</li>
  <li>Depends on natural grazing</li>
</ul>
<h4> Example</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Keeping cattle in dry regions like savannah areas</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Livestock movement for pasture.</p>
<hr>
<h4>4. Crop Farming Systems</h4>
<p>
This system focuses only on growing crops for food, cash, or industrial use.
</p>
<h4> Characteristics</h4>
<ul>
  <li>No livestock kept</li>
  <li>Focus on crop production</li>
  <li>Can be subsistence or commercial</li>
</ul>
<h4> Examples</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Tea farming</li>
  <li>Rice farming</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Plant-based farming system only.</p>
  ',
  '[{"q":"Define farming systems. (1 mark)","ans":"They are ways in which crops and livestock are organized on a farm.","hint":"Think farm organization.","reason":"Basic definition recall."},{"q":"State one product of dairy farming. (1 mark)","ans":"Milk.","hint":"Think milk production.","reason":"Identification skill."},{"q":"What is pastoralism? (2 marks)","ans":"It is livestock farming involving movement in search of pasture and water.","hint":"Think nomadic herding.","reason":"Concept understanding."},{"q":"Give one example of crop farming. (1 mark)","ans":"Tea farming.","hint":"Think plantations.","reason":"Application knowledge."},{"q":"Explain mixed farming. (2 marks)","ans":"It is a system where crops and livestock are kept on the same farm.","hint":"Think integration.","reason":"Understanding integration."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> agriculture -> Types of Farming (Methods)
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'agriculture',
  'Types of Farming (Methods)',
  '<h2> Types of Farming (Methods) — Advanced Mastery Notes</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Types of farming (methods) refer to the different ways crops are grown and livestock are reared based on land use, intensity, and farming techniques.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Shifting Cultivation</h4>
<p>
Shifting cultivation is a farming method where farmers clear a piece of land, cultivate it for a few years, then move to a new area when soil fertility declines.
</p>
<h4> Characteristics</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Land is cleared by cutting and burning vegetation</li>
  <li>Farmers move to new land after exhaustion</li>
  <li>Low input of technology</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Farming moves from one land to another.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Irrigation Farming</h4>
<p>
Irrigation farming is the artificial supply of water to crops in areas where rainfall is insufficient.
</p>
<h4> Characteristics</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Uses canals, pipes, or sprinklers</li>
  <li>Supports farming in dry areas</li>
  <li>Allows year-round farming</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Water is supplied artificially.</p>
<hr>
<h4>3. Plantation Agriculture</h4>
<p>
Plantation agriculture is large-scale farming of a single cash crop, usually for export.
</p>
<h4> Characteristics</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Monoculture (one crop)</li>
  <li>Large estates</li>
  <li>Uses hired labor and machinery</li>
</ul>
<h4> Examples</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Tea plantations</li>
  <li>Coffee plantations</li>
  <li>Sugarcane estates</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> One crop grown on a large scale.</p>
<hr>
<h4>4. Intensive Farming</h4>
<p>
Intensive farming is a system where a small piece of land is used to produce high output through heavy use of labor and inputs.
</p>
<h4> Characteristics</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Small land area</li>
  <li>High labor input</li>
  <li>Use of fertilizers and technology</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> High output from small land.</p>
<hr>
<h4>5. Extensive Farming</h4>
<p>
Extensive farming is a method where large areas of land are used with low inputs of labor and capital.
</p>
<h4> Characteristics</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Large land area</li>
  <li>Low population density</li>
  <li>Low use of fertilizers and machinery</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Low input, large land use.</p>
  ',
  '[{"q":"Define shifting cultivation. (1 mark)","ans":"It is a farming method where farmers move to new land when soil fertility declines.","hint":"Think moving farms.","reason":"Basic definition recall."},{"q":"State one feature of irrigation farming. (1 mark)","ans":"Water is supplied artificially to crops.","hint":"Think water systems.","reason":"Identification skill."},{"q":"What is plantation agriculture? (2 marks)","ans":"It is large-scale farming of a single cash crop for export.","hint":"Think monoculture.","reason":"Concept understanding."},{"q":"Differentiate intensive and extensive farming. (2 marks)","ans":"Intensive farming uses small land with high inputs while extensive farming uses large land with low inputs.","hint":"Think land and input levels.","reason":"Comparison skill."},{"q":"Give one example of plantation agriculture. (1 mark)","ans":"Tea farming.","hint":"Think cash crops.","reason":"Application knowledge."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> agriculture -> Factors Influencing Agriculture
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'agriculture',
  'Factors Influencing Agriculture',
  '<h2> Factors Influencing Agriculture (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Factors influencing agriculture are the physical and human conditions that affect where, how, and what type of farming is carried out.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4> 1. Physical Factors</h4>
<h4> (a) Climate</h4>
<p>
Climate affects rainfall, temperature, and growing seasons.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>High rainfall → good crop growth</li>
  <li>Low rainfall → drought farming or irrigation needed</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Climate determines what crops can grow.</p>
<hr>
<h4> (b) Soil</h4>
<p>
Soil type and fertility affect crop production.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Fertile soils → high yields</li>
  <li>Sandy soils → poor crop growth</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Soil quality controls productivity.</p>
<hr>
<h4> (c) Relief (Topography)</h4>
<p>
Relief refers to the shape and slope of the land.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Flat land → easy farming and mechanization</li>
  <li>Steep slopes → soil erosion risk</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Flat land favors agriculture.</p>
<hr>
<h4>‍ 2. Human Factors</h4>
<h4> (a) Technology</h4>
<p>
Use of machines, fertilizers, and improved seeds increases production.
</p>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Technology increases efficiency.</p>
<hr>
<h4> (b) Capital</h4>
<p>
Money is needed for buying inputs like seeds, machines, and labor.
</p>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Agriculture needs investment.</p>
<hr>
<h4> (c) Labour</h4>
<p>
Human effort is required for planting, harvesting, and animal care.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Skilled labour improves output</li>
  <li>Labour shortage reduces production</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4> (d) Market Access</h4>
<p>
Farmers need markets to sell their products.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Good markets → high production motivation</li>
  <li>Poor markets → low farming activity</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Farmers grow what can be sold.</p>
  ',
  '[{"q":"State one physical factor influencing agriculture. (1 mark)","ans":"Climate.","hint":"Think weather conditions.","reason":"Basic recall."},{"q":"How does soil affect agriculture? (2 marks)","ans":"Fertile soil increases crop yields while poor soil reduces production.","hint":"Think fertility.","reason":"Concept understanding."},{"q":"State one human factor influencing agriculture. (1 mark)","ans":"Capital.","hint":"Think money.","reason":"Identification skill."},{"q":"Explain the role of technology in agriculture. (2 marks)","ans":"Technology increases efficiency and improves crop yields through machines and improved seeds.","hint":"Think mechanization.","reason":"Application understanding."},{"q":"Explain how market access influences agriculture. (2 marks)","ans":"Good markets encourage farmers to produce more because they can sell their products easily.","hint":"Think demand and supply.","reason":"Economic understanding."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> agriculture -> Agricultural Inputs and Outputs
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'agriculture',
  'Agricultural Inputs and Outputs',
  '<h2> Agricultural Inputs and Outputs (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>
<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Agricultural inputs are the resources used in farming production, while outputs are the final products obtained from farming activities.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Agricultural Inputs</h4>
<p>
Inputs are all resources used in farming to produce crops and livestock products.
</p>
<h4> (a) Seeds and Fertilizers</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Seeds are used for planting crops</li>
  <li>Fertilizers improve soil fertility and crop yield</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Inputs help start and improve production.</p>
<hr>
<h4> (b) Tools and Machinery</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Hand tools: hoes, pangas, spades</li>
  <li>Machinery: tractors, harvesters, ploughs</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Tools increase efficiency and reduce labor.</p>
<hr>
<h4> (c) Labour</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Human effort used in farming activities</li>
  <li>Includes planting, weeding, harvesting, and animal care</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Labour is essential for farm operations.</p>
<hr>
<h4> (d) Water and Irrigation</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Used for crop growth and animal survival</li>
  <li>Important in dry regions</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4>2. Agricultural Outputs</h4>
<p>
Outputs are the final products obtained after farming activities.
</p>
<h4> (a) Crop Products</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Maize, wheat, rice</li>
  <li>Cash crops like tea, coffee, cotton</li>
</ul>
<h4> (b) Livestock Products</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Milk, meat, eggs</li>
  <li>Hides and skins</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Outputs are what farming produces for use or sale.</p>
  ',
  '[{"q":"Define agricultural inputs. (1 mark)","ans":"They are resources used in farming production.","hint":"Think resources used in farming.","reason":"Basic definition recall."},{"q":"State two examples of inputs. (2 marks)","ans":"Seeds and fertilizers.","hint":"Think planting materials.","reason":"Identification skill."},{"q":"State one agricultural output. (1 mark)","ans":"Milk.","hint":"Think livestock products.","reason":"Basic recall."},{"q":"Differentiate inputs and outputs. (2 marks)","ans":"Inputs are resources used in farming while outputs are products obtained from farming.","hint":"Think before and after.","reason":"Comparison understanding."},{"q":"Explain the importance of fertilizers. (2 marks)","ans":"They improve soil fertility and increase crop yields.","hint":"Think soil nutrients.","reason":"Application understanding."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> agriculture -> Problems Facing Agriculture
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'agriculture',
  'Problems Facing Agriculture',
  '<h2> Problems Facing Agriculture (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Problems facing agriculture are the challenges that reduce agricultural productivity and affect farmers’ ability to produce enough food and income.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Drought</h4>
<p>
Drought is a long period of low rainfall that leads to water shortage for crops and livestock.
</p>

<ul>
  <li>Crops dry and fail to grow</li>
  <li>Livestock lack water and pasture</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Water shortage reduces agricultural output.</p>

<hr>

<h4>2. Pests and Diseases</h4>
<p>
Pests (insects, rodents) and diseases attack crops and livestock, reducing productivity.
</p>

<ul>
  <li>Destroy crops in the field</li>
  <li>Kill or weaken animals</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Biological threats reduce yields.</p>

<hr>

<h4>3. Poor Markets</h4>
<p>
Farmers may lack reliable markets to sell their products.
</p>

<ul>
  <li>Low prices for farm produce</li>
  <li>Loss of income for farmers</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Without markets, farming becomes unprofitable.</p>

<hr>

<h4>4. Land Degradation</h4>
<p>
Soil becomes less fertile due to overuse, erosion, and poor farming methods.
</p>

<ul>
  <li>Low crop yields</li>
  <li>Soil erosion and nutrient loss</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Poor soil reduces productivity.</p>

<hr>

<h4>5. Lack of Capital</h4>
<p>
Farmers may not have enough money to buy inputs like seeds, fertilizers, and machinery.
</p>

<ul>
  <li>Low use of modern technology</li>
  <li>Small-scale production</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Money is needed for farming development.</p>
  ',
  '[{"q":"State one problem facing agriculture. (1 mark)","ans":"Drought.","hint":"Think lack of rain.","reason":"Basic recall."},{"q":"How do pests affect agriculture? (2 marks)","ans":"They destroy crops and reduce yields.","hint":"Think crop damage.","reason":"Impact understanding."},{"q":"Why is lack of capital a problem? (2 marks)","ans":"Farmers cannot buy inputs like seeds, fertilizers, and machinery.","hint":"Think money constraints.","reason":"Economic understanding."},{"q":"State one effect of poor markets on farmers. (1 mark)","ans":"Low income from farm produce.","hint":"Think sales.","reason":"Basic recall."},{"q":"Explain how land degradation affects agriculture. (2 marks)","ans":"It reduces soil fertility leading to low crop yields.","hint":"Think soil quality.","reason":"Environmental understanding."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> agriculture -> Solutions to Agricultural Problems
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'agriculture',
  'Solutions to Agricultural Problems',
  '<h2> Solutions to Agricultural Problems (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Solutions to agricultural problems are the strategies and practices used to reduce farming challenges and improve agricultural productivity.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Irrigation</h4>
<p>
Irrigation is the artificial supply of water to crops in areas with insufficient rainfall.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Ensures farming during dry seasons</li>
  <li>Increases crop yields</li>
  <li>Reduces dependence on rainfall</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Irrigation solves drought problems.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Pest and Disease Control</h4>
<p>
This involves using chemicals, biological methods, and good farming practices to reduce pests and diseases.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Use of pesticides and herbicides</li>
  <li>Use of disease-resistant crop varieties</li>
  <li>Proper farm hygiene</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Protects crops and livestock from damage.</p>
<hr>
<h4>3. Improved Seeds</h4>
<p>
Improved seeds are high-yield, disease-resistant, and fast-growing crop varieties.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Increase production per hectare</li>
  <li>Resist pests and diseases</li>
  <li>Adapt to different climates</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Better seeds lead to better harvests.</p>
<hr>
<h4>4. Extension Services</h4>
<p>
Agricultural extension services involve educating farmers on modern farming methods.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Training farmers on new techniques</li>
  <li>Providing advice and support</li>
  <li>Improving farming knowledge</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Knowledge improves farming efficiency.</p>
<hr>
<h4>5. Mechanization</h4>
<p>
Mechanization is the use of machines instead of manual labor in farming.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Use of tractors and harvesters</li>
  <li>Reduces labor costs</li>
  <li>Increases speed of production</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Machines improve efficiency and output.</p>
  ',
  '[{"q":"State one solution to drought. (1 mark)","ans":"Irrigation.","hint":"Think water supply.","reason":"Basic recall."},{"q":"How does pest control help agriculture? (2 marks)","ans":"It reduces crop damage and increases yields.","hint":"Think protection.","reason":"Impact understanding."},{"q":"State one benefit of improved seeds. (1 mark)","ans":"They increase crop production.","hint":"Think yield.","reason":"Identification skill."},{"q":"Explain the importance of mechanization. (2 marks)","ans":"It increases efficiency and reduces labor costs in farming.","hint":"Think machines.","reason":"Application understanding."},{"q":"Explain the role of extension services. (2 marks)","ans":"They educate farmers on modern farming methods and improve productivity.","hint":"Think training.","reason":"Knowledge application."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> glaciation -> Introduction to Glaciation
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'glaciation',
  'Introduction to Glaciation',
  '<h2> Introduction to Glaciation (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Glaciation is the process by which large masses of ice (glaciers) form, move, and shape the Earth''s surface through erosion, transportation, and deposition.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Meaning of Glaciation</h4>
<p>
Glaciation refers to long-term processes involving the accumulation, movement, and melting of ice that modifies landscapes.
</p>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Glaciation is slow but powerful landscape shaping by ice.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Formation of Glaciers</h4>
<p>
Glaciers form when snow accumulates over many years, compresses, and turns into ice.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Snowfall exceeds melting</li>
  <li>Layers of snow compress into ice</li>
  <li>Ice begins to move under gravity</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Glaciers are formed from compacted snow.</p>
<hr>
<h4>3. Conditions for Glaciation</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Very low temperatures (below 0°C)</li>
  <li>High snowfall</li>
  <li>Cool summers (minimal melting)</li>
  <li>High altitude or polar regions</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Glaciers form where snow accumulates faster than it melts.</p>
  ',
  '[{"q":"Define glaciation. (1 mark)","ans":"It is the process involving the formation and movement of glaciers.","hint":"Think ice processes.","reason":"Basic definition recall."},{"q":"How are glaciers formed? (2 marks)","ans":"They form when snow accumulates and is compressed into ice over time.","hint":"Think snow to ice.","reason":"Process understanding."},{"q":"State one condition for glaciation. (1 mark)","ans":"Very low temperatures.","hint":"Think cold climate.","reason":"Identification skill."},{"q":"Why are glaciers found in polar regions? (2 marks)","ans":"Because temperatures are low enough for snow to accumulate and not melt.","hint":"Think climate.","reason":"Environmental understanding."},{"q":"State three conditions necessary for glaciation. (3 marks)","ans":"Low temperatures, high snowfall, and cool summers.","hint":"Think cold conditions.","reason":"Comprehensive recall."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> glaciation -> Types of Glaciers
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'glaciation',
  'Types of Glaciers',
  '<h2> Types of Glaciers (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Glaciers are large masses of ice that move slowly over land. They are mainly classified based on their size and location.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Valley Glaciers</h4>
<p>
Valley glaciers are long, narrow rivers of ice that flow down mountain valleys.
</p>

<h4> Characteristics</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Found in mountainous regions</li>
  <li>Flow through pre-existing valleys</li>
  <li>Move downhill due to gravity</li>
  <li>Smaller compared to ice sheets</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Valley glaciers behave like slow-moving ice rivers.</p>

<hr>

<h4> Examples of Valley Glaciers</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Glaciers in the Himalayas</li>
  <li>Alpine glaciers in Europe</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h4>2. Continental Glaciers (Ice Sheets)</h4>

<p>
Continental glaciers are very large masses of ice that cover vast areas of land, usually in polar regions.
</p>

<h4> Characteristics</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Cover large continental areas</li>
  <li>Not restricted to valleys</li>
  <li>Move outward in all directions</li>
  <li>Found in polar regions</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Ice sheets cover entire landscapes.</p>

<hr>

<h4> Examples of Continental Glaciers</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Antarctica Ice Sheet</li>
  <li>Greenland Ice Sheet</li>
</ul>
  ',
  '[{"q":"State two types of glaciers. (2 marks)","ans":"Valley glaciers and continental glaciers.","hint":"Think mountains and ice sheets.","reason":"Basic classification recall."},{"q":"What are valley glaciers? (2 marks)","ans":"They are long, narrow glaciers that flow through mountain valleys.","hint":"Think ice rivers.","reason":"Concept understanding."},{"q":"State one characteristic of continental glaciers. (1 mark)","ans":"They cover large areas of land.","hint":"Think size.","reason":"Identification skill."},{"q":"Where are continental glaciers found? (2 marks)","ans":"In polar regions such as Antarctica and Greenland.","hint":"Think cold regions.","reason":"Geographical knowledge."},{"q":"Differentiate valley and continental glaciers. (2 marks)","ans":"Valley glaciers are found in mountains while continental glaciers cover large land areas in polar regions.","hint":"Think location and size.","reason":"Comparison understanding."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> glaciation -> Glacial Processes
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'glaciation',
  'Glacial Processes',
  '<h2> Glacial Processes (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Glacial processes are the actions by which glaciers erode, transport, and deposit materials as they move over the Earth''s surface.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>

<h4>1. Glacial Erosion</h4>
<p>
Glacial erosion is the wearing away of the Earth''s surface by moving ice and embedded rock debris.
</p>

<h4> (a) Plucking</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Ice freezes onto rocks and pulls them away as it moves</li>
  <li>Large chunks of rock are removed from the ground</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Rocks are “plucked” from the ground by ice.</p>

<hr>

<h4> (b) Abrasion</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Rock fragments in ice scrape and grind the ground surface</li>
  <li>Produces smooth, polished rock surfaces</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Glaciers act like sandpaper on the ground.</p>

<hr>

<h4>2. Glacial Transportation</h4>
<p>
This is the movement of rock debris and sediment carried by glaciers as they flow.
</p>

<ul>
  <li>Materials are carried within, on top, or at the base of ice</li>
  <li>Includes rocks, sand, and clay</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Glaciers move large amounts of debris.</p>

<hr>

<h4>3. Glacial Deposition</h4>
<p>
Deposition occurs when a glacier loses energy and drops the material it was carrying.
</p>

<ul>
  <li>Occurs when ice melts</li>
  <li>Leaves behind unsorted materials</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Melting ice releases transported material.</p>
  ',
  '[{"q":"State three glacial processes. (3 marks)","ans":"Erosion, transportation, and deposition.","hint":"Think full cycle.","reason":"Basic process recall."},{"q":"Explain plucking. (2 marks)","ans":"It is when ice freezes onto rocks and pulls them away as it moves.","hint":"Think ice grip.","reason":"Concept understanding."},{"q":"What is abrasion? (2 marks)","ans":"It is the grinding of the Earth''s surface by rocks carried in ice.","hint":"Think scraping.","reason":"Process understanding."},{"q":"When does glacial deposition occur? (2 marks)","ans":"It occurs when a glacier melts and drops the materials it was carrying.","hint":"Think melting ice.","reason":"Application understanding."},{"q":"How do glaciers transport materials? (2 marks)","ans":"They carry rocks and sediment within, on top, or at the base of the ice.","hint":"Think movement of debris.","reason":"Mechanism understanding."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> glaciation -> Glacial Erosional Landforms
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'glaciation',
  'Glacial Erosional Landforms',
  '<h2> Glacial Erosional Landforms (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>
<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Glacial erosional landforms are physical features created when moving glaciers erode the land through plucking and abrasion over long periods.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. U-Shaped Valleys</h4>
<p>
U-shaped valleys are wide, steep-sided valleys with a flat bottom formed by the erosion of glaciers.
</p>
<h4> Formation</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Glaciers move through river valleys</li>
  <li>They erode both sides and the bottom</li>
  <li>River valleys are reshaped into a U-shape</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Glaciers transform V-shaped river valleys into U-shaped valleys.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Cirques (Corries)</h4>
<p>
Cirques are bowl-shaped hollows found at the head of glacial valleys.
</p>
<h4> Formation</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Snow accumulates in mountain depressions</li>
  <li>Ice erodes the ground through plucking and abrasion</li>
  <li>A deep hollow is formed</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Cirques are starting points of valley glaciers.</p>
<hr>
<h4>3. Arêtes</h4>
<p>
Arêtes are sharp, knife-edge ridges formed between two cirques or glacial valleys.
</p>
<h4> Formation</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Two glaciers erode parallel valleys</li>
  <li>Side walls become steep and narrow</li>
  <li>A sharp ridge is left in between</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Arêtes are narrow ridges between glaciers.</p>
<hr>
<h4>4. Horns</h4>
<p>
Horns are pointed mountain peaks formed when several cirques erode a mountain from different sides.
</p>
<h4> Formation</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Multiple cirques surround a mountain</li>
  <li>Erosion occurs from all sides</li>
  <li>A sharp peak is formed</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Horns are pyramidal mountain peaks.</p>
<hr>
<h4>5. Hanging Valleys</h4>
<p>
Hanging valleys are smaller valleys that enter a main U-shaped valley at a higher level.
</p>
<h4> Formation</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Main glacier erodes deeper than tributary glaciers</li>
  <li>When ice melts, tributary valley is left “hanging”</li>
  <li>Often forms waterfalls</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Side valleys are left suspended above main valleys.</p>
  ',
  '[{"q":"State two glacial erosional landforms. (2 marks)","ans":"U-shaped valleys and cirques.","hint":"Think valley shapes.","reason":"Basic recall."},{"q":"How is a U-shaped valley formed? (2 marks)","ans":"It is formed when a glacier erodes a river valley, widening and deepening it.","hint":"Think valley reshaping.","reason":"Process understanding."},{"q":"What is a cirque? (2 marks)","ans":"It is a bowl-shaped hollow formed by glacial erosion at the head of a valley.","hint":"Think mountain hollow.","reason":"Concept definition."},{"q":"Differentiate arêtes and horns. (2 marks)","ans":"Arêtes are sharp ridges between valleys while horns are pointed peaks formed by erosion from multiple sides.","hint":"Think ridge vs peak.","reason":"Comparison skill."},{"q":"Why do hanging valleys form? (2 marks)","ans":"Because main glaciers erode deeper than tributary glaciers, leaving side valleys higher.","hint":"Think erosion depth.","reason":"Process reasoning."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> glaciation -> Glacial Depositional Landforms
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'glaciation',
  'Glacial Depositional Landforms',
  '<h2> Glacial Depositional Landforms (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Glacial depositional landforms are features created when glaciers lose energy and drop (deposit) the rocks, sand, and clay they were carrying.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Moraines</h4>
<p>
Moraines are accumulations of unsorted glacial debris (till) deposited by melting ice.
</p>
<h4> Types of Moraines</h4>
<ul>
  <li><b>Lateral Moraine:</b> Deposited along the sides of a glacier</li>
  <li><b>Medial Moraine:</b> Formed when two glaciers merge and their debris joins in the middle</li>
  <li><b>Terminal Moraine:</b> Deposited at the furthest point reached by a glacier</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Moraines are ridges of debris left by glaciers.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Drumlins</h4>
<p>
Drumlins are smooth, oval-shaped hills made of glacial till, formed under moving ice.
</p>
<h4> Characteristics</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Streamlined shape (like an inverted spoon)</li>
  <li>Indicate direction of glacier movement</li>
  <li>Occur in groups called drumlin fields</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Drumlins show the flow direction of glaciers.</p>
<hr>
<h4>3. Eskers</h4>
<p>
Eskers are long, winding ridges of sand and gravel deposited by meltwater streams flowing under glaciers.
</p>
<h4> Formation</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Meltwater flows inside or beneath glacier tunnels</li>
  <li>Sediments are deposited in channels</li>
  <li>Ice melts, leaving ridges behind</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Eskers are former sub-glacial river deposits.</p>
<hr>
<h4>4. Outwash Plains</h4>
<p>
Outwash plains are flat areas formed by meltwater deposits spread in front of a glacier.
</p>
<h4> Formation</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Meltwater carries sediments away from glacier front</li>
  <li>Heavier materials settle first</li>
  <li>Finer materials are carried further away</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Outwash plains are sorted deposits from melting ice.</p>
  ',
  '[{"q":"State two glacial depositional landforms. (2 marks)","ans":"Moraines and drumlins.","hint":"Think debris features.","reason":"Basic recall."},{"q":"What are moraines? (2 marks)","ans":"They are ridges of unsorted debris deposited by glaciers.","hint":"Think glacial till.","reason":"Definition understanding."},{"q":"How are eskers formed? (2 marks)","ans":"They are formed by deposition of sediments in meltwater streams under glaciers.","hint":"Think sub-glacial rivers.","reason":"Process understanding."},{"q":"What do drumlins indicate? (1 mark)","ans":"They indicate the direction of glacier movement.","hint":"Think ice flow.","reason":"Identification skill."},{"q":"Explain the formation of outwash plains. (2 marks)","ans":"They are formed when meltwater carries and spreads sediments in front of a glacier.","hint":"Think water sorting.","reason":"Process explanation."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> glaciation -> Importance of Glaciation
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'glaciation',
  'Importance of Glaciation',
  '<h2> Importance of Glaciation (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
The importance of glaciation refers to the benefits and positive roles that glaciers and glacial processes play in the environment and human activities.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Water Supply</h4>
<p>
Glaciers store large amounts of freshwater that is released slowly when they melt.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Feed rivers during dry seasons</li>
  <li>Provide drinking water</li>
  <li>Support irrigation systems</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Glaciers act as natural water reservoirs.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. River Sources</h4>
<p>
Many major rivers originate from melting glaciers in high mountains.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Ensure continuous river flow</li>
  <li>Support agriculture and settlements downstream</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Glaciers are the “birthplace” of rivers.</p>
<hr>
<h4>3. Tourism</h4>
<p>
Glacial landscapes attract tourists due to their unique and beautiful scenery.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Ice caves and snowy mountains</li>
  <li>Adventure tourism (skiing, climbing)</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Glaciers support tourism industries.</p>
<hr>
<h4>4. Soil Formation</h4>
<p>
As glaciers move and retreat, they grind rocks into fine particles that contribute to soil formation.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Creates fertile soils in some regions</li>
  <li>Supports vegetation growth after glaciation</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Glaciers help create new soils.</p>
  ',
  '[{"q":"State two importance of glaciation. (2 marks)","ans":"Water supply and river formation.","hint":"Think water systems.","reason":"Basic recall."},{"q":"How do glaciers provide water supply? (2 marks)","ans":"They store freshwater and release it slowly when they melt.","hint":"Think melting ice.","reason":"Process understanding."},{"q":"State one importance of glaciers to tourism. (1 mark)","ans":"They create beautiful landscapes that attract tourists.","hint":"Think scenery.","reason":"Identification skill."},{"q":"Explain how glaciers contribute to soil formation. (2 marks)","ans":"They grind rocks into fine particles that form soil.","hint":"Think erosion products.","reason":"Environmental understanding."},{"q":"Why are glaciers important to rivers? (2 marks)","ans":"They act as sources of rivers and maintain flow during dry seasons.","hint":"Think river origin.","reason":"Hydrological understanding."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> glaciation -> Effects of Glaciation
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'glaciation',
  'Effects of Glaciation',
  '<h2> Effects of Glaciation (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
The effects of glaciation refer to the changes and impacts that glaciers have on the Earth''s surface, climate, and environment over time.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Landscape Shaping</h4>
<p>
Glaciers reshape the Earth''s surface through erosion and deposition.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Create U-shaped valleys and ridges</li>
  <li>Form sharp peaks and depressions</li>
  <li>Leave behind moraines and other deposits</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Glaciers are powerful agents of landscape transformation.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Climate Influence</h4>
<p>
Large ice masses affect global and local climate conditions.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Reflect sunlight (high albedo effect)</li>
  <li>Help cool the Earth’s surface</li>
  <li>Affect wind and rainfall patterns</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Glaciers contribute to global temperature regulation.</p>
<hr>
<h4>3. Formation of Lakes</h4>
<p>
Glaciers create depressions that later fill with water to form lakes.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>U-shaped valleys may fill with meltwater</li>
  <li>Depression basins form glacial lakes</li>
  <li>Moraines can block valleys and trap water</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Many lakes are glacial in origin.</p>
  ',
  '[{"q":"State one effect of glaciation on landscapes. (1 mark)","ans":"Formation of U-shaped valleys.","hint":"Think valley shape.","reason":"Basic recall."},{"q":"How do glaciers affect climate? (2 marks)","ans":"They reflect sunlight and help cool the Earth''s surface.","hint":"Think albedo effect.","reason":"Climate understanding."},{"q":"How are glacial lakes formed? (2 marks)","ans":"They form when depressions created by glaciers fill with water.","hint":"Think meltwater.","reason":"Process understanding."},{"q":"State two effects of glaciation. (2 marks)","ans":"Landscape shaping and lake formation.","hint":"Think physical changes.","reason":"Identification skill."},{"q":"Why do glaciers influence temperature? (2 marks)","ans":"Because ice reflects solar radiation, reducing heat absorption.","hint":"Think reflection of sunlight.","reason":"Environmental understanding."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> deserts_and_arid_regions -> Introduction to Deserts
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'deserts_and_arid_regions',
  'Introduction to Deserts',
  '<h2> Introduction to Deserts (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>
<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
A desert is a region that receives very low rainfall (usually less than 250 mm annually), resulting in sparse vegetation and extreme environmental conditions.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Meaning of Deserts</h4>
<p>
Deserts are dry regions where evaporation exceeds precipitation, making water scarce.
</p>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Deserts are defined by dryness, not temperature.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Types of Deserts</h4>
<h4> (a) Hot Deserts</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Very high daytime temperatures</li>
  <li>Low rainfall</li>
  <li>Example: Sahara Desert</li>
</ul>
<h4> (b) Cold Deserts</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Low temperatures (often below freezing)</li>
  <li>Low precipitation (mostly snow)</li>
  <li>Example: Gobi Desert</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Deserts can be hot or cold depending on climate.</p>
<hr>
<h4>3. Characteristics of Deserts</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Very low rainfall</li>
  <li>High evaporation rates</li>
  <li>Sparse vegetation</li>
  <li>Extreme temperatures (hot or cold)</li>
</ul>
  ',
  '[{"q":"Define a desert. (1 mark)","ans":"A region that receives very low rainfall.","hint":"Think dry area.","reason":"Basic definition recall."},{"q":"State two types of deserts. (2 marks)","ans":"Hot deserts and cold deserts.","hint":"Think climate extremes.","reason":"Classification recall."},{"q":"Give one example of a desert. (1 mark)","ans":"Sahara Desert.","hint":"Think Africa.","reason":"Identification skill."},{"q":"Why is vegetation sparse in deserts? (2 marks)","ans":"Because there is very little rainfall.","hint":"Think water shortage.","reason":"Environmental understanding."},{"q":"Differentiate hot and cold deserts. (2 marks)","ans":"Hot deserts are very warm while cold deserts have low temperatures and snow.","hint":"Think temperature differences.","reason":"Comparison skill."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> deserts_and_arid_regions -> Formation of Deserts
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'deserts_and_arid_regions',
  'Formation of Deserts',
  '<h2> Formation of Deserts (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Desert formation refers to the natural processes and climatic conditions that lead to very low rainfall and the development of dry, arid environments.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Atmospheric Circulation (Hadley Cells)</h4>
<p>
Deserts often form near 30° north and south of the equator due to global air circulation patterns.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Warm air rises at the equator</li>
  <li>It cools and loses moisture as rain</li>
  <li>Dry air descends at subtropical regions</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Descending dry air creates desert conditions.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Rain Shadow Effect</h4>
<p>
Deserts form on the leeward side of mountains where moist air loses its moisture on the windward side.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Moist air rises over mountains</li>
  <li>Rain falls on windward side</li>
  <li>Dry air descends on leeward side</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Mountains block rainfall, creating dry zones.</p>
<hr>
<h4>3. Cold Ocean Currents</h4>
<p>
Cold ocean currents reduce evaporation and moisture in the air, leading to desert formation along coasts.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Cool air above cold currents holds less moisture</li>
  <li>Little rainfall forms over nearby land</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Cold currents suppress rainfall.</p>
<hr>
<h4>4. Continentality</h4>
<p>
Areas far from oceans receive less moisture, leading to dry conditions.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Moisture decreases inland</li>
  <li>Rain-bearing winds lose water before reaching interior</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Distance from sea increases dryness.</p>
  ',
  '[{"q":"State two causes of desert formation. (2 marks)","ans":"Rain shadow effect and Hadley cells.","hint":"Think climate systems.","reason":"Basic recall."},{"q":"Explain the rain shadow effect. (2 marks)","ans":"Moist air rises over mountains, rains on windward side, leaving dry air on leeward side.","hint":"Think mountain barrier.","reason":"Process understanding."},{"q":"How do Hadley cells cause deserts? (2 marks)","ans":"Dry air descends at subtropical regions causing low rainfall.","hint":"Think sinking air.","reason":"Atmospheric understanding."},{"q":"What is continentality? (1 mark)","ans":"It is the condition where areas far from the sea receive low rainfall.","hint":"Think distance from ocean.","reason":"Definition recall."},{"q":"How do cold ocean currents affect rainfall? (2 marks)","ans":"They reduce evaporation, leading to low rainfall.","hint":"Think cold water.","reason":"Climate process understanding."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> deserts_and_arid_regions -> Desert Landforms
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'deserts_and_arid_regions',
  'Desert Landforms',
  '<h2> Desert Landforms (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Desert landforms are physical features shaped by wind erosion, transportation, and deposition in dry desert environments.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Erosional Desert Landforms</h4>
<h4> (a) Yardangs</h4>
<p>
Yardangs are long, narrow ridges carved by wind erosion.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Formed by abrasion from wind-blown sand</li>
  <li>Aligned in the direction of prevailing winds</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Wind carves streamlined rock ridges.</p>
<hr>
<h4> (b) Mushroom Rocks</h4>
<p>
Mushroom rocks are rock formations with a narrow base and a wider top.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Formed by wind abrasion near ground level</li>
  <li>Lower parts erode faster than upper parts</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Strong wind erosion near the ground creates “mushroom” shape.</p>
<hr>
<h4> (c) Inselbergs</h4>
<p>
Inselbergs are isolated rock hills rising abruptly from flat desert plains.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Formed from resistant rock left after erosion</li>
  <li>Surrounded by flat desert surface</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Inselbergs are remnants of old landscapes.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Depositional Desert Landforms</h4>
<h4> (a) Sand Dunes</h4>
<p>
Sand dunes are hills of sand formed by wind deposition.
</p>
<h4> Types of Dunes</h4>
<ul>
  <li><b>Barchan:</b> Crescent-shaped dunes</li>
  <li><b>Seif:</b> Long, straight dunes</li>
  <li><b>Transverse:</b> Formed perpendicular to wind direction</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Wind deposits sand in different shaped dunes.</p>
<hr>
<h4> (b) Sand Sheets</h4>
<p>
Sand sheets are flat or gently undulating areas covered with sand.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Formed where wind speed is not strong enough to form dunes</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Uniform sand deposits create flat surfaces.</p>
<hr>
<h4> (c) Loess Deposits</h4>
<p>
Loess is fine, wind-blown silt deposited over large areas.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Very fertile soil when weathered</li>
  <li>Carried over long distances by wind</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Wind can transport fine particles far from deserts.</p>
  ',
  '[{"q":"State two desert landforms. (2 marks)","ans":"Yardangs and sand dunes.","hint":"Think wind features.","reason":"Basic recall."},{"q":"How are yardangs formed? (2 marks)","ans":"They are formed by wind abrasion carving long ridges in rock.","hint":"Think wind erosion.","reason":"Process understanding."},{"q":"What is a mushroom rock? (2 marks)","ans":"A rock with a narrow base and wide top formed by wind erosion.","hint":"Think shape.","reason":"Definition skill."},{"q":"State one type of sand dune. (1 mark)","ans":"Barchan dune.","hint":"Think crescent shape.","reason":"Identification skill."},{"q":"What is loess? (1 mark)","ans":"Fine wind-blown silt deposited over large areas.","hint":"Think dust deposits.","reason":"Definition recall."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> deserts_and_arid_regions -> Desert Processes
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'deserts_and_arid_regions',
  'Desert Processes',
  '<h2> Desert Processes (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Desert processes are the actions of wind that erode, transport, and deposit materials in arid regions.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Wind Erosion</h4>
<p>
Wind erosion is the wearing away of the Earth''s surface by wind in dry areas.
</p>
<hr>
<h4> (a) Deflation</h4>
<p>
Deflation is the removal of loose, fine particles from the ground by wind.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Leaves behind rocky desert surfaces</li>
  <li>Forms deflation hollows in some areas</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Wind lifts and removes loose material.</p>
<hr>
<h4> (b) Abrasion</h4>
<p>
Abrasion is the scraping of rock surfaces by wind-blown sand particles.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Polishes rocks</li>
  <li>Creates smooth surfaces and rock carvings</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Wind acts like sandpaper on rocks.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Wind Transportation</h4>
<p>
Wind transports materials in deserts in different ways depending on particle size and wind strength.
</p>
<hr>
<h4> (a) Suspension</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Very fine particles are carried in the air</li>
  <li>Can travel long distances</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Light particles float in air currents.</p>
<hr>
<h4> (b) Saltation</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Medium-sized particles bounce along the ground</li>
  <li>Most common method of sand movement</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Sand “hops” along the surface.</p>
<hr>
<h4> (c) Traction</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Large particles are rolled along the ground</li>
  <li>Requires strong wind force</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Heavy materials are pushed along the surface.</p>
  ',
  '[{"q":"State two desert processes. (2 marks)","ans":"Deflation and abrasion.","hint":"Think wind erosion.","reason":"Basic recall."},{"q":"What is deflation? (2 marks)","ans":"It is the removal of loose particles by wind.","hint":"Think blowing away dust.","reason":"Concept understanding."},{"q":"Explain saltation. (2 marks)","ans":"It is the bouncing movement of sand particles by wind.","hint":"Think hopping sand.","reason":"Process understanding."},{"q":"Which particles are carried in suspension? (1 mark)","ans":"Very fine particles.","hint":"Think dust.","reason":"Identification skill."},{"q":"How does abrasion occur? (2 marks)","ans":"Wind-blown sand scrapes and wears down rock surfaces.","hint":"Think sandpaper effect.","reason":"Mechanism understanding."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> deserts_and_arid_regions -> Desert Vegetation and Adaptations
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'deserts_and_arid_regions',
  'Desert Vegetation and Adaptations',
  '<h2> Desert Vegetation and Adaptations (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>
<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Desert vegetation refers to plants that survive in arid regions by developing special adaptations to conserve water and withstand extreme conditions.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Xerophytes</h4>
<p>
Xerophytes are plants adapted to survive in dry conditions with very little water.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Store water in stems or leaves</li>
  <li>Have reduced leaf size</li>
  <li>Slow growth to conserve energy</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Xerophytes are drought-resistant plants.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Deep Root Systems</h4>
<p>
Many desert plants develop deep or widespread root systems to access underground water.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Tap roots reach deep water tables</li>
  <li>Some spread wide to absorb surface moisture quickly</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Roots are designed to maximize water intake.</p>
<hr>
<h4>3. Waxy Leaves (Cuticle Layer)</h4>
<p>
Desert plants often have a thick waxy layer on leaves to reduce water loss.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Reduces evaporation (transpiration)</li>
  <li>Protects plant from intense heat</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Waxy surfaces conserve water.</p>
<hr>
<h4>4. Additional Adaptations</h4>
<h4> (a) Small or No Leaves</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Reduces surface area for water loss</li>
</ul>
<h4> (b) Spines Instead of Leaves</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Found in cactus plants</li>
  <li>Protects plant and reduces water loss</li>
</ul>
<h4> (c) Short Life Cycles</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Some plants grow and reproduce quickly after rainfall</li>
</ul>
  ',
  '[{"q":"State two adaptations of desert plants. (2 marks)","ans":"Deep roots and waxy leaves.","hint":"Think water conservation.","reason":"Basic recall."},{"q":"What are xerophytes? (2 marks)","ans":"Plants adapted to survive in dry conditions.","hint":"Think dry plants.","reason":"Definition understanding."},{"q":"Why do desert plants have deep roots? (2 marks)","ans":"To access water deep underground.","hint":"Think groundwater.","reason":"Process understanding."},{"q":"What is the function of waxy leaves? (2 marks)","ans":"To reduce water loss through evaporation.","hint":"Think transpiration.","reason":"Concept application."},{"q":"Why do some desert plants have spines? (2 marks)","ans":"To reduce water loss and protect the plant.","hint":"Think cactus.","reason":"Adaptation reasoning."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> deserts_and_arid_regions -> Desertification
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'deserts_and_arid_regions',
  'Desertification',
  '<h2> Desertification (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Desertification is the process by which fertile land gradually turns into desert due to natural factors and human activities.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Causes of Desertification</h4>
<h4> (a) Overgrazing</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Too many animals eat vegetation faster than it can regrow</li>
  <li>Leaves land bare and exposed to erosion</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Livestock pressure destroys vegetation cover.</p>
<hr>
<h4> (b) Deforestation</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Cutting down trees reduces soil protection</li>
  <li>Increases wind and water erosion</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Trees protect soil from degradation.</p>
<hr>
<h4> (c) Drought</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Long periods of low rainfall reduce soil moisture</li>
  <li>Vegetation dies, exposing soil</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Lack of rainfall accelerates land degradation.</p>
<hr>
<h4> (d) Poor Farming Practices</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Over-cultivation exhausts soil nutrients</li>
  <li>Uncontrolled irrigation may lead to salinization</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4>2. Effects of Desertification</h4>
<h4> (a) Land Degradation</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Loss of fertile soil</li>
  <li>Reduced agricultural productivity</li>
</ul>
<h4> (b) Food Shortages</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Reduced crop yields</li>
  <li>Increased hunger and famine risk</li>
</ul>
<h4> (c) Migration</h4>
<ul>
  <li>People move from affected areas</li>
  <li>Leads to overcrowding in other regions</li>
</ul>
<h4> (d) Loss of Biodiversity</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Destruction of plant and animal habitats</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4>3. Control Measures</h4>
<h4> (a) Afforestation and Reforestation</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Planting trees to restore vegetation cover</li>
</ul>
<h4> (b) Controlled Grazing</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Limiting number of livestock per area</li>
</ul>
<h4> (c) Irrigation Management</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Proper water use to prevent soil damage</li>
</ul>
<h4> (d) Soil Conservation Methods</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Terracing and contour ploughing</li>
  <li>Mulching to retain soil moisture</li>
</ul>
  ',
  '[{"q":"Define desertification. (1 mark)","ans":"It is the process where fertile land turns into desert.","hint":"Think land degradation.","reason":"Basic definition recall."},{"q":"State two causes of desertification. (2 marks)","ans":"Overgrazing and deforestation.","hint":"Think human activities.","reason":"Basic recall."},{"q":"Explain how deforestation causes desertification. (2 marks)","ans":"It removes trees, exposing soil to erosion.","hint":"Think soil protection.","reason":"Process understanding."},{"q":"State one effect of desertification. (1 mark)","ans":"Food shortages.","hint":"Think agriculture loss.","reason":"Impact identification."},{"q":"State two ways of controlling desertification. (2 marks)","ans":"Afforestation and controlled grazing.","hint":"Think conservation.","reason":"Solution recall."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> oceans -> Importance of Oceans
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'oceans',
  'Importance of Oceans',
  '<h2> Importance of Oceans (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>
<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
The importance of oceans refers to the essential roles oceans play in supporting life, regulating climate, enabling economic activities, and sustaining ecosystems on Earth.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Climate Regulation</h4>
<p>
Oceans help regulate Earth''s temperature by absorbing, storing, and distributing heat.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Ocean currents redistribute warm and cold water</li>
  <li>Reduce extreme temperature variations</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Oceans act as Earth’s temperature stabilizer.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Food Supply</h4>
<p>
Oceans provide a major source of food through fishing and marine resources.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Fish, shellfish, and seaweed</li>
  <li>Support livelihoods for coastal communities</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Oceans are a major global food source.</p>
<hr>
<h4>3. Transport and Trade</h4>
<p>
Oceans provide the cheapest and most efficient transport routes for global trade.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Ships carry heavy and bulk goods</li>
  <li>Connect countries across continents</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Oceans are global trade highways.</p>
<hr>
<h4>4. Natural Resources</h4>
<p>
Oceans contain valuable resources used by humans.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Oil and natural gas extraction</li>
  <li>Salt and minerals</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Oceans are rich in energy and minerals.</p>
<hr>
<h4>5. Tourism and Recreation</h4>
<p>
Coastal and marine environments attract tourists.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Beaches and resorts</li>
  <li>Activities like swimming, diving, surfing</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Oceans support tourism economies.</p>
<hr>
<h4>6. Water Cycle Support</h4>
<p>
Oceans are the main source of evaporation in the water cycle.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Water evaporates → forms clouds → causes rainfall</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Oceans drive the global water cycle.</p>
  ',
  '[{"q":"State two importance of oceans. (2 marks)","ans":"Climate regulation and food supply.","hint":"Think global systems.","reason":"Basic recall."},{"q":"How do oceans regulate climate? (2 marks)","ans":"They absorb and distribute heat through ocean currents.","hint":"Think temperature balance.","reason":"Process understanding."},{"q":"State one importance of oceans in transport. (1 mark)","ans":"They provide cheap routes for moving goods.","hint":"Think shipping.","reason":"Identification skill."},{"q":"How do oceans support the water cycle? (2 marks)","ans":"They provide water for evaporation that forms rainfall.","hint":"Think evaporation.","reason":"System understanding."},{"q":"State one economic importance of oceans. (1 mark)","ans":"They provide fish for food and trade.","hint":"Think resources.","reason":"Application recall."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> oceans -> Disadvantages of Oceans
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'oceans',
  'Disadvantages of Oceans',
  '<h2> Disadvantages of Oceans (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
The disadvantages of oceans refer to the harmful effects, risks, and challenges caused by oceans to human life, infrastructure, and the environment.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Natural Hazards</h4>
<h4> (a) Storms and Hurricanes</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Strong winds and heavy rainfall originate over oceans</li>
  <li>Cause destruction in coastal areas</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Oceans generate powerful weather systems.</p>
<hr>
<h4> (b) Tsunamis</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Large ocean waves caused by underwater earthquakes or volcanic eruptions</li>
  <li>Can destroy entire coastal settlements</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Ocean floor movement creates deadly waves.</p>
<hr>
<h4> (c) Coastal Flooding</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Sea water floods low-lying coastal areas</li>
  <li>Caused by storms or rising sea levels</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4>2. Environmental Problems</h4>
<h4> (a) Pollution</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Oil spills damage marine ecosystems</li>
  <li>Plastic waste harms sea life</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Human activity severely pollutes oceans.</p>
<hr>
<h4> (b) Overfishing</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Excessive fishing reduces fish populations</li>
  <li>Disrupts marine food chains</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Unsustainable fishing threatens ecosystems.</p>
<hr>
<h4>3. Transport Risks</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Shipwrecks and accidents at sea</li>
  <li>Piracy in some ocean regions</li>
  <li>Navigation difficulties during storms</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Oceans are unpredictable and dangerous for transport.</p>
<hr>
<h4>4. Coastal Erosion</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Waves continuously wear away land</li>
  <li>Destroys buildings and infrastructure near coasts</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Ocean waves reshape and destroy coastlines.</p>
  ',
  '[{"q":"State two disadvantages of oceans. (2 marks)","ans":"Tsunamis and coastal erosion.","hint":"Think hazards.","reason":"Basic recall."},{"q":"What causes tsunamis? (2 marks)","ans":"Underwater earthquakes or volcanic eruptions.","hint":"Think sea floor movement.","reason":"Process understanding."},{"q":"State one effect of ocean pollution. (1 mark)","ans":"It kills marine life.","hint":"Think ecosystems.","reason":"Impact recall."},{"q":"Why is overfishing a problem? (2 marks)","ans":"It reduces fish populations and disrupts ecosystems.","hint":"Think food chain.","reason":"Environmental understanding."},{"q":"How do oceans affect transport? (2 marks)","ans":"They can cause shipwrecks and navigation difficulties during storms.","hint":"Think sea danger.","reason":"Application skill."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> oceans -> Ocean Currents and Their Effects
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'oceans',
  'Ocean Currents and Their Effects',
  '<h2> Ocean Currents and Their Effects (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Ocean currents are continuous, directed movements of seawater that flow through the oceans, influencing climate, weather, and marine life.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>

<h4>1. Types of Ocean Currents</h4>

<h4> (a) Warm Currents</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Flow from equator towards the poles</li>
  <li>Carry warm water</li>
  <li>Increase temperatures of coastal regions</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Example:</b> Gulf Stream</p>

<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Warm currents heat nearby land areas.</p>

<hr>

<h4> (b) Cold Currents</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Flow from polar regions towards the equator</li>
  <li>Carry cold water</li>
  <li>Lower temperatures of coastal areas</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Example:</b> Benguela Current</p>

<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Cold currents cool nearby land areas.</p>

<hr>

<h4>2. Effects on Climate</h4>

<h4> (a) Temperature Regulation</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Warm currents raise coastal temperatures</li>
  <li>Cold currents lower coastal temperatures</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Currents balance global heat distribution.</p>

<hr>

<h4> (b) Rainfall Influence</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Warm currents increase evaporation and rainfall</li>
  <li>Cold currents reduce evaporation and rainfall</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h4>3. Effects on Fishing</h4>

<h4> (a) Fishing Grounds</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Cold currents bring nutrients to the surface</li>
  <li>Support plankton growth</li>
  <li>Attract large fish populations</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Cold currents create rich fishing zones.</p>

<hr>

<h4>4. Effects on Human Activities</h4>

<ul>
  <li>Influence settlement patterns along coasts</li>
  <li>Affect agriculture through rainfall changes</li>
  <li>Impact sea transport routes</li>
</ul>
  ',
  '[{"q":"Define ocean currents. (1 mark)","ans":"Continuous movements of seawater in a specific direction.","hint":"Think flowing water.","reason":"Basic definition recall."},{"q":"State two types of ocean currents. (2 marks)","ans":"Warm currents and cold currents.","hint":"Think temperature.","reason":"Classification recall."},{"q":"How do warm currents affect climate? (2 marks)","ans":"They increase temperatures and rainfall in coastal areas.","hint":"Think heating effect.","reason":"Process understanding."},{"q":"Why are cold currents important for fishing? (2 marks)","ans":"They bring nutrients that support plankton and fish life.","hint":"Think marine food chain.","reason":"Ecological understanding."},{"q":"State one example of a warm current. (1 mark)","ans":"Gulf Stream.","hint":"Think Atlantic Ocean.","reason":"Identification skill."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> oceans -> Marine Resources and Economic Importance
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'oceans',
  'Marine Resources and Economic Importance',
  '<h2> Marine Resources and Economic Importance (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Marine resources are natural resources obtained from the oceans and seas that are useful to humans for economic development and survival.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Fishing Resources</h4>
<p>
Oceans provide fish and other seafood which are important sources of food and income.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Supports commercial fishing industries</li>
  <li>Provides employment to millions of people</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Oceans are major global food suppliers.</p>
<hr>
<h4>2. Oil and Natural Gas</h4>
<p>
Many oil and gas deposits are found under the ocean floor.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Used for energy production</li>
  <li>Important for industrial development</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Oceans are major energy sources.</p>
<hr>
<h4>3. Salt and Minerals</h4>
<p>
Sea water contains dissolved minerals such as salt.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Salt is extracted through evaporation</li>
  <li>Used in food and industries</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Oceans are rich in mineral resources.</p>
<hr>
<h4>4. Tourism and Recreation</h4>
<p>
Coastal and marine environments attract tourists.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Beaches and marine parks</li>
  <li>Activities such as diving and boating</li>
</ul
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Oceans support tourism economies.</p>
<hr>
<h4>5. Transport and Trade Routes</h4>
<p>
Oceans provide natural highways for global trade.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Cheapest form of transport for heavy goods</li>
  <li>Connects continents and countries</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Oceans enable global commerce.</p>
  ',
  '[{"q":"State two marine resources. (2 marks)","ans":"Fish and oil.","hint":"Think ocean products.","reason":"Basic recall."},{"q":"How are oceans important for energy? (2 marks)","ans":"They contain oil and natural gas deposits.","hint":"Think fossil fuels.","reason":"Concept understanding."},{"q":"How is salt obtained from oceans? (1 mark)","ans":"By evaporating sea water.","hint":"Think drying water.","reason":"Process recall."},{"q":"Why are oceans important for transport? (2 marks)","ans":"They provide cheap routes for moving goods across countries.","hint":"Think shipping routes.","reason":"Application understanding."},{"q":"State one importance of tourism in oceans. (1 mark)","ans":"It generates income from beaches and marine activities.","hint":"Think recreation.","reason":"Economic recall."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> oceans -> Ocean Pollution and Conservation
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'oceans',
  'Ocean Pollution and Conservation',
  '<h2> Ocean Pollution and Conservation </h2>
<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Ocean pollution is the introduction of harmful substances into the ocean, damaging marine ecosystems, while conservation refers to efforts to protect and manage ocean resources sustainably.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>

<h4>1. Causes of Ocean Pollution</h4>

<h4> (a) Oil Spills</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Accidental release of oil from ships or drilling</li>
  <li>Forms a layer on water surface</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Oil blocks oxygen exchange in water.</p>

<hr>

<h4> (b) Plastic Waste</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Non-biodegradable materials dumped into oceans</li>
  <li>Harm marine animals when ingested</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Plastics persist for long periods in oceans.</p>

<hr>

<h4> (c) Industrial and Sewage Waste</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Chemicals and untreated waste released into water</li>
  <li>Increases toxicity of marine environments</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h4>2. Effects of Ocean Pollution</h4>

<h4> (a) Death of Marine Life</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Fish and other organisms are poisoned or suffocated</li>
</ul>

<h4> (b) Food Chain Disruption</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Pollution affects plankton → fish → humans</li>
</ul>

<h4>‍‍ (c) Human Health Risks</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Consumption of contaminated seafood causes diseases</li>
</ul>

<h4> (d) Damage to Tourism</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Dirty beaches reduce tourist attraction</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h4>3. Ocean Conservation Measures</h4>

<h4> (a) Waste Management</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Proper disposal of plastics and industrial waste</li>
</ul>

<h4> (b) Ban on Dumping Waste</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Laws to prevent dumping of harmful materials into oceans</li>
</ul>

<h4> (c) Marine Protected Areas</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Zones where human activity is restricted</li>
</ul>

<h4> (d) Sustainable Fishing</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Controlling fishing to prevent overexploitation</li>
</ul>

<h4> (e) Public Awareness</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Educating people about ocean protection</li>
</ul>
  ',
  '[{"q":"Define ocean pollution. (1 mark)","ans":"The introduction of harmful substances into the ocean.","hint":"Think contamination.","reason":"Basic definition recall."},{"q":"State two causes of ocean pollution. (2 marks)","ans":"Oil spills and plastic waste.","hint":"Think human activity.","reason":"Cause identification."},{"q":"How do plastics affect marine life? (2 marks)","ans":"They harm or kill animals when ingested.","hint":"Think animals eating plastic.","reason":"Impact understanding."},{"q":"State one effect of ocean pollution on humans. (1 mark)","ans":"Diseases from contaminated seafood.","hint":"Think health risks.","reason":"Application recall."},{"q":"State one way of conserving oceans. (1 mark)","ans":"Proper waste management.","hint":"Think pollution control.","reason":"Solution recall."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> oceans -> Ocean Processes: Erosion, Transportation and Deposition
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'oceans',
  'Ocean Processes: Erosion, Transportation and Deposition',
  '<h2> Ocean Processes: Erosion, Transportation and Deposition (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Ocean processes are the actions of waves, currents, and tides that erode, transport, and deposit materials along coastlines and ocean floors.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>

<h4>1. Ocean Erosion</h4>
<p>
Ocean erosion is the wearing away of coastal land by waves, tides, and currents.</p>

<h4> (a) Hydraulic Action</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Waves force air and water into cracks in rocks</li>
  <li>Pressure weakens and breaks rocks</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Water pressure breaks coastal rocks.</p>

<hr>

<h4> (b) Abrasion (Corrasion)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Rock fragments carried by waves scrape against cliffs</li>
  <li>Acts like sandpaper on coastlines</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Waves use debris to wear away land.</p>

<hr>

<h4> (c) Solution (Corrosion)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Chemical action dissolves soluble rocks like limestone</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Seawater chemically dissolves rocks.</p>

<hr>

<h4>2. Ocean Transportation</h4>
<p>
Ocean transportation is the movement of eroded materials by waves and currents along the coast.</p>

<h4> Types of Transportation</h4>

<ul>
  <li><b>Suspension:</b> Fine particles carried in water</li>
  <li><b>Saltation:</b> Small particles bounce along seabed</li>
  <li><b>Traction:</b> Large rocks rolled along the sea floor</li>
  <li><b>Longshore Drift:</b> Zig-zag movement of material along coast</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Waves constantly move sediment along the coast.</p>

<hr>

<h4>3. Ocean Deposition</h4>
<p>
Deposition occurs when waves lose energy and drop transported material.</p>

<ul>
  <li>Occurs in sheltered or shallow areas</li>
  <li>Heavier particles are deposited first</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Weak waves drop sediments.</p>

<hr>

<h4> Conditions for Deposition</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Low wave energy</li>
  <li>Shallow water</li>
  <li>Sheltered coastlines</li>
</ul>
  ',
  '[{"q":"State two types of ocean erosion. (2 marks)","ans":"Hydraulic action and abrasion.","hint":"Think wave force.","reason":"Basic recall."},{"q":"What is longshore drift? (2 marks)","ans":"The zig-zag movement of sediment along the coast.","hint":"Think coastal movement.","reason":"Process understanding."},{"q":"When does deposition occur? (2 marks)","ans":"When waves lose energy and drop sediments.","hint":"Think weak waves.","reason":"Concept understanding."},{"q":"State one type of ocean transportation. (1 mark)","ans":"Suspension.","hint":"Think fine particles.","reason":"Identification skill."},{"q":"Explain abrasion. (2 marks)","ans":"Rock fragments carried by waves scrape and wear away cliffs.","hint":"Think sandpaper effect.","reason":"Mechanism understanding."}]'
);

-- Topic: geography -> rivers -> Introduction to Rivers
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'geography',
  'rivers',
  'Introduction to Rivers',
  '<h2> Introduction to Rivers (Advanced Mastery Notes)</h2>

<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
A river is a natural flowing stream of water that moves from a higher area (source) to a lower area (mouth) where it enters a lake, sea, or ocean.
</p>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Parts of a River</h4>
<h4> (a) Source</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Where the river begins</li>
  <li>Usually in mountains or highlands</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Rivers start in high areas.</p>
<hr>
<h4> (b) Course</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Path followed by a river from source to mouth</li>
</ul>
<h4> Upper Course</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Steep slope</li>
  <li>Fast flow</li>
</ul>
<h4> Middle Course</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Gentler slope</li>
  <li>Wider valley</li>
</ul>
<h4> Lower Course</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Very gentle slope</li>
  <li>Wide river mouth</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4> (c) Mouth</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Where the river ends</li>
  <li>Flows into sea, ocean, or lake</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Key Idea:</b> Rivers end in larger water bodies.</p>
  ',
  '[{"q":"Define a river. (1 mark)","ans":"A natural flowing stream of water from highland to lowland.","hint":"Think flowing water.","reason":"Basic definition recall."},{"q":"State the parts of a river system. (2 marks)","ans":"Source, course, and mouth.","hint":"Think start, path, end.","reason":"Structural recall."},{"q":"Where does a river start? (1 mark)","ans":"At the source in highland areas.","hint":"Think mountains.","reason":"Location identification."},{"q":"Name the three courses of a river. (2 marks)","ans":"Upper, middle, and lower course.","hint":"Think stages.","reason":"Concept structure."},{"q":"What happens at the mouth of a river? (2 marks)","ans":"It flows into a sea, lake, or ocean.","hint":"Think ending point.","reason":"System understanding."}]'
);

-- Topic: history -> human_evolution -> Human Evolution and Origins of Early Humans
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'history',
  'human_evolution',
  'Human Evolution and Origins of Early Humans',
  '
<h2> Human Evolution and Origins of Early Humans</h2>
<h3> WELL DETAILED NOTES</h3>
<p>
Human evolution is the scientific process explaining how modern humans 
(<b>Homo sapiens</b>) gradually developed from earlier primate ancestors over 
millions of years through biological and behavioral change.
</p>
<p>
Evolution did not happen instantly or in a straight line. It was a long process 
of adaptation driven by environmental challenges, survival pressure, competition, 
genetic variation, and reproduction.
</p>
<p>
The human species is part of the animal kingdom and shares common ancestry with 
other primates. Over time, different populations adapted differently to their 
environments, leading to the emergence of new species.
</p>
<h3> UNDERSTANDING EVOLUTION DEEPLY</h3>
<p>
Evolution is not simply about becoming “better.” It is about becoming 
<b>better adapted</b> to a specific environment.
</p>
<p>
Traits that increase survival and reproduction become more common over 
many generations because individuals carrying those traits are more likely 
to pass them to offspring.
</p>
<p>
For example:
</p>
<ul>
<li>Better walking ability helped early humans travel long distances.</li>
<li>Larger brains improved problem-solving and planning.</li>
<li>Social cooperation increased group survival.</li>
<li>Language improved communication and coordination.</li>
<li>Tool-making allowed humans to manipulate the environment.</li>
</ul>
<p>
<h3> KEY CHARACTERISTICS OF HUMAN EVOLUTION</h3>
<ul>
<li>
<b>1. Bipedalism (Walking Upright)</b>
<p>
One of the earliest major adaptations was walking on two legs.
This freed the hands for carrying tools, food, and children.
It also improved long-distance travel and allowed early humans 
to see predators over tall grass.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<b>2. Brain Development</b>
<p>
Over millions of years, human brain size increased significantly.
A larger brain improved memory, planning, learning, creativity,
language, and social coordination.
</p>
<p>
This development allowed humans to:
</p>
<ul>
<li>Create tools</li>
<li>Use fire</li>
<li>Build shelters</li>
<li>Develop culture</li>
<li>Transmit knowledge</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>
<b>3. Tool Use</b>
<p>
Early humans learned to shape stones into tools for cutting,
hunting, and protection.
Tool-making reflects the growth of intelligence and problem-solving ability.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<b>4. Social Cooperation</b>
<p>
Humans survived more effectively in groups.
Cooperation improved hunting, protection, child care, and food sharing.
This strengthened communication and emotional bonding.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<b>5. Language Development</b>
<p>
Language became one of humanity’s greatest survival tools.
It allowed humans to share information, teach skills,
organize groups, and preserve knowledge across generations.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3> MAJOR STAGES OF HUMAN EVOLUTION</h3>
<ul>
<li>
<b>Australopithecus</b>
<p>
These were among the earliest human ancestors.
They walked upright but still had many ape-like features.
Their brains were relatively small, and survival depended heavily
on gathering food and adapting to changing environments.
</p>
<p>
<li>
<b>Homo habilis</b>
<p>
Known as “handy man,” Homo habilis is associated with some of the
earliest stone tools.
This stage marks increased intelligence and intentional tool-making.
</p>
<li>
<b>Homo erectus</b>
<p>
Homo erectus had a more modern body structure and greater endurance.
They were the first humans known to control fire and migrate out of Africa.
</p>
<p>
The use of fire transformed survival by:
</p>
<ul>
<li>Cooking food</li>
<li>Providing warmth</li>
<li>Protection from predators</li>
<li>Improving nutrition</li>
</ul>
<li>
<b>Homo sapiens</b>
<p>
Modern humans developed advanced reasoning,
symbolic thought, language, art, and culture.
They built complex societies and rapidly transformed environments.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3> DEEP CORE PRINCIPLE</h3>
<pre>
Humans are not fixed creations —
they are adaptive organisms shaped by millions of years of survival pressure.
</pre>
<p>
Many modern human behaviors are deeply connected
to ancient survival environments.
</p>
<p>
Examples:
</p>
<ul>
<li>Fear helped humans avoid danger.</li>
<li>Curiosity improved learning and exploration.</li>
<li>Cooperation improved group survival.</li>
<li>Aggression sometimes protected resources.</li>
<li>Social bonding improved protection and child survival.</li>
</ul>
<h3> REAL-WORLD CONNECTIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Modern stress responses like fight, flight, or freeze originated as survival mechanisms.</li>
<li>Humans naturally seek social belonging because group survival increased survival chances.</li>
<li>The human brain still carries ancient survival instincts even in modern societies.</li>
<li>Competition, cooperation, curiosity, and fear all have evolutionary roots.</li>
</ul>
<h3> FINAL INSIGHT</h3>
<pre>
The modern human mind is a product of ancient survival history.
Every human behavior today carries traces of millions of years
of adaptation, struggle, learning, and survival.
</pre>
',
  '[{"q":"What is evolution?","hint":"think long-term change","steps":["Step 1: Identify concept of change over time","Step 2: Apply to living organisms","Step 3: Define as gradual biological change"],"ans":"Gradual change in organisms over time","why":"It explains how species develop and adapt"},{"q":"Name the species closest to modern humans","hint":"Homo something","steps":["Step 1: Recall human classification","Step 2: Identify modern species name","Step 3: Select correct answer"],"ans":"Homo sapiens","why":"This is the scientific name for modern humans"},{"q":"Why did humans evolve intelligence?","hint":"survival advantage","steps":["Step 1: Identify survival challenges","Step 2: Link intelligence to tools and planning","Step 3: Conclude survival advantage"],"ans":"To improve survival and problem-solving","why":"Smarter humans survived and reproduced more"}]'
);

-- Topic: history -> migration_out_of_africa -> Migration of Early Humans Out of Africa
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'history',
  'migration_out_of_africa',
  'Migration of Early Humans Out of Africa',
  '
<h2> Migration of Early Humans Out of Africa</h2>
<h3> WELL DETAILED NOTES</h3>
<p>Modern humans (<b>Homo sapiens</b>) originated in Africa and gradually spread across the world through migration over tens of thousands of years. This migration was one of the most important turning points in human history because it transformed a relatively small African population into the global human population that exists today.</p>
<p>Human migration was not random wandering. It was a survival-driven process influenced by environmental pressure, climate change, food availability, population growth, curiosity, and adaptation.</p>
<p>As conditions changed in one region, humans moved in search of:</p>
<ul>
<li> Water sources</li>
<li> Food and hunting opportunities</li>
<li> Better vegetation and climate</li>
<li> Safety from danger</li>
<li> More stable environments</li>
</ul>
<p>
Migration happened slowly through small groups moving step-by-step over generations.
Some groups settled permanently while others continued moving into new territories.
</p>
<h3> UNDERSTANDING HUMAN MIGRATION DEEPLY</h3>
<p>
Migration is deeply connected to survival.
Early humans lived in environments that constantly changed due to:
</p>
<ul>
<li>Climate shifts</li>
<li>Ice ages</li>
<li>Droughts</li>
<li>Changing animal populations</li>
<li>Competition for resources</li>
<li>Natural disasters</li>
</ul>
<p>
Humans who could adapt and move successfully had a better chance of survival.
Over time, migration allowed humans to:
</p>
<ul>
<li>Occupy new environments</li>
<li>Develop new technologies</li>
<li>Create different cultures</li>
<li>Expand population size</li>
<li>Increase genetic diversity</li>
</ul>
<p>Migration therefore became one of humanity’s greatest survival strategies.</p>
<h3> THE OUT OF AFRICA THEORY</h3>
<p>The <b>Out of Africa Theory</b> states that all modern humans originated in Africa before migrating to other parts of the world.</p>
<p>Scientific evidence supporting this theory includes:
</p>
<ul>
<li> Genetic evidence (DNA similarities)</li>
<li> Fossil evidence</li>
<li> Archaeological discoveries</li>
<li> Population studies</li>
</ul>
<p>According to this theory:</p>
<ul>
<li>Modern humans first evolved in Africa.</li>
<li>Small groups migrated outward.</li>
<li>These groups spread into Asia, Europe, Australia, and eventually the Americas.</li>
<li>Over time they adapted to different climates and environments.</li>
</ul>
<h3> MAJOR HUMAN MIGRATION ROUTES</h3>
<ul>
<li>
<b>1. Migration Out of Africa</b>
<p>
The first major migrations likely moved through the Middle East.
This region acted as a bridge connecting Africa to Asia and Europe.
</p>
<p>Humans likely followed:</p>
<ul>
<li>River systems</li>
<li>Animal migration routes</li>
<li>Coastal pathways</li>
</ul>
</li>

<li>
<b>2. Migration Into Asia</b>

<p>
Asia became one of the largest areas of human settlement.
Humans adapted to forests, mountains, deserts, and cold climates.
</p>
<p>This required:</p>
<ul>
<li>Better clothing</li>
<li>Improved hunting tools</li>
<li>Fire use</li>
<li>Shelter construction</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>
<b>3. Migration Into Europe</b>
<p>
Europe presented colder climates and ice-age conditions.
Humans adapted by developing stronger survival strategies and social cooperation.
</p>
<p>Key adaptations included:</p>
<ul>
<li>Animal skin clothing</li>
<li>Advanced fire use</li>
<li>Stronger shelters</li>
<li>Cooperative hunting</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>
<b>4. Migration Into Australia</b>
<p>
Humans reached Australia through coastal migration routes and short sea crossings.
This demonstrates growing problem-solving ability and environmental adaptation.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<b>5. Migration Into the Americas</b>
<p>
Humans eventually entered the Americas through land bridges and coastal routes during ice-age periods when sea levels were lower.
</p>
<p>
Over time, populations spread throughout North and South America.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3> ADAPTATION DURING MIGRATION</h3>
<p>
As humans entered new environments, survival required adaptation.
</p>
<p>
Adaptation occurred in three major ways:
</p>
<ul>
<li>
<b>1. Biological Adaptation</b>
<p>
Physical changes developed over generations.
</p>

<p>
Examples:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Skin pigmentation differences</li>
<li>Body size variation</li>
<li>Cold-weather tolerance</li>
<li>Disease resistance</li>
</ul>
</li>

<li>
<b>2. Cultural Adaptation</b>

<p>
Humans developed new customs and lifestyles suited to local conditions.
</p>

<p>
Examples:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Different food systems</li>
<li>Housing styles</li>
<li>Social structures</li>
<li>Belief systems</li>
</ul>
</li>

<li>
<b>3. Technological Adaptation</b>
<p>
Humans created tools and technologies to improve survival.
</p>
<p>
Examples:
</p>
<ul>
<li>Clothing for cold climates</li>
<li>Fishing tools</li>
<li>Hunting weapons</li>
<li>Boats and shelters</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
',
  '[{"q":"Where did modern humans originate?","hint":"continent","steps":["Step 1: Recall scientific theories of human origin","Step 2: Identify fossil and archaeological evidence location","Step 3: Conclude the continent of origin"],"ans":"Africa","why":"Fossil and genetic evidence strongly supports Africa as the origin of modern humans before global migration"},{"q":"What is migration?","hint":"movement of people","steps":["Step 1: Identify the concept of movement","Step 2: Recognize movement across regions or borders","Step 3: Define population movement in human history"],"ans":"The movement of people from one place to another","why":"Migration explains how humans spread across continents and adapted to different environments"},{"q":"Why did early humans migrate out of Africa?","hint":"survival and resources","steps":["Step 1: Identify environmental and climate changes","Step 2: Recognize need for food and survival opportunities","Step 3: Link migration to adaptation and exploration"],"ans":"To find better resources, food, and survival conditions","why":"Changing environments and population growth pushed humans to explore new regions"},{"q":"What is human evolution?","hint":"gradual change over time","steps":["Step 1: Identify biological change over time","Step 2: Recognize adaptation to environments","Step 3: Define evolutionary process"],"ans":"The gradual development and adaptation of humans over time","why":"Evolution explains how humans changed physically and behaviorally over long periods"}]'
);

-- Topic: history -> stone_age_life -> Stone Age Life and Early Human Survival
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'history',
  'stone_age_life',
  'Stone Age Life and Early Human Survival',
  '
<h2> Stone Age Life and Early Human Survival</h2>
<h3> WELL DETAILED NOTES</h3>
<p>
The Stone Age is the earliest and longest period in human history,lasting for millions of years before the discovery of metals.
During this era, humans depended heavily on stone tools,fire, environmental knowledge, and group cooperation to survive.
</p>
<p>The Stone Age represents the foundation of human civilization because manyimportant human behaviors and systems first developed during this period.These include:</p>
<ul>
<li> Problem-solving and intelligence</li>
<li> Tool-making and technology</li>
<li> Social cooperation</li>
<li> Fire control</li>
<li> Communication and learning</li>
<li> Early farming and settlement</li>
</ul>
<p>Although Stone Age humans lacked modern machines and cities,they possessed advanced survival intelligence and deep environmental understanding.</p>
<p>Each stage reflects increasing human control over nature and survival conditions.</p>
<h3> UNDERSTANDING THE STONE AGE DEEPLY</h3>
<p>The Stone Age was shaped by constant survival pressure.Humans lived in dangerous environments filled with:</p>
<ul><li>Wild animals</li><li>Harsh climates</li><li>Food shortages</li><li>Disease and injury</li><li>Natural disasters</li></ul>
<p>To survive, humans needed to:</p>
<ul><li>Understand nature</li><li>Create tools</li><li>Work together</li><li>Learn from experience</li><li>Pass knowledge to future generations</li></ul>
<p>This period therefore became the foundation of human intelligence,adaptation, and social organization.</p>
<h3> MAJOR PHASES OF THE STONE AGE</h3>
<ul>
<li>
<b>1. Paleolithic Era (Old Stone Age)</b>
<p>The Paleolithic Era was the longest phase of the Stone Age.Humans survived mainly as hunter-gatherers,depending entirely on nature for food and survival.</p>
<p>People lived in:</p>
<ul><li>Caves</li><li>Temporary shelters</li><li>Open campsites</li></ul>
<p>Key Characteristics:</p>
<ul><li>Simple stone tools</li><li>Hunting and gathering</li><li>Small nomadic groups</li><li>Use of fire</li><li>Constant migration</li></ul>
<p>Fire was one of humanity’s greatest discoveries because it provided:</p>
<ul><li>Warmth</li><li>Protection</li><li>Cooked food</li><li>Light at night</li><li>Social gathering points</li></ul>
</li>
<li>
<b>2. Mesolithic Era (Middle Stone Age)</b>
<p>The Mesolithic Era acted as a transition period betweenpure hunting lifestyles and farming societies.</p>
<p>Humans developed:</p>
<ul><li>Better tools</li><li>Fishing techniques</li><li>Improved hunting methods</li><li>More stable settlements</li></ul>
<p>Environmental changes after the Ice Age forced humansto adapt to new climates and ecosystems.</p>
<p>Key Characteristics:</p>
<ul><li>Smaller refined tools</li><li>Fishing and trapping</li><li>Semi-permanent settlements</li><li>Increased environmental adaptation</li></ul>
</li>
<li>
<b>3. Neolithic Era (New Stone Age)</b>
<p>The Neolithic Era marked one of the greatest turning pointsin human history: the development of agriculture.</p>
<p>Humans began:</p>
<ul><li>Growing crops</li><li>Domesticating animals</li><li>Building permanent settlements</li><li>Producing food instead of only collecting it</li></ul>
<p>This transformation is known as the:</p>
<p><b>Neolithic Revolution</b></p>
<p>Agriculture changed human life completely because it allowed:</p>
<ul><li>Population growth</li><li>Food storage</li><li>Permanent villages</li><li>Trade development</li><li>Social specialization</li></ul>
<p>Key Characteristics:</p>
<ul><li>Farming</li><li>Animal domestication</li><li>Permanent settlements</li><li>Advanced stone tools</li><li>Beginning of organized communities</li></ul>
</li>
</ul>
<h3> IMPORTANT DEFINITIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Stone Age</b><p>The earliest and longest period of human history,characterized by the use of stone tools.</p></li>
<li><b>Hunter-Gatherer</b><p>A lifestyle based on hunting animals and gathering wild plants for survival.</p></li>
<li><b>Paleolithic Era</b><p>The earliest Stone Age phase where humans relied entirely on hunting and gathering.</p></li>
<li><b>Mesolithic Era</b><p>A transitional phase between hunting societies and farming societies.</p></li>
<li><b>Neolithic Era</b><p>The Stone Age phase marked by agriculture and permanent settlements.</p></li>
<li><b>Neolithic Revolution</b><p>The major shift from hunting and gathering to farming and settled life.</p></li>
<li>
<b>Domestication</b>
<p>The process of taming plants and animals for human use.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3> STONE AGE TOOLS AND TECHNOLOGY</h3>
<p>Stone tools were among humanity’s first technologies.They improved efficiency, protection, and survival chances.</p>
<p>Common tools included:</p>
<ul>
<li> Stone knives</li>
<li> Hand axes</li>
<li> Spears and hunting weapons</li>
<li> Fishing hooks and harpoons</li>
<li> Scrapers for animal skins</li>
</ul>
<p>Tool-making required:</p>
<ul>
<li>Observation</li>
<li>Creativity</li>
<li>Planning</li>
<li>Skill development</li>
<li>Knowledge transfer</li>
</ul>
<p>This shows that Stone Age humans were intelligent problem-solvers.</p>
<h3> HOW STONE AGE SOCIETY WORKED</h3>
<p><b>Step 1: Environmental Observation</b></p>
<p>Humans studied seasons, animal behavior, weather patterns, and water sources.</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Tool Creation</b></p>
<p>Stone tools were developed to improve hunting, cutting, building, and protection.</p>
<p><b>Step 3: Group Cooperation</b></p>
<p>Humans formed small groups to improve survival and security.</p>
<p><b>Step 4: Division of Roles</b></p>
<p>Different responsibilities emerged such as hunting, gathering, tool-making, and protection.</p>
<p><b>Step 5: Knowledge Sharing</b></p>
<p>Skills were passed from older generations to younger generations through teaching and imitation.</p>
<p><b>Step 6: Gradual Advancement</b></p>
<p>Improved survival methods slowly created more stable living conditions.</p>
<h3> SOCIAL LIFE IN THE STONE AGE</h3>
<p>Humans rarely survived alone. Group cooperation became essential for:</p>
<ul>
<li>Protection from predators</li>
<li>Hunting large animals</li>
<li>Food sharing</li>
<li>Child care</li>
<li>Knowledge transfer</li>
</ul>
<p>This cooperation strengthened:</p>
<ul>
<li>Communication</li>
<li>Trust</li>
<li>Leadership</li>
<li>Social bonding</li>
</ul>
<pMany modern human social behaviors have roots in Stone Age survival systems.</p>
<h3> IMPORTANCE OF FIRE</h3>
<p>The discovery and control of fire transformed human survival.</p>
<p>Fire allowed humans to:</p>
<ul>
<li>Cook food for better nutrition</li>
<li>Stay warm in cold climates</li>
<li>Protect themselves from predators</li>
<li>Extend activity into nighttime</li>
<li>Gather socially for communication and learning</li>
</ul>
<p>Fire therefore increased both survival and social development.</p>
<h3> DEEP CORE PRINCIPLE</h3>
<pre>
Human success in the Stone Age depended on:
tools + cooperation + environmental understanding
</pre>
<p>
The humans who survived best were not always the strongest,
but those who:
</p>
<ul>
<li>Adapted quickly</li>
<li>Worked together</li>
<li>Learned efficiently</li>
<li>Created better tools</li>
<li>Understood nature deeply</li>
</ul>
<h3> REAL-WORLD CONNECTIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li> Modern teamwork reflects ancient survival cooperation.</li>
<li> Modern technology evolved from early tool-making.</li>
<li> Permanent cities developed from early settlements.</li>
<li> Agriculture today began during the Neolithic Revolution.</li>
<li> Human social behavior still reflects Stone Age survival instincts.</li>
</ul>
<h3> IMPORTANCE OF STUDYING THE STONE AGE</h3>
<ul>
<li>Explains the origins of human civilization</li>
<li>Shows how technology began</li>
<li>Helps understand human survival behavior</li>
<li>Explains the development of farming and settlements</li>
<li>Shows how cooperation shaped human success</li>
</ul>
<h3> FINAL INSIGHT</h3>
<pre>
Modern civilization did not begin with machines or cities.
It began when early humans learned to cooperate,
understand nature, control fire, and create tools
to improve survival in the Stone Age.
</pre>
',
  '[{"q":"What is a hunter-gatherer?","hint":"food collection method","steps":["Step 1: Identify sources of food in early human societies","Step 2: Combine hunting of animals and gathering of wild plants","Step 3: Define lifestyle based on mobility and survival needs"],"ans":"People who obtain food by hunting animals and gathering wild plants","why":"This was the main survival strategy during the Stone Age before the development of agriculture"},{"q":"Why did hunter-gatherers move frequently?","hint":"resource availability","steps":["Step 1: Identify dependence on natural food sources","Step 2: Recognize depletion of local resources over time","Step 3: Link movement to survival needs"],"ans":"Because they followed food sources as animals migrated and plants became scarce","why":"Mobility ensured access to fresh resources and survival in changing environments"},{"q":"What tools did hunter-gatherers use?","hint":"simple tools","steps":["Step 1: Identify available natural materials (stone, wood, bone)","Step 2: Recognize basic tool functions like cutting and hunting","Step 3: Define tool usage in daily survival"],"ans":"Simple tools made from stone, wood, and bone for hunting and gathering","why":"These tools improved survival efficiency before advanced technology existed"},{"q":"How did hunter-gatherer societies organize themselves?","hint":"small groups","steps":["Step 1: Identify group size (small bands)","Step 2: Recognize shared survival responsibilities","Step 3: Link to cooperation and equality"],"ans":"They lived in small groups that shared food and responsibilities","why":"Cooperation increased survival chances in harsh environments"},{"q":"Why did hunter-gatherer societies decline?","hint":"rise of farming","steps":["Step 1: Identify development of agriculture","Step 2: Compare farming stability vs hunting mobility","Step 3: Recognize shift to permanent settlements"],"ans":"Because agriculture provided more stable and reliable food sources","why":"Farming supported larger populations and permanent settlements, replacing nomadic life"}]'
);

-- Topic: history -> neolithic_revolution -> Neolithic Revolution and Birth of Agriculture
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'history',
  'neolithic_revolution',
  'Neolithic Revolution and Birth of Agriculture',
  '
<h2> Neolithic Revolution and Birth of Agriculture</h2>
<h3> WELL DETAILED NOTES</h3>
<p>The <b>Neolithic Revolution</b> was one of the greatest transformations in human history. It marks the shift from a nomadic hunting-and-gathering lifestyle to a settled agricultural lifestyle based on farming and animal domestication.</p>
<p>Before this revolution, humans survived mainly by:</p>
<ul>
<li>Hunting wild animals</li>
<li>Gathering fruits and plants</li>
<li>Moving constantly in search of food</li>
<li>Depending heavily on natural conditions</li>
</ul>
<p>After the Neolithic Revolution, humans began to:</p>
<ul>
<li>Grow crops intentionally</li>
<li>Domesticate animals</li>
<li>Store surplus food</li>
<li>Build permanent settlements</li>
<li>Create organized societies</li>
</ul>
<p>This transformation fundamentally changed:</p>
<ul>
<li> Food production</li>
<li> Human settlement patterns</li>
<li> Social organization</li>
<li> Population growth</li>
<li> Economic systems</li>
<li> Civilization development</li>
</ul>
<h3> UNDERSTANDING THE NEOLITHIC REVOLUTION DEEPLY</h3>
<p>The Neolithic Revolution did not happen instantly.It developed gradually over thousands of years in different regions independently.</p>
<p>Humans slowly discovered that:</p>
<ul>
<li>Seeds could be planted intentionally</li>
<li>Animals could be controlled and bred</li>
<li>Food could be stored for future use</li>
<li>Permanent living areas increased stability</li>
</ul>
<p>This was revolutionary because humans moved from:</p>
<p>Food production gave humans greater control over survival and reduced dependence on unpredictable hunting conditions.</p>
<p>However, this change also introduced new challenges such as:</p>
<ul>
<li>Disease spread in crowded settlements</li>
<li>Land competition</li>
<li>Social inequality</li>
<li>Resource conflict</li>
<li>Hard physical labor</li>
</ul>
<h3> IMPORTANT DEFINITIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Agriculture</b><p>The systematic cultivation of crops and domestication of animals for food production.</p></li>
<li><b>Settlement</b><p>A permanent or semi-permanent place where humans live.</p></li>
<li><b>Domestication</b><p>The process of adapting plants and animals for human use through selective breeding and controlled environments.</p></li>
<li><b>Surplus</b><p>Extra food or resources produced beyond immediate survival needs.</p></li>
<li><b>Neolithic Revolution</b><p>The major transition from hunting-and-gathering lifestyles to farming and settled communities.</p></li>
<li><b>Specialization</b><p>The development of different occupations and roles within society.</p></li>
</ul>
<h3> CAUSES OF THE NEOLITHIC REVOLUTION</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1. Climate Change</b><p>At the end of the Ice Age, climates became warmer and more stable in many regions.This created better conditions for plant growth and farming.</p></li>
<li>
<b>2. Population Pressure</b>
<p>Growing human populations increased demand for food.Hunting and gathering became less reliable in some regions.</p>
</li>
<li><b>3. Observation of Nature</b><p>Humans noticed that seeds grow into plants and animals can reproduce under controlled conditions.</p></li>
<li>
<b>4. Survival Stability</b>
<p>Agriculture provided a more predictable food supply compared to hunting
</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3> HOW THE NEOLITHIC REVOLUTION WORKED</h3>
<p><b>Step 1: Environmental Pressure</b></p>
<p>Climate changes and resource shortages made traditional hunting less reliable.</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Observation</b></p>
<p>Humans observed plant growth cycles and animal behavior.</p>
<p><b>Step 3: Experimentation</b></p>
<p>Small-scale farming and animal keeping began near camps and settlements.</p>
<p><b>Step 4: Food Stability</b></p>
<p>Agriculture created more predictable food production.</p>
<p><b>Step 5: Permanent Settlements</b></p>
<p>Humans began building villages and staying in one location.</p>
<p><b>Step 6: Population Growth</b></p>
<p>Reliable food allowed populations to increase rapidly.</p>
<p><b>Step 7: Social Transformation</b></p>
<p>New social systems and occupations emerged.</p>
<p><b>Step 8: Civilization Formation</b></p>
<p>Complex societies, trade systems, leadership structures, and eventually civilizations developed.</p>
<h3> MAJOR CHANGES CAUSED BY THE NEOLITHIC REVOLUTION</h3>
<ul>
<li>
<b>1. Permanent Settlements</b>
<p>Humans stopped migrating constantly and built stable communities.</p>
<p>This led to:</p>
<ul>
<li>Villages</li>
<li>Towns</li>
<li>Early cities</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>
<b>2. Food Surplus</b>
<p>Agriculture often produced more food than immediately needed.</p>
<p>Surplus food allowed:</p>
<ul>
<li>Storage</li>
<li>Trade</li>
<li>Population growth</li>
<li>Emergency reserves</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>
<b>3. Population Growth</b>
<p>Stable food supplies increased survival rates and birth rates.</p>
<p>Human populations expanded rapidly.</p>
</li>
<li>
<b>4. Social Specialization</b>
<p>Not everyone needed to hunt or farm.New occupations emerged such as:</p>
<ul>
<li>Builders</li>
<li>Traders</li>
<li>Leaders</li>
<li>Craft workers</li>
<li>Religious figures</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>
<b>5. Trade Development</b>
<p>Communities exchanged goods, tools, food, and resources.</p>
<p>Trade encouraged economic growth and cultural interaction.</p>
</li>
<li>
<b>6. Social Hierarchy</b>
<p>Ownership of land and surplus food created differences in wealth and power.</p>
<p>This contributed to:</p>
<ul>
<li>Social classes</li>
<li>Leadership systems</li>
<li>Inequality</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<h3> EFFECTS ON HUMAN LIFE</h3>
<p>The Neolithic Revolution transformed nearly every aspect of human existence.</p>
<ul>
<li> Humans became more settled and community-based.</li>
<li> Food became more controllable and storable.</li>
<li> Population density increased.</li>
<li> Ownership and property systems emerged.</li>
<li> Technology and tool development accelerated.</li>
<li> Foundations of governments and civilizations developed.</li>
</ul>
<h3> NEGATIVE EFFECTS OF THE NEOLITHIC REVOLUTION</h3>
<p>Although agriculture increased stability, it also introduced problems.</p>
<ul>
<li>Disease spread more easily in crowded settlements.</li>
<li>Farming often required harder physical labor.</li>
<li>Diet variety sometimes decreased.</li>
<li>Conflict over land and resources increased.</li>
<li>Social inequality became more common.</li>
</ul>
<p>This shows that the Neolithic Revolution created both progress and complexity.</p>
<h3> DEEP CORE PRINCIPLE</h3>
<pre>
The Neolithic Revolution transformed humans from
“mobile survivors”
into
“stationary system builders.”
</pre>
<p>Humans no longer depended only on nature.Instead, they began reshaping environments to support long-term survival and growth.</p>
<h3> MODERN CONNECTIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li> Modern agriculture began during the Neolithic Revolution.</li>
<li> Modern cities evolved from early farming settlements.</li>
<li> Property ownership systems originated from settled land use.</li>
<li> Modern population growth depends on food production systems.</li>
<li> Governments and economies developed from organized agricultural societies.</li>
</ul>
<h3> IMPORTANCE OF STUDYING THE NEOLITHIC REVOLUTION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Explains the origins of civilization</li>
<li>Shows how agriculture transformed society</li>
<li>Helps understand urbanization and population growth</li>
<li>Explains the origins of trade and specialization</li>
<li>Reveals how humans learned to reshape environments</li>
</ul>
<h3> FINAL INSIGHT</h3>
<pre>
The Neolithic Revolution changed more than food production.
It transformed human society,
created permanent communities,
introduced economic systems,
and laid the foundation for civilization itself.
</pre>
',
  '[{"q":"What is agriculture?","hint":"food production","steps":["Step 1: Identify shift from natural food collection to controlled production","Step 2: Recognize farming of crops and domestication of animals","Step 3: Link agriculture to stable food supply and settlements","Step 4: Define the system clearly"],"ans":"The practice of growing crops and domesticating animals for food and other resources","why":"It replaced hunting and gathering by providing a stable and predictable food supply, which allowed permanent settlements and the rise of civilizations"},{"q":"Why did agriculture change human societies?","hint":"settlements and surplus","steps":["Step 1: Identify stable food production","Step 2: Recognize food surplus creation","Step 3: Link surplus to population growth and settlements"],"ans":"Because it created food surplus and allowed people to live in permanent settlements","why":"Surplus food enabled specialization, trade, and the development of complex societies"},{"q":"What is a food surplus?","hint":"extra production","steps":["Step 1: Identify food production levels","Step 2: Compare production vs consumption","Step 3: Define excess output"],"ans":"More food produced than is needed for survival","why":"It allows societies to support non-farming roles like builders, leaders, and artisans"},{"q":"Why was the shift from hunting and gathering to farming important?","hint":"stability vs mobility","steps":["Step 1: Identify lifestyle differences","Step 2: Compare nomadic and settled life","Step 3: Link farming to stability"],"ans":"Because farming created stable food sources and permanent settlements","why":"It enabled population growth and the development of complex civilizations"}]'
);

-- Topic: history -> mesopotamia -> Mesopotamia: Birth of Cities, Writing, and Law
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'history',
  'mesopotamia',
  'Mesopotamia: Birth of Cities, Writing, and Law',
  '
<h2> Mesopotamia: Birth of Cities, Writing, and Law</h2>
<h3> WELL DETAILED NOTES</h3>
<p>
Mesopotamia is known as the <b>“Cradle of Civilization”</b>
because it was one of the first regions in the world where humans developed fully organized civilizations.
</p>
<p>In Mesopotamia, humans moved beyond simple farming communities and created:</p>
<ul>
<li> Cities</li>
<li> Laws</li>
<li> Writing systems</li>
<li> Governments</li>
<li> Trade systems</li>
<li> Administrative structures</li>
<li> Organized armies</li>
</ul>
<p>Mesopotamia was located between the<b>Tigris River</b> and the <b>Euphrates River</b>in the region of modern-day Iraq.</p>
<p>The name “Mesopotamia” means:</p>
<pre>“Land Between Rivers”</pre>
<p>These rivers were extremely important because they provided:</p>
<ul>
<li>Water for farming</li>
<li>Fertile soil</li>
<li>Transportation routes</li>
<li>Trade connections</li>
<li>Population support</li>
</ul>
<p>Mesopotamia is important because it represents one of the first times humans created large-scale systems for organizing society.</p>
<h3> UNDERSTANDING MESOPOTAMIA DEEPLY</h3>
<p>Before civilizations developed, most humans lived in small farming villages.As agriculture improved, food surplus increased and populations grew.</p>
<p>Larger populations created new challenges:</p>
<ul>
<li>Resource distribution</li>
<li>Conflict management</li>
<li>Trade organization</li>
<li>Land ownership</li>
<li>Leadership coordination</li>
</ul>
<p>To solve these problems, Mesopotamian societies developed systems of:</p>
<ul>
<li>Government</li>
<li>Law</li>
<li>Writing</li>
<li>Administration</li>
<li>Taxation</li>
<li>Military organization</li>
</ul>
<p>This transformation marks the beginning of civilization itself.</p>
<h3> IMPORTANT DEFINITIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Civilization</b><p>A highly organized society with cities, governments,social classes, writing systems, and economic activity.</p></li>
<li><b>City-State</b><p>An independent city and its surrounding territory governed as its own political unit.</p></li>
<li><b>Cuneiform</b><p>One of the earliest writing systems,using wedge-shaped symbols pressed into clay tablets.</p></li>
<li><b>Code of Hammurabi</b><p>One of the earliest written law codes,created to standardize rules and punishments.</p></li>
<li><b>Empire</b><p>A large political system where one state controls multiple territories or peoples.</p></li>
<li><b>Surplus</b><p>Extra food or resources produced beyond immediate survival needs.</p></li>
</ul>
<h3> WHY MESOPOTAMIA DEVELOPED CIVILIZATION</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1. Fertile Land</b>
<p>The Tigris and Euphrates rivers created fertile soil ideal for farming.</p>
<p>This increased food production and supported large populations.</p>
</li>
<li><b>2. Agricultural Surplus</b>
<p>Farming produced more food than people immediately needed.</p>
<p>Surplus food allowed:</p>
<ul>
<li>Population growth</li>
<li>Trade</li>
<li>Specialized occupations</li>
<li>Urban development</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>
<b>3. Population Growth</b>
<p>As populations increased,larger settlements formed and eventually became cities.</p>
</li>
<li>
<b>4. Need for Organization</b>
<p>Large populations required systems to manage:</p>
<ul>
<li>Resources</li>
<li>Trade</li>
<li>Laws</li>
<li>Construction</li>
<li>Defense</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>
<b>5. Trade and Transportation</b>
<p>The rivers supported transportation and trade between communities.</p>
<p>Trade increased wealth and cultural interaction.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3> MAJOR MESOPOTAMIAN CIVILIZATIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li>
<b>Sumerians</b>
<p>The Sumerians created some of the earliest cities,including:</p>
<ul>
<li>Ur</li>
<li>Uruk</li>
<li>Eridu</li>
</ul>
<p>Major Contributions:</p>
<ul>
<li>Cuneiform writing</li>
<li>Wheel technology</li>
<li>Irrigation systems</li>
<li>Organized government</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>
<b>Akkadians</b>
<p>The Akkadians formed one of the first empires under Sargon of Akkad.</p>
<p>They united multiple city-states under centralized rule.</p>
</li>
<li>
<b>Babylonians</b>
<p>The Babylonians became famous for:</p>
<ul>
<li>The city of Babylon</li>
<li>The Code of Hammurabi</li>
<li>Advanced mathematics and astronomy</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>
<b>Assyrians</b>
<p>The Assyrians built a powerful military empire known for:</p>
<ul>
<li>Military organization</li>
<li>Expansion</li>
<li>Administrative efficiency</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<h3> THE IMPORTANCE OF WRITING (CUNEIFORM)</h3>
<p>One of Mesopotamia’s greatest achievements was the invention of writing.</p>
<p>Cuneiform was first developed mainly for:</p>
<ul>
<li>Trade records</li>
<li>Tax collection</li>
<li>Food storage management</li>
<li>Administrative tracking</li>
</ul>
<p>Over time, writing expanded into:</p>
<ul>
<li>Laws</li>
<li>Literature</li>
<li>Religious texts</li>
<li>Historical records</li>
</ul>
<p>Writing transformed civilization because it allowed knowledge to survive beyond memory.</p>
<h3> THE CODE OF HAMMURABI</h3>
<p>The Code of Hammurabi was one of the earliest written legal systems.</p>
<p>It established:</p>
<ul>
<li>Rules</li>
<li>Punishments</li>
<li>Social expectations</li>
<li>Property regulations</li>
</ul>
<p>The code aimed to create order and predictability in society.</p>
<p>However, punishments often depended on:</p>
<ul>
<li>Social class</li>
<li>Status</li>
<li>Gender</li>
</ul>
<p>This shows that early legal systems reflected social inequality.</p>
<h3> HOW MESOPOTAMIAN CIVILIZATION WORKED</h3>
<p><b>Step 1: Agricultural Surplus</b></p>
<p>Farming near rivers produced stable food supplies.</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Population Growth</b></p>
<p>Stable food increased population size.</p>
<p><b>Step 3: Urban Development</b></p>
<p>Villages expanded into organized city-states.</p>
<p><b>Step 4: Need for Administration</b></p>
<p>Large societies required systems for managing resources and people.</p>
<p><b>Step 5: Development of Writing</b></p>
<p>Cuneiform was created to record information and transactions.</p>
<p><b>Step 6: Government Formation</b></p>
<p>Kings and officials emerged to organize society and maintain order.</p>
<p><b>Step 7: Legal Systems</b></p>
<p>Written laws standardized justice and authority.</p>
<p><b>Step 8: Empire Expansion</b></p>
<p>Powerful states conquered surrounding regions and built empires.</p>
<h3> SOCIAL STRUCTURE IN MESOPOTAMIA</h3>
<p>Mesopotamian society became highly organized into social classes.</p>
<ul>
<li> Kings and rulers</li>
<li> Priests and government officials</li>
<li> Skilled workers and merchants</li>
<li> Farmers and laborers</li>
<li> Slaves</li>
</ul>
<p>This hierarchy reflected increasing specialization and inequality within civilization.</p>
<h3> MESOPOTAMIAN ACHIEVEMENTS</h3>
<ul>
<li> Development of writing systems</li>
<li> Written legal systems</li>
<li> Urban planning and cities</li>
<li> Irrigation and farming systems</li>
<li> Wheel technology</li>
<li> Mathematics and astronomy</li>
<li> Government administration</li>
</ul>
<h3> DEEP CORE PRINCIPLE</h3>
<pre>
Civilization begins when human survival becomes organized
through systems of writing, law, administration, and centralized control.
</pre>
<p>
Mesopotamia marks the point where humans moved from small survival communities into structured societies capable of managing large populations.
</p>
<h3> MODERN CONNECTIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li> Modern databases and records evolved from early writing systems.</li>
<li> Modern legal systems still rely on written laws.</li>
<li> Cities today require organized governance similar to early civilizations.</li>
<li> Trade and taxation systems trace back to early administrative systems.</li>
<li> Information management remains central to civilization today.</li>
</ul>
<h3> IMPORTANCE OF STUDYING MESOPOTAMIA</h3>
<ul>
<li>Explains the origins of civilization</li>
<li>Shows how governments and legal systems developed</li>
<li>Explains the importance of writing and record-keeping</li>
<li>Helps understand urbanization and empire-building</li>
<li>Reveals how organized systems transformed human society</li>
</ul>
<h3> FINAL INSIGHT</h3>
<pre>
Mesopotamia was the moment humans transformed
from survival-based communities
into organized civilizations built on law,
writing, administration, and structured power.
Many systems used in the modern world
still reflect foundations first created there.
</pre>
<hr>
',
  '[{"q":"Why is Mesopotamia called the cradle of civilization?","hint":"first major developments","steps":["Step 1: Identify early innovations in human society","Step 2: Recognize development of cities, writing systems, and legal codes in the region","Step 3: Link these developments to the start of organized civilization","Step 4: Conclude why the region is historically significant"],"ans":"Because it was one of the first places where cities, writing systems, agriculture, and laws developed","why":"Mesopotamia represents the earliest known transition from simple settlements to organized complex societies"},{"q":"What is cuneiform?","hint":"writing system","steps":["Step 1: Identify the need for communication and record-keeping","Step 2: Observe early symbols carved into clay tablets","Step 3: Recognize wedge-shaped script development","Step 4: Define the writing system"],"ans":"An early writing system developed in Mesopotamia using wedge-shaped symbols on clay tablets","why":"It allowed humans to record laws, trade, and history, making complex administration possible"},{"q":"Why was writing important in early civilizations?","hint":"record keeping","steps":["Step 1: Identify administrative needs (trade, taxes, laws)","Step 2: Recognize limits of oral communication","Step 3: Show advantage of permanent records"],"ans":"Because it allowed societies to record information like trade, laws, and events permanently","why":"Writing enabled governments and economies to become more organized and complex"},{"q":"What made Mesopotamia suitable for early civilization?","hint":"fertile land between rivers","steps":["Step 1: Identify geography (Tigris and Euphrates rivers)","Step 2: Recognize fertile soil and irrigation potential","Step 3: Connect to farming surplus and city growth"],"ans":"Fertile land between the Tigris and Euphrates rivers supported agriculture and settlement","why":"Agricultural surplus allowed population growth and the rise of cities"}]'
);

-- Topic: history -> ancient_egypt -> Ancient Egypt: Nile Civilization and Pharaoh Power
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'history',
  'ancient_egypt',
  'Ancient Egypt: Nile Civilization and Pharaoh Power',
  '
<h2> Ancient Egypt: Nile Civilization and Pharaoh Power</h2>
<h3> WELL DETAILED NOTES</h3>

<p>
Ancient Egypt was one of the most powerful, stable, and long-lasting civilizations in human history. It developed along the <b>Nile River</b> in northeastern Africa and survived for thousands of years because of a strong connection between:
</p>

<ul>
<li> Environment</li>
<li> Agriculture</li>
<li> Political authority</li>
<li> Religion</li>
<li> Social organization</li>
</ul>
<p>
Egyptian civilization was built around the Nile River, which provided:
</p>
<ul>
<li>Reliable water supply</li>
<li>Fertile soil</li>
<li>Transportation routes</li>
<li>Food stability</li>
<li>Economic growth</li>
</ul>
<p>
Unlike many other regions surrounded by unpredictable environments,
Egypt benefited from a relatively stable natural system.
</p>
<p>
Most of Egypt was desert, so the Nile became the center of life.
Nearly all major settlements formed close to the river.
</p>
<h3> UNDERSTANDING ANCIENT EGYPT DEEPLY</h3>
<p>Ancient Egypt succeeded because its environment supported predictable survival.</p>
<p>Every year, the Nile River flooded nearby land and deposited nutrient-rich soil.This process created ideal farming conditions.</p>
<p>Because food production was stable:</p>
<ul>
<li>Populations increased</li>
<li>Permanent settlements developed</li>
<li>Trade expanded</li>
<li>Governments became organized</li>
<li>Large building projects became possible</li>
</ul>
<p>Egyptian society was also highly unified because religion and government were deeply connected.</p>
<p>The Pharaoh was not viewed as an ordinary king.He was believed to be:</p>
<ul>
<li>A divine ruler</li>
<li>A protector of order</li>
<li>A link between gods and humans</li>
</ul>
<p>This belief strengthened political authority and social stability.</p>
<h3> IMPORTANT DEFINITIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Pharaoh</b><p>The supreme ruler of ancient Egypt,considered both a political leader and a divine or semi-divine figure.</p></li>
<li><b>Hieroglyphics</b><p>A symbolic writing system using pictures and symbols to represent sounds, objects, and ideas.</p></li>
<li><b>Mummification</b><p>The preservation of dead bodies to prepare them for the afterlife.</p></li>
<li><b>Nile Flood Cycle</b><p>The annual flooding of the Nile River that deposited fertile soil for farming.</p></li>
<li><b>Dynasty</b><p>A series of rulers from the same family line.</p></li>
<li><b>Afterlife</b><p>The belief that life continues after death.</p></li>
</ul>
<h3> THE IMPORTANCE OF THE NILE RIVER</h3>
<p>
The Nile River was the foundation of Egyptian civilization.
Without it, large-scale civilization in the desert environment would have been nearly impossible.
</p>
<p>The Nile provided:</p>
<ul>
<li> Water for survival</li>
<li> Fertile soil for farming</li>
<li> Transportation and trade routes</li>
<li> Food resources like fish</li>
<li> Support for cities and settlements</li>
</ul>
<p>The Nile flood cycle was predictable compared to rivers in many other civilizations.</p>
<p>This predictability created:</p>
<ul>
<li>Economic stability</li>
<li>Population stability</li>
<li>Political continuity</li>
</ul>
<h3> THE PHARAOH SYSTEM</h3>
<p>The Pharaoh was the center of Egyptian political and religious life.</p>
<p>Pharaohs controlled:</p>
<ul>
<li>Government administration</li>
<li>Military systems</li>
<li>Tax collection</li>
<li>Religious ceremonies</li>
<li>Large construction projects</li>
</ul>
<p>Because Pharaohs were viewed as divine,their authority became extremely powerful.</p>
<p>This created:</p>
<ul>
<li>Obedience</li>
<li>Political unity</li>
<li>Social order</li>
<li>Long-term stability</li>
</ul>
<h3> RELIGION IN ANCIENT EGYPT</h3>
<p>Religion influenced nearly every part of Egyptian life.</p>
<p>Egyptians believed in:</p>
<ul>
<li>Multiple gods and goddesses</li>
<li>The afterlife</li>
<li>Divine order and balance</li>
<li>Spiritual judgment after death</li>
</ul>
<p>Religion shaped:</p>
<ul>
<li>Government authority</li>
<li>Architecture</li>
<li>Burial practices</li>
<li>Daily life</li>
<li>Art and culture</li>
</ul>
<p>One important concept was:</p>
<p><b>Ma’at</b></p>
<p>Ma’at represented:</p>
<ul>
<li>Order</li>
<li>Truth</li>
<li>Balance</li>
<li>Harmony</li>
</ul>
<p>The Pharaoh was expected to maintain Ma’at throughout society.</p>
<h3> MUMMIFICATION AND THE AFTERLIFE</h3>
<p>Egyptians strongly believed in life after death.</p>
<p>They believed the body needed preservation for the soul to continue existing.</p>
<p>This belief led to:</p>
<ul>
<li>Mummification</li>
<li>Tomb construction</li>
<li>Burial rituals</li>
<li>Pyramids and monuments</li>
</ul>
<p>Mummification involved:</p>
<ul>
<li>Removing internal organs</li>
<li>Drying the body</li>
<li>Wrapping it carefully</li>
<li>Preparing it for burial</li>
</ul>
<p>These practices reflect the importance of religion in Egyptian civilization.</p>
<h3> PYRAMIDS AND MONUMENTAL ARCHITECTURE</h3>
<p>The pyramids are among the most famous structures in human history.</p>
<p>They served mainly as:</p>
<ul>
<li>Tombs for Pharaohs</li>
<li>Symbols of political power</li>
<li>Religious monuments</li>
</ul>
<p>Building pyramids required:</p>
<ul>
<li>Engineering knowledge</li>
<li>Organized labor</li>
<li>Resource management</li>
<li>Government coordination</li>
</ul>
<p>Evidence suggests pyramids were largely built by:</p>
<ul>
<li>Skilled laborers</li>
<li>Organized workers</li>
<li>Seasonal farmers during flood periods</li>
</ul>
<p>This shows the strength of Egypt’s centralized system.</p>
<h3> WRITING SYSTEM: HIEROGLYPHICS</h3>
<p>Egyptians developed a complex writing system called hieroglyphics.</p>
<p>It was used for:</p>
<ul>
<li>Religious texts</li>
<li>Government records</li>
<li>Historical documentation</li>
<li>Monument inscriptions</li>
</ul>
<p>Writing helped preserve:</p>
<ul>
<li>Knowledge</li>
<li>Administration</li>
<li>Cultural identity</li>
</ul>
<h3> SOCIAL STRUCTURE IN EGYPT</h3>
<p>Egyptian society was highly organized into social classes.</p>
<ul>
<li> Pharaoh</li>
<li> Priests and nobles</li>
<li> Scribes and officials</li>
<li> Craftsmen and merchants</li>

<li>
 Farmers and laborers
</li>

<li>
 Slaves and servants
</li>

</ul>

<p>
Each group had specific roles that helped maintain civilization.
</p>

<h3> HOW ANCIENT EGYPT WORKED</h3>

<p><b>Step 1: Environmental Stability</b></p>

<p>
The Nile River created predictable agricultural conditions.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2: Agricultural Surplus</b></p>

<p>
Reliable farming produced stable food supplies.
</p>

<p><b>Step 3: Population Growth</b></p>

<p>
Food stability allowed settlements and cities to expand.
</p>

<p><b>Step 4: Centralized Authority</b></p>

<p>
Pharaohs organized labor, governance, and resources.
</p>

<p><b>Step 5: Religious Integration</b></p>

<p>
Religion reinforced political power and social unity.</p>
<p><b>Step 6: Infrastructure Development</b></p>
<p>Temples, pyramids, irrigation systems, and administrative centers were built.</p>
<p><b>Step 7: Long-Term Stability</b></p>
<p>Strong alignment between environment, religion, and governance sustained civilization for thousands of years.</p>
<h3> MAJOR ACHIEVEMENTS OF ANCIENT EGYPT</h3>
<ul>
<li> Monumental architecture</li>
<li> Hieroglyphic writing</li>
<li> Advanced irrigation and farming</li>
<li> Government administration</li>
<li> Mathematics and engineering</li>
<li> Medical knowledge</li>
<li> River transportation systems</li>
</ul>
<h3> DEEP CORE PRINCIPLE</h3>
<pre>
Ancient Egypt survived for thousands of years because
environment, governance, and belief systems
worked together as one stable structure.
</pre>
<p>
The Nile supported agriculture,
agriculture supported population growth,
and religion strengthened centralized political authority.
</p>
<h3> MODERN CONNECTIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li> Modern farming still depends on water systems and irrigation.</li>
<li> Governments still rely on centralized administration.</li>
<li> Political authority often connects with cultural beliefs.</li>
<li> Infrastructure planning still depends heavily on geography.</li>
<li> Written records remain essential for organized societies.</li>
</ul>
<h3> IMPORTANCE OF STUDYING ANCIENT EGYPT</h3>
<ul>
<li>Explains how environment shapes civilization</li>
<li>Shows the relationship between religion and government</li>
<li>Reveals the importance of agriculture in societal stability</li>
<li>Demonstrates the role of centralized leadership</li>
<li>Helps understand early architecture, engineering, and writing systems</li>
</ul>
<h3> FINAL INSIGHT</h3>
<pre>
Ancient Egypt demonstrates that civilizations become stable
when geography, agriculture, political authority,
and belief systems reinforce one another.
Its survival for thousands of years
was not accidental —
it was the result of a deeply interconnected system.
</pre>
',
  '[{"q":"Why was the Nile River important?","hint":"survival in desert","steps":["Step 1: Identify environment (desert conditions with scarce rainfall)","Step 2: Nile provides water, fertile soil (silt), and farming zones along its banks","Step 3: Link survival, agriculture, settlement, and civilization growth","Step 4: Conclude overall importance to economy and society"],"ans":"It provided water, fertile land for farming, transportation, and supported the growth of civilization in Egypt","why":"Without the Nile, large-scale agriculture, settlement stability, and the development of Egyptian civilization would not have been possible"},{"q":"How did the Nile support agriculture?","hint":"flooding cycle","steps":["Step 1: Identify annual flooding pattern","Step 2: Floodwaters deposit nutrient-rich silt","Step 3: Farmers use fertile land for crops"],"ans":"By depositing fertile soil during seasonal floods that improved crop growth","why":"The predictable flooding cycle made farming reliable in an otherwise desert environment"},{"q":"Why did settlements form along rivers in ancient civilizations?","hint":"water source","steps":["Step 1: Identify need for water access","Step 2: Link water to farming and survival","Step 3: Explain settlement concentration near rivers"],"ans":"Because rivers provided water, fertile land, and transportation routes","why":"Early societies depended on rivers for food production and movement of goods"},{"q":"What role did rivers play in early trade?","hint":"transport routes","steps":["Step 1: Identify transportation challenge on land","Step 2: Recognize rivers as natural highways","Step 3: Link to movement of goods and people"],"ans":"They acted as natural transportation routes for trade and communication","why":"River systems made it easier and cheaper to move goods compared to land travel"}]'
);

-- Topic: history -> indus_valley -> Indus Valley Civilization: Urban Planning Mystery Civilization
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'history',
  'indus_valley',
  'Indus Valley Civilization: Urban Planning Mystery Civilization',
  '
<h2> Indus Valley Civilization: Urban Planning Mystery Civilization</h2>
<h3> WELL DETAILED NOTES</h3>

<p>
The <b>Indus Valley Civilization</b> (also called the <b>Harappan Civilization</b>) was one of the earliest and most advanced urban civilizations in human history.
</p>

<p>
It existed around <b>2500–1900 BCE</b> in regions that are now:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Pakistan</li>
<li>Northwest India</li>
</ul>

<p>
The civilization developed mainly around the:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Indus River</li>
<li>Nearby river systems</li>
</ul>

<p>
The Indus Valley Civilization is unique because its intelligence was expressed less through giant monuments or visible kings and more through:
</p>

<ul>
<li> Urban planning</li>
<li> Sanitation systems</li>
<li> Standardization</li>
<li> Organized housing</li>
<li> Trade systems</li>
<li> Infrastructure design</li>
</ul>

<p>
This civilization demonstrates that human advancement can exist quietly through systems of organization embedded into everyday life.
</p>

<pre>
Early Farming Settlements
            ↓
Agricultural Stability
            ↓
Population Growth
            ↓
Urban Planning & Grid Cities
            ↓
Drainage & Sanitation Systems
            ↓
Trade Networks & Standardization
            ↓
Large Urban Civilization
            ↓
Gradual Decline (Unclear Causes)
</pre>

<h3> UNDERSTANDING THE INDUS VALLEY CIVILIZATION DEEPLY</h3>

<p>
Many ancient civilizations are remembered for:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Pyramids</li>
<li>Kings</li>
<li>Massive monuments</li>
<li>Military empires</li>
</ul>

<p>
The Indus Valley Civilization is different.
</p>

<p>
Archaeologists discovered:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Carefully planned cities</li>
<li>Standardized brick construction</li>
<li>Advanced drainage systems</li>
<li>Public wells</li>
<li>Organized street layouts</li>
</ul>

<p>
However, there is very little clear evidence of:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Large palaces</li>
<li>Massive royal tombs</li>
<li>Dominant rulers</li>
<li>Large standing armies</li>
</ul>

<p>
This suggests the civilization may have functioned differently from many other early societies.
</p>

<p>
Its strength appears to have come from:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Coordination</li>
<li>Infrastructure</li>
<li>Standardization</li>
<li>Urban management</li>
</ul>

rather than visible centralized power alone.
</p>

<h3> IMPORTANT DEFINITIONS</h3>

<ul>

<li>
<b>Urban Planning</b>

<p>
The deliberate organization and design of cities,
including roads, buildings, drainage, and public spaces.
</p>
</li>

<li>
<b>Drainage System</b>

<p>
A network of channels and pipes used to remove wastewater and maintain sanitation.
</p>
</li>

<li>
<b>Harappa</b>

<p>
One of the major cities of the Indus Valley Civilization.
</p>
</li>

<li>
<b>Mohenjo-Daro</b>

<p>
Another major Indus Valley city known for advanced urban planning and public infrastructure.
</p>
</li>

<li>
<b>Standardization</b>

<p>
The use of uniform systems such as equal brick sizes, weights, and measurements.
</p>
</li>

<li>
<b>Indus Script</b>

<p>
A writing system used by the civilization that has not yet been fully decoded.
</p>
</li>

</ul>

<h3> THE IMPORTANCE OF RIVER SYSTEMS</h3>

<p>
Like many early civilizations,
the Indus Valley Civilization developed near rivers.
</p>

<p>
Rivers provided:
</p>

<ul>

<li>
 Water supply
</li>

<li>
 Fertile agricultural land
</li>

<li>
 Transportation routes
</li>

<li>
 Food resources
</li>

<li>
 Support for population growth
</li>

</ul>

<p>
Agriculture allowed settlements to become larger and more permanent.
</p>

<p>
Food surplus supported:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Trade</li>
<li>Construction</li>
<li>Urban development</li>
<li>Specialized occupations</li>
</ul>

<h3> URBAN PLANNING IN THE INDUS VALLEY</h3>

<p>
One of the civilization’s most impressive achievements was city planning.
</p>

<p>
Many Indus cities were built using:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Grid patterns</li>
<li>Straight streets</li>
<li>Organized neighborhoods</li>
<li>Public infrastructure systems</li>
</ul>

<p>
This suggests careful planning and coordination.
</p>

<p>
Cities often included:
</p>

<ul>

<li>
 Residential housing
</li>

<li>
 Public wells
</li>

<li>
 Bathing areas
</li>

<li>
 Drainage systems
</li>

<li>
 Storage facilities
</li>

</ul>

<p>
The organization of these cities was highly advanced for its time.
</p>

<h3> ADVANCED SANITATION SYSTEMS</h3>

<p>
The Indus Valley Civilization is especially famous for its sanitation and drainage systems.
</p>

<p>
Many houses were connected to:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Covered drains</li>
<li>Wastewater channels</li>
<li>Public drainage systems</li>
</ul>

<p>
Some homes even had:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Private bathing spaces</li>
<li>Water access systems</li>
</ul>

<p>
This shows that:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Public health mattered</li>
<li>Urban cleanliness was organized</li>
<li>Infrastructure planning was sophisticated</li>
</ul>

<p>
These systems reflect engineering principles still important in modern cities today.
</p>

<h3> STANDARDIZATION AND ORGANIZATION</h3>

<p>
The Indus Valley Civilization used standardized systems across many cities.
</p>

<p>
Archaeologists found:
</p>

<ul>

<li>
 Uniform weights and measures
</li>

<li>
 Standard brick sizes
</li>

<li>
 Similar city layouts
</li>

</ul>

<p>
This suggests:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Strong coordination</li>
<li>Economic organization</li>
<li>Shared cultural systems</li>
</ul>

<p>
Standardization improved:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Trade efficiency</li>
<li>Construction consistency</li>
<li>Regional integration</li>
</ul>

<h3> TRADE NETWORKS</h3>

<p>
The Indus Valley Civilization participated in regional and long-distance trade.
</p>

<p>
Trade connected Indus cities with:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Nearby regions</li>
<li>Central Asia</li>
<li>Mesopotamia</li>
</ul>

<p>
Traded goods included:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Beads</li>
<li>Metals</li>
<li>Textiles</li>
<li>Pottery</li>
<li>Craft products</li>
</ul>

<p>
Trade increased:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Economic growth</li>
<li>Cultural interaction</li>
<li>Resource distribution</li>
</ul>

<h3> THE INDUS SCRIPT</h3>

<p>
The civilization developed a writing system known as the <b>Indus Script</b>.
</p>

<p>
However, this script has not yet been fully decoded.
</p>

<p>
This means:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Much of their history remains uncertain</li>
<li>Their political systems are not fully understood</li>
<li>Many cultural details are still unknown</li>
</ul>

<p>
The undecoded script reminds us that:
</p>

<pre>
Knowledge can exist
even when it cannot yet be fully understood.
</pre>

<h3> POSSIBLE SOCIAL ORGANIZATION</h3>

<p>
Unlike Egypt or Mesopotamia,
the Indus Valley Civilization shows limited evidence of:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Powerful kings</li>
<li>Massive palaces</li>
<li>Large royal monuments</li>
</ul>

<p>
This has led historians to suggest that the civilization may have relied more on:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Administrative coordination</li>
<li>Collective organization</li>
<li>Urban management systems</li>
</ul>

<p>
rather than highly visible centralized rulers.
</p>

<h3> DECLINE OF THE INDUS VALLEY CIVILIZATION</h3>

<p>
The civilization gradually declined around 1900 BCE.
</p>

<p>
The exact cause remains uncertain,
but possible explanations include:
</p>

<ul>

<li>
 Climate change
</li>

<li>
 River shifts
</li>

<li>
 Agricultural decline
</li>

<li>
 Trade disruptions
</li>

<li>
 Urban system breakdown
</li>

</ul>

<p>
Most historians believe the decline happened gradually rather than through sudden destruction.
</p>

<h3> HOW THE INDUS VALLEY CIVILIZATION WORKED</h3>

<p><b>Step 1: Environmental Foundation</b></p>

<p>
River systems supported farming and settlement growth.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2: Agricultural Stability</b></p>

<p>
Food surplus increased population size.
</p>

<p><b>Step 3: Urban Planning</b></p>

<p>
Cities were carefully organized using grid systems.
</p>

<p><b>Step 4: Infrastructure Development</b></p>

<p>
Drainage systems, wells, and sanitation improved city life.
</p>

<p><b>Step 5: Standardization</b></p>

<p>
Uniform systems improved construction and trade.
</p>

<p><b>Step 6: Trade Expansion</b></p>

<p>
Regional and long-distance trade connected cities economically.
</p>

<p><b>Step 7: Gradual Decline</b></p>

<p>
Environmental and economic disruptions weakened the civilization.
</p>

<h3> MAJOR ACHIEVEMENTS OF THE INDUS VALLEY CIVILIZATION</h3>

<ul>

<li>
 Advanced urban planning
</li>

<li>
 Sophisticated sanitation systems
</li>

<li>
 Standardized construction
</li>

<li>
 Uniform measurement systems
</li>

<li>
 Long-distance trade
</li>

<li>
 Organized infrastructure management
</li>

<li>
 Development of a writing system
</li>

</ul>

<h3> DEEP CORE PRINCIPLE</h3>

<pre>
A civilization can become highly advanced
through systems of organization,
infrastructure,
and everyday efficiency —
even without visible monuments or fully understood written history.
</pre>

<p>
The Indus Valley Civilization demonstrates that societal intelligence can exist quietly inside systems rather than dramatic displays of power.
</p>

<h3> COMMON MISUNDERSTANDINGS</h3>

<ul>

<li>
 <b>“Ancient cities were always chaotic.”</b>

<p>
<b>Reality:</b>
Indus cities were highly organized using planned grid systems.
</p>
</li>

<li>
 <b>“Writing always survives and can be read.”</b>

<p>
<b>Reality:</b>
The Indus script still cannot be fully decoded.
</p>
</li>

<li>
 <b>“Ancient engineering was primitive.”</b>

<p>
<b>Reality:</b>
Indus drainage and sanitation systems were highly sophisticated.
</p>
</li>

<li>
 <b>“Civilization requires giant monuments.”</b>

<p>
<b>Reality:</b>
The Indus Valley shows that organization and infrastructure can also represent advancement.
</p>
</li>

<li>
 <b>“Power is always visible.”</b>

<p>
<b>Reality:</b>
Complex coordination can exist without obvious rulers or massive palaces.
</p>
</li>

</ul>

<h3> MODERN CONNECTIONS</h3>

<ul>

<li>
 Modern city grids reflect early urban planning principles.
</li>

<li>
 Modern sanitation systems rely on similar infrastructure concepts.
</li>

<li>
 Standardization remains essential in engineering and trade.
</li>

<li>
 Urban coordination still depends on organized infrastructure systems.
</li>

<li>
 Modern societies still rely on invisible systems working in the background.
</li>

</ul>

<h3> IMPORTANCE OF STUDYING THE INDUS VALLEY CIVILIZATION</h3>

<ul>

<li>
Shows the importance of infrastructure in civilization
</li>

<li>
Explains the origins of urban planning
</li>

<li>
Demonstrates advanced ancient engineering
</li>

<li>
Reveals that civilization can exist without visible monuments
</li>

<li>
Helps understand how organization shapes human life
</li>

</ul>

<h3> FINAL INSIGHT</h3>

<pre>
The Indus Valley Civilization reveals that true advancement
is not always loud, visible, or centered around rulers.

Sometimes the highest intelligence of a civilization
exists quietly inside systems of order,
planning,
sanitation,
and everyday coordination.
</pre>

