<h2> Columbian Exchange: Global Transfer of Life Systems</h2>
<h3> WELL DETAILED NOTES</h3>

<p>
The Columbian Exchange (beginning after 1492) was one of the most important turning points in world history.
</p>

<p>
It refers to the massive exchange of:
</p>

<ul>

<li> Crops</li>

<li> Animals</li>

<li> Diseases</li>

<li> People</li>

<li> Ideas</li>

<li> Religions</li>

<li> Technologies</li>

<li> Economic systems</li>

</ul>

<p>
between:
</p>

<pre>
Old World
(Europe, Africa, Asia)

↔

New World
(North and South America)
</pre>

<p>
after the voyages of Christopher Columbus in 1492.
</p>

<p>
For the first time in history,
all major continents became directly connected in a continuous biological,
economic,
and cultural system.
</p>

<p>
The Columbian Exchange permanently changed:
</p>

<ul>

<li>Human diets</li>

<li>Population growth</li>

<li>Global trade</li>

<li>Ecosystems</li>

<li>Agriculture</li>

<li>Labor systems</li>

<li>World economies</li>

