            ↓
Global Transformation
</pre>

<h3> UNDERSTANDING THE COLUMBIAN EXCHANGE DEEPLY</h3>

<p>
Before 1492,
many parts of the world developed separately for thousands of years.
</p>

<p>
Different continents had:
</p>

<ul>

<li>Different crops</li>

<li>Different animals</li>

<li>Different diseases</li>

<li>Different ecosystems</li>

<li>Different technologies</li>

</ul>

<p>
The voyages across the Atlantic suddenly connected these previously isolated systems.
</p>

<p>
This created:
</p>

<pre>
Global biological exchange
Global trade systems
Global migration
Global cultural interaction
and Global environmental change.
</pre>

<p>
The Columbian Exchange was not simply trade.
</p>

<p>
It was:
</p>

<pre>
An ecological revolution
A demographic revolution
An economic revolution
and a global historical transformation.
</pre>

<h3> IMPORTANT DEFINITIONS</h3>

<ul>

<li>
<b>Columbian Exchange</b>

<p>
The transfer of plants,
animals,
diseases,
people,
culture,
and technology between the Old World and New World after 1492.
</p>

</li>

<li>
<b>Old World</b>

<p>
Europe,
Africa,
and Asia —
regions connected before contact with the Americas.
</p>

</li>

<li>
<b>New World</b>

<p>
North and South America,
which became connected to Afro-Eurasian systems after European exploration.
</p>

</li>

<li>
<b>Biological Exchange</b>

<p>
The movement of living organisms such as crops,
animals,
and microbes between ecosystems.
</p>

</li>

<li>
<b>Ecological Impact</b>

<p>
Changes caused to environments,
ecosystems,
species,
and biodiversity.
</p>

</li>

<li>
<b>Pandemic Disease</b>

<p>
A disease that spreads across large populations and regions.
</p>

</li>

<li>
<b>Cash Crops</b>

<p>
Agricultural products grown mainly for sale and export rather than local use.
</p>

</li>

</ul>

<h3> CROPS EXCHANGED</h3>

<p>
One of the biggest effects of the Columbian Exchange was the movement of crops across continents.
</p>

<p>
The Americas introduced crops such as:
</p>

<ul>

<li> Potatoes</li>

<li> Maize (corn)</li>

<li> Tomatoes</li>

<li> Cacao</li>

<li> Peppers</li>

<li> Peanuts</li>

<li> Pineapples</li>

</ul>

<p>
These crops transformed food systems in:
</p>

<ul>

<li>Europe</li>

<li>Africa</li>

<li>Asia</li>

</ul>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
The potato became extremely important in Europe because it produced large amounts of food in small areas.
</p>

<p>
This contributed to:
</p>

<pre>
Population growth
Urban expansion
and Economic development.
</pre>

<p>
Meanwhile,
Europe introduced crops such as:
</p>

<ul>

<li> Wheat</li>

<li> Rice</li>

<li> Sugarcane</li>

<li> Coffee</li>

</ul>

<p>
into the Americas.
