<p>
These animals changed:
</p>

<ul>

<li>Agriculture</li>

<li>Transportation</li>

<li>Warfare</li>

<li>Food systems</li>

<li>Economic life</li>

</ul>

<p>
The horse especially transformed many indigenous societies by increasing mobility,
trade,
and military power.
</p>

<h3> DISEASES AND POPULATION COLLAPSE</h3>

<p>
One of the most devastating parts of the Columbian Exchange was disease transmission.
</p>

<p>
Europeans brought diseases such as:
</p>

<ul>

<li> Smallpox</li>

<li> Measles</li>

<li> Influenza</li>

<li> Typhus</li>

</ul>

<p>
to the Americas.
</p>

<p>
Many indigenous populations had:
</p>

<pre>
No immunity
No prior exposure
and No medical protection.
</pre>

<p>
This caused massive population decline across many regions.
</p>

<p>
Entire communities were weakened or destroyed by epidemics.
</p>

<p>
Disease became one of the most powerful forces shaping colonization and conquest.
</p>

<h3> ECONOMIC TRANSFORMATION</h3>

<p>
The Columbian Exchange greatly expanded the global economy.
</p>

<p>
New crops and resources created:
</p>

<ul>

<li> International trade systems</li>

<li> Global markets</li>

<li> Oceanic trade networks</li>

<li> Economic growth</li>

</ul>

<p>
European empires increasingly depended on:
</p>

<ul>

