<h2> Urbanization: Growth of Cities and Social Transformation</h2>
<h3> WELL DETAILED NOTES</h3>

<p>
Urbanization is one of the most important social and economic transformations in human history.
</p>

<p>
It refers to the large-scale movement of people from:
</p>

<pre>
Rural Areas (villages, farming regions)
            ↓
Urban Areas (cities and towns)
</pre>

<p>
Urbanization became especially powerful during the:
</p>

<p>
<b>Industrial Revolution.</b>
</p>

<p>
As factories expanded,
cities became major centers of:
</p>

<ul>

<li> Industrial production</li>

<li> Employment opportunities</li>

<li> Transportation systems</li>

<li> Trade networks</li>

<li> Political administration</li>

<li> Innovation and culture</li>

</ul>

<p>
This caused millions of people to leave rural farming life and move into growing cities.
</p>

<p>
Urbanization is not simply population growth.
</p>

<p>
It is:
</p>

<pre>
A complete restructuring
of human civilization.
</pre>

<p>
It changes:
</p>

<ul>

<li>How people work</li>

<li>How economies function</li>

<li>How families live</li>

<li>How societies organize themselves</li>

<li>How infrastructure develops</li>

<li>How culture evolves</li>

</ul>

<pre>
Agricultural Society
(farming, rural life)
            ↓
Industrialization
(factory systems develop)
            ↓
Urban Migration
(people move into cities)
            ↓
City Expansion
(housing, roads, transport)
            ↓
New Social Classes Form
            ↓
Modern Urban Civilization
</pre>

<h3> UNDERSTANDING URBANIZATION DEEPLY</h3>

<p>
Urbanization happens when:
</p>

<pre>
Economic opportunity becomes concentrated inside cities.
</pre>

<p>
Before industrialization,
most people lived in rural areas because economies depended mainly on:
</p>

<ul>

<li> Agriculture</li>

<li> Livestock</li>

<li> Farming systems</li>

<li> Land ownership</li>

</ul>

<p>
Industrialization changed this structure completely.
</p>

<p>
Factories required:
</p>

<ul>

<li>Large labor forces</li>

<li>Transportation access</li>

<li>Centralized production</li>

<li>Markets and trade systems</li>

</ul>

<p>
As factories concentrated in cities,
people followed the jobs.
</p>

<p>
This created:
</p>

<pre>
Mass Rural-to-Urban Migration.
</pre>

<p>
Urbanization therefore became directly connected to:
</p>

<ul>

<li>Industrial growth</li>

<li>Economic modernization</li>

<li>Technological development</li>

<li>Population expansion</li>

<li>Capitalism</li>

</ul>

<h3> IMPORTANT DEFINITIONS</h3>

<ul>

<li>
<b>Urbanization</b>

<p>
The process where increasing numbers of people move from rural areas into cities, causing urban growth and expansion.
</p>
</li>

<li>
<b>Migration</b>

<p>
The movement of people from one place to another for economic, social, political, or environmental reasons.
</p>
</li>

<li>
<b>Industrial City</b>

<p>
A city whose growth is mainly driven by factories, industrial production, and wage labor opportunities.
</p>
</li>

<li>
<b>Infrastructure</b>

<p>
The physical systems that support society such as roads, housing, electricity, sanitation, transport, and communication.
</p>
</li>

<li>
<b>Working Class</b>

<p>
People who earn wages by selling labor, especially in factories and industrial systems.
</p>
</li>

<li>
<b>Urban Economy</b>

<p>
An economy centered around trade, services, manufacturing, and industrial production within cities.
</p>
</li>

<li>
<b>Overcrowding</b>

<p>
A condition where too many people live in a limited urban space, often creating poor living conditions.
</p>
</li>

<li>
<b>Sanitation</b>

<p>
Systems designed to maintain public health through waste management, clean water, and hygiene infrastructure.
</p>
</li>

</ul>

<h3> WHY PEOPLE MOVED TO CITIES</h3>

<p>
Urbanization happened because cities offered:
</p>

<ul>

<li> Jobs</li>

<li> Factory wages</li>

<li> Trade opportunities</li>

<li> Transportation access</li>

<li> Education systems</li>

<li> Medical services</li>

</ul>

<p>
At the same time,
many rural areas experienced:
</p>

<ul>

<li>Limited farmland</li>

<li>Poverty</li>

<li>Agricultural mechanization</li>

<li>Population pressure</li>

<li>Economic hardship</li>

</ul>

<p>
This created:
</p>

<pre>
Push Factors
(rural hardship)

+

Pull Factors
(city opportunities)
</pre>

<p>
which accelerated migration into cities.
</p>

<h3> HOW URBANIZATION WORKS</h3>

<p><b>Step 1: Industrial Development</b></p>

<p>
Factories and industries develop inside cities.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2: Job Concentration</b></p>

<p>
Large numbers of jobs become available in urban centers.
</p>

<p><b>Step 3: Rural Push Factors</b></p>

<p>
Agricultural limitations and poverty push people out of rural areas.
</p>

<p><b>Step 4: Mass Migration</b></p>

<p>
People move into cities seeking:
</p>

<ul>

<li>Employment</li>

<li>Higher income</li>

<li>Better survival opportunities</li>

</ul>

<p><b>Step 5: Rapid City Expansion</b></p>

<p>
Cities grow rapidly and require:
</p>

<ul>

<li>Housing</li>

<li>Roads</li>

<li>Transport systems</li>

<li>Water systems</li>

<li>Sanitation infrastructure</li>

