-- Topic: history -> space_race -> Space Race: Technological Competition Between Superpowers
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'history',
  'space_race',
  'Space Race: Technological Competition Between Superpowers',
  '
<h2> Space Race: USA vs USSR Technological Competition</h2>
<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<p>The Space Race (approximately 1957–1975) was one of the most important technological competitions in modern history. It occurred during the Cold War between the United States (USA) and the Soviet Union (USSR). Although it focused on space exploration, it was deeply connected to political power, military strength, scientific advancement, and global influence.</p>
<p>After World War II, both superpowers wanted to prove that their political and economic systems were superior. The Soviet Union promoted communism, while the United States promoted capitalism and democracy. Space achievements became symbolic demonstrations of national intelligence, engineering power, and future potential.</p>
<p>The Space Race accelerated technological development faster than almost any previous competition in history. It led to advances in satellites, computers, telecommunications, rocket engineering, weather forecasting, navigation systems, and modern space exploration.</p>

<p>The Space Race transformed space into a new arena of global competition and permanently changed science, technology, and communication systems worldwide.</p>
<h3> DEEPER HISTORICAL BACKGROUND</h3>
<p>The origins of the Space Race began after World War II when both the USA and USSR captured German rocket technology and scientists. During the war, Germany had developed advanced missile systems such as the V-2 rocket. After the war, both superpowers used this knowledge to build long-range missiles and space rockets.</p>
<p>At first, rocket development was mainly military. Intercontinental ballistic missiles (ICBMs) were designed to carry nuclear weapons across long distances. However, the same rocket technology could also launch satellites and humans into space.</p>
<p>This meant the Space Race was closely linked to nuclear strategy and military power. A country capable of sending rockets into space also had the ability to deliver powerful weapons across the world.</p>
<h3> DEFINITIONS (EXPANDED)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Space Race:</b> A Cold War competition between the United States and Soviet Union to achieve major space exploration milestones and demonstrate technological superiority.</li>
<li><b>Satellite:</b> A human-made object placed into orbit around Earth to collect information, provide communication, navigation, or scientific observation.</li>
<li><b>Orbit:</b> The curved path followed by an object around a planet due to gravitational force.</li>
<li><b>Rocket:</b> A vehicle that produces thrust by expelling gas at high speed, allowing movement through the atmosphere and space.</li>
<li><b>ICBM (Intercontinental Ballistic Missile):</b> A long-range missile capable of carrying nuclear weapons across continents.</li>
<li><b>Apollo Program:</b> The United States space program that successfully landed humans on the Moon.</li>
<li><b>Sputnik:</b> The first artificial satellite launched into space by the Soviet Union in 1957.</li>
<li><b>Cosmonaut:</b> A Soviet/Russian astronaut trained for space missions.</li>
<li><b>Astronaut:</b> A space traveler trained by the United States or other countries for missions beyond Earth.</li>
<li><b>NASA:</b> The National Aeronautics and Space Administration, the US agency responsible for space exploration and research.</li>
</ul>
<h3> MAJOR SPACE EXPLORATION MILESTONES</h3>
<h4> Early Space Age (1957–1965)</h4>
<ul>
<li> 1957 — Sputnik 1 becomes the first artificial satellite (USSR)</li>
<li> 1957 — Laika becomes the first living creature to orbit Earth</li>
<li>‍ 1961 — Yuri Gagarin becomes the first human in space</li>
<li> 1965 — First spacewalk (Alexei Leonov)</li>
</ul>
<h4> Moon Landing Era (1969–1972)</h4>
<ul>
<li> 1969 — <b>Apollo 11</b>: <b>Neil Armstrong</b> becomes the first human to walk on the Moon</li>
<li>‍ 1969 — <b>Buzz Aldrin</b> follows Armstrong onto the lunar surface</li>
<li> Mission Control: “That’s one small step for man…” becomes a global historical moment</li>
<li> 1972 — <b>Eugene Cernan</b> (Apollo 17) becomes the last human to walk on the Moon</li>
</ul>
<h4> Expansion of Space Systems (1970s–2000s)</h4>
<ul>
<li> Global satellite networks expand communication, navigation, and weather systems</li>
<li> Space Shuttle program enables reusable spacecraft missions</li>
<li> International Space Station (ISS) begins long-term human presence in orbit</li>
</ul>
<h4> Modern Exploration Era (2000s–Today)</h4>
<ul>
<li> Mars rovers (Spirit, Opportunity, Curiosity, Perseverance) explore planetary surfaces</li>
<li> James Webb Space Telescope reveals deep-universe galaxies and early cosmic structures</li>
<li> Private space companies (SpaceX, Blue Origin) reshape launch and orbital access</li>
<li> Artemis Program prepares return of humans to the Moon</li>
<li> Ongoing exploration of Mars, Jupiter’s moons, and deep space continues</li>
</ul>
<h4> CORE IDEA (DEEP STRUCTURE)</h4>
<pre>
Human curiosity → Technological breakthroughs → Space access expansion → Global systems integration → Deep space exploration
</pre>
<h4> TODAY’S SPACE REALITY</h4>
<ul>
<li> Space is now part of daily life (GPS, internet, weather systems)</li>
<li> Space is no longer only state-driven — private + public systems coexist</li>
<li> Focus has shifted from “reaching space” → “living and expanding in space”</li>
</ul>
<h3> KEY IDEA (DEEP CORE PRINCIPLE)</h3>
<pre>The Space Race proved that technological innovation can become a form of geopolitical power, where scientific achievement influences global influence, military strength, and ideological prestige.</pre>
<p>The competition was not only about reaching space—it was about controlling the future through science and advanced technology.</p>
<h3> HOW THE SPACE RACE WORKED (SYSTEM MODEL)</h3>
<p><b>Step 1: Post-WWII Rivalry</b><br>After World War II, the USA and USSR emerged as rival superpowers competing for global influence.</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Rocket Technology Development</b><br>Both nations invested heavily in missile and rocket engineering using captured German research.</p>
<p><b>Step 3: Sputnik Launch (1957)</b><br>The USSR launched Sputnik, the first artificial satellite, shocking the world and intensifying competition.</p>
<p><b>Step 4: Human Spaceflight</b><br>The USSR achieved another milestone when Yuri Gagarin became the first human in space in 1961.</p>
<p><b>Step 5: US Technological Response</b><br>The USA increased investment in science, engineering, education, and NASA programs.</p>
<p><b>Step 6: Apollo Moon Missions</b><br>The Apollo Program culminated in Apollo 11 landing humans on the Moon in 1969.</p>
<p><b>Step 7: Satellite Expansion</b><br>Space technology expanded into communication, military observation, navigation, and weather systems.</p>
<p><b>Step 8: International Cooperation</b><br>Later missions involved cooperation between nations, leading to projects like the International Space Station (ISS).</p>

<h3> SCIENTIFIC & TECHNOLOGICAL IMPACT</h3>
<p>The Space Race accelerated multiple scientific fields:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Computer science:</b> Space missions required faster and smaller computers.</li>
<li><b>Materials science:</b> Engineers developed heat-resistant and lightweight materials.</li>
<li><b>Telecommunications:</b> Satellites improved global communication systems.</li>
<li><b>Meteorology:</b> Weather satellites improved forecasting accuracy.</li>
<li><b>Navigation:</b> GPS systems evolved from military satellite programs.</li>
<li><b>Medicine:</b> Space research improved understanding of the human body under extreme conditions.</li>
</ul>
<h3> CONNECTION TO THE COLD WAR</h3>
<p>The Space Race was deeply tied to Cold War ideology:</p>
<pre>
Scientific Achievement
        ↓
National Prestige
        ↓
Political Influence
        ↓
Military Confidence
        ↓
Global Superpower Status
</pre>
<p>Each success in space became propaganda used to demonstrate national superiority.</p>
<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION (DEEP INSIGHT)</h3>
<ul>
<li> Modern smartphones depend on satellite systems for GPS and communication</li>
<li> Global internet, television, and communication networks use satellite infrastructure</li>
<li> Weather forecasting and climate monitoring rely on space observation systems</li>
<li>
 Modern space exploration is now driven by private and national organizations such as 
SpaceX (commercial rockets and Mars missions), 
Blue Origin (space tourism and reusable rockets), 
Rocket Lab (small satellite launches), 
and NASA (global space research and exploration leadership).
</li>
<li> STEM education expanded worldwide because governments prioritized scientific innovation after Sputnik</li>
<li> Space technology now supports banking systems, navigation, agriculture, disaster monitoring, and global logistics</li>
</ul>
<h3> FINAL INSIGHT</h3>
<pre>The Space Race transformed humanity’s relationship with technology and space, proving that scientific innovation can reshape global power, communication, and civilization itself.</pre>
<p>It marked the moment humans moved from being Earth-bound civilizations to becoming a species capable of exploring beyond its own planet.</p>
',
  '[[{"q":"What was the Cold War?","steps":["Step 1: Identify the main rivals (USA and USSR)","Step 2: Note absence of direct military conflict","Step 3: Define the type of competition involved"],"ans":"A period of political and ideological tension between the USA and USSR without direct warfare","why":"The conflict was fought through influence, alliances, and indirect confrontations"},{"q":"What was the Space Race?","steps":["Step 1: Identify the competing superpowers (USA and USSR)","Step 2: List major space achievements","Step 3: Define the nature of competition"],"ans":"A competition between the USA and USSR to achieve major space exploration milestones","why":"It was part of Cold War rivalry expressed through technological achievement"},{"q":"Why was the Space Race important beyond science?","steps":["Step 1: Identify technological achievements","Step 2: Link achievements to global prestige","Step 3: Recognize ideological competition between superpowers"],"ans":"Because it demonstrated political and ideological superiority between superpowers","why":"Space achievements were used as symbols of national power and influence"},{"q":"What are proxy wars?","steps":["Step 1: Identify major powers involved indirectly","Step 2: Observe smaller nations as battlegrounds","Step 3: Explain external influence on conflicts"],"ans":"Conflicts where major powers support opposing sides indirectly in smaller wars","why":"Superpowers avoid direct war but compete through influence in other regions"},{"q":"Why do global systems become interconnected?","steps":["Step 1: Identify expansion of trade and markets","Step 2: Recognize role of technology and communication","Step 3: Explain formation of global networks"],"ans":"Because trade, technology, and communication connect countries into shared systems","why":"Efficiency, access, and profit drive increasing global interdependence"}]]'
);

-- Topic: history -> decolonization -> Decolonization: Independence Movements in Asia and Africa
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'history',
  'decolonization',
  'Decolonization: Independence Movements in Asia and Africa',
  '
<h2> Decolonization: End of Colonial Rule and Independence Movements</h2>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<p>Decolonization refers to the global process through which colonized regions in Africa, Asia, the Caribbean, and the Pacific gained independence from European imperial powers, mainly after World War II (1945–1975, with some later cases).</p>
<p>It was not a single event but a worldwide political transformation shaped by weakening European empires, rising nationalist movements, international pressure from organizations like the United Nations, and Cold War competition between the United States and the Soviet Union</p>
<p>Decolonization also went beyond political independence. It involved rebuilding national identity, redefining borders, restoring cultures, reforming education systems, and restructuring economies that had been shaped by colonial rule for decades or centuries.</p>
<p>Decolonization reshaped the world map and marked the transition from empires to a global system of sovereign nation-states.</p>
<h3> DEEPER HISTORICAL CONTEXT</h3>
<p>Before decolonization, much of the world was controlled by European empires such as Britain, France, Spain, Portugal, and Belgium. These empires controlled land, resources, trade routes, and political systems across continents.</p>
<p>However, World War II severely weakened these empires. European countries faced economic collapse, military exhaustion, and loss of global authority. At the same time, colonized populations had contributed heavily to the war effort and became more politically aware and organized.</p>
<p>This combination created conditions where colonial control became increasingly difficult to maintain.</p>
<h3> DEFINITIONS (EXPANDED)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Decolonization:</b> The process by which colonies gain independence from foreign control and become sovereign nations.</li>
<li><b>Colonialism:</b> A system where one country dominates another region politically, economically, and culturally, often for resource extraction.</li>
<li><b>Nationalism:</b> A belief that people with shared identity, culture, or history should govern themselves as an independent nation.</li>
<li><b>Self-determination:</b> The principle that people have the right to choose their own government and political future.</li>
<li><b>Post-colonial state:</b> A country that has recently gained independence and is building its political, economic, and social systems.</li>
<li><b>Independence movement:</b> A political struggle or organized effort aimed at ending colonial rule.</li>
</ul>
<h3> KEY IDEA (DEEP CORE PRINCIPLE)</h3>
<pre>Decolonization was a global shift of power from imperial empires to independent nation-states, reshaping political boundaries, identities, and global relations.</pre>
<p>However, independence did not automatically remove the long-term economic, cultural, and institutional effects of colonial rule.</p>
<h3> HOW DECOLONIZATION WORKED (SYSTEM MODEL)</h3>
<p><b>Step 1: Weakening of Colonial Powers</b><br>World War II weakened European economies and military control over colonies.</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Rise of Political Awareness</b><br>Educated elites and local leaders began organizing resistance and independence movements.</p>
<p><b>Step 3: Formation of Liberation Movements</b><br>Political parties, unions, and armed groups demanded self-rule and independence.</p>
<p><b>Step 4: Global Support for Independence</b><br>The United Nations promoted self-determination as a global principle.</p>
<p><b>Step 5: Cold War Involvement</b><br>The USA and USSR supported different movements to gain influence in newly emerging nations.</p>
<p><b>Step 6: Negotiation or Conflict</b><br>Some countries achieved independence peacefully, others through prolonged struggle.</p>
<p><b>Step 7: Independence Achieved</b><br>New nations formed governments, constitutions, and national identities.</p>
<p><b>Step 8: Post-Colonial Development</b><br>New states focused on economic growth, education, infrastructure, and political stability.</p>

<h3> MAJOR GLOBAL EFFECTS OF DECOLONIZATION</h3>
<ul>
<li> Creation of dozens of new independent countries across Africa and Asia</li>
<li> Redrawing of global political maps and borders</li>
<li> Emergence of new international relationships and diplomacy systems</li>
<li> Long-term economic inequality debates linked to colonial history</li>
<li> Cultural revival movements reclaiming indigenous identity and history</li>
<li> Expansion of the United Nations membership and global representation</li>
</ul>
<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION (DEEP INSIGHT)</h3>
<ul>
<li> Modern African and Asian states’ borders largely originate from decolonization-era negotiations and colonial boundaries</li>
<li> Global inequality discussions often connect back to colonial economic systems and post-colonial development challenges</li>
<li> Many modern constitutions and legal systems were shaped during the post-independence period</li>
<li> Identity movements today often focus on reclaiming cultural history lost or altered during colonial rule</li>
<li> International aid, trade agreements, and development policies still reflect historical power imbalances</li>
</ul>
<h3> FINAL INSIGHT</h3>
<pre>Decolonization was not just the collapse of empires—it was a global restructuring of political power, identity, and economic systems that created the modern world of independent nation-states while leaving behind long-lasting historical consequences.</pre>
',
  '[{"q":"What is decolonization?","hint":"end of colonial rule","steps":["Step 1: Identify colonial system and foreign control over territories","Step 2: Observe resistance and independence movements within colonies","Step 3: Track withdrawal or loss of control by colonial powers","Step 4: Define the transition into self-governing states"],"ans":"The process where colonized territories gain independence and end foreign political control","why":"It reshaped global political boundaries and created many modern nation-states"},{"q":"Why did decolonization accelerate after World War II?","hint":"weakening of empires","steps":["Step 1: Identify weakened European powers after WWII","Step 2: Recognize rise of independence movements in colonies","Step 3: Observe international pressure for self-determination","Step 4: Link to rapid political transitions in Asia, Africa, and elsewhere"],"ans":"Because European colonial powers were weakened and independence movements gained momentum","why":"World War II disrupted imperial control and increased demands for self-rule"},{"q":"What forces drive independence movements?","hint":"identity + resistance","steps":["Step 1: Identify national identity and cultural awareness","Step 2: Observe economic and political dissatisfaction under colonial rule","Step 3: Recognize leadership and organized resistance","Step 4: Link to collective demand for sovereignty"],"ans":"National identity, resistance to foreign control, and desire for self-governance","why":"People seek control over their political, economic, and cultural systems"},{"q":"How did decolonization change global power structures?","hint":"shift of authority","steps":["Step 1: Identify decline of European empires","Step 2: Observe emergence of new independent states","Step 3: Recognize shift in global political influence","Step 4: Link to new international organizations and alliances"],"ans":"It shifted power from colonial empires to newly independent nation-states","why":"The global system moved from imperial dominance to a state-based international order"},{"q":"What challenges followed decolonization?","hint":"post-independence instability","steps":["Step 1: Identify newly independent states","Step 2: Observe political instability and weak institutions","Step 3: Recognize economic dependence on former colonial powers","Step 4: Link to internal conflict and development challenges"],"ans":"Political instability, economic dependency, and nation-building challenges","why":"New states had to build systems from structures shaped under colonial rule"}]'
);

-- Topic: history -> globalization -> Globalization: The Modern Connected World System
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'history',
  'globalization',
  'Globalization: The Modern Connected World System',
  '
<h2> Globalization: The Modern Connected World System</h2>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<p>
Globalization is the ongoing process through which countries become increasingly interconnected through trade, technology, communication, finance, migration, and cultural exchange. While it existed in earlier forms (e.g., Silk Road, colonial trade), it dramatically accelerated after World War II and especially after the Cold War with the rise of digital technology and global markets.
</p>
<p>
In the modern world, globalization means that economies, cultures, and political systems are no longer isolated. Instead, they function as part of a single, interconnected global network where decisions in one region can rapidly influence outcomes across the entire world.
</p>
<p>
Globalization is not only economic—it also includes cultural blending, technological diffusion, environmental interdependence, and shared global challenges such as climate change and pandemics.
</p>
<pre>
Drivers of Globalization:
Trade Liberalization + Technology + Internet + Transport + Finance + Migration
Global Structure:
Countries → Corporations → Institutions → Digital Networks → Global System
Outcome:
Interconnected World → Interdependence → Rapid Information Flow → Shared Global Risks + Opportunities
</pre>
<h3> DEFINITIONS (EXPANDED)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Globalization:</b> The process of increasing integration and interconnection between countries through trade, technology, culture, and communication systems.</li>
<li><b>Interdependence:</b> A condition where countries, economies, or systems rely on each other for goods, services, information, or resources.</li>
<li><b>Multinational company:</b> A business organization that operates, produces, or sells goods and services in multiple countries.</li>
<li><b>Digital globalization:</b> The global flow of data, information, and services through digital platforms such as the internet and mobile networks.</li>
<li><b>Cultural globalization:</b> The spread and blending of cultural ideas, values, music, food, and lifestyles across borders.</li>
</ul>
<h3> KEY IDEA (DEEP CORE PRINCIPLE)</h3>
<pre>
Globalization has transformed the world from separate national systems into one interconnected network where economic, technological, and cultural events are interdependent and rapidly transmitted across borders.
</pre>
<p>
This means local events can become global events within seconds, and global systems continuously shape local realities.
</p>
<h3> COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS (DEEPER EXPLANATION)</h3>
<ul>
<li> “Globalization benefits everyone equally”
<p><b>Reality:</b> Benefits are uneven; some regions and groups gain more economic and technological advantages than others.</p></li>
<li> “Cultures become identical in globalization”
<p><b>Reality:</b> While some cultural blending occurs, many societies also strengthen local identities in response to global influence.</p></li>
<li> “Globalization is only about trade”
<p><b>Reality:</b> It also includes politics, migration, communication systems, environmental impact, and cultural exchange.</p></li>
<li> “Countries are independent in decision-making”
<p><b>Reality:</b> Most countries are influenced by global markets, institutions, and supply chains.</p></li>
</ul>
<h3> HOW GLOBALIZATION WORKS (SYSTEM MODEL)</h3>
<p><b>Step 1: Technological Advancement</b><br>
Improvements in communication (internet, satellites) and transport (air travel, shipping) reduce global distance.</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Trade Expansion</b><br>
Countries begin specializing in production and trading goods globally.</p>
<p><b>Step 3: Rise of Multinational Corporations</b><br>
Companies expand operations across borders, linking economies.</p>
<p><b>Step 4: Financial Integration</b><br>
Global banking, investments, and markets connect economies in real time.</p>
<p><b>Step 5: Cultural Exchange</b><br>
Ideas, entertainment, food, and lifestyles spread across nations.</p>
<p><b>Step 6: Digital Transformation</b><br>
Information flows instantly through digital platforms and networks.</p>
<p><b>Step 7: Global Interdependence</b><br>
Countries rely on each other for resources, production, and services.</p>
<p><b>Step 8: Shared Global Challenges</b><br>
Issues like climate change, pandemics, and economic crises affect all nations simultaneously.</p>
<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION (DEEP INSIGHT)</h3>
<ul>
<li> Smartphones are built using components from multiple countries (global supply chains).</li>
<li> Products in shops are often designed, manufactured, and assembled across different continents.</li>
<li> Global financial markets influence local prices, inflation, and employment.</li>
<li> Cultural exchange is visible in music, fashion, food, and media consumed worldwide.</li>
<li> Global crises (pandemics, climate change) require coordinated international responses.</li>
</ul>
<h3> FINAL INSIGHT</h3>
<pre>
Globalization has created a single interconnected human system where economies, cultures, and technologies continuously interact, making local events global and global events local within moments.
</pre>
<h3> QUIZ QUESTIONS</h3>
',
  '[{"q":"What is globalization?","hint":"world connection","steps":["Step 1: Identify connection between countries","Step 2: Observe flows of trade, money, culture, people, and technology","Step 3: Recognize increasing interdependence between nations","Step 4: Define the system-level integration of the world"],"ans":"The process of increasing economic, cultural, technological, and political interconnection between countries and societies","why":"It transforms the world into an interdependent system where actions in one region affect others globally"},{"q":"Why is globalization considered a system rather than just trade?","hint":"interconnected networks","steps":["Step 1: Identify multiple linked components (economies, media, transport, internet)","Step 2: Observe continuous interactions between these components","Step 3: Recognize feedback effects across countries","Step 4: Understand the global network structure"],"ans":"Because it is a network of interconnected systems including trade, communication, culture, and technology","why":"Globalization operates through multiple interacting systems, not just the exchange of goods"},{"q":"How does globalization change economies?","hint":"interdependence","steps":["Step 1: Identify global supply chains","Step 2: Observe cross-border production and labor distribution","Step 3: Recognize dependence between countries for goods and services","Step 4: Link to price, employment, and market changes"],"ans":"It creates interdependent economies connected through global supply chains and markets","why":"Countries now rely on each other for production, resources, and consumption"},{"q":"What role does technology play in globalization?","hint":"speed and connection","steps":["Step 1: Identify communication technologies (internet, smartphones, satellites)","Step 2: Observe real-time global communication","Step 3: Recognize reduction of distance and time barriers","Step 4: Link to faster cultural and economic exchange"],"ans":"Technology enables instant communication and coordination across the world","why":"Digital systems remove barriers of distance, making global interaction immediate"},{"q":"What are the main effects of globalization on societies?","hint":"change + inequality","steps":["Step 1: Identify cultural exchange and diffusion","Step 2: Observe economic growth and competition","Step 3: Recognize inequality between regions","Step 4: Link to social and cultural transformation"],"ans":"It increases cultural exchange, economic growth, and interdependence but also creates inequality","why":"Global systems distribute benefits unevenly across regions and populations"},{"q":"Why does globalization increase interdependence?","hint":"shared systems","steps":["Step 1: Identify shared production systems","Step 2: Observe reliance on global trade and resources","Step 3: Recognize shared risks and benefits","Step 4: Link to global coordination needs"],"ans":"Because countries rely on each other for goods, services, resources, and stability","why":"No single country is fully self-sufficient in a global system"}]'
);

-- Topic: history -> digital_revolution -> Digital Revolution: Internet, Computers, and Information Age
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'history',
  'digital_revolution',
  'Digital Revolution: Internet, Computers, and Information Age',
  '
<h2> Digital Revolution: Internet, Computers, and Information Age</h2>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<p>
The Digital Revolution is the ongoing transformation of human society from analog, mechanical, and industrial systems into digital, data-driven systems powered by computers, the internet, mobile devices, and artificial intelligence.
</p>
<p>
Unlike earlier revolutions (Agricultural, Industrial), the Digital Revolution is based on information as the central resource. Instead of land, labor, or machines being the most important assets, data and computation have become the primary drivers of power, innovation, and global influence.
</p>
<p>
This revolution has reshaped communication, education, economics, governance, culture, and even human identity by creating a world where nearly every activity can be recorded, processed, and optimized through digital systems.
</p>
<pre>
Technological Evolution:
Analog Systems → Early Computers → Internet → Mobile Devices → Cloud Computing → Artificial Intelligence
Core Transformation:
Physical Processes → Digital Information Systems
Global Outcome:
Information Society → Data Economy → AI Integration → Hyper-Connected World
</pre>
<h3> DEFINITIONS (EXPANDED)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Digital Revolution:</b> A major shift in human civilization where analog and mechanical systems are replaced by digital technologies that process, store, and transmit information electronically.</li>
<li><b>Internet:</b> A global network of interconnected computers that allows instant communication, data sharing, and access to information worldwide.</li>
<li><b>Artificial Intelligence (AI):</b> Computer systems designed to perform tasks that normally require human intelligence, such as learning, reasoning, decision-making, and pattern recognition.</li>
<li><b>Data:</b> Raw information collected from digital interactions, sensors, and systems that can be analyzed to produce insights.</li>
<li><b>Cloud computing:</b> The delivery of computing services (storage, processing, software) over the internet instead of local machines.</li>
<li><b>Algorithm:</b> A set of instructions used by computers to process data and make decisions or predictions.</li>
</ul>
<h3> KEY IDEA (DEEP CORE PRINCIPLE)</h3>
<pre>
The Digital Revolution transformed information into the most powerful global resource, where data flows determine economic power, social influence, and technological progress.
</pre>
<p>
Control over information systems now often equals control over modern societies, economies, and even political outcomes.
</p>
<h3> COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS (DEEPER EXPLANATION)</h3>
<ul>
<li> “The internet is just communication”
<p><b>Reality:</b> It is a full economic, political, educational, and cultural infrastructure shaping nearly all modern systems.</p></li>
<li> “Technology is neutral”
<p><b>Reality:</b> Digital systems influence behavior through algorithms, recommendation systems, and data control.</p></li>
<li> “AI is only future technology”
<p><b>Reality:</b> AI is already integrated into search engines, social media, healthcare, finance, and education systems.</p></li>
<li> “Data is meaningless”
<p><b>Reality:</b> Data is the foundation of modern decision-making, business strategy, and governance systems.</p></li>
</ul>
<h3> HOW THE DIGITAL REVOLUTION WORKS (SYSTEM MODEL)</h3>
<p><b>Step 1: Digitization of Information</b><br>
Physical and analog data (books, records, signals) are converted into digital formats.</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Computerization</b><br>
Machines begin processing information faster and more accurately than humans.</p>
<p><b>Step 3: Internet Connectivity</b><br>
Computers are connected globally, enabling instant communication and data exchange.</p>
<p><b>Step 4: Mobile Revolution</b><br>
Smartphones make digital access personal, portable, and continuous.</p>
<p><b>Step 5: Data Economy Emergence</b><br>
Companies and governments begin using data to predict behavior and optimize decisions.</p>
<p><b>Step 6: Platform Systems</b><br>
Large digital platforms (social media, e-commerce) centralize information flow.</p>
<p><b>Step 7: Artificial Intelligence Integration</b><br>
Machines begin learning from data and making autonomous decisions.</p>
<p><b>Step 8: Hyper-Connected Society</b><br>
Nearly all human activities become linked through digital systems.</p>
<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION (DEEP INSIGHT)</h3>
<ul>
<li> Smartphones act as personal information, communication, and economic tools.</li>
<li> Remote work and digital economies allow global labor participation.</li>
<li> AI systems assist in medical diagnosis, education, finance, and creative industries.</li>
<li> Governments use digital data for planning, security, and service delivery.</li>
<li> Social media influences culture, politics, and global communication patterns in real time.</li>
</ul>
<h3> FINAL INSIGHT</h3>
<pre>
The Digital Revolution has redefined power in the modern world by making information, data, and algorithms the central forces shaping economies, societies, and human behavior.
</pre>
<h3> QUIZ QUESTIONS</h3>
',
  '[{"q":"What is the Digital Revolution?","hint":"technology shift","steps":["Step 1: Identify shift from analog to digital systems","Step 2: Recognize key technologies (computers, internet, mobile devices)","Step 3: Observe transformation of communication, work, and data systems","Step 4: Understand global connectivity and information flow increase"],"ans":"The transition from analog systems to digital, computer-based technologies that transformed global communication, work, and information systems","why":"It changed how humans store, process, and transmit information at global scale"},{"q":"Why is the Digital Revolution considered a system transformation?","hint":"structure change","steps":["Step 1: Identify old system (manual + analog processes)","Step 2: Introduce new digital infrastructure","Step 3: Observe changes in speed, scale, and access to information","Step 4: Recognize restructuring of economy and society"],"ans":"Because it replaced analog systems with digital networks that reshaped how society functions","why":"It changed the underlying infrastructure of communication, economy, and knowledge"},{"q":"How did the Digital Revolution change the nature of information?","hint":"data becomes power","steps":["Step 1: Identify information before digitization (slow, physical, limited access)","Step 2: Observe digital storage and instant transmission","Step 3: Recognize global accessibility of data","Step 4: Link information to power and decision-making"],"ans":"Information became digital, instantly accessible, and globally shared","why":"Data became a central resource that influences decisions, markets, and behavior"},{"q":"Why did the internet accelerate global change?","hint":"connection speed","steps":["Step 1: Identify global communication barriers before the internet","Step 2: Introduce instant digital communication networks","Step 3: Observe reduction in distance and time limitations","Step 4: Recognize acceleration of trade, ideas, and culture"],"ans":"Because it enabled instant global communication and connection","why":"It removed traditional barriers of distance and time in human interaction"},{"q":"What is the long-term impact of the Digital Revolution on society?","hint":"structural change","steps":["Step 1: Identify changes in jobs and economy (automation, digital work)","Step 2: Observe shifts in communication and culture","Step 3: Recognize rise of AI, platforms, and data-driven systems","Step 4: Link to ongoing transformation of human behavior"],"ans":"It permanently transformed economy, communication, culture, and human behavior","why":"Society now operates through interconnected digital systems rather than physical-only structures"}]'
);

-- Topic: history -> global_challenges -> Global Challenges: Climate Change, Inequality, and Migration
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'history',
  'global_challenges',
  'Global Challenges: Climate Change, Inequality, and Migration',
  '
<h2> Global Challenges: Climate Change, Inequality, and Migration</h2>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<p>
Modern globalization has created a deeply interconnected world where economic activity, technology, production, and consumption in one region directly influence conditions in other regions. While this system has increased wealth, innovation, and access to goods, it has also intensified global challenges such as climate change, inequality, resource depletion, and migration pressures.
</p>
<p>
At its core, globalization creates a feedback loop:
human demand increases production → production increases resource use → resource use impacts environment and society → those impacts reshape human systems again.
</p>
<pre>
Industrial Growth + Global Consumption
        ↓
High Energy Use + Resource Extraction
        ↓
Environmental Pressure + Pollution + Inequality
        ↓
Climate Change + Social Displacement + Migration
</pre>
<h3> KEY GLOBAL CHALLENGES</h3>
<h4> 1. Climate Change</h4>
<p>
Climate change refers to long-term shifts in global temperature and weather patterns caused largely by human activities such as burning fossil fuels, deforestation, and industrial production.
It is not limited to one country because the atmosphere is shared globally.
</p>
<h4> 2. Economic Inequality</h4>
<p>
Economic inequality refers to the unequal distribution of wealth, income, and opportunities between individuals, regions, and countries.
Global trade and industrial systems often concentrate wealth in specific regions while others remain underdeveloped.
</p>
<h4> 3. Migration Pressure</h4>
<p>
Migration increases when people move due to economic opportunity, conflict, climate stress, or lack of resources.
Modern globalization accelerates migration by connecting economies but also exposing inequalities.
</p>
<h4> 4. Resource Depletion</h4>
<p>
Resource depletion occurs when natural resources such as water, forests, minerals, and fossil fuels are consumed faster than they can be replenished.
Global consumption patterns increase this pressure significantly.
</p>
<h3> DEFINITIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Climate change:</b> Long-term alteration of global weather patterns due to natural and human causes</li>
<li><b>Inequality:</b> Uneven distribution of wealth, power, and opportunities across populations</li>
<li><b>Migration:</b> Movement of people from one region or country to another for survival or opportunity</li>
<li><b>Resource depletion:</b> Reduction of natural resources due to overuse</li>
<li><b>Globalization:</b> Increasing interconnection of economies, cultures, and systems worldwide</li>
</ul>
<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Global systems generate both advancement and instability at the same time.
Progress in one area often creates pressure in another.
</pre>
<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li> Thinking climate change is purely natural without human influence</li>
<li> Believing inequality is caused only by individual effort rather than systems</li>
<li> Assuming migration happens for a single reason instead of multiple combined factors</li>
<li> Ignoring how global consumption affects local environments</li>
</ul>
<h3> HOW THESE PROBLEMS ARE CONNECTED</h3>
<p><b>Step 1: Industrial Expansion</b></p>
<p>Countries increase production to grow economies and meet global demand.</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Resource Extraction</b></p>
<p>Natural resources are heavily used to support industries and populations.</p>
<p><b>Step 3: Environmental Impact</b></p>
<p>Pollution, deforestation, and emissions increase climate instability.</p>
<p><b>Step 4: Social Consequences</b></p>
<p>Unequal development creates poverty in some regions and wealth in others.</p>
<p><b>Step 5: Human Movement</b></p>
<p>People migrate in response to environmental stress, war, or economic imbalance.</p>
<h3> HISTORICAL LINK</h3>
<p>
These challenges did not appear suddenly. They evolved from earlier systems:
</p>
<pre>
Industrial Revolution → Mass Production → Global Trade → High Consumption → Modern Global Challenges
</pre>
<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li> International climate agreements like COP meetings</li>
<li> Economic policies addressing global inequality and development aid</li>
<li> Refugee and migration systems shaped by global crises</li>
<li> Sustainable development strategies in industries and cities</li>
<li> Helps understand why global problems require global cooperation</li>
</ul>
<h3> FINAL INSIGHT</h3>
<pre>
Modern globalization creates both progress and serious shared problems. While technology and trade connect the world, they also intensify environmental and social pressures that require new forms of cooperation and responsibility.
</pre>

<h3> QUIZ QUESTIONS</h3>
',
  '[{"q":"What causes climate change?","hint":"human activity","steps":["Step 1: Identify major human activities (industry, transport, agriculture)","Step 2: Observe emission of greenhouse gases (CO₂, methane, etc.)","Step 3: Understand how gases accumulate in the atmosphere","Step 4: Link to energy imbalance in Earth''s climate system","Step 5: Observe long-term global temperature rise and weather pattern shifts"],"ans":"Human industrial activity that increases greenhouse gas concentrations in the atmosphere","why":"Greenhouse gases trap heat, disrupting Earth''s natural energy balance and causing long-term climate shifts"},{"q":"How do greenhouse gases affect Earth’s temperature?","hint":"heat trapping","steps":["Step 1: Solar energy enters Earth’s atmosphere","Step 2: Earth reflects part of this energy back as heat","Step 3: Greenhouse gases absorb and re-radiate this heat","Step 4: Heat becomes trapped, increasing overall temperature"],"ans":"They trap outgoing heat and increase Earth’s average temperature","why":"This process is known as the greenhouse effect and is intensified by human emissions"},{"q":"Why is climate change considered a system problem, not just an environmental one?","hint":"interconnected systems","steps":["Step 1: Identify environmental changes (temperature, rainfall, sea level)","Step 2: Link to economic systems (industry, energy, transport)","Step 3: Recognize social and political impacts (migration, conflict, policy)","Step 4: Understand feedback loops between human systems and nature"],"ans":"Because it is driven by and affects interconnected environmental, economic, and social systems","why":"Climate change is a feedback loop between human activity and Earth''s natural systems"},{"q":"Why do small increases in emissions lead to large long-term effects?","hint":"accumulation over time","steps":["Step 1: Identify continuous emission sources","Step 2: Understand atmospheric accumulation over time","Step 3: Recognize delayed system response","Step 4: Observe compounding long-term effects"],"ans":"Because emissions accumulate over time and have delayed but compounding effects","why":"Climate systems respond slowly, so small continuous changes build into large impacts"},{"q":"How does climate change affect human societies?","hint":"system disruption","steps":["Step 1: Identify environmental changes (droughts, floods, heatwaves)","Step 2: Observe impacts on food, water, and infrastructure","Step 3: Recognize economic pressure and migration patterns","Step 4: Link to political and social instability"],"ans":"It disrupts food systems, economies, infrastructure, and population stability","why":"Human societies depend on stable environmental conditions to function effectively"}]'
);

-- Topic: history -> systems_of_history -> Systems of History: Patterns, Cycles, and How Civilizations Really Work
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'history',
  'systems_of_history',
  'Systems of History: Patterns, Cycles, and How Civilizations Really Work',
  '
<h2> Systems of History: Patterns, Cycles, and How Civilizations Really Work</h2>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<p>
History is not just a collection of isolated events—it functions as an interconnected system shaped by recurring patterns in human behavior, resource availability, technological change, environmental limits, and power structures. When these factors interact over time, societies tend to follow similar developmental cycles, even across different continents and eras.
</p>
<p>
These cycles do not mean history repeats in an identical way, but rather that similar conditions often produce similar outcomes. Civilizations grow when systems are stable and resources are abundant, and they weaken when internal pressure, inequality, and external competition exceed their capacity to adapt.
</p>
<pre>
RESOURCE GROWTH + ORGANIZATION + INNOVATION
            ↓
        RISE PHASE
            ↓
   EXPANSION OF TERRITORY + TRADE
            ↓
   COMPLEXITY (ADMINISTRATION + CLASS SYSTEMS)
            ↓
CRISIS (INEQUALITY + CORRUPTION + RESOURCE STRAIN)
            ↓
 COLLAPSE OR TRANSFORMATION
            ↓
   NEW SYSTEM / NEW CIVILIZATION
</pre>

<h3> CORE COMPONENTS OF HISTORICAL SYSTEMS</h3>
<h4> 1. Resources</h4>
<p>
Every civilization depends on access to essential resources such as food, water, land, minerals, and energy. Resource abundance supports growth, while scarcity creates pressure that leads to migration, conflict, or innovation.
</p>
<h4> 2. Technology</h4>
<p>
Technology determines how efficiently a society can use its resources. Innovations such as farming tools, writing systems, steam engines, and digital systems increase productivity and complexity.
</p>
<h4> 3. Power Structures</h4>
<p>
Power structures refer to how authority is organized—whether through kings, empires, governments, corporations, or global institutions. These structures decide how resources and decisions are distributed.
</p>
<h4> 4. Human Behavior</h4>
<p>
Human psychology—fear, ambition, cooperation, competition, and belief systems—drives political decisions, revolutions, innovation, and conflict.
</p>
<h4> 5. Environment</h4>
<p>
Environmental conditions such as climate, geography, and natural disasters shape where civilizations grow and how stable they remain.
</p>
<h3> DEFINITIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>System:</b> A network of interconnected elements that influence each other to produce outcomes over time</li>
<li><b>Civilization cycle:</b> The repeating pattern of growth, peak development, crisis, and transformation in societies</li>
<li><b>Power structure:</b> The arrangement of authority, leadership, and control within a society</li>
<li><b>Complexity:</b> A stage where systems become large, interconnected, and harder to manage</li>
<li><b>Transformation:</b> A shift from one system to another rather than complete disappearance</li>
</ul>
<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
History follows patterns because human systems operate under constraints:
resources are limited, power concentrates, and complexity eventually creates pressure that demands change.
</pre
<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li> Thinking history is random or unpredictable chaos</li>
<li> Assuming technological progress automatically prevents collapse</li>
<li> Believing all civilizations collapse in the same way</li>
<li> Ignoring the role of inequality and resource distribution</li>
<li> Overlooking how internal instability is often more important than external invasion</li>
</ul>
<h3> DETAILED CYCLE BREAKDOWN</h3>
<p><b>1. Rise Phase</b></p>
<p>
A civilization begins forming stable systems such as agriculture, trade, governance, or technology. Population grows and cooperation increases.
</p>
<p><b>2. Expansion Phase</b></p>
<p>
The society expands geographically, economically, or culturally. Trade networks grow, armies strengthen, and influence spreads.
</p>
<p><b>3. Complexity Phase</b></p>
<p>
Institutions multiply. Bureaucracy grows. Social classes become more defined. Managing the system becomes harder.
</p>
<p><b>4. Crisis Phase</b></p>
<p>
Inequality increases, resources become strained, corruption rises, or external pressures intensify. Internal dissatisfaction grows.
</p>
<p><b>5. Collapse or Transformation</b></p>
<p>
The system either breaks down or reorganizes into a new structure. Sometimes this leads to a new empire, state, or civilization model.
</p>
<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li> Helps analyze modern nations using historical patterns</li>
<li> Explains economic cycles like boom and recession</li>
<li> Helps understand inequality and political instability</li>
<li> Useful in studying governance and institutional design</li>
<li> Builds systems thinking for economics, politics, and social science</li>
<li> Helps anticipate risks in global systems by recognizing early warning patterns</li>
</ul>
<h3> FINAL INSIGHT</h3>
<pre>
Understanding history as an interconnected system helps us see beyond isolated events. By recognizing recurring patterns in how societies develop, face crises, and transform, we gain insight into our own time and can make more informed decisions about the future.
</pre>
<h3> QUIZ QUESTIONS</h3>
',
  '[[{"q":"Why do civilizations rise and fall over time?","steps":["Step 1: Identify the growth phase (expansion and development)","Step 2: Recognize increasing complexity and internal strain","Step 3: Explain collapse or transformation due to unsustainability"],"ans":"Because of repeating cycles of growth, complexity, and eventual collapse or transformation","why":"Systems expand until pressure and inefficiency make them unsustainable"},{"q":"Why is it incorrect to study historical events in isolation?","steps":["Step 1: Identify connections between events","Step 2: Recognize cause-and-effect chains","Step 3: Explain system-wide interactions"],"ans":"Because events are interconnected within larger systems","why":"Isolated analysis ignores deeper causes and wider consequences"},{"q":"Why do systems become unstable over time?","steps":["Step 1: Identify growth and expansion of the system","Step 2: Recognize accumulation of inequality and inefficiency","Step 3: Link these to rising internal pressure"],"ans":"Because pressure, inequality, and inefficiency build up over time","why":"No system can sustain imbalance indefinitely"},{"q":"What is the difference between collapse and transformation in a system?","steps":["Step 1: Identify breakdown of the existing system","Step 2: Determine whether the system disappears or adapts","Step 3: Compare outcomes of collapse vs transformation"],"ans":"Collapse is total breakdown, while transformation is restructuring into a new system","why":"Most systems evolve rather than completely disappear"},{"q":"Why is adaptability essential for the survival of historical systems?","steps":["Step 1: Identify changing internal and external conditions","Step 2: Observe how systems respond to these changes","Step 3: Link flexibility to long-term survival"],"ans":"Because adaptable systems can adjust to change and survive longer","why":"Rigid systems fail when they cannot respond to new pressures"}]]'
);

-- Topic: history -> human_drivers -> Human Drivers System: The Core Engine of All History
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'history',
  'human_drivers',
  'Human Drivers System: The Core Engine of All History',
  '
<h2> Human Drivers System: The Core Engine of All History</h2>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<p>
All historical events can be understood as large-scale expressions of repeated human psychological needs interacting with environment, resources, and social systems. History is not random; it is the outcome of billions of individual decisions shaped by survival pressures, emotions, beliefs, and group dynamics.
</p>
<p>
When human needs are not fully satisfied, societies adapt through innovation, cooperation, competition, conflict, migration, or restructuring of systems. Over time, these individual motivations scale up into collective behavior, which then becomes what we call “history.”
</p>
<pre>
Human Psychological Needs
        ↓
Individual Decisions
        ↓
Group Behavior (crowds, societies, nations)
        ↓
Institutions & Power Systems
        ↓
Historical Events (wars, revolutions, trade, migration, innovation)
</pre>
<h3> CORE HUMAN DRIVERS (DEEPER EXPLANATION)</h3>
<h4> 1. Survival</h4>
<p>
The most basic driver of human behavior. Includes food, water, shelter, and physical safety. When survival is threatened, societies become more competitive, aggressive, or innovative.
</p>
<h4> 2. Status</h4>
<p>
Humans naturally seek recognition, influence, and dominance within groups. This creates hierarchies, leadership systems, empires, and political competition.
</p>
<h4> 3. Security</h4>
<p>
People and societies seek stability and predictability. Governments, laws, borders, and military systems are built to reduce uncertainty and protect against threats.
</p>
<h4> 4. Identity</h4>
<p>
Humans form meaning through belonging to groups such as nations, religions, ethnicities, or ideologies. Identity often becomes a powerful force in shaping alliances and conflicts.
</p>
<h4> 5. Belonging</h4>
<p>
People need connection with others. This creates families, tribes, communities, nations, and global networks. Social belonging influences cooperation and conflict behavior.
</p>
<h4> 6. Meaning</h4>
<p>
Humans seek purpose and explanation of existence. This leads to religion, philosophy, ideology, science, and cultural systems that shape civilizations.
</p>
<h3> DEFINITIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Human driver:</b> A fundamental psychological need that influences behavior at individual and collective levels</li>
<li><b>Collective behavior:</b> Actions of groups of people influenced by shared emotions and needs</li>
<li><b>Institution:</b> A structured system (government, religion, economy) created to manage human needs at scale</li>
<li><b>Ideology:</b> A system of beliefs that explains how society should function</li>
<li><b>Historical causation:</b> The underlying forces that produce historical events over time</li>
</ul>
<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
History is the large-scale expression of human needs interacting with constraints like resources, environment, and power structures.
</pre>
<h3> COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li> Thinking leaders alone create history without mass human behavior</li>
<li> Assuming events are purely logical rather than emotional and psychological</li>
<li> Believing economic or political systems operate independently of human needs</li>
<li> Ignoring how fear, identity, and belonging shape large-scale decisions</li>
</ul>
<h3> HOW HUMAN NEEDS CREATE HISTORY (STEP MODEL)</h3>
<p><b>Step 1: Internal Need Emerges</b></p>
<p>
A population experiences pressure: lack of resources, insecurity, inequality, or desire for status or meaning.
</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Decision-Making</b></p>
<p>
Individuals and leaders make choices to reduce pressure (e.g., migrate, trade, reform, fight, innovate).
</p>
<p><b>Step 3: Collective Alignment</b></p>
<p>
Many individuals begin making similar choices, forming movements, economies, or political systems.
</p>
<p><b>Step 4: Institutional Formation</b></p>
<p>
Structures like governments, empires, markets, or religions stabilize these collective behaviors.</p>
<p><b>Step 5: Historical Event</b></p>
<p>
The outcome becomes visible as war, revolution, migration wave, technological breakthrough, or collapse.
</p>
<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li> Explains modern political behavior and voting patterns</li>
<li> Helps understand workplace competition and leadership struggles</li>
<li> Explains migration, refugee flows, and urban growth</li>
<li> Useful in economics, sociology, and political science analysis</li>
<li> Helps design systems that reduce conflict by addressing human needs</li>
</ul>
<h3> FINAL INSIGHT</h3>
<pre>
Human drivers are the hidden engine of all history. Survival, status, security, identity, belonging, and meaning push people to create societies, technologies, empires, and cultures. When these drivers align in large-scale patterns, they produce everything from trade routes and cities to wars and revolutions. Recognizing these underlying motivations helps us understand why history unfolds the way it does and how we can shape a better future.
</pre>
<h3> QUIZ QUESTIONS</h3>
',
  '[[{"q":"What is the deepest cause of historical events?","steps":["Step 1: Identify human motivations (survival, power, status)","Step 2: Link individual motivations to group behavior","Step 3: Connect collective behavior to historical outcomes"],"ans":"Human needs and motivations driving collective behavior","why":"All major events originate from shared human psychological drivers"},{"q":"Why are human motivations more important than single events in history?","steps":["Step 1: Identify underlying motivations","Step 2: Distinguish between root causes and trigger events","Step 3: Recognize repeated patterns across time"],"ans":"Because motivations are the root causes, while events are only triggers","why":"The same human drives consistently produce similar outcomes in history"},{"q":"How do individual human needs turn into large historical events?","steps":["Step 1: Identify basic human needs (survival, security, status)","Step 2: Scale these needs across large populations","Step 3: Observe how collective actions form historical patterns"],"ans":"Individual needs scale into collective behavior that shapes events","why":"Mass actions emerge when many individuals act on similar motivations"},{"q":"Why do similar historical patterns repeat across different civilizations?","steps":["Step 1: Identify universal human needs and emotions","Step 2: Observe similar behaviors across different societies","Step 3: Link these behaviors to recurring outcomes"],"ans":"Because all humans share similar psychological drivers","why":"Shared human nature leads to repeating patterns across history"},{"q":"What happens when multiple human needs conflict at the same time?","steps":["Step 1: Identify competing needs (e.g., security vs freedom)","Step 2: Recognize the system cannot satisfy all needs simultaneously","Step 3: Observe resulting tension and instability"],"ans":"Conflict between needs creates tension and instability","why":"Systems break down when they cannot balance competing demands"}]]'
);

-- Topic: history -> power_formation -> Power Formation System: How Authority, Leadership, and Control Structures Emerge
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'history',
  'power_formation',
  'Power Formation System: How Authority, Leadership, and Control Structures Emerge',
  '
<h2> Power Formation System: How Authority and Control Are Built</h2>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<p>
Power in history is a structured outcome of human coordination under pressure. It does not appear fully formed; instead, it evolves gradually as groups attempt to solve survival, security, and cooperation problems. When chaos exists, humans naturally organize. Over time, this organization becomes stable authority, and eventually institutional systems that regulate entire societies.
</p>
<p>
Power systems are therefore not only political—they are psychological and social structures built to reduce uncertainty, manage conflict, and coordinate large populations efficiently. The stronger and more complex a society becomes, the more formalized its power structures must become.
</p>
<pre>
CHAOS / ANARCHY
        ↓
INFORMAL LEADERSHIP (influence, respect, skill)
        ↓
STRUCTURED AUTHORITY (rules, roles, hierarchy)
        ↓
INSTITUTIONS (government, religion, law, education)
        ↓
STABLE POWER SYSTEM (state, empire, organization networks)
        ↓
SELF-REINFORCING ORDER (culture + law + belief systems)
</pre>
<h3> CORE COMPONENTS OF POWER SYSTEMS</h3>
<h4> 1. Authority</h4>
<p>
Authority is the recognized right to make decisions for others. It only works when people accept it. Without acceptance, authority collapses into coercion or resistance.
</p>
<h4> 2. Leadership</h4>
<p>
Leadership is the ability to organize and influence group behavior toward shared goals. Leaders emerge naturally in times of uncertainty, crisis, or opportunity.
</p>
<h4> 3. Institutions</h4>
<p>
Institutions are stable systems of rules, roles, and procedures that outlive individual leaders. Examples include governments, schools, legal systems, and religious organizations.
</p>
<h4> 4. Legitimacy</h4>
<p>
Legitimacy is the belief that authority is justified and should be obeyed. It is the psychological foundation of stable power. Without legitimacy, enforcement becomes expensive and unstable.
</p>
<h4> 5. Coordination</h4>
<p>
Power systems exist to coordinate large groups efficiently. The more complex a society becomes, the more coordination it requires to avoid collapse or fragmentation.
</p>
<h3> DEFINITIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Authority:</b> Socially recognized right to make decisions and enforce rules</li>
<li><b>Leadership:</b> Ability to guide group behavior toward a shared outcome</li>
<li><b>Institution:</b> A stable system of rules and roles that organizes society over time</li>
<li><b>Legitimacy:</b> Collective belief that authority is valid and should be followed</li>
<li><b>Hierarchy:</b> Structured ranking system of power and responsibility</li>
<li><b>Coercion:</b> Enforcement of behavior through force or threat</li>
</ul>
<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Power systems emerge when human groups trade individual freedom for collective stability and coordination.
Legitimacy is the “invisible glue” that keeps authority stable.
</pre>
<h3> COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li> Thinking power is only based on force or violence</li>
<li> Believing leaders create systems without social support</li>
<li> Assuming authority exists without collective acceptance</li>
<li> Ignoring cultural and psychological foundations of obedience</li>
<li> Overestimating the stability of institutions without legitimacy</li>
</ul>
<h3> HOW POWER SYSTEMS FORM (DETAILED STEP MODEL)</h3>
<p><b>Step 1: System Pressure Emerges</b></p>
<p>
A group faces instability such as conflict, resource scarcity, or coordination failure.
</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Informal Leadership Appears</b></p>
<p>
Individuals who are skilled, persuasive, or strong begin influencing group decisions.
</p>
<p><b>Step 3: Coordination Improves Survival</b></p>
<p>
People follow leadership because it reduces chaos and improves survival outcomes.
</p>
<p><b>Step 4: Rules and Structure Develop</b></p>
<p>
Repeated coordination leads to formal rules, roles, and expectations.
</p>
<p><b>Step 5: Institutionalization</b></p>
<p>
Structures become permanent institutions that operate beyond individual leaders.
</p>
<p><b>Step 6: Legitimacy Stabilization</b></p>
<p>
Cultural beliefs, laws, and traditions reinforce acceptance of authority.
</p>
<p><b>Step 7: Self-Reinforcing System</b></p>
<p>
The system becomes stable because institutions, culture, and authority continuously support each other.
</p>
<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li> Explains formation and evolution of governments and states</li>
<li> Explains corporate leadership, management, and organizational design</li>
<li> Explains schools, religious institutions, and legal systems</li>
<li> Helps understand why hierarchies appear in all human societies</li>
<li> Useful in analyzing political stability and institutional collapse</li>
<li> Helps design better systems of governance and coordination</li>
</ul>
<h3> FINAL INSIGHT</h3>
<pre>
Power is not simply about control—it is a social technology that evolved to enable complex societies. By understanding how leadership, institutions, and legitimacy form together, we can see how societies coordinate, maintain order, and adapt over time. Power is never static; it constantly shifts between centralized systems and decentralized networks, reflecting humanity''s ongoing struggle to balance freedom with order.
</pre>
<h3> QUIZ QUESTIONS</h3>
',
  '[[{"q":"How does power typically originate in a society?","steps":["Step 1: Identify initial condition of disorder or need","Step 2: Recognize emergence of leadership for coordination","Step 3: Observe formation of structured systems"],"ans":"Power originates when leadership emerges to solve coordination and survival problems","why":"Groups organize under those who provide stability and solutions"},{"q":"What makes authority stable over time?","steps":["Step 1: Identify need for acceptance by the population","Step 2: Recognize role of institutions and rules","Step 3: Understand importance of legitimacy"],"ans":"Legitimacy supported by institutions","why":"Power lasts when people believe it is justified and accept it"},{"q":"Why is legitimacy more powerful than force alone?","steps":["Step 1: Force creates fear and resistance","Step 2: Legitimacy creates belief and trust","Step 3: Belief leads to voluntary compliance"],"ans":"Because legitimacy creates voluntary obedience rather than forced compliance","why":"Systems based on belief are more stable than those based only on force"},{"q":"How do power systems maintain control over large populations?","steps":["Step 1: Use institutions and laws for structure","Step 2: Influence beliefs through narratives and culture","Step 3: Apply enforcement when necessary"],"ans":"Through a combination of institutions, belief systems, and enforcement","why":"Control requires both psychological acceptance and structural organization"},{"q":"What happens when power becomes too concentrated?","steps":["Step 1: Identify extreme centralization of authority","Step 2: Observe rising inequality and dissatisfaction","Step 3: Recognize increased risk of instability or collapse"],"ans":"It leads to instability, resistance, or potential system collapse","why":"Excessive concentration creates imbalance and social tension"}]]'
);

-- Topic: history -> resource_control -> Resource Control System: How Wealth, Trade, and Scarcity Shape Civilizations
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'history',
  'resource_control',
  'Resource Control System: How Wealth, Trade, and Scarcity Shape Civilizations',
  '
<h2> Resource Control System: The Hidden Engine of Wealth and Power</h2>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<p>
Every civilization in history is fundamentally organized around one central reality: the control, distribution, and management of resources. Resources determine not only survival, but also the structure of economies, the rise of power systems, patterns of inequality, expansion of empires, and eventual collapse or transformation of societies.
</p>
<p>
Resources are not valuable in isolation—they become powerful when they are scarce, strategically important, and controlled by organized systems. Throughout history, shifts in dominant resources (land, metals, oil, and now data) have reshaped entire civilizations and global power structures.
</p>
<pre>
NATURAL RESOURCES + SCARCITY + HUMAN DEMAND
            ↓
     CONTROL SYSTEMS (ownership, empire, trade)
            ↓
     WEALTH ACCUMULATION
            ↓
     POWER STRUCTURES
            ↓
     POLITICAL + ECONOMIC INFLUENCE
            ↓
     GLOBAL SYSTEM DESIGN
</pre>
<h3> CORE CONCEPTS OF RESOURCE-BASED HISTORY</h3>
<h4> 1. Resource</h4>
<p>
A resource is anything that can be used to support human survival, production, or development. This includes physical resources (land, water, minerals), economic resources (money, labor), and modern digital resources (data, attention, algorithms).
</p>
<h4> 2. Scarcity</h4>
<p>
Scarcity means resources are limited. Scarcity creates competition, which leads to trade, conflict, innovation, and inequality.
</p>
<h4> 3. Control</h4>
<p>
Control refers to who has access to, ownership of, or influence over resources. Control determines who benefits from resource use.
</p>
<h4> 4. Wealth</h4>
<p>
Wealth is accumulated control over valuable resources. It is not just money—it represents stored access to energy, labor, and materials.
</p>
<h4> 5. Power</h4>
<p>
Power emerges when resource control becomes organized into systems such as states, empires, corporations, or financial institutions.
</p>
<h3> DEFINITIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Resource:</b> Any material, energy, or information used to support human life or economic activity</li>
<li><b>Scarcity:</b> The condition where demand exceeds available supply</li>
<li><b>Control:</b> The ability to manage, access, or restrict resources</li>
<li><b>Wealth:</b> Accumulated control over valuable resources or assets</li>
<li><b>Economic system:</b> A structured way societies produce, distribute, and consume resources</li>
</ul>
<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
History is shaped by control over resources—not by money itself, but by what money represents: access to energy, labor, land, and information.
</pre>
<h3> COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li> Thinking wealth is purely individual success rather than systemic control of resources</li>
<li> Ignoring the physical and material basis of economic systems</li>
<li> Assuming trade is always balanced or fair across regions</li>
<li> Believing modern economies are separate from natural resource limits</li>
<li> Overlooking how new technologies redefine what counts as a “resource”</li>
</ul>
<h3> RESOURCE EVOLUTION ACROSS HISTORY</h3>
<p><b>1. Agricultural Age</b></p>
<p>
Primary resources: fertile land, water, labor. Power belonged to those who controlled farmland and food production.
</p>
<p><b>2. Trade and Classical Age</b></p>
<p>
Primary resources: gold, salt, spices, luxury goods. Wealth came from controlling trade routes and exchange networks.
</p>
<p><b>3. Colonial Age</b></p>
<p>
Primary resources: raw materials and forced labor. Global inequality expanded through extraction systems.
</p>
<p><b>4. Industrial Age</b></p>
<p>
Primary resources: coal, oil, steel, and factory systems. Power shifted to industrial production and energy control.
</p>
<p><b>5. Digital Age</b></p>
<p>
Primary resources: data, attention, algorithms, computing power. Influence now depends on information control and digital networks.
</p>
<h3> HOW RESOURCE CONTROL SHAPES HISTORY (STEP MODEL)</h3>
<p><b>Step 1: Resource Identification</b></p>
<p>A valuable resource becomes important due to scarcity or demand.</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Competition</b></p>
<p>Groups, states, or individuals compete to access or control the resource.</p>
<p><b>Step 3: Organization</b></p>
<p>Systems such as trade networks, governments, or corporations form to manage control.</p>
<p><b>Step 4: Power Concentration</b></p>
<p>Control becomes concentrated in specific institutions or elites.</p>
<p><b>Step 5: System Expansion</b></p>
<p>Controlled resources are used to expand influence, territory, or technological capacity.</p>
<p><b>Step 6: Inequality Formation</b></p>
<p>Uneven distribution of resources creates social and economic hierarchy.</p>
<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li> Explains global inequality between nations and regions</li>
<li> Helps understand corporate competition and market control</li>
<li> Explains modern energy politics and resource conflicts</li>
<li> Shows why data and technology dominate modern economies</li>
<li> Helps analyze economic systems through resource dependency</li>
<li> Useful in policy design, economics, and geopolitical strategy analysis</li>
</ul>
<h3> FINAL INSIGHT</h3>
<pre>
Resources are the hidden foundation of all history. From fertile land that built early civilizations to the data that shapes modern economies, control over essential resources determines who rises to power, how wealth is distributed, and how global systems evolve. Understanding resource dynamics reveals that history is not random—it is a continuous story of competition, control, and adaptation driven by humanity''s need to secure what sustains it.
</pre>
<h3> QUIZ QUESTIONS</h3>
',
  '[[{"q":"What is the primary driver of economic and political power in history?","steps":["Step 1: Identify essential resources needed by societies","Step 2: Recognize competition over these resources","Step 3: Link control of resources to influence and authority"],"ans":"Control of key resources","why":"Power comes from controlling what people need to survive and function"},{"q":"How has the most important resource changed over time?","steps":["Step 1: Identify land as key in early societies","Step 2: Recognize shift to raw materials and energy during industrialization","Step 3: Identify data as a modern critical resource"],"ans":"From land, to raw materials, to energy, to data","why":"Each era is shaped by the most valuable and scarce resource"},{"q":"Why does control of resources lead to power?","steps":["Step 1: Identify dependence on essential resources","Step 2: Recognize control over access to these resources","Step 3: Observe influence over decisions and systems"],"ans":"Because others depend on those resources to survive and operate","why":"Dependency creates leverage and control"},{"q":"Why is data considered a powerful resource in the modern world?","steps":["Step 1: Identify the role of information in decision-making","Step 2: Recognize its ability to predict behavior","Step 3: Observe influence over systems and populations"],"ans":"Because it enables control over decisions, behavior, and systems","why":"Information provides predictive power and large-scale influence"},{"q":"Why do conflicts often arise over resources?","steps":["Step 1: Identify scarcity of valuable resources","Step 2: Recognize competing needs and interests","Step 3: Observe escalation into conflict"],"ans":"Because multiple groups compete for limited valuable resources","why":"Scarcity combined with importance creates tension and conflict"}]]'
);

-- Topic: history -> inequality_cycle -> Inequality & System Stability Cycle: How Societies Build, Break, and Reset
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'history',
  'inequality_cycle',
  'Inequality & System Stability Cycle: How Societies Build, Break, and Reset',
  '
<h2> Inequality & System Stability Cycle</h2>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<p>
All large systems—empires, economies, governments, corporations, and even informal communities—naturally develop inequality as they grow. This is not an accident but a structural outcome of how resources, power, and opportunities are distributed over time. Initially, inequality can improve efficiency and organization, but if it becomes extreme, it creates instability and forces transformation or collapse.
</p>
<p>
At the core, systems try to balance two forces:
</p>
<pre>
Efficiency (specialization, hierarchy, productivity)
VS
Equity (fair distribution, access, stability)
</pre>
<p>
When efficiency dominates too strongly, inequality rises. When equity dominates too strongly, systems may slow down or become inefficient. History is shaped by the tension between these two forces.
</p>
<h3> KEY DEFINITIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Inequality:</b> Uneven distribution of wealth, power, status, or opportunity within a system</li>
<li><b>Stability:</b> A condition where a system maintains order and predictability over time</li>
<li><b>Tension:</b> Social, economic, or political pressure caused by imbalance</li>
<li><b>Reform:</b> Controlled adjustments made to reduce pressure and preserve the system</li>
<li><b>Collapse:</b> Breakdown of structures when pressure exceeds system capacity</li>
<li><b>Redistribution:</b> Reallocation of resources to reduce imbalance</li>
<li><b>Feedback loop:</b> A process where outcomes reinforce or weaken the system that created them</li>
</ul>
<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Stability is not a permanent state—it is a temporary balance maintained between inequality and correction forces.
</pre>
<h3> COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li> Thinking inequality is always caused by corruption alone (it can emerge structurally)</li>
<li> Believing systems collapse without warning (collapse is usually the final stage of long pressure buildup)</li>
<li> Assuming reforms fully eliminate inequality (they usually reduce or reshape it)</li>
<li> Thinking all inequality is negative (some level can increase coordination and productivity)</li>
</ul>
<h3> EXPANDED SYSTEM CYCLE MODEL</h3>
<pre>
1. Expansion → population, economy, or system grows
2. Differentiation → roles and hierarchies emerge
3. Accumulation → resources concentrate in certain groups
4. Inequality gap increases → widening access differences
5. Feedback stress → dissatisfaction, inefficiency, resistance
6. System response:
   a) Reform (adjust rules, redistribute resources)
   b) Control (force, enforcement, suppression)
   c) Breakdown (collapse or fragmentation)
7. Reorganization → new system structure emerges
</pre>
<h3> HOW INEQUALITY BUILDS (STEP-BY-STEP)</h3>
<p><b>Step 1: Unequal Starting Conditions</b></p>
<p>Different access to land, education, capital, or networks creates initial differences.</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Compounding Advantage</b></p>
<p>Those with early advantages accumulate more resources over time (wealth, influence, education).</p>
<p><b>Step 3: Institutional Reinforcement</b></p>
<p>Rules, laws, and systems often unintentionally strengthen existing advantages.</p>
<p><b>Step 4: Opportunity Gap Expansion</b></p>
<p>Access to quality education, healthcare, and networks becomes uneven.</p>
<p><b>Step 5: Social Awareness</b></p>
<p>Groups begin to recognize imbalance and compare conditions.</p>
<p><b>Step 6: Pressure Increase</b></p>
<p>Protests, reforms, migration, or conflict emerge as pressure responses.</p>
<h3> TYPES OF SYSTEM RESPONSES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Reform Path:</b> taxation, policy changes, welfare systems, redistribution</li>
<li><b>Control Path:</b> stronger laws, enforcement, surveillance, restriction</li>
<li><b>Collapse Path:</b> revolution, war, economic breakdown, fragmentation</li>
</ul>
<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li> Explains why governments implement taxes and welfare systems</li>
<li> Helps understand wealth gaps and economic cycles</li>
<li> Explains organizational hierarchies and corporate structures</li>
<li> Helps predict political instability and social movements</li>
<li> Builds systems thinking for analyzing history and modern society</li>
</ul>
<h3> FINAL INSIGHT</h3>
<pre>
Inequality is a persistent feature of organized human systems—but it is not destiny. By understanding how inequality forms, the pressures it creates, and the potential responses (reform, control, or collapse), we can better navigate the dynamics of power and stability in any era. History shows that balance is temporary and must be actively maintained, not passively awaited.
</pre>
<h3> QUIZ QUESTIONS</h3>
',
  '[[{"q":"Why does inequality naturally emerge in systems?","steps":["Step 1: Identify how resources are distributed","Step 2: Recognize unequal accumulation over time","Step 3: Link to system structure and efficiency"],"ans":"Because resources and power tend to accumulate unevenly over time","why":"Systems naturally favor accumulation and control, leading to imbalance"},{"q":"Why does wealth concentrate over time?","steps":["Step 1: Identify initial advantages in resources","Step 2: Recognize reinvestment and growth","Step 3: Observe compounding effects"],"ans":"Because those with resources can grow them faster than those without","why":"Advantages compound over time, increasing inequality"},{"q":"When does inequality become dangerous in a system?","steps":["Step 1: Identify widening gaps between groups","Step 2: Recognize reduced opportunity and fairness","Step 3: Observe rising dissatisfaction and tension"],"ans":"When it limits opportunity and creates widespread dissatisfaction","why":"High inequality destabilizes systems and increases risk of conflict"},{"q":"Why can inequality lead to long-term instability?","steps":["Step 1: Identify persistent imbalance in resources","Step 2: Recognize growing frustration over time","Step 3: Observe pressure buildup within the system"],"ans":"Because unresolved inequality builds pressure over time","why":"Long-term imbalance eventually leads to conflict or systemic change"},{"q":"Why do some systems fail to correct inequality?","steps":["Step 1: Identify groups benefiting from inequality","Step 2: Recognize resistance to change","Step 3: Observe delayed or blocked reforms"],"ans":"Because powerful groups resist changes that reduce their advantage","why":"Those with power often act to preserve the existing system"}]]'
);

-- Topic: history -> innovation_cycle -> Innovation & Breakthrough Cycle: How Change Happens in Sudden Historical Jumps
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'history',
  'innovation_cycle',
  'Innovation & Breakthrough Cycle: How Change Happens in Sudden Historical Jumps',
  '
<h2> Innovation & Breakthrough Cycle</h2>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<p>
History does not evolve smoothly. It moves in bursts. Major changes in society happen when pressure builds inside a system until new inventions or ideas disrupt the existing structure. These breakthroughs do not just improve systems—they replace them, reorganize them, or force them to evolve into something new.
</p>
<p>
This means progress is not a straight line. It is a cycle of buildup, disruption, and reorganization.
</p>
<pre>
Pressure / Crisis → Innovation → Resistance → Adoption → Disruption → System Transformation → New Era → New Pressure
</pre>
<h3> KEY DEFINITIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Innovation:</b> A new idea, method, or technology that improves or replaces an existing system</li>
<li><b>Breakthrough:</b> A major discovery that changes how humans solve problems</li>
<li><b>Adoption:</b> The process of society accepting and using an innovation</li>
<li><b>Disruption:</b> When new systems weaken or replace older systems</li>
<li><b>Technological diffusion:</b> The spread of technology across societies and regions</li>
<li><b>System inertia:</b> The resistance of existing systems to change</li>
<li><b>Paradigm shift:</b> A complete change in how a system or society understands reality</li>
</ul>
<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Major historical change happens when internal pressure forces a system to accept innovation that it initially resisted.
</pre>
<h3> COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li> Thinking progress is smooth, continuous, and evenly distributed</li>
<li> Believing inventions automatically improve society immediately</li>
<li> Assuming everyone adopts innovation at the same speed</li>
<li> Ignoring resistance from institutions that benefit from old systems</li>
</ul>
<h3> EXPANDED CYCLE OF INNOVATION</h3>
<p><b>Step 1: System Pressure Builds</b></p>
<p>Existing systems become inefficient, unfair, or too complex to manage effectively.</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Problem Recognition</b></p>
<p>Individuals or groups identify failures in the current system (economic, social, technological, or political).</p>
<p><b>Step 3: Innovation Emerges</b></p>
<p>A new idea, tool, or method is developed to solve a specific limitation.</p>
<p><b>Step 4: Resistance Phase</b></p>
<p>Established systems resist change due to fear, cost, or loss of power.</p>
<p><b>Step 5: Early Adoption</b></p>
<p>Small groups begin using the innovation, often gaining advantages over others.</p>
<p><b>Step 6: Rapid Expansion</b></p>
<p>Once proven effective, the innovation spreads quickly through society.</p>
<p><b>Step 7: System Restructuring</b></p>
<p>Economies, jobs, institutions, and behaviors reorganize around the new innovation.</p>
<p><b>Step 8: Stabilization</b></p>
<p>A new “normal” forms until new pressures begin building again.</p>
<h3> WHY INNOVATIONS CREATE DISRUPTION</h3>
<ul>
<li> They change how resources are produced and distributed</li>
<li> They destroy old jobs while creating new ones</li>
<li> They weaken old institutions and strengthen new ones</li>
<li> They change how people think, communicate, and behave</li>
</ul>
<h3> HISTORICAL EXAMPLES</h3>
<ul>
<li> <b>Steam Engine:</b> Replaced manual production → Industrial Revolution</li>
<li> <b>Printing Press:</b> Spread knowledge → Reformation and scientific growth</li>
<li> <b>Internet:</b> Transformed communication → Digital globalization</li>
<li> <b>Agriculture:</b> Enabled settlement → First civilizations</li>
<li> <b>Electricity:</b> Reshaped industry, cities, and daily life</li>
</ul>
<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li> Explains rapid changes in technology like AI and smartphones</li>
<li> Helps understand industrial and economic revolutions</li>
<li> Predicts how future technologies may disrupt jobs and societies</li>
<li> Builds systems thinking for analyzing change in any field</li>
<li> Shows why crises often trigger major breakthroughs</li>
</ul>
<h3> FINAL INSIGHT</h3>
<pre>
Innovation is the engine of historical change—but it operates within cycles of pressure and resistance. When systems stagnate, innovation emerges to solve problems, reshape economies, and alter power structures. Understanding this pattern reveals why progress is often disruptive, why established powers resist change, and how human societies continuously reorganize around new technologies and ideas.
</pre>
<h3> QUIZ QUESTIONS</h3>
',
  '[[{"q":"Why does innovation often emerge during crises?","steps":["Step 1: Identify system failure or pressure","Step 2: Recognize urgent need for solutions","Step 3: Observe innovation as a response to survival"],"ans":"Because crises create pressure that forces new solutions to develop","why":"Systems must adapt under stress or risk collapse"},{"q":"What is the difference between invention and innovation?","steps":["Step 1: Define invention as creation of something new","Step 2: Define innovation as practical use of that idea","Step 3: Compare impact on society"],"ans":"Invention creates new ideas, while innovation applies them successfully","why":"Impact comes from use, not just creation"},{"q":"What makes an innovation disruptive?","steps":["Step 1: Identify an existing system","Step 2: Recognize its limitations","Step 3: Observe replacement or major transformation"],"ans":"A disruptive innovation replaces or significantly changes an existing system","why":"It alters how systems operate, not just improves them"},{"q":"Why do some innovations fail to spread?","steps":["Step 1: Identify resistance to change","Step 2: Recognize cost or complexity barriers","Step 3: Observe lack of widespread adoption"],"ans":"Because they face resistance, high cost, or are difficult to adopt","why":"An innovation must be both useful and accessible to succeed"},{"q":"How do innovations reshape power structures in society?","steps":["Step 1: Identify who controls the old system","Step 2: Recognize advantages of the new system","Step 3: Observe shift in control to new adopters"],"ans":"They shift power to those who control or adapt to new systems","why":"New technologies create new forms of influence and advantage"}]]'
);

-- Topic: history -> narrative_control -> Narrative Control System: How Stories Shape Reality, Identity, and Historical Truth
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'history',
  'narrative_control',
  'Narrative Control System: How Stories Shape Reality, Identity, and Historical Truth',
  '
<h2> Narrative Control System: How Stories Control Societies</h2>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<p>
History is not only shaped by events—it is shaped by how those events are interpreted, recorded, and remembered over time. The same event can produce multiple “truths” depending on perspective, power, culture, and access to information. This is why history is both a record of reality and a constructed narrative system.
</p>
<p>
Institutions such as schools, governments, religious organizations, and media systems act as “meaning filters,” selecting, emphasizing, or simplifying events into stories that shape collective understanding.
</p>
<pre>
Event → Selection → Interpretation → Narrative → Distribution → Belief System → Collective Behavior
</pre>
<h3> KEY DEFINITIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Narrative:</b> A structured explanation of events that assigns meaning, cause, and consequence</li>
<li><b>Propaganda:</b> Information selectively designed to influence opinions, emotions, or behavior</li>
<li><b>Framing:</b> The way information is presented to influence how it is interpreted</li>
<li><b>Collective memory:</b> Shared understanding of the past formed by society, not just individual experience</li>
<li><b>Bias:</b> A tendency to interpret information in a way that favors a particular perspective</li>
<li><b>Historical interpretation:</b> The process of explaining past events using evidence, perspective, and context</li>
</ul>
<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
People do not respond to raw events—they respond to interpreted stories about those events.
</pre>
<h3> COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li> Thinking history is purely objective and identical for everyone</li>
<li> Believing facts alone determine understanding without interpretation</li>
<li> Assuming media or education are neutral by default</li>
<li> Ignoring how power influences which stories are widely shared</li>
</ul>
<h3> HOW NARRATIVE SYSTEMS WORK</h3>
<p><b>Step 1: Event Occurs</b></p>
<p>A real-world event happens (war, discovery, election, crisis, invention).</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Data Collection</b></p>
<p>Information is gathered, but never completely (limited perspective, access, and recording).</p>
<p><b>Step 3: Selection</b></p>
<p>Only certain details are highlighted while others are ignored or minimized.</p>
<p><b>Step 4: Interpretation</b></p>
<p>Different groups explain the event based on their values, interests, or beliefs.</p>
<p><b>Step 5: Narrative Construction</b></p>
<p>A simplified story is created to communicate meaning and coherence.</p>
<p><b>Step 6: Institutional Distribution</b></p>
<p>The narrative spreads through schools, media, religion, politics, and cultural systems.</p>
<p><b>Step 7: Internalization</b></p>
<p>People accept the narrative as “common sense” or “truth” and integrate it into identity.</p>
<p><b>Step 8: Behavioral Output</b></p>
<p>Beliefs formed from narratives influence decisions, politics, and group behavior.</p>
<h3> TYPES OF HISTORICAL NARRATIVES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Victorious narrative:</b> Focuses on success, progress, and achievement</li>
<li><b>Victim narrative:</b> Focuses on suffering, injustice, or oppression</li>
<li><b>National narrative:</b> Builds identity around shared history of a country</li>
<li><b>Religious narrative:</b> Interprets events through spiritual meaning</li>
<li><b>Economic narrative:</b> Explains events through trade, resources, and wealth systems</li>
</ul>
<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li> Explains differences in global media reporting</li>
<li> Shows how education systems shape identity and worldview</li>
<li> Helps analyze political messaging and persuasion</li>
<li> Builds critical thinking about information, bias, and sources</li>
<li> Explains why societies interpret global events differently</li>
</ul>
<h3> FINAL INSIGHT</h3>
<pre>
Historical narratives are constructed systems of meaning, not neutral records of fact. They emerge from interpretation, selection, and distribution—and are used to build identity, justify actions, and maintain social order. Understanding narrative control is essential for critical thinking in any era.
</pre>
<h3> QUIZ QUESTIONS</h3>
',
  '[[{"q":"What is a historical narrative and why is it important?","steps":["Step 1: Identify an event or series of events","Step 2: Recognize interpretation and meaning given to the event","Step 3: Understand how it shapes understanding of history"],"ans":"A historical narrative is a structured story that explains and gives meaning to past events","why":"Narratives shape how people understand history and reality"},{"q":"Why do different groups interpret the same historical event differently?","steps":["Step 1: Identify different perspectives and experiences","Step 2: Recognize influence of culture, identity, and interests","Step 3: Understand how interpretation changes meaning"],"ans":"Because each group views events through its own perspective and interests","why":"Perception is shaped by identity, experience, and context"},{"q":"Why is control of narratives considered a form of power?","steps":["Step 1: Identify control over information and storytelling","Step 2: Recognize influence on beliefs and opinions","Step 3: Connect beliefs to actions and decisions"],"ans":"Because controlling narratives influences how people think and act","why":"Power depends on shaping beliefs and guiding behavior"},{"q":"How does media shape public perception of events?","steps":["Step 1: Identify what information is selected","Step 2: Recognize how it is framed and presented","Step 3: Observe how audiences interpret and react"],"ans":"Media shapes perception by selecting and framing information","why":"People rely on media to understand events beyond their direct experience"},{"q":"Why do narratives persist even when evidence contradicts them?","steps":["Step 1: Identify emotional attachment to beliefs","Step 2: Recognize confirmation bias","Step 3: Observe resistance to changing viewpoints"],"ans":"Because people protect beliefs that align with their identity and emotions","why":"Psychological biases reinforce existing beliefs despite new evidence"}]]'
);

-- Topic: history -> system_collapse_reset -> System Collapse & Reset Engine: Why Civilizations Break Down and Rebuild
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'history',
  'system_collapse_reset',
  'System Collapse & Reset Engine: Why Civilizations Break Down and Rebuild',
  '
<h2> System Collapse & Reset Engine</h2>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<p>
All complex systems—empires, economies, governments, ecosystems, organizations, and even technologies—eventually reach limits. These limits are not single points of failure but accumulated pressures spread across time. When the demand placed on a system exceeds its ability to adapt, it begins to lose efficiency, stability, and coordination. This leads to breakdown and eventual transformation.
</p>
<p>
Importantly, collapse is not the end of structure—it is the transition point between one system and another. What looks like failure is often reorganization at a higher or different level.
</p>
<pre>
Growth → Complexity → Inefficiency → Stress Accumulation → Shock Event → Breakdown → Collapse → Fragmentation/Reset → New System Formation
</pre>
<h3> KEY DEFINITIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Collapse:</b> The breakdown of a system’s ability to maintain order, coordination, and stability</li>
<li><b>Complexity:</b> The accumulation of interconnected rules, institutions, and dependencies within a system</li>
<li><b>Stress:</b> Pressure created by resource limits, inequality, conflict, or inefficiency</li>
<li><b>Shock event:</b> A triggering crisis that exposes existing weaknesses (war, famine, financial crash, disaster)</li>
<li><b>Fragmentation:</b> Breaking into smaller, less centralized systems after collapse</li>
<li><b>Adaptation:</b> Adjustments made by systems to survive pressure or change conditions</li>
<li><b>Reorganization:</b> Formation of a new system structure after breakdown</li>
</ul>
<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Systems do not collapse because of one failure—they collapse when accumulated stress overwhelms their capacity to adapt.
</pre>
<h3> COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li> Thinking collapse happens suddenly without long-term buildup</li>
<li> Assuming collapse means complete disappearance of structure</li>
<li> Believing powerful systems are permanent or immune to failure</li>
<li> Ignoring gradual internal decline before visible breakdown</li>
</ul>
<h3> FULL SYSTEM COLLAPSE MODEL (EXPANDED)</h3>
<p><b>Step 1: Expansion Phase</b></p>
<p>The system grows in size, population, wealth, or influence. Expansion creates opportunity but also increases coordination demands.</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Structural Complexity</b></p>
<p>To manage growth, new rules, institutions, and hierarchies are created. This improves control but increases system rigidity.</p>
<p><b>Step 3: Inefficiency Emergence</b></p>
<p>Communication slows, bureaucracy grows, corruption or mismanagement increases, and decision-making becomes less effective.</p>
<p><b>Step 4: Resource Strain</b></p>
<p>Resources become unevenly distributed. Some areas or groups become overburdened while others are under-resourced.</p>
<p><b>Step 5: Social and Political Stress</b></p>
<p>Inequality, dissatisfaction, conflict, or resistance increases within the system.</p>
<p><b>Step 6: External or Internal Shock</b></p>
<p>A major disruption (war, financial crisis, environmental change, technological disruption) exposes existing weaknesses.</p>
<p><b>Step 7: Breakdown Phase</b></p>
<p>Institutions fail to coordinate effectively. Trust in systems declines, and control weakens.</p>
<p><b>Step 8: Collapse or Fragmentation</b></p>
<p>The system loses centralized control and breaks into smaller units or dissolves entirely.</p>
<p><b>Step 9: Reset and Reorganization</b></p>
<p>New systems form based on lessons, power shifts, or new technologies. The cycle restarts in a different structure.</p>
<h3> WHY COMPLEXITY LEADS TO COLLAPSE</h3>
<ul>
<li> More connections = more points of failure</li>
<li> Increased coordination difficulty slows response time</li>
<li> Resource demands grow faster than supply in some cases</li>
<li> Institutions become rigid and resistant to adaptation</li>
<li> Decision-making becomes disconnected from ground reality</li>
</ul>
<h3> HISTORICAL EXAMPLES (EXPANDED VIEW)</h3>
<ul>
<li> <b>Roman Empire:</b> Overexpansion, administrative overload, economic strain, external invasions</li>
<li> <b>Feudal Europe:</b> Shift to centralized nation-states due to inefficiency of fragmented power</li>
<li> <b>Colonial Empires:</b> Resource extraction pressure + nationalist independence movements</li>
<li> <b>Economic Systems:</b> Market crashes reveal hidden debt and structural imbalance</li>
<li> <b>Industrial Systems:</b> Technological disruption forces restructuring of labor and production</li>
</ul>
<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li> Explains political revolutions and government reforms</li>
<li> Helps understand economic crises and recoveries</li>
<li> Explains corporate growth, failure, and restructuring</li>
<li> Helps analyze modern global instability patterns</li>
<li> Builds systems thinking for predicting change and transformation</li>
</ul>
<h3> QUIZ QUESTIONS</h3>
',
  '[{"q":"Is system collapse usually caused by a single event?","steps":["Step 1: Identify long-term buildup of stress","Step 2: Recognize internal weaknesses and complexity","Step 3: Distinguish between cause and final trigger"],"ans":"No, system collapse results from long-term accumulation of stress and complexity","why":"Single events trigger collapse but do not cause it alone"},{"q":"Why do complex systems become fragile over time?","steps":["Step 1: Identify increasing layers of rules and dependencies","Step 2: Recognize reduced flexibility and adaptability","Step 3: Explain vulnerability to sudden shocks"],"ans":"Because increasing complexity reduces adaptability and increases vulnerability","why":"Highly complex systems are harder to manage and easier to disrupt"},{"q":"What is the role of a crisis in system collapse?","steps":["Step 1: Identify underlying hidden weaknesses","Step 2: Recognize crisis as an exposure point","Step 3: Distinguish trigger from root cause"],"ans":"A crisis exposes and accelerates existing system weaknesses","why":"Collapse occurs when hidden problems are revealed under pressure"},{"q":"Why do some systems survive crises while others collapse?","steps":["Step 1: Identify level of system flexibility","Step 2: Recognize ability to adapt or reform","Step 3: Compare responses to stress"],"ans":"Because adaptable systems adjust while rigid systems fail","why":"Survival depends on the ability to respond to change"},{"q":"What is a tipping point in system collapse?","steps":["Step 1: Identify gradual accumulation of stress","Step 2: Recognize the critical threshold","Step 3: Explain rapid breakdown after crossing it"],"ans":"The tipping point is when accumulated stress exceeds the system’s capacity to cope","why":"After this threshold, collapse accelerates quickly and becomes difficult to stop"}]'
);

-- Topic: history -> modern_global_control_system -> Modern Global Control System: How Today’s World Actually Operates
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'history',
  'modern_global_control_system',
  'Modern Global Control System: How Today’s World Actually Operates',
  '
<h2> Modern Global Control System</h2>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<p>The modern world is not governed by a single empire or central controller. Instead, it operates as a <b>distributed global system</b> made up of interconnected institutions: states, corporations, financial networks, and digital data systems.</p>
<p>These parts do not operate independently. They constantly interact, compete, and cooperate. This creates a system where power is not located in one place, but in <b>flows of influence</b> moving through the system.</p>
<p>In this system, no single actor fully controls everything. Instead, influence emerges from <b>networks of dependency</b>. Each part needs the others to function, which creates stability—but also complexity and vulnerability.</p>
<h3> KEY DEFINITIONS (STRUCTURED VERSION)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Global System</b><br>An integrated transnational structure composed of states, markets, institutions, infrastructure, and information networks operating through continuous interdependence.</li><br>
<li><b>Corporation</b><br>A scalable organizational entity designed for capital expansion, resource coordination, market influence, and long-range economic control across multiple regions.</li><br>
<li><b>Financial System</b><br>A network of capital circulation mechanisms—including credit, investment, debt, liquidity, and monetary regulation—that governs resource allocation and economic stability.</li>
<br><li><b>Data</b><br>Digitally structured behavioral information processed to model patterns, optimize systems, predict outcomes, and influence large-scale decision environments.</li>
</ul>
<h3> KEY IDEA (DEEP SYSTEM PRINCIPLE)</h3>
<pre>
Modern power does not come from control of land alone, but from control of interconnected flows:
money, information, technology, energy, and data.
</pre>
<p>This means power is no longer fixed—it is dynamic. It moves through networks like electricity through a circuit. Whoever influences the flow can shape outcomes without direct force.</p>
<h3> COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS (DEEP CLARIFICATION)</h3>
<ul>
<li> “Only governments control the world”
<p><b>Reality:</b> Governments are one layer in a larger system that includes corporations, markets, and digital platforms.</p>
</li>
<li> “Power is only military or physical”
<p><b>Reality:</b> Modern power is often informational and financial rather than physical.</p>
</li>
<li> “Systems are controlled by a single group”
<p><b>Reality:</b> Power emerges from interaction between many competing and cooperating actors.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3> HOW THE MODERN GLOBAL SYSTEM WORKS (DETAILED MODEL)</h3>
<p><b>Step 1: Governance Layer (States)</b></p>
<p>Governments create rules, enforce laws, and define national frameworks for economic and social activity.</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Economic Layer (Corporations)</b></p>
<p>Corporations produce goods, services, innovation, and employment while competing globally for markets and resources.</p>
<p><b>Step 3: Allocation Layer (Financial Systems)</b></p>
<p>Banks, markets, and investment systems determine where capital flows, shaping growth and decline of regions and industries.</p>
<p><b>Step 4: Infrastructure Layer (Technology)</b></p>
<p>Digital and physical systems connect the world, enabling instant communication, automation, and coordination across borders.</p>
<p><b>Step 5: Intelligence Layer (Data Systems)</b></p>
<p>Data systems observe human behavior, generate predictions, and influence decisions in economics, politics, and culture.</p>
<p><b>Result:</b> These layers interact continuously, producing a self-adjusting global system.</p>
<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION (SYSTEM THINKING USE)</h3>
<ul>
<li> Economic crises spread globally due to financial interdependence</li>
<li> Social media shapes political and cultural behavior across countries instantly</li>
<li> Corporations influence supply chains, employment, and innovation globally</li>
<li> Policy decisions in one country affect markets, migration, and technology worldwide</li>
</ul>
<h3> FINAL INSIGHT</h3>
<pre>
To understand the modern world, do not ask “who controls it?”
Instead ask:
“How do the systems connect, and how do flows of information, money, and resources move through them?”
</pre>
<h3> QUIZ QUESTIONS</h3>
',
  '[{"q":"What defines the modern global system?","steps":["Step 1: Identify key actors (states, corporations)","Step 2: Recognize financial and technological systems","Step 3: Combine into a single interconnected structure"],"ans":"An interconnected network of states, corporations, finance, and technology","why":"Modern power operates through systems rather than isolated entities"},{"q":"Why is the modern world described as interdependent?","steps":["Step 1: Identify global supply chains","Step 2: Recognize shared resources and trade","Step 3: Explain mutual reliance between countries"],"ans":"Because countries depend on each other for goods, services, and stability","why":"No nation can operate completely independently in today''s global system"},{"q":"Why do global crises spread quickly in the modern world?","steps":["Step 1: Identify interconnected global systems","Step 2: Recognize speed of communication and transport","Step 3: Explain chain reactions across regions"],"ans":"Because global systems are highly interconnected","why":"A problem in one region rapidly affects others through shared networks"},{"q":"What role does technology play in modern global power?","steps":["Step 1: Identify communication and data systems","Step 2: Recognize automation and scalability","Step 3: Link to influence and control"],"ans":"Technology amplifies communication, control, and influence at a global scale","why":"It allows faster decision-making and wider reach than ever before"},{"q":"Why is globalization both beneficial and risky?","steps":["Step 1: Identify global connections and cooperation","Step 2: Recognize benefits like trade and innovation","Step 3: Identify risks such as rapid spread of crises"],"ans":"Because it creates opportunities while also spreading risks globally","why":"Interconnected systems amplify both progress and problems"}]'
);

-- Topic: history -> mass_psychology_system -> Mass Psychology System: How Crowds, Movements, and Collective Behavior Shape History
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'history',
  'mass_psychology_system',
  'Mass Psychology System: How Crowds, Movements, and Collective Behavior Shape History',
  '
<h2> Mass Psychology System</h2>
<h2> MASS PSYCHOLOGY: COLLECTIVE HUMAN BEHAVIOR SYSTEM</h2>
<h3> CORE OVERVIEW</h3>
<p>
Mass psychology explains how individual human thinking transforms when people operate inside groups.
In isolation, humans reason individually. In groups, cognition becomes distributed, emotional, and network-driven.
This creates a system where societies behave less like collections of individuals and more like <b>emergent emotional intelligence networks</b>.
</p>
<p>At scale, history is shaped not just by decisions—but by <b>how emotions propagate through human networks</b>.</p>
<hr/>
<h3> FUNDAMENTAL MECHANISMS OF MASS BEHAVIOR</h3>
<h4>1. Emotional Contagion System</h4><p>Emotions spread faster than rational thought. This creates synchronized emotional states across large populations.</p>
<h4>2. Cognitive Compression</h4><p>Complex reality is reduced into symbols, narratives, slogans, or identities for easier group processing.</p>
<h4>3. Social Conformity Pressure</h4><p>Individuals unconsciously adjust beliefs and behavior to match perceived group norms to maintain belonging.</p>
<h4>4. Responsibility Diffusion</h4><p>In groups, personal accountability weakens, increasing the likelihood of emotionally driven decisions.</p>
<h4>5. Narrative Locking</h4><p>Once a dominant interpretation spreads, alternative perspectives become socially costly or rejected.</p>
<hr/>
<h3> KEY CONCEPT DEFINITIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Mass Psychology:</b> The study of how individual cognition changes when embedded in large-scale social systems.</li>
<li><b>Emotional Contagion:</b> The transfer of emotional states through observation, communication, or digital exposure.</li>
<li><b>Collective Behavior:</b> Emergent group actions that arise without centralized coordination.</li>
<li><b>Social Identity Formation:</b> The process by which individuals define themselves through group membership.</li>
<li><b>Emergent System:</b> A system where macro-behavior arises from micro-interactions without central control.</li>
</ul>
<hr/>
<h3> CORE PRINCIPLE</h3>
<pre>
Human groups do not behave like individuals multiplied.
They behave like a single adaptive system shaped by emotion, identity, and communication flow.
</pre>
<hr/>
<h3> DEEP SYSTEM MISUNDERSTANDINGS</h3>
<ul>
<li> “Crowds are irrational”<p><b>Correction:</b> Crowds follow distributed emotional logic, not individual analytical logic.</p></li>
<li> “People fully control their opinions”<p><b>Correction:</b> Opinions are co-constructed through environment, repetition, and social reinforcement.</p></li>
<li> “Mass movements are centrally planned”<p><b>Correction:</b> Many emerge from decentralized emotional synchronization.</p></li>
</ul>
<hr/>
<h3> STAGE MODEL OF MASS FORMATION</h3>
<p><b>Stage 1 — Emotional Trigger</b><br>A strong emotional stimulus emerges (injustice, hope, threat, inspiration).</p>
<p><b>Stage 2 — Signal Amplification</b><br>The emotional signal spreads through communication networks.</p>
<p><b>Stage 3 — Network Reinforcement</b><br>Repeated exposure strengthens perceived importance and validity.</p>
<p><b>Stage 4 — Identity Crystallization</b><br>Individuals adopt group-based identity structures.</p>
<p><b>Stage 5 — Norm Consolidation</b><br>Shared beliefs stabilize into collective “truth systems.”</p>
<p><b>Stage 6 — Coordinated Action</b><br>Large-scale synchronized behavior emerges (social, political, economic).</p>
<p><b>Stage 7 — Systemic Impact</b><br>Institutions, markets, or governments respond and adapt.</p>
<hr/>
<h3> REAL-WORLD SYSTEM APPLICATIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li> Social media virality = real-time emotional contagion network</li>
<li> Political elections = identity + emotion-driven decision systems</li>
<li> Financial markets = collective belief fluctuations (fear/greed cycles)</li>
<li> Social movements = distributed coordination through shared perception</li>
<li> Culture = long-term stabilized mass behavior patterns</li>
</ul>
<hr/>
<h3> FINAL INSIGHT</h3>
<pre>
History is not only the story of events—
it is the story of how emotions propagate through human networks and become collective reality.
</pre>
',
  '[[{"q":"Why is group behavior often less rational than individual behavior?","steps":["Step 1: Compare individual thinking vs group setting","Step 2: Identify emotional influence in groups","Step 3: Explain how emotions spread and amplify"],"ans":"Group behavior is less rational because emotions spread quickly and reduce individual control","why":"Crowds create feedback loops that amplify emotions and override logical thinking"},{"q":"Why do societies resist new ideas before eventually accepting them?","steps":["Step 1: Identify stability of existing systems","Step 2: Recognize fear of change and uncertainty","Step 3: Explain gradual acceptance after pressure builds"],"ans":"Societies resist new ideas because they threaten stability and existing systems","why":"People prefer predictability, and systems are designed to protect themselves"},{"q":"What causes rapid social change in societies?","steps":["Step 1: Identify pressure such as crisis or instability","Step 2: Introduce new ideas or technologies","Step 3: Explain sudden adoption and transformation"],"ans":"Rapid change occurs when pressure forces adoption of new ideas or solutions","why":"Systems only change quickly when they can no longer function as they are"},{"q":"Why do people conform to group opinions even when they disagree?","steps":["Step 1: Identify human need for belonging","Step 2: Recognize social pressure and fear of rejection","Step 3: Explain behavior alignment with the group"],"ans":"People conform because they want acceptance and avoid conflict","why":"Humans are social and prioritize belonging over individual disagreement"},{"q":"Why do systems collapse instead of adapting to change?","steps":["Step 1: Identify increasing pressure or stress","Step 2: Recognize slow or failed adaptation","Step 3: Explain breakdown of the system"],"ans":"Systems collapse when they fail to adapt to growing pressure","why":"Rigid systems cannot adjust fast enough to survive change"}]]'
);

-- Topic: homescience -> food_preparation -> Food Preparation & Cookery (Full Master Notes)
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'homescience',
  'food_preparation',
  'Food Preparation & Cookery (Full Master Notes)',
  '<h2>Food Preparation & Cookery</h2>
<p>Food preparation and cookery is the process of preparing raw food into edible meals using heat, tools, and techniques while maintaining nutrition and safety.</p>
<h3> METHODS OF COOKING</h3>
<h4>1. Boiling</h4>
<p>Cooking food in water at 100°C.</p>
<p><b>Example:</b> Boiled potatoes, rice.</p>
<p><b>Advantages:</b> Easy, safe, no oil needed.</p>
<h4>2. Frying</h4>
<p>Cooking food in hot oil or fat.</p>
<p><b>Example:</b> Chips, eggs.</p>
<p><b>Disadvantages:</b> High fat content.</p>
<h4>3. Steaming</h4>
<p>Cooking using steam without direct water contact.</p>
<p><b>Example:</b> Vegetables, dumplings.</p>
<pre>
Water boils → Steam rises → Food cooks
</pre>
<h4>4. Roasting</h4>
<p>Cooking food using dry heat (fire/oven).</p>
<p><b>Example:</b> Meat, maize.</p>
<h4>5. Baking</h4>
<p>Cooking food in an enclosed oven using dry heat.</p>
<p><b>Example:</b> Bread, cakes.</p>
<hr>
<h3> KITCHEN EQUIPMENT & UTENSILS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Pots – boiling food</li>
<li>Pans – frying</li>
<li>Knives – cutting</li>
<li>Spoons – stirring</li>
<li>Oven – baking</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Knife → Cutting
Pot → Boiling
Pan → Frying
Oven → Baking
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> KITCHEN SAFETY RULES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Keep knives safely</li>
<li>Do not touch hot surfaces</li>
<li>Wash hands before cooking</li>
<li>Keep kitchen clean</li>
<li>Turn off gas/electricity after use</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> FOOD PREPARATION TECHNIQUES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Washing</li>
<li>Peeling</li>
<li>Chopping</li>
<li>Mixing</li>
<li>Grinding</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> FOOD CONTAMINATION & PREVENTION</h3>
<p>Food contamination is when food becomes unsafe due to germs or chemicals.</p>
<h4>Causes:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Dirty hands</li>
<li>Uncovered food</li>
<li>Flies and pests</li>
</ul>
<h4>Prevention:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Wash food properly</li>
<li>Cover food</li>
<li>Store in clean containers</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> MENU PLANNING</h3>
<h4>Breakfast Example</h4>
<p>Tea + bread + eggs</p>
<h4>Lunch Example</h4>
<p>Rice + beans + vegetables</p>
<h4>Dinner Example</h4>
<p>Ugali + meat + soup</p>
<pre>
Breakfast → Energy start
Lunch → Main meal
Dinner → Light meal
</pre>
',
  '[{"q":"Define food preparation","ans":"It is the process of turning raw food into edible meals","explain":"It involves cooking and handling food safely","why":"Ensures food is safe and nutritious"},{"q":"State 3 methods of cooking","ans":"Boiling, frying, steaming","explain":"Different heat methods are used","why":"Helps in food variety"},{"q":"Explain food contamination","ans":"It is when food becomes unsafe due to germs or chemicals","explain":"Occurs due to poor hygiene","why":"Can cause disease"},{"q":"Name two cooking methods using oil","ans":"Frying and roasting","why":"These methods use fats or oils to transfer heat to the food."},{"q":"Why is steaming healthier than frying?","ans":"It does not require extra fats or oils","why":"Steaming retains nutrients without adding excess calories."},{"q":"List 3 kitchen utensils and their uses","ans":"Pots for boiling, pans for frying, and knives for cutting","why":"Different utensils are designed for specific cooking methods."}]'
);

-- Topic: homescience -> food_preservation -> Food Preservation (Full Master Notes)
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'homescience',
  'food_preservation',
  'Food Preservation (Full Master Notes)',
  '<h2>Food Preservation</h2>
<p>Food preservation is the process of treating and handling food to prevent spoilage and extend its shelf life while maintaining quality and safety.</p>
<h3> IMPORTANCE OF FOOD PRESERVATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Prevents food wastage</li>
<li>Extends shelf life of food</li>
<li>Maintains nutritional value</li>
<li>Ensures food availability during scarcity</li>
<li>Reduces food poisoning risks</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> CAUSES OF FOOD SPOILAGE</h3>
<ul>
<li>Bacteria and microorganisms</li>
<li>Air and oxidation</li>
<li>Moisture and humidity</li>
<li>Heat and sunlight</li>
<li>Insects and pests</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Food Spoilage Cycle:
Microbes → Growth → Decomposition → Bad smell/decay
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> DRYING METHOD</h3>
<p>Drying removes moisture from food to stop bacteria growth.</p>
<h4>Examples:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Fish drying in sun</li>
<li>Dry grains (maize, beans)</li>
<li>Dried fruits</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Food → Sun/heat → Moisture removed → Long storage life
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> FREEZING METHOD</h3>
<p>Freezing preserves food by storing it at very low temperatures.</p>
<h4>Examples:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Meat in freezer</li>
<li>Frozen vegetables</li>
<li>Ice cream storage</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Food → Freezer (-18°C) → Microbes inactive → Food preserved
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> SALTING & SMOKING</h3>
<h4>Salting</h4>
<p>Salt removes moisture and kills bacteria.</p>
<h4>Smoking</h4>
<p>Food is exposed to smoke which dries and preserves it.</p>
<h4>Examples:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Salted fish</li>
<li>Smoked meat</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> CANNING & BOTTLING</h3>
<p>Food is sealed in airtight containers and heated to kill microorganisms.</p>
<pre>
Food → Container → Heat sterilization → Airtight seal → Long storage
</pre>
<h4>Examples:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Canned beans</li>
<li>Bottled juice</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> FERMENTATION</h3>
<p>Fermentation uses microorganisms to preserve food and improve flavor.</p>
<h4>Examples:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Yogurt</li>
<li>Fermented milk</li>
<li>Bread dough rising</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Sugars → Bacteria/Yeast → Acid/Alcohol → Preserved food
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> COMPARISON OF METHODS</h3>
<pre>
Drying      → Removes water
Freezing    → Stops microbes
Salting     → Draws out moisture
Smoking     → Heat + chemicals
Canning     → Airtight + heat
Fermentation → Microbial action
</pre>
<hr>
',
  '[{"q":"Define food preservation","ans":"It is the process of preventing food from spoiling and extending its shelf life","explain":"It involves different methods like drying and freezing","why":"Ensures food safety and availability"},{"q":"State 3 causes of food spoilage","ans":"Bacteria, moisture, heat","explain":"These conditions allow food to decay","why":"Helps in prevention"},{"q":"Explain freezing method","ans":"It is preserving food by storing it at very low temperatures","explain":"It slows microbial activity","why":"Keeps food fresh for long"},{"q":"Give 3 examples of preserved food","ans":"Dried fish, canned beans, smoked meat","explain":"These use different preservation methods","why":"Shows application in real life"},{"q":"Why does drying preserve food?","ans":"It removes moisture","why":"Bacteria and microorganisms need moisture to survive and grow."},{"q":"Name two foods preserved by salting","ans":"Fish and meat","why":"Salt draws out moisture from these foods, preventing spoilage."},{"q":"What temperature is used in freezing?","ans":"-18 degrees Celsius","why":"Extremely low temperatures make microbes inactive."}]'
);

-- Topic: homescience -> home_management -> Home Management (Advanced Full Master Notes)
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'homescience',
  'home_management',
  'Home Management (Advanced Full Master Notes)',
  '<h2>Home Management</h2>
<p>Home management is the process of organizing, planning, and controlling family resources such as time, money, energy, and skills to achieve family goals efficiently.</p>
<h3> HOUSEHOLD DUTIES & ROLES</h3>
<p>Every family member contributes to running the home smoothly.</p>
<ul>
<li>Parents → provide income and guidance</li>
<li>Children → cleaning, studying, helping</li>
<li>Extended family → support when needed</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Family System:
Father → Income
Mother → Care + management
Children → Support tasks
</pre>
<hr>
<h3>‍‍ FAMILY RESPONSIBILITIES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Providing food and shelter</li>
<li>Educating children</li>
<li>Maintaining discipline</li>
<li>Ensuring health and safety</li>
<li>Managing resources</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> CONFLICT RESOLUTION IN FAMILIES</h3>
<p>Conflict resolution is solving disagreements peacefully in the family.</p>
<h4>Methods:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Communication</li>
<li>Listening to both sides</li>
<li>Compromise</li>
<li>Mediation by elders</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Conflict → Discussion → Understanding → Solution
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> HOME ORGANIZATION SKILLS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Keeping things in order</li>
<li>Cleaning regularly</li>
<li>Arranging furniture properly</li>
<li>Labeling storage items</li>
<li>Planning daily routines</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Organized Home = Less stress + Efficiency
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> WORK SIMPLIFICATION TECHNIQUES</h3>
<p>These are methods used to make household tasks easier and faster.</p>
<h4>Techniques:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Using tools and machines</li>
<li>Combining tasks</li>
<li>Reducing unnecessary movement</li>
<li>Planning work before starting</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Before: Slow manual work
After: Efficient planned work
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> LABOR-SAVING DEVICES</h3>
<p>These are machines that reduce human effort in the home.</p>
<h4>Examples:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Washing machine</li>
<li>Electric blender</li>
<li>Vacuum cleaner</li>
<li>Gas/electric cooker</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Human effort ↓
Machine support ↑
Efficiency ↑
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> IMPORTANCE OF HOME MANAGEMENT</h3>
<ul>
<li>Improves family living standards</li>
<li>Reduces stress</li>
<li>Saves time and money</li>
<li>Promotes teamwork</li>
<li>Ensures proper use of resources</li>
</ul>
<hr>
',
  '[{"q":"Define home management","ans":"It is the planning and organizing of family resources to achieve family goals","explain":"It involves time, money, and energy use","why":"Improves family efficiency"},{"q":"State 3 family responsibilities","ans":"Providing food, education, and shelter","explain":"These ensure family survival and growth","why":"Maintains family stability"},{"q":"Explain conflict resolution","ans":"It is solving disagreements peacefully in a family","explain":"It involves communication and compromise","why":"Prevents family breakdown"},{"q":"Give 3 labor-saving devices","ans":"Washing machine, blender, vacuum cleaner","explain":"These reduce human effort","why":"Save time and energy"},{"q":"Name two ways of solving family conflict","ans":"Communication and compromise","why":"Listening and finding a middle ground resolves disagreements peacefully."},{"q":"Give 3 examples of household labor-saving devices","ans":"Washing machine, vacuum cleaner, and blender","why":"These machines reduce human effort and save time."},{"q":"What is work simplification?","ans":"Using methods to make household tasks easier and faster","why":"It includes combining tasks or using tools to improve efficiency."}]'
);

-- Topic: homescience -> child_care_development -> Child Care & Development (Full Master Notes)
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'homescience',
  'child_care_development',
  'Child Care & Development (Full Master Notes)',
  '<h2>Child Care & Development</h2>
<p>Child care and development is the study of how children grow physically, emotionally, socially, and mentally, and how to provide proper care for healthy development.</p>
<h3> STAGES OF CHILD DEVELOPMENT</h3>
<pre>
Infancy (0–2 years)
↓
Early Childhood (2–6 years)
↓
Middle Childhood (6–12 years)
↓
Adolescence (12–18 years)
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> TYPES OF DEVELOPMENT</h3>
<h4>1. Physical Development</h4>
<ul>
<li>Growth of body size</li>
<li>Development of muscles and bones</li>
<li>Improved coordination</li>
</ul>
<h4>2. Emotional Development</h4>
<ul>
<li>Ability to express feelings</li>
<li>Learning self-control</li>
<li>Building confidence</li>
</ul>
<h4>3. Social Development</h4>
<ul>
<li>Interaction with others</li>
<li>Sharing and cooperation</li>
<li>Forming friendships</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> CHILD NUTRITION</h3>
<p>Children need a balanced diet for proper growth and development.</p>
<pre>
Carbohydrates → Energy
Proteins → Growth
Vitamins → Protection
Minerals → Body strength
Water → Body functions
</pre>
<h4>Example Diet:</h4>
<p>Milk + eggs + fruits + vegetables + cereals</p>
<hr>
<h3> IMMUNIZATION SCHEDULE (BASIC)</h3>
<ul>
<li>BCG → Prevents tuberculosis</li>
<li>Polio vaccine → Prevents polio</li>
<li>Measles vaccine → Prevents measles</li>
<li>DPT → Prevents diphtheria, pertussis, tetanus</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Vaccine → Antibody formation → Disease protection
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> BABY CARE PRACTICES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Proper feeding (breastfeeding/formula)</li>
<li>Keeping baby clean</li>
<li>Safe sleeping position</li>
<li>Regular health checks</li>
<li>Protection from cold and infection</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> COMMON CHILDHOOD DISEASES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Measles</li>
<li>Chickenpox</li>
<li>Diarrhea</li>
<li>Malaria</li>
<li>Pneumonia</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Germs → Weak immunity → Disease → Treatment/prevention
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> TOYS & LEARNING DEVELOPMENT</h3>
<p>Toys help children develop mental and physical skills.</p>
<h4>Types of toys:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Educational toys (puzzles, blocks)</li>
<li>Physical toys (balls, skipping ropes)</li>
<li>Creative toys (drawing kits)</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Play → Learning → Brain development
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> IMPORTANCE OF CHILD CARE</h3>
<ul>
<li>Ensures healthy growth</li>
<li>Prevents diseases</li>
<li>Improves intelligence</li>
<li>Builds social skills</li>
<li>Promotes emotional stability</li>
</ul>
<hr>
',
  '[{"q":"Define child development","ans":"It is the process of growth in physical, emotional, social, and mental aspects of a child","explain":"It covers all areas of growth","why":"Helps understand child needs"},{"q":"State 3 stages of child development","ans":"Infancy, childhood, adolescence","explain":"Each stage has different growth patterns","why":"Important for care planning"},{"q":"Explain importance of immunization","ans":"It protects children from dangerous diseases","explain":"It builds immunity against infections","why":"Prevents illness and death"},{"q":"Give 3 examples of childhood diseases","ans":"Measles, malaria, chickenpox","explain":"Common diseases affecting children","why":"Helps in prevention awareness"},{"q":"State 3 importance of toys","ans":"Improve learning, creativity, and physical development","explain":"Toys support child growth","why":"Enhances development skills"},{"q":"Name the four stages of child development","ans":"Infancy, early childhood, middle childhood, and adolescence","why":"These represent the progressive stages of growth."},{"q":"Give 3 examples of vaccines for children","ans":"BCG, Polio, and Measles vaccines","why":"These protect children from dangerous infections early in life."},{"q":"How do toys help child development?","ans":"They improve mental and physical skills","why":"Educational and physical toys stimulate learning and coordination."}]'
);

-- Topic: homescience -> laundry_work -> Laundry Work (Clothing Care Full Master Notes)
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'homescience',
  'laundry_work',
  'Laundry Work (Clothing Care Full Master Notes)',
  '<h2>Laundry Work (Clothing Care)</h2>
<p>Laundry work is the process of washing, cleaning, drying, ironing, and storing clothes to maintain hygiene, appearance, and durability.</p>
<h3> LAUNDRY EQUIPMENT & MATERIALS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Washing basin – for soaking clothes</li>
<li>Washing machine – automatic cleaning</li>
<li>Soap and detergents – remove dirt</li>
<li>Brush – scrubbing stains</li>
<li>Iron – removing wrinkles</li>
<li>Cloth pegs – hanging clothes</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Clothes → Soap → Water → Washing → Drying → Ironing → Storage
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> WASHING METHODS</h3>
<h4>1. Hand Washing</h4>
<ul>
<li>Done using hands and water</li>
<li>Suitable for delicate clothes</li>
</ul>

<h4>2. Machine Washing</h4>
<ul>
<li>Uses electric washing machines</li>
<li>Faster and more efficient</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Hand Wash = Gentle care
Machine Wash = Fast cleaning
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> STAIN REMOVAL TECHNIQUES</h3>
<h4>Types of stains:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Oil stains</li>
<li>Ink stains</li>
<li>Blood stains</li>
<li>Food stains</li>
</ul>
<h4>Methods:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Soaking in detergent</li>
<li>Using stain removers</li>
<li>Rubbing gently with brush</li>
<li>Cold water for blood stains</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Stain → Identify type → Apply treatment → Wash → Dry
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> IRONING TECHNIQUES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Check fabric type before ironing</li>
<li>Use correct temperature</li>
<li>Iron inside out for delicate clothes</li>
<li>Avoid burning fabric</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Low heat → Silk
Medium heat → Cotton
High heat → Linen
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> DRYING & STORAGE METHODS</h3>
<h4>Drying:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Sun drying (natural method)</li>
<li>Shade drying (for colored clothes)</li>
</ul>
<h4>Storage:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Fold neatly</li>
<li>Store in clean cupboards</li>
<li>Use moth repellents</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Wash → Dry → Iron → Fold → Store
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> CARE LABELS ON CLOTHES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Washing symbols → show washing method</li>
<li>Ironing symbols → heat level</li>
<li>Bleaching symbols → safe or unsafe</li>
<li>Dry cleaning symbols → professional cleaning</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Label = Instruction guide for clothing care
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> IMPORTANCE OF LAUNDRY WORK</h3>
<ul>
<li>Keeps clothes clean and hygienic</li>
<li>Improves appearance</li>
<li>Extends clothing life</li>
<li>Prevents skin diseases</li>
<li>Maintains personal dignity</li>
</ul>
<hr>
',
  '[{"q":"Define laundry work","ans":"It is the process of washing, drying, ironing, and storing clothes","explain":"It ensures clean and well-maintained clothes","why":"Promotes hygiene and appearance"},{"q":"State 3 laundry equipment","ans":"Washing machine, iron, brush","explain":"These are used in cleaning clothes","why":"Makes laundry easier"},{"q":"Differentiate hand washing and machine washing","ans":"Hand washing uses hands while machine washing uses electricity","explain":"They differ in speed and effort","why":"Helps choose correct method"},{"q":"Explain stain removal process","ans":"Identify stain, apply treatment, wash and dry","explain":"Different stains need different methods","why":"Prevents permanent damage"},{"q":"Give 3 importance of laundry work","ans":"Clean clothes, hygiene, good appearance","explain":"Improves health and social image","why":"Important daily life skill"},{"q":"Name two washing methods","ans":"Hand washing and machine washing","why":"Both are used depending on the fabric delicacy and equipment available."},{"q":"Why should delicate clothes be hand washed?","ans":"To prevent damage from the rough agitation of machines","why":"Gentle hand washing preserves the fabric structure."},{"q":"Give 3 types of stains","ans":"Oil, ink, and blood stains","why":"Stains require different removal techniques based on their composition."}]'
);

-- Topic: homescience -> household_cleaning -> Household Cleaning & Cleaning Agents (Full Master Notes)
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'homescience',
  'household_cleaning',
  'Household Cleaning & Cleaning Agents (Full Master Notes)',
  '<h2>Household Cleaning & Cleaning Agents</h2>
<h2> Household Cleaning (Advanced Exam Notes)</h2>
<p>
Household cleaning is the process of removing dirt, germs, stains, and unwanted materials from surfaces using water, cleaning agents, and tools in order to maintain hygiene, health, and a safe living environment.
</p>
<h3> TYPES OF CLEANING AGENTS (DETAILED)</h3>
<h4>1. Soap</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Mild cleaning agent used for general washing</li>
  <li>Removes dirt and grease from surfaces and skin</li>
</ul>
<h4>2. Detergents</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Strong chemical cleaners used for tough stains</li>
  <li>Effective in both hard and soft water</li>
</ul>
<h4>3. Disinfectants</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Chemicals that kill germs and bacteria</li>
  <li>Used in toilets, hospitals, and kitchen surfaces</li>
</ul>
<h4>4. Abrasives</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Used for scrubbing hard stains and dirt</li>
  <li>Example: scouring powder, steel wool</li>
</ul>
<h4> Exam Summary</h4>
<pre>
Soap        → Removes dirt
Detergent   → Deep cleaning
Disinfectant→ Kills germs
Abrasive    → Scrubs tough stains
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> CLEANING TOOLS & EQUIPMENT (WITH FUNCTIONS)</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Brooms:</b> Used for sweeping dust and dry dirt</li>
  <li><b>Mops:</b> Used for wet cleaning floors</li>
  <li><b>Brushes:</b> Used for scrubbing rough surfaces</li>
  <li><b>Cloths:</b> Used for wiping and polishing surfaces</li>
  <li><b>Dustpans:</b> Used for collecting swept dirt</li>
</ul>
<h4> Tool Function Link</h4>
<pre>
Broom → Removes dust
Mop → Cleans wet surfaces
Cloth → Wipes surfaces
Brush → Removes stubborn stains
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> CLEANING DIFFERENT SURFACES (METHODS)</h3>
<h4>1. Floor Cleaning</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Sweep to remove dust and debris</li>
  <li>Mop using water mixed with detergent</li>
  <li>Allow floor to dry to avoid slipping</li>
</ul>
<h4>2. Glass Cleaning</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Use glass cleaner or vinegar solution</li>
  <li>Wipe with soft lint-free cloth</li>
  <li>Polish to avoid streaks</li>
</ul>
<h4>3. Kitchen Cleaning</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Remove food waste immediately</li>
  <li>Wash surfaces with detergent</li>
  <li>Disinfect to kill bacteria</li>
</ul>
<h4> Exam Flow</h4>
<pre>
Floor → Sweep → Mop → Dry
Glass → Spray → Wipe → Polish
Kitchen → Clean → Disinfect → Maintain hygiene
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> CLEANING SCHEDULES (TIME MANAGEMENT)</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Daily:</b> Floors, kitchen surfaces, utensils</li>
  <li><b>Weekly:</b> Bathrooms, windows, dusting furniture</li>
  <li><b>Monthly:</b> Deep cleaning (mattresses, curtains, hidden areas)</li>
</ul>
<h4> Exam Idea</h4>
<pre>
Daily → Maintenance cleaning  
Weekly → Deep cleaning  
Monthly → Intensive cleaning
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> SAFETY WHEN USING CLEANING CHEMICALS</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Do not mix cleaning chemicals (may produce harmful gases)</li>
  <li>Wear gloves to protect skin</li>
  <li>Avoid inhaling strong chemical fumes</li>
  <li>Store chemicals away from children</li>
  <li>Always read product labels before use</li>
</ul>
<h4> Safety Rule</h4>
<pre>
Protect skin + Follow instructions + Avoid mixing chemicals = Safe cleaning
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> IMPORTANCE OF HOUSEHOLD CLEANING</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Prevents spread of diseases and infections</li>
  <li>Removes dirt, germs, and unpleasant smells</li>
  <li>Improves appearance and comfort of home</li>
  <li>Reduces pests like cockroaches and rats</li>
  <li>Creates a healthy and safe living environment</li>
</ul>
<h3> COMMON EXAM MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Confusing detergents with disinfectants</li>
  <li>Not linking tools to their correct uses</li>
  <li>Missing safety precautions</li>
  <li>Listing cleaning steps without order</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> FINAL MASTER IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Household Cleaning = Hygiene + Tools + Chemicals + Safety
Clean home → Healthy family → Disease prevention → Better living environment
</pre>
',
  '[{"q":"Define household cleaning","ans":"It is the removal of dirt and germs from surfaces using cleaning agents","explain":"It keeps the home hygienic","why":"Prevents diseases"},{"q":"State 3 types of cleaning agents","ans":"Soap, detergents, disinfectants","explain":"Each has a different cleaning role","why":"Used in different cleaning tasks"},{"q":"Explain function of disinfectants","ans":"They kill germs and bacteria","explain":"Used in toilets and hospitals","why":"Prevents disease spread"},{"q":"Give 3 cleaning tools","ans":"Broom, mop, brush","explain":"Used for cleaning different surfaces","why":"Improves cleaning efficiency"},{"q":"Why is safety important when using chemicals?","ans":"To prevent harm and accidents","explain":"Chemicals can be dangerous if misused","why":"Protects human health"},{"q":"What is the difference between soap and detergent?","ans":"Soap is made from natural fats, detergents are synthetic","why":"Detergents often clean better in hard water."},{"q":"How often should bathrooms be cleaned?","ans":"Daily","why":"Frequent cleaning prevents the buildup of harmful bacteria."},{"q":"Why should chemicals not be mixed?","ans":"They can produce toxic gases","why":"Mixing cleaners like bleach and ammonia is extremely dangerous."}]'
);

-- Topic: homescience -> energy_use_home -> Energy Use in the Home (Full Master Notes)
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'homescience',
  'energy_use_home',
  'Energy Use in the Home (Full Master Notes)',
  '<h2>Energy Use in the Home</h2>
<h2> Energy in the Home (Advanced Exam Notes)</h2>
<p>
Energy in the home refers to different sources used for cooking, lighting, heating, and powering appliances. Proper use and conservation of energy improves safety, reduces cost, and supports environmental sustainability.
</p>
<h3> SOURCES OF ENERGY (DETAILED)</h3>
<h4>1. Electricity</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Used for lighting, heating, and powering appliances</li>
  <li>Clean, efficient, and widely used in modern homes</li>
</ul>
<h4>2. Gas (LPG)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Common for cooking and heating</li>
  <li>Produces instant heat and is easy to control</li>
</ul>
<h4>3. Solar Energy</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Energy obtained from the sun using solar panels</li>
  <li>Renewable, clean, and environmentally friendly</li>
</ul>
<h4>4. Firewood</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Traditional fuel used for cooking and heating</li>
  <li>Readily available in rural areas but produces smoke</li>
</ul>
<h4> Classification Idea</h4>
<pre>
Renewable → Solar, Firewood (partially renewable if replanted)
Non-renewable → Gas, Electricity (depends on source)
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> KITCHEN FUEL TYPES (APPLICATION)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Electric cookers (modern kitchens)</li>
  <li>Gas stoves (fast cooking)</li>
  <li>Charcoal jikos (common in many homes)</li>
  <li>Firewood stoves (rural areas)</li>
</ul>
<h4> Exam Idea</h4>
<pre>
Modern fuels → Electricity, Gas  
Traditional fuels → Firewood, Charcoal
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> ENERGY CONSERVATION METHODS (IMPORTANT)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Switch off lights and appliances when not in use</li>
  <li>Use energy-saving bulbs (LEDs)</li>
  <li>Cover pots while cooking to reduce heat loss</li>
  <li>Use appliances efficiently (avoid overuse)</li>
  <li>Maintain electrical devices regularly</li>
</ul>
<h4> Key Idea</h4>
<pre>
Energy conservation → Lower bills + Environmental protection
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> SAFE USE OF ELECTRICITY (VERY IMPORTANT)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Do not touch electrical sockets with wet hands</li>
  <li>Switch off appliances after use</li>
  <li>Avoid overloading sockets with many devices</li>
  <li>Use properly insulated and safe wiring</li>
  <li>Keep children away from electrical outlets</li>
</ul>
<h4> Safety Rule</h4>
<pre>
Dry hands + Proper wiring + Avoid overload = Electrical safety
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> ADVANTAGES & DISADVANTAGES (EXAM COMPARISON)</h3>
<h4>Electricity</h4>
<ul>
  <li><b>Advantage:</b> Clean, efficient, versatile</li>
  <li><b>Disadvantage:</b> Expensive, risk of shock/fire</li>
</ul>
<h4>Gas (LPG)</h4>
<ul>
  <li><b>Advantage:</b> Fast cooking, easy to control heat</li>
  <li><b>Disadvantage:</b> Risk of explosion and leakage</li>
</ul>
<h4>Firewood</h4>
<ul>
  <li><b>Advantage:</b> Cheap and widely available</li>
  <li><b>Disadvantage:</b> Causes smoke pollution and deforestation</li>
</ul>
<h4>Solar Energy</h4>
<ul>
  <li><b>Advantage:</b> Free, renewable, environmentally friendly</li>
  <li><b>Disadvantage:</b> Depends on sunlight (weather conditions)</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> ENERGY COMPARISON TABLE</h3>
<pre>
Electricity → Efficient but costly and risky if misused
Gas        → Fast but safety hazards
Solar      → Clean but weather-dependent
Firewood   → Cheap but polluting
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> IMPORTANCE OF ENERGY MANAGEMENT</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Reduces household expenses</li>
  <li>Prevents accidents and hazards</li>
  <li>Protects the environment from pollution</li>
  <li>Ensures efficient use of resources</li>
  <li>Supports sustainable living for future generations</li>
</ul>
<h3> COMMON EXAM MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Mixing renewable and non-renewable sources</li>
  <li>Not linking energy use to safety</li>
  <li>Listing points without explanation</li>
  <li>Confusing advantages with disadvantages</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> FINAL MASTER IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Home Energy = Sources + Safety + Conservation + Efficiency
Smart energy use → Lower cost + Safety + Sustainable living
</pre>
',
  '[{"q":"Define energy in the home","ans":"Energy in the home refers to sources used for cooking, lighting, and heating","explain":"It includes electricity, gas, solar, and firewood","why":"Supports daily activities"},{"q":"State 3 sources of energy","ans":"Electricity, gas, solar","explain":"These are common household energy sources","why":"Used in daily life"},{"q":"Explain energy conservation","ans":"It is the careful use of energy to avoid waste","explain":"Includes switching off unused devices","why":"Reduces cost and saves energy"},{"q":"Give 3 safety rules of electricity","ans":"Do not touch wet sockets, avoid overload, switch off appliances","explain":"Prevents accidents","why":"Ensures safety"},{"q":"State 2 disadvantages of firewood","ans":"Produces smoke and causes deforestation","explain":"It is not environmentally friendly","why":"Helps understand cleaner alternatives"},{"q":"Name two modern energy sources","ans":"Electricity and solar power","why":"These are commonly used in households today."},{"q":"Why is solar energy considered clean?","ans":"It does not produce pollution or harmful emissions","why":"It relies on the sun rather than burning fossil fuels."},{"q":"What happens when sockets are overloaded?","ans":"It can cause a short circuit or fire","why":"Drawing too much current overheats the wiring."}]'
);

-- Topic: homescience -> consumer_education -> Consumer Education (Full Master Notes)
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'homescience',
  'consumer_education',
  'Consumer Education (Full Master Notes)',
  '<h2>Consumer Education</h2>
<h2>‍ Consumer Education (Advanced Exam Notes)</h2>
<p>
Consumer education is the process of learning how to make informed decisions when buying goods and services, and understanding consumer rights, responsibilities, and protection systems in the market.
</p>
<h3>‍ CONSUMER RIGHTS (DETAILED)</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Right to safety:</b> Protection from dangerous goods and services</li>
  <li><b>Right to information:</b> Access to correct product details (price, ingredients, expiry)</li>
  <li><b>Right to choose:</b> Ability to select from different products and brands</li>
  <li><b>Right to be heard:</b> Ability to complain and be listened to</li>
  <li><b>Right to consumer education:</b> Access to knowledge on buying decisions</li>
  <li><b>Right to redress:</b> Compensation or replacement when wronged</li>
</ul>
<h4> Exam Idea</h4>
<pre>
Rights = Protection + Fair treatment in the market
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> CONSUMER RESPONSIBILITIES (DETAILED)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Checking product quality before purchase</li>
  <li>Using products according to instructions</li>
  <li>Paying for goods and services honestly</li>
  <li>Reporting fake or unsafe products</li>
  <li>Avoiding impulsive or unnecessary buying</li>
</ul>
<h4> Exam Comparison</h4>
<pre>
Rights = What the consumer is entitled to
Responsibilities = What the consumer must do
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> PRODUCT LABELING (INTERPRETATION SKILLS)</h3>
<p>
Product labels provide essential information that helps consumers make informed decisions before purchase.
</p>
<h4> Label Contains:</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Product name and brand</li>
  <li>Ingredients used</li>
  <li>Expiry or manufacture date</li>
  <li>Instructions for use</li>
  <li>Manufacturer details</li>
</ul>
<h4> Exam Idea</h4>
<pre>
Label = Product identity + Safety information + Usage guide
</pre>
<h3> ADVERTISING (CRITICAL THINKING)</h3>
<h4> Types of Advertising:</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Television advertisements</li>
  <li>Radio advertisements</li>
  <li>Social media marketing</li>
  <li>Posters and billboards</li>
</ul>
<h4> Positive Effects</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Informs consumers about products</li>
  <li>Promotes competition among companies</li>
  <li>Creates awareness of new products</li>
</ul>
<h4> Negative Effects</h4>
<ul>
  <li>May mislead consumers with false claims</li>
  <li>Encourages unnecessary spending</li>
  <li>Creates pressure to buy expensive goods</li>
</ul>
<h4> Exam Flow</h4>
<pre>
Advertisement → Awareness → Interest → Buying decision
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> MARKET SCAMS & FRAUD (APPLICATION)</h3>
<h4> Common Examples:</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Selling fake or counterfeit goods</li>
  <li>Overpricing products</li>
  <li>Selling expired items as fresh</li>
  <li>False or misleading advertising</li>
</ul>
<h4> Prevention Methods:</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Check expiry dates before buying</li>
  <li>Buy from trusted and licensed sellers</li>
  <li>Always request receipts</li>
  <li>Compare prices and product labels</li>
</ul>
<h4> Exam Idea</h4>
<pre>
Awareness → Verification → Safe purchasing → Consumer protection
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> QUALITY CONTROL (IMPORTANT CONCEPT)</h3>
<p>
Quality control is the process of checking products to ensure they meet safety and performance standards before reaching consumers.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Ensures product safety</li>
  <li>Maintains consistency in quality</li>
  <li>Protects consumers from harmful goods</li>
</ul>
<h4> Process Flow</h4>
<pre>
Production → Inspection → Testing → Approval → Market sale
</pre>
<h3> COMMON EXAM MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Mixing rights with responsibilities</li>
  <li>Listing advertising types without explanation</li>
  <li>Ignoring label interpretation questions</li>
  <li>Not linking quality control to consumer safety</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> FINAL MASTER IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Consumer Education = Rights + Responsibilities + Awareness + Protection
Informed buyer → Smart decisions → Safe market → Fair trade system
</pre>
',
  '[{"q":"Define consumer education","ans":"It is the study of how consumers make informed buying decisions and understand their rights","explain":"It helps people buy wisely","why":"Prevents exploitation"},{"q":"State 3 consumer rights","ans":"Right to safety, information, and choice","explain":"These protect buyers","why":"Ensures fairness"},{"q":"What is product labeling?","ans":"It is information written on a product package","explain":"Includes expiry date and ingredients","why":"Helps consumers make decisions"},{"q":"Give 3 examples of market fraud","ans":"Fake goods, overpricing, expired products","explain":"These trick consumers","why":"Helps in awareness"},{"q":"Explain importance of quality control","ans":"It ensures products meet required standards before sale","explain":"Protects consumers from harmful products","why":"Ensures safety and quality"},{"q":"Name two consumer responsibilities","ans":"Reading labels and comparing prices","why":"Being informed prevents consumers from being exploited."},{"q":"Why is labeling important?","ans":"It provides information on ingredients and expiration dates","why":"It helps the consumer make safe and informed choices."},{"q":"How can consumers avoid fraud?","ans":"By buying from reputable shops and checking receipts","why":"Verifying purchases ensures product authenticity."}]'
);

-- Topic: homescience -> personal_care_grooming -> Personal Care & Grooming (Full Master Notes)
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'homescience',
  'personal_care_grooming',
  'Personal Care & Grooming (Full Master Notes)',
  '<h2>Personal Care & Grooming</h2>
<h2> Personal Care & Grooming (Advanced Exam Notes)</h2>
<p>
Personal care and grooming refers to practices that maintain cleanliness, health, and appearance of the body, improving hygiene, confidence, and social well-being.
</p>
<h3> BODY CARE ROUTINES (DETAILED)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Daily bathing with clean water and soap to remove sweat and bacteria</li>
  <li>Wearing clean clothes to prevent skin infections and odor</li>
  <li>Trimming nails regularly to avoid dirt accumulation and disease spread</li>
  <li>Using deodorant or natural methods to control body odor</li>
  <li>Regular hand washing especially before eating and after using the toilet</li>
</ul>
<h4> Exam Idea</h4>
<pre>
Clean body → Removes germs → Prevents disease → Improves confidence
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> ORAL HYGIENE (DETAILED)</h3>
<p>Oral hygiene is the care of teeth, gums, and mouth to prevent dental diseases and maintain fresh breath.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Brush teeth twice daily (morning and before bed)</li>
  <li>Use fluoride toothpaste to strengthen enamel</li>
  <li>Floss to remove food particles between teeth</li>
  <li>Reduce intake of sugary foods and drinks</li>
  <li>Visit dentist regularly for check-ups</li>
</ul>
<h4> Exam Flow</h4>
<pre>
Sugar + bacteria → Acid → Tooth enamel damage → Cavities
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> HAIR CARE (DETAILED)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Wash hair regularly to remove dirt and oil buildup</li>
  <li>Use appropriate shampoo and hair oil for scalp health</li>
  <li>Avoid sharing combs, brushes, or hats (prevents lice infection)</li>
  <li>Keep hair neat, clean, and well-groomed</li>
</ul>
<h4> Key Idea</h4>
<pre>
Clean hair → Healthy scalp → Better appearance → Confidence
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> SKIN CARE (DETAILED)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Wash skin daily to remove sweat and bacteria</li>
  <li>Use moisturizer to prevent dryness and cracking</li>
  <li>Protect skin from excessive sunlight using clothing or sunscreen</li>
  <li>Avoid harsh chemicals that damage skin cells</li>
</ul>
<h4> Exam Idea</h4>
<pre>
Skin protection → Prevents infections + maintains appearance
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> PERSONAL APPEARANCE & SELF-IMAGE</h3>
<p>
Self-image is how a person sees themselves physically, emotionally, and socially.
</p>
<h4> Positive Self-Image</h4>
<ul>
  <li>High confidence</li>
  <li>Good hygiene habits</li>
  <li>Healthy social relationships</li>
</ul>
<h4> Negative Self-Image</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Low confidence</li>
  <li>Poor grooming habits</li>
  <li>Social withdrawal</li>
</ul>
<h4> Exam Summary</h4>
<pre>
Good grooming → Positive self-image → Confidence → Social success
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> EFFECTS OF POOR PERSONAL CARE (EXAM IMPORTANT)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Body odor and unpleasant smell</li>
  <li>Skin infections and rashes</li>
  <li>Tooth decay and gum diseases</li>
  <li>Low self-esteem and embarrassment</li>
  <li>Social rejection or isolation</li>
</ul>
<h4> Chain Effect</h4>
<pre>
Poor hygiene → Germ growth → Disease → Low confidence → Social problems
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> IMPORTANCE OF PERSONAL CARE</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Prevents communicable diseases</li>
  <li>Improves physical appearance</li>
  <li>Builds self-confidence</li>
  <li>Enhances social acceptance</li>
  <li>Promotes lifelong healthy habits</li>
</ul>
<h3> COMMON EXAM MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Confusing hygiene with appearance only</li>
  <li>Not linking hygiene to disease prevention</li>
  <li>Listing points without explanation</li>
  <li>Ignoring self-image concept</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> FINAL MASTER IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Personal Care = Hygiene + Grooming + Health + Confidence
Clean body → Healthy life → Positive self-image → Social success
</pre>
',
  '[{"q":"Define personal care","ans":"It is the practice of maintaining cleanliness and appearance of the body","explain":"It includes bathing, grooming, and hygiene","why":"Promotes health and confidence"},{"q":"State 3 oral hygiene practices","ans":"Brushing teeth, flossing, using toothpaste","explain":"These keep teeth and gums healthy","why":"Prevents dental diseases"},{"q":"Explain self-image","ans":"It is how a person views themselves physically and socially","explain":"It affects confidence and behavior","why":"Important for mental well-being"},{"q":"Give 3 effects of poor hygiene","ans":"Body odor, skin infections, tooth decay","explain":"Caused by lack of cleanliness","why":"Shows importance of hygiene"},{"q":"State 3 importance of personal care","ans":"Improves health, appearance, and confidence","explain":"Supports overall well-being","why":"Enhances quality of life"},{"q":"What is personal grooming?","ans":"Practicing good hygiene and keeping oneself neat","why":"It involves taking care of one’s skin, hair, and teeth."},{"q":"Why is brushing teeth important?","ans":"It prevents tooth decay and bad breath","why":"Removing plaque maintains dental health."},{"q":"How does grooming affect self-confidence?","ans":"It makes a person feel good and acceptable to others","why":"Positive physical appearance boosts mental well-being."}]'
);

-- Topic: homescience -> food_science -> Food Science Basics (Full Master Notes)
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'homescience',
  'food_science',
  'Food Science Basics (Full Master Notes)',
  '<h2>Food Science Basics</h2>
<h2> Food Science (Advanced Mastery Notes – Exam Structured)</h2>
<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Food science is the study of nutrients in food, how the body uses them, how food is digested, and how food components are identified and tested using scientific methods.
</p>
<h3> FOOD NUTRIENTS (DETAILED)</h3>
<h4> Macronutrients</h4>
<ul>
  <li><b>Carbohydrates:</b> Provide immediate energy (e.g., maize, rice, bread)</li>
  <li><b>Proteins:</b> Build and repair body tissues (e.g., meat, beans, eggs)</li>
  <li><b>Fats:</b> Store energy and insulate the body (e.g., oil, butter, nuts)</li>
</ul>
<h4> Micronutrients</h4>
<ul>
  <li><b>Vitamins:</b> Protect against diseases (e.g., Vitamin C prevents scurvy)</li>
  <li><b>Minerals:</b> Support body functions (e.g., calcium builds bones)</li>
</ul>
<h4> Examiner Summary</h4>
<pre>
Carbohydrates → Quick energy
Proteins → Growth and repair
Fats → Long-term energy storage
Vitamins/Minerals → Protection and regulation
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> DIGESTION PROCESS (WITH FUNCTIONS)</h3>
<pre>
Mouth → Esophagus → Stomach → Small Intestine → Large Intestine
</pre>
<h4> Detailed Explanation</h4>
<ul>
  <li><b>Mouth:</b> Mechanical digestion (chewing) + chemical digestion (saliva breaks starch)</li>
  <li><b>Esophagus:</b> Moves food using peristalsis (wave-like movement)</li>
  <li><b>Stomach:</b> Produces acid and enzymes to digest proteins</li>
  <li><b>Small Intestine:</b> Main site of digestion and absorption of nutrients into blood</li>
  <li><b>Large Intestine:</b> Absorbs water and forms feces</li>
</ul>
<h4> Exam Flow</h4>
<pre>
Ingestion → Digestion → Absorption → Assimilation → Egestion
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> FOOD TESTS (WITH PROCEDURES)</h3>
<h4>1. Starch Test (Iodine)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Add iodine solution to food sample</li>
  <li><b>Result:</b> Blue-black = starch present</li>
</ul>
<h4>2. Protein Test (Biuret)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Add sodium hydroxide + copper sulfate</li>
  <li><b>Result:</b> Purple = protein present</li>
</ul>
<h4>3. Fat Test</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Rub food on paper</li>
  <li><b>Result:</b> Translucent spot = fat present</li>
</ul>
<h4> Comparison Table</h4>
<pre>
Starch  → Blue-black
Protein → Purple
Fat     → Greasy transparent spot
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> FOOD ENERGY VALUES (APPLICATION)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Carbohydrates = 4 kcal/g</li>
  <li>Proteins = 4 kcal/g</li>
  <li>Fats = 9 kcal/g</li>
</ul>
<h4> Exam Insight</h4>
<pre>
A diet high in fat provides more energy but increases health risks if excessive.
</pre>
<h4> Example Question</h4>
<p><b>Q:</b> Why do fats provide more energy than carbohydrates?</p>
<p><b>A:</b> Because fats contain more energy-rich bonds per gram than carbohydrates.</p>
<hr>
<h3> MALNUTRITION (DEEPER UNDERSTANDING)</h3
<h4>Kwashiorkor</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Cause: Protein deficiency</li>
  <li>Effect: Swollen belly, weak immunity</li>
</ul>
<h4>Marasmus</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Cause: Severe lack of all nutrients</li>
  <li>Effect: Severe weight loss, muscle wasting</li>
</ul>
<h4>Obesity</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Cause: Excess calorie intake</li>
  <li>Effect: Heart disease, diabetes risk</li>
</ul>
<h4> Comparison</h4>
<pre>
Kwashiorkor → Protein lack
Marasmus → Total starvation
Obesity → Excess energy intake
</pre>
<h3> COMMON EXAM MISTAKES (IMPORTANT)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Confusing absorption with digestion</li>
  <li>Wrong food test results interpretation</li>
  <li>Mixing functions of nutrients</li>
  <li>Skipping digestion stages in order</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> FINAL MASTER IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Food Science = Nutrition + Digestion + Testing + Health Application
Food → Energy → Growth → Maintenance → Survival
Balanced diet = Healthy body system
</pre>
',
  '[{"q":"Define food science","ans":"It is the study of nutrients, digestion, and food composition","explain":"It explains how food works in the body","why":"Helps improve nutrition"},{"q":"State 3 macronutrients","ans":"Carbohydrates, proteins, fats","explain":"They are needed in large amounts","why":"Provide energy and growth"},{"q":"Explain iodine test","ans":"It is used to test for starch in food","explain":"Food turns blue-black if starch is present","why":"Identifies food composition"},{"q":"Differentiate kwashiorkor and marasmus","ans":"Kwashiorkor is protein deficiency while marasmus is total nutrient deficiency","explain":"Both are malnutrition diseases","why":"Helps in diagnosis"},{"q":"State 3 importance of food science","ans":"Prevents malnutrition, improves health, supports diet planning","explain":"Helps understand food and nutrition","why":"Promotes healthy living"},{"q":"Name two micronutrients","ans":"Vitamins and minerals","why":"They are needed in smaller amounts for bodily protection."},{"q":"What is the function of fats in the body?","ans":"To provide insulation and concentrated energy","why":"Fats store energy for later use."},{"q":"What color change shows starch is present?","ans":"Blue-black","why":"Iodine reacts with starch to form a deep blue-black color."}]'
);

-- Topic: homescience -> community_health -> Community Health (Full Master Notes)
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'homescience',
  'community_health',
  'Community Health (Full Master Notes)',
  '<h2>Community Health</h2>
<h2> Community Health (Advanced Mastery Notes – Exam Structured)</h2>
<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Community health is a branch of health science that focuses on protecting, preventing, and improving the health of people in a community through organized services, education, and environmental management.
</p>
<h3> PRIMARY HEALTH CARE (PHC)</h3>
<p>
Primary health care refers to basic health services provided at community level to prevent diseases, promote health, and treat common illnesses.
</p>
<h4> Examples</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Vaccination and immunization</li>
  <li>Health education and awareness</li>
  <li>Maternal and child health services</li>
  <li>Treatment of minor illnesses (flu, malaria, wounds)</li>
</ul>
<h4> Process Flow</h4>
<pre>
Community → Clinic/Health Post → Basic Treatment → Referral to Hospital (if severe)
</pre>
<h4> Exam Idea</h4>
<p>PHC reduces disease burden by preventing illness before it becomes severe.</p>
<hr>
<h3> HEALTH CENTERS VS HOSPITALS</h3>
<h4> Health Centers</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Provide basic medical care</li>
  <li>Treat minor illnesses</li>
  <li>Located within communities for easy access</li>
  <li>Focus on prevention and early treatment</li>
</ul>
<h4> Hospitals</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Provide specialized and advanced treatment</li>
  <li>Have surgeons and medical specialists</li>
  <li>Handle emergencies and complex diseases</li>
  <li>Offer surgery and intensive care</li>
</ul>
<h4> Comparison Idea</h4>
<pre>
Health Center = First line / Basic care
Hospital = Advanced / Specialized care
</pre>
<hr>
<h3>‍ ROLES OF HEALTH WORKERS</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Doctors:</b> Diagnose diseases and prescribe treatment</li>
  <li><b>Nurses:</b> Care for patients and administer medication</li>
  <li><b>Lab Technicians:</b> Conduct medical tests and analysis</li>
  <li><b>Community Health Workers:</b> Educate and support households</li>
  <li><b>Pharmacists:</b> Dispense and explain medicines</li>
</ul>
<h4> Key Idea</h4>
<pre>
Health system = Teamwork of professionals working together
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> ENVIRONMENTAL HEALTH SERVICES</h3>
<p>
These are services that ensure a clean, safe, and healthy environment to prevent diseases.
</p>
<h4> Examples</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Waste collection and disposal</li>
  <li>Provision of clean water</li>
  <li>Sanitation and sewage systems</li>
  <li>Pest and vector control (mosquitoes, rats)</li>
  <li>Food safety inspection</li>
</ul>
<h4> Cause–Effect Idea</h4>
<pre>
Clean environment → Reduced disease transmission → Healthy community
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> PUBLIC HEALTH EDUCATION PROGRAMS</h3>
<p>
These are organized programs that teach communities how to prevent diseases and improve overall health behavior.
</p>
<h4> Examples</h4>
<ul>
  <li>HIV/AIDS awareness campaigns</li>
  <li>Immunization drives</li>
  <li>Nutrition education programs</li>
  <li>Hygiene and sanitation campaigns</li>
</ul>
<h4> Learning Flow</h4>
<pre>
Education → Awareness → Behavior Change → Disease Prevention
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> IMPORTANCE OF COMMUNITY HEALTH</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Prevents disease outbreaks and epidemics</li>
  <li>Improves hygiene and sanitation practices</li>
  <li>Reduces pressure on hospitals</li>
  <li>Encourages healthy lifestyles</li>
  <li>Increases life expectancy</li>
  <li>Improves productivity in society</li>
</ul>
<h3> COMMON EXAM MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Confusing PHC with hospital services</li>
  <li>Listing roles without explanation</li>
  <li>Not linking environment to health</li>
  <li>Ignoring examples in answers</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> FINAL MASTER IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Community Health = Prevention + Education + Treatment + Environment
Healthy people → Strong community → Productive nation
</pre>
',
  '[{"q":"Define community health","ans":"It is the branch of health that focuses on improving health in a community","explain":"It involves prevention and education","why":"Improves public well-being"},{"q":"State 3 roles of health workers","ans":"Doctors treat diseases, nurses care for patients, lab technicians test samples","explain":"Each has a specific role","why":"Ensures proper healthcare delivery"},{"q":"Differentiate health centers and hospitals","ans":"Health centers provide basic care while hospitals provide advanced treatment","explain":"They differ in level of service","why":"Helps patients seek correct care"},{"q":"Explain primary health care","ans":"Basic health services provided at community level","explain":"Includes vaccination and treatment of minor illnesses","why":"Prevents serious diseases"},{"q":"Give 3 importance of community health","ans":"Prevents diseases, improves hygiene, promotes health education","explain":"Focuses on prevention","why":"Improves quality of life"},{"q":"Name two environmental health services","ans":"Waste disposal and water treatment","why":"These prevent community-wide diseases."},{"q":"What is the role of nurses?","ans":"To provide medical care and health education","why":"They support doctors and patients in healthcare facilities."},{"q":"Why are health centers important?","ans":"They provide accessible healthcare to the local community","why":"Proximity ensures quick medical intervention."}]'
);

-- Topic: homescience -> textile_design_culture -> Textile Design & Culture (Full Master Notes)
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'homescience',
  'textile_design_culture',
  'Textile Design & Culture (Full Master Notes)',
  '<h2>Textile Design & Culture</h2>
<h2> Textile Design & Culture (Advanced Mastery Notes – Exam Structured)</h2>
<h3> Definition</h3>
<p>
Textile design and culture refers to the study of fabrics, clothing traditions, fashion trends, and decorative techniques used in creating garments that express identity, creativity, and cultural heritage.
</p>
<h3> TRADITIONAL CLOTHING & CULTURE</h3>
<p>
Traditional clothing reflects cultural identity, history, environment, and social values of a community.
</p>
<h4> Examples (Kenyan Communities)</h4>
<ul>
  <li><b>Kikuyu:</b> Shukas, bead ornaments, animal skin (historically)</li>
  <li><b>Maasai:</b> Red shukas, beadwork, symbolic jewelry</li>
  <li><b>Luo:</b> Animal skin (traditional), modern adapted clothing styles</li>
</ul>
<h4> Exam Idea</h4>
<pre>
Clothing = Identity + Culture + History + Environment
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> FASHION TRENDS</h3>
<p>
Fashion trends refer to changing styles of clothing influenced by society, media, technology, and culture.
</p>
<h4> Types of Fashion</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Traditional fashion</li>
  <li>Modern fashion</li>
  <li>Casual wear</li>
  <li>Formal wear</li>
  <li>Street fashion</li>
</ul>
<h4> Key Idea</h4>
<pre>
Fashion change = Society + Culture + Technology + Media influence
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> TEXTILE DYEING & PRINTING</h3>
<h4> Dyeing</h4>
<p>
Dyeing is the process of adding permanent color to fabric using dyes.
</p>
<h4> Printing</h4>
<p>
Printing is the application of patterns or designs onto fabric surfaces.
</p>
<h4> Methods</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Tie and dye</li>
  <li>Block printing</li>
  <li>Screen printing</li>
</ul>
<h4> Process Diagram</h4>
<pre>
White fabric → Dye/print → Patterned colored fabric
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> FABRIC DECORATION TECHNIQUES</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Embroidery</li>
  <li>Appliqué</li>
  <li>Beadwork</li>
  <li>Fabric painting</li>
</ul>
<h4> Concept Flow</h4>
<pre>
Plain fabric → Decoration → Attractive textile product
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> EMBROIDERY & BEADWORK</h3>
<h4> Embroidery</h4>
<p>
Embroidery is decorating fabric using needle and thread to create patterns and designs.
</p>
<h4> Beadwork</h4>
<p>
Beadwork involves attaching beads onto fabric or accessories to form decorative patterns.
</p>
<h4> Common Stitches</h4>
<pre>
Running stitch:   ---- ---- ----
Back stitch:      ========
Chain stitch:     O-O-O-O-O
Cross stitch:     X X X X X
</pre>
<h4> Exam Tip</h4>
<p>Always state both the function and appearance when describing stitches.</p>
<hr>
<h3> IMPORTANCE OF TEXTILE DESIGN</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Preserves cultural identity and heritage</li>
  <li>Creates employment opportunities (fashion industry)</li>
  <li>Encourages creativity and innovation</li>
  <li>Supports local and global economies</li>
  <li>Enhances beauty and personal expression</li>
  <li>Promotes tourism through cultural fashion</li>
</ul>
<h3> COMMON EXAM MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Confusing dyeing with printing</li>
  <li>Listing techniques without explanation</li>
  <li>Not linking clothing to culture</li>
  <li>Ignoring examples in answers</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> FINAL MASTER IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Textile Design = Culture + Creativity + Identity
Fabric → Design → Cultural expression → Fashion industry
</pre>
 EXAM QUESTIONS
',
  '[{"q":"Define textile design","ans":"It is the process of creating and decorating fabrics and clothing designs","explain":"It includes dyeing, printing, and embroidery","why":"Enhances fabric appearance"},{"q":"State 3 methods of fabric decoration","ans":"Embroidery, beadwork, fabric painting","explain":"These improve fabric appearance","why":"Used in fashion design"},{"q":"Explain tie and dye","ans":"It is a method of decorating fabric by tying parts before dyeing","explain":"Creates patterns on cloth","why":"Used for artistic designs"},{"q":"Give 3 importance of fashion","ans":"Expresses identity, creates jobs, supports culture","explain":"Fashion has social and economic value","why":"Important in society"},{"q":"Differentiate dyeing and printing","ans":"Dyeing colors the entire fabric while printing adds patterns on surface","explain":"They are different decoration methods","why":"Helps in textile production understanding"},{"q":"What is embroidery?","ans":"The art of decorating fabric using a needle and thread","why":"It adds aesthetic value to textiles."},{"q":"Give 2 examples of textile printing methods","ans":"Block printing and screen printing","why":"These methods transfer patterns onto cloths."},{"q":"Why is beadwork important in culture?","ans":"It represents traditions and social status","why":"Different colors and patterns hold specific cultural meanings."}]'
);

-- Topic: kiswahili -> sarufi -> Sarufi (Grammar) Full Master Notes
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'kiswahili',
  'sarufi',
  'Sarufi (Grammar) Full Master Notes',
  '<h2>Sarufi (Grammar)</h2>

<p>Sarufi ni mfumo wa kanuni zinazotumika katika lugha ya Kiswahili ili kupanga maneno kwa njia sahihi na yenye maana.</p>

<hr>

<h3> MUUNDO WA SENTENSI</h3>

<pre>
Mhusika + Kitenzi + Kijalizo

Mfano:
Mtoto anasoma kitabu
</pre>

<hr>

<h3> 7 SUBTOPICS</h3>

<ul>
<li>Nomino (Majina)</li>
<li>Viwakilishi</li>
<li>Vitenzi</li>
<li>Vivumishi</li>
<li>Vielezi</li>
<li>Viunganishi</li>
<li>Miundo ya sentensi</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> MIFANO ILIYOELEZWA</h3>

<h4>1. Nomino</h4>
<p>Nomino ni maneno yanayotaja vitu, watu au mahali.</p>
<p><b>Mfano:</b> mtoto, shule, Kenya</p>

<h4>2. Kitenzi</h4>
<p>Kitenzi ni neno linaloonyesha kitendo.</p>
<p><b>Mfano:</b> kula, kusoma, kukimbia</p>

<h4>3. Kivumishi</h4>
<p>Kivumishi huelezea nomino.</p>
<p><b>Mfano:</b> mtoto mzuri, nyumba kubwa</p>

<hr>

<h3> MFANO WA SENTENSI</h3>

<pre>
Mtoto mzuri anasoma kitabu kikubwa
</pre>

<p><b>Uchambuzi:</b></p>
<ul>
<li>Mtoto = nomino</li>
<li>Mzuri = kivumishi</li>
<li>Anasoma = kitenzi</li>
<li>Kitabu = nomino</li>
<li>Kikubwa = kivumishi</li>
</ul>
',
  '[{"q":"Sarufi ni nini?","hint":"Mfumo wa lugha","ans":"Sarufi ni kanuni za kupanga maneno katika lugha","explain":"Inasaidia kuunda sentensi sahihi","why":"Ili kuelewa lugha vizuri"},{"q":"Nomino ni nini?","hint":"Majina ya vitu","ans":"Nomino ni maneno yanayotaja watu, vitu au mahali","explain":"Mfano: shule, mtoto, Nairobi","why":"Kutambua vitu katika lugha"},{"q":"Tofautisha nomino na kitenzi","hint":"Jina vs kitendo","ans":"Nomino ni jina la kitu wakati kitenzi ni kitendo","explain":"Kula ni kitenzi, mtoto ni nomino","why":"Kuelewa muundo wa sentensi"},{"q":"Eleza kitenzi","hint":"Action word","ans":"Kitenzi ni neno linaloonyesha kitendo","explain":"Mfano: kusoma, kucheza","why":"Huonyesha kinachofanyika"},{"q":"Tunga sentensi sahihi","hint":"Subject + verb","ans":"Mtoto anasoma kitabu","explain":"Ina mhusika, kitendo na kitu","why":"Kuonyesha matumizi ya sarufi"},{"q":"Taja aina 3 za nomino","hint":"Types","ans":"Nomino za kawaida, nomino maalum, nomino za mahali","explain":"Zinatofautiana kwa matumizi","why":"Kupanua msamiati"},{"q":"Eleza kivumishi","hint":"Describe noun","ans":"Kivumishi ni neno linaloelezea nomino","explain":"Mfano: mzuri, kubwa","why":"Huongeza maelezo"},{"q":"Tofautisha kivumishi na kielezi","hint":"Noun vs verb description","ans":"Kivumishi huelezea nomino, kielezi huelezea kitenzi","explain":"Mzuri vs haraka","why":"Kuelewa tofauti za sarufi"},{"q":"Eleza muundo wa sentensi","hint":"Structure","ans":"Mhusika + kitenzi + kijalizo","explain":"Mfano: mtoto anasoma kitabu","why":"Kujenga sentensi sahihi"},{"q":"Kwa nini sarufi ni muhimu?","hint":"Importance","ans":"Inasaidia kuwasiliana kwa usahihi","explain":"Bila sarufi lugha haieleweki","why":"Huimarisha mawasiliano"}]'
);

-- Topic: kiswahili -> ufahamu -> Ufahamu (Comprehension) Full Master Notes
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'kiswahili',
  'ufahamu',
  'Ufahamu (Comprehension) Full Master Notes',
  '<h2>Ufahamu (Comprehension)</h2>

<p>Ufahamu ni uwezo wa kusoma, kuelewa na kutafsiri habari ili kujibu maswali kwa usahihi.</p>

<hr>

<h3> MCHAKATO WA UFAHAMU</h3>

<pre>
Soma → Elewa → Changanua → Jibu → Hakiki
</pre>

<hr>

<h3> 7 SUBTOPICS</h3>

<ul>
<li>Kusoma kwa makini</li>
<li>Kutambua mawazo makuu</li>
<li>Kutafuta maana ya maneno</li>
<li>Kujibu maswali ya moja kwa moja</li>
<li>Kujibu maswali ya kudokeza</li>
<li>Kufupisha habari</li>
<li>Uchambuzi wa picha/chati</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> MIFANO ILIYOELEZWA</h3>

<h4>1. Mawazo makuu</h4>
<p><b>Maelezo:</b> Haya ni ujumbe mkuu wa kifungu.</p>
<p><b>Mfano:</b> Kifungu kuhusu mazingira → “kulinda mazingira”</p>

<h4>2. Maana ya maneno</h4>
<p><b>Maelezo:</b> Kutafuta maana ya neno kulingana na muktadha.</p>
<p><b>Mfano:</b> “jasiri” = mtu asiyeogopa</p>

<h4>3. Kujibu maswali ya kudokeza</h4>
<p><b>Maelezo:</b> Majibu hayapo moja kwa moja kwenye kifungu.</p>
<p><b>Mfano:</b> Unatakiwa kufikiri zaidi ya maandishi</p>

<hr>

<h3> MFANO WA UFAHAMU</h3>

<pre>
Kifungu:
"Mtoto alisoma kwa bidii na kufaulu mtihani wake."

Swali: Kwa nini mtoto alifaulu?
Jibu: Kwa sababu alisoma kwa bidii.
</pre>
',
  '[{"q":"Ufahamu ni nini?","hint":"Kuelewa habari","ans":"Ufahamu ni uwezo wa kusoma na kuelewa habari","explain":"Huhusisha kusoma na kujibu maswali","why":"Kuelewa maandishi"},{"q":"Taja hatua 3 za ufahamu","hint":"Process","ans":"Kusoma, kuelewa, kujibu","explain":"Hizi ni hatua kuu za ufahamu","why":"Kufanikisha kuelewa habari"},{"q":"Mawazo makuu ni nini?","hint":"Main idea","ans":"Ni ujumbe mkuu wa kifungu","explain":"Hueleza kinachozungumziwa","why":"Kuelewa lengo la kifungu"},{"q":"Tofautisha maswali ya moja kwa moja na ya kudokeza","hint":"Direct vs indirect","ans":"Moja kwa moja yanapatikana kwenye kifungu, kudokeza yanahitaji kufikiria","explain":"Moja ni wazi, nyingine si wazi","why":"Kuboresha uelewa wa maswali"},{"q":"Eleza maana ya neno kulingana na muktadha","hint":"Context meaning","ans":"Ni kutafuta maana ya neno kulingana na sentensi","explain":"Maana hubadilika kulingana na matumizi","why":"Kuelewa lugha vizuri"},{"q":"Soma: ''Mvua ilinyesha sana na mafuriko yakatokea.'' Eleza tatizo lililotokea","hint":"Floods","ans":"Mafuriko yalitokea","explain":"Mvua nyingi ilisababisha maji kujaa","why":"Kupima ufahamu"},{"q":"Kwa nini kusoma kwa makini ni muhimu?","hint":"Careful reading","ans":"Ili kuelewa habari vizuri","explain":"Hupunguza makosa ya uelewa","why":"Huongeza ufanisi wa kujibu"},{"q":"Eleza ufupisho wa habari","hint":"Summary","ans":"Ni kuandika habari kwa maneno machache","explain":"Huchagua pointi muhimu","why":"Hupunguza urefu wa maandishi"},{"q":"Taja aina 3 za maswali ya ufahamu","hint":"Types","ans":"Moja kwa moja, kudokeza, na ya msamiati","explain":"Kila aina ina mbinu tofauti","why":"Kuboresha kujibu mitihani"},{"q":"Kwa nini ufahamu ni muhimu?","hint":"Importance","ans":"Husaidia kuelewa na kujibu maswali kwa usahihi","explain":"Ni msingi wa mitihani","why":"Huongeza alama"}]'
);

-- Topic: kiswahili -> insha -> Insha (Composition Writing) Full Master Notes
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'kiswahili',
  'insha',
  'Insha (Composition Writing) Full Master Notes',
  '<h2>Insha (Composition Writing)</h2>

<p>Insha ni maandishi yanayoandikwa kwa mpangilio maalum ili kuwasilisha mawazo, habari au hisia.</p>

<hr>

<h3> MUUNDO WA INSHA</h3>

<pre>
Utangulizi → Kiini → Hitimisho
</pre>

<hr>

<h3> 7 SUBTOPICS</h3>

<ul>
<li>Insha za masimulizi</li>
<li>Insha za maelezo</li>
<li>Insha za hoja</li>
<li>Insha za mazungumzo</li>
<li>Muundo wa insha</li>
<li>Matumizi ya lugha fasaha</li>
<li>Alama za uandishi</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> AINA ZA INSHA (MAELEZO)</h3>

<h4>1. Insha ya masimulizi</h4>
<p>Hueleza hadithi au tukio kwa mpangilio wa wakati.</p>

<h4>2. Insha ya maelezo</h4>
<p>Hueleza jambo kwa undani ili msomaji aelewe.</p>

<h4>3. Insha ya hoja</h4>
<p>Hutoa mawazo na sababu za kuunga au kupinga jambo.</p>

<h4>4. Insha ya mazungumzo</h4>
<p>Huandikwa kama maongezi kati ya watu wawili au zaidi.</p>

<hr>

<h3> MFANO WA MUUNDO</h3>

<pre>
UTANGULIZI:
Eleza mada kwa ufupi

KIINI:
Maelezo ya kina (paragraphs)

HITIMISHO:
Muhtasari na maoni
</pre>

<hr>

<h3> MFANO WA SENTENSI</h3>

<p><b>Mada:</b> “Safari ya shuleni”</p>

<p><b>Sentensi:</b> Niliondoka nyumbani mapema nikielekea shuleni.</p>
',
  '[{"q":"Insha ni nini?","hint":"Writing composition","ans":"Insha ni maandishi yanayoeleza mawazo au habari kwa mpangilio","explain":"Ina utangulizi, kiini na hitimisho","why":"Kuwasiliana kwa maandishi"},{"q":"Taja sehemu 3 za insha","hint":"Structure","ans":"Utangulizi, kiini, hitimisho","explain":"Hizi ni sehemu kuu za insha","why":"Kusaidia kupanga mawazo"},{"q":"Tofautisha insha ya masimulizi na maelezo","hint":"Story vs description","ans":"Masimulizi hueleza hadithi, maelezo hueleza jambo kwa undani","explain":"Moja ni tukio, nyingine ni ufafanuzi","why":"Kuelewa aina za insha"},{"q":"Eleza insha ya hoja","hint":"Argumentative","ans":"Ni insha inayotoa mawazo ya kuunga au kupinga jambo","explain":"Ina sababu na ushahidi","why":"Kuimarisha fikra za hoja"},{"q":"Kwa nini utangulizi ni muhimu?","hint":"Introduction","ans":"Hutambulisha mada ya insha","explain":"Humsaidia msomaji kuelewa mada","why":"Kuvutia msomaji"},{"q":"Andika mfano wa sentensi ya insha ya masimulizi","hint":"Story sentence","ans":"Nilifika shuleni mapema na kuanza masomo","explain":"Inaonyesha tukio kwa mpangilio","why":"Mfano wa matumizi ya lugha"},{"q":"Taja alama 3 za uandishi","hint":"Punctuation","ans":"Koma, nukta, alama ya kuuliza","explain":"Zinasaidia kupanga sentensi","why":"Huongeza uelewa"},{"q":"Eleza umuhimu wa insha","hint":"Importance","ans":"Husaidia kuwasilisha mawazo kwa maandishi","explain":"Inaboresha uandishi","why":"Muhimu katika mitihani"},{"q":"Andika tofauti ya kiini na hitimisho","hint":"Body vs conclusion","ans":"Kiini ni maelezo ya kina, hitimisho ni muhtasari","explain":"Zina kazi tofauti","why":"Kumaliza insha vizuri"},{"q":"Kwa nini lugha fasaha ni muhimu?","hint":"Clear language","ans":"Ili msomaji aelewe kwa urahisi","explain":"Inaepuka makosa ya lugha","why":"Huongeza alama"}]'
);

-- Topic: kiswahili -> fasihi_simulizi -> Fasihi Simulizi (Oral Literature) Full Master Notes
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'kiswahili',
  'fasihi_simulizi',
  'Fasihi Simulizi (Oral Literature) Full Master Notes',
  '<h2>Fasihi Simulizi</h2>
<p>Fasihi simulizi ni aina ya fasihi inayopitishwa kwa mdomo kutoka kizazi kimoja hadi kingine.</p>

<hr>

<h3> AINA KUU</h3>

<pre>
Methali → Nahau → Vitendawili → Hadithi → Nyimbo → Misemo → Majigambo
</pre>

<hr>

<h3> 7 SUBTOPICS</h3>

<ul>
<li>Methali</li>
<li>Nahau</li>
<li>Vitendawili</li>
<li>Hadithi za jadi</li>
<li>Nyimbo za jadi</li>
<li>Misemo</li>
<li>Majigambo</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> MAELEZO NA MIFANO</h3>

<h4>1. Methali</h4>
<p>Ni maneno mafupi yenye funzo.</p>
<p><b>Mfano:</b> Haraka haraka haina baraka</p>

<h4>2. Nahau</h4>
<p>Ni maneno yenye maana fiche.</p>
<p><b>Mfano:</b> Kuosha mikono = kuacha jambo</p>

<h4>3. Kitendawili</h4>
<p>Ni swali la fumbo linalohitaji jibu la kufikirika.</p>
<p><b>Mfano:</b> Kula usiku mchana hulia? (Jibu: taa)</p>

<h4>4. Hadithi</h4>
<p>Ni simulizi ya tukio au maisha ya kubuni au ya kweli.</p>

<h4>5. Nyimbo</h4>
<p>Zinaimbwa kuelimisha au kuburudisha.</p>

<hr>

<h3> MFANO WA FASIHI</h3>

<pre>
Methali: Mvumilivu hula mbivu
Maana: Uvumilivu huleta mafanikio
</pre>
',
  '[{"q":"Fasihi simulizi ni nini?","hint":"Oral literature","ans":"Ni fasihi inayopitishwa kwa mdomo","explain":"Haitumii maandishi","why":"Kuhifadhi utamaduni"},{"q":"Taja aina 3 za fasihi simulizi","hint":"Types","ans":"Methali, nahau, vitendawili","explain":"Hizi ni aina kuu za fasihi simulizi","why":"Kuelewa fasihi"},{"q":"Methali ni nini?","hint":"Proverb","ans":"Ni usemi mfupi wenye funzo","explain":"Una maana ya elimu","why":"Kufundisha maadili"},{"q":"Tofautisha methali na nahau","hint":"Meaning vs hidden meaning","ans":"Methali ni usemi wenye funzo wazi, nahau ni maana fiche","explain":"Nahau huficha maana","why":"Kuelewa matumizi ya lugha"},{"q":"Eleza kitendawili","hint":"Riddle","ans":"Ni swali la fumbo linalohitaji jibu la kufikirika","explain":"Huchochea fikra","why":"Kukuza ubunifu"},{"q":"Toa mfano wa methali","hint":"Example","ans":"Haraka haraka haina baraka","explain":"Inaonya dhidi ya haraka","why":"Inafundisha uvumilivu"},{"q":"Eleza maana ya nahau ''kuosha mikono''","hint":"idiom meaning","ans":"Kuacha jambo au kujitoa","explain":"Si maana halisi ya kuosha mikono","why":"Lugha ya kifasihi"},{"q":"Taja kazi ya nyimbo","hint":"Function","ans":"Kuburudisha na kuelimisha","explain":"Zina ujumbe na hisia","why":"Kuhifadhi utamaduni"},{"q":"Kwa nini fasihi simulizi ni muhimu?","hint":"Importance","ans":"Huhifadhi utamaduni na maadili","explain":"Hupitishwa kizazi hadi kizazi","why":"Kuhifadhi historia"},{"q":"Eleza majigambo","hint":"Pride speech","ans":"Ni usemi wa kujisifu","explain":"Hutumika kuonyesha uwezo","why":"Utambulisho wa jamii"}]'
);

-- Topic: kiswahili -> fasihi_andishi -> A — Riwaya (Novels)
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'kiswahili',
  'fasihi_andishi',
  'A — Riwaya (Novels)',
  '<h2>Riwaya (Novels)</h2>

<h3> MAELEZO / NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Riwaya</b> → ni hadithi ndefu iliyoandikwa kwa maandishi</li>
<li><b>Urefu</b> → huwa na kurasa nyingi na matukio mengi</li>
<li><b>Wahusika</b> → huwa wengi (wakuu na wadogo)</li>
<li><b>Mandhari</b> → inaweza kuwa kijijini, mjini au sehemu mbalimbali</li>
<li><b>Dhamira</b> → ujumbe mkuu kama umaskini, mapenzi au uadilifu</li>
<li><b>Mtiririko</b> → huonyesha mwanzo, kati na mwisho wa hadithi</li>
<li><b>Mtindo</b> → mwandishi hutumia lugha ya kisanaa na maelezo marefu</li>
</ul>

<h3> WAZO KUU</h3>
<pre>
Riwaya ni hadithi ndefu inayoeleza maisha ya watu kwa undani kupitia maandishi.
</pre>

<h3> SIFA ZA RIWAYA</h3>
<ul>
<li> Ndefu kuliko hadithi fupi</li>
<li> Ina wahusika wengi</li>
<li> Ina dhamira zaidi ya moja</li>
<li> Ina mandhari mbalimbali</li>
<li> Huandikwa kwa mtindo wa kifasihi</li>
</ul>

<h3> MFANO WA RIWAYA</h3>
<pre>
"Chozi la Heri" — riwaya inayoonyesha maisha ya majonzi na changamoto za kijamii
</pre>

<h3> MAKOSA YA KAWAIDA</h3>
<ul>
<li> Kudhani riwaya ni hadithi fupi</li>
<li> Kuchanganya riwaya na tamthilia</li>
<li> Kushindwa kutambua dhamira kuu</li>
<li> Kutokuchambua wahusika vizuri</li>
</ul>

<h3> MATUMIZI HALISI</h3>
<ul>
<li> Hufundisha maadili na maisha</li>
<li> Hukuza fikra na ubunifu</li>
<li> Husaidia kuelewa jamii</li>
<li> Hutumika katika masomo ya fasihi</li>
<li> Hutoa mafunzo ya maisha kupitia hadithi</li>
</ul>
',
  '[{"q":"Riwaya ni nini?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria hadithi ndefu","Step 2: maandishi"],"ans":"Ni hadithi ndefu iliyoandikwa kwa maandishi","why":"ufafanuzi"},{"q":"Taja sifa moja ya riwaya","steps":["Step 1: fikiria urefu","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Ni ndefu na ina wahusika wengi","why":"sifa"},{"q":"Riwaya hutofautianaje na hadithi fupi?","steps":["Step 1: linganisha urefu","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Riwaya ni ndefu zaidi kuliko hadithi fupi","why":"tofauti"},{"q":"Wahusika wa riwaya ni akina nani?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria watu","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Ni watu wanaoshiriki katika hadithi","why":"wahusika"},{"q":"Kwa nini riwaya ni muhimu?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria elimu","Step 2: jamii"],"ans":"Hufundisha maadili na maisha","why":"umuhimu"}]'
);

-- Topic: kiswahili -> fasihi_andishi -> B — Tamthilia (Drama)
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'kiswahili',
  'fasihi_andishi',
  'B — Tamthilia (Drama)',
  '<h2>Tamthilia (Drama)</h2>

<h3> MAELEZO / NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Tamthilia</b> → ni kazi ya fasihi inayoandikwa kwa mazungumzo na kuigizwa jukwaani</li>
<li><b>Uigizaji</b> → wahusika huigiza matendo mbele ya hadhira</li>
<li><b>Maelekezo ya jukwaa</b> → mwandishi huonyesha jinsi matukio yanavyotendeka</li>
<li><b>Wahusika</b> → huzungumza kwa kupokezana kama mazungumzo</li>
<li><b>Mandhari</b> → inaweza kubadilika kulingana na tukio</li>
<li><b>Dhamira</b> → ujumbe kama uadilifu, mapambano au rushwa</li>
</ul>

<h3> WAZO KUU</h3>
<pre>
Tamthilia ni kazi ya fasihi inayoandikwa kwa mazungumzo na kuigizwa jukwaani mbele ya hadhira.
</pre>

<h3> SIFA ZA TAMTHILIA</h3>
<ul>
<li> Huigizwa jukwaani</li>
<li> Hutumia mazungumzo ya moja kwa moja</li>
<li> Ina wahusika wanaopokezana kuzungumza</li>
<li> Ina maelekezo ya jukwaa (stage directions)</li>
<li> Huonyesha mgogoro kati ya wahusika</li>
</ul>

<h3> MFANO WA TAMTHILIA</h3>
<pre>
"Kigogo" — tamthilia inayoonyesha masuala ya uongozi na rushwa
</pre>

<h3> MUUNDO WA TAMTHILIA</h3>
<pre>
Onyesho (Scene) → Mgawanyo wa matukio

Mazungumzo → Wahusika wanapokezana

Maelekezo ya jukwaa → jinsi tukio linavyofanyika
</pre>

<h3> MAKOSA YA KAWAIDA</h3>
<ul>
<li> Kuchanganya tamthilia na riwaya</li>
<li> Kutotambua maelekezo ya jukwaa</li>
<li> Kushindwa kuelewa mgogoro</li>
<li> Kudhani ni maandishi ya kawaida tu</li>
</ul>

<h3> MATUMIZI HALISI</h3>
<ul>
<li> Hutumika katika michezo ya kuigiza</li>
<li> Hufundisha maadili na jamii</li>
<li> Hukuza ubunifu na uigizaji</li>
<li> Hutumika shuleni katika fasihi</li>
<li> Msingi wa filamu na maigizo</li>
</ul>
',
  '[{"q":"Tamthilia ni nini?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria jukwaa","Step 2: uigizaji"],"ans":"Ni kazi ya fasihi inayoigizwa jukwaani kwa mazungumzo","why":"ufafanuzi"},{"q":"Tamthilia hutofautianaje na riwaya?","steps":["Step 1: linganisha","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Tamthilia huigizwa, riwaya husomwa kama hadithi","why":"tofauti"},{"q":"Wahusika wa tamthilia hufanya nini?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria mazungumzo","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Huzungumza na kuigiza matukio","why":"wahusika"},{"q":"Maelekezo ya jukwaa ni nini?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria mwandishi","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Ni maelezo yanayoonyesha jinsi tukio linavyofanyika","why":"ufafanuzi"},{"q":"Kwa nini tamthilia ni muhimu?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria jamii","Step 2: elimu"],"ans":"Hufundisha maadili na kuburudisha","why":"umuhimu"}]'
);

-- Topic: kiswahili -> fasihi_andishi -> C — Ushairi (Poetry)
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'kiswahili',
  'fasihi_andishi',
  'C — Ushairi (Poetry)',
  '<h2>Ushairi (Poetry)</h2>

<h3> MAELEZO / NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Ushairi</b> → ni aina ya fasihi inayotumia lugha ya kisanaa yenye mpangilio maalum</li>
<li><b>Vina</b> → mwisho wa mistari unaofanana kwa sauti</li>
<li><b>Mizani</b> → idadi ya silabi katika mstari</li>
<li><b>Mishororo</b> → mistari ya shairi</li>
<li><b>Dhamira</b> → ujumbe wa shairi (mfano: mapenzi, maadili, umaskini)</li>
<li><b>Lugha ya kisanaa</b> → hutumia taswira, sitiari na tashbihi</li>
</ul>

<h3> WAZO KUU</h3>
<pre>
Ushairi ni matumizi ya lugha ya kisanaa yenye mpangilio wa vina na mizani ili kuwasilisha hisia na ujumbe.
</pre>

<h3> SIFA ZA USHAIRI</h3>
<ul>
<li> Una vina na mizani</li>
<li> Hutumia lugha ya picha</li>
<li> Huwa na mpangilio maalum wa mistari</li>
<li> Huonyesha hisia kali</li>
<li> Hutumia mbinu za kisanaa</li>
</ul>

<h3> MFANO WA SHAIRI</h3>
<pre>
Mapenzi ni moto unaowaka,
Moyo hupenda bila kusita,
Furaha na huzuni hukutana,
Katika safari ya mapenzi.
</pre>

<h3> AINA ZA USHAIRI</h3>
<pre>
Shairi la jadi → lina vina na mizani kali

Shairi huru → halina masharti makali ya vina
</pre>

<h3> MAKOSA YA KAWAIDA</h3>
<ul>
<li> Kudhani ushairi ni sentensi za kawaida</li>
<li> Kutotambua vina na mizani</li>
<li> Kuchanganya shairi na riwaya</li>
<li> Kushindwa kuelewa dhamira</li>
</ul>

<h3> MATUMIZI HALISI</h3>
<ul>
<li> Hutumika katika nyimbo</li>
<li> Hufundisha maadili na hisia</li>
<li> Hutumika katika burudani</li>
<li> Hukuza ubunifu wa lugha</li>
<li> Huwasilisha ujumbe kwa njia fupi na yenye nguvu</li>
</ul>
',
  '[{"q":"Ushairi ni nini?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria lugha ya kisanaa","Step 2: mpangilio"],"ans":"Ni matumizi ya lugha ya kisanaa yenye vina na mizani","why":"ufafanuzi"},{"q":"Taja kipengele kimoja cha ushairi","steps":["Step 1: fikiria muundo","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Vina","why":"kipengele"},{"q":"Mizani ni nini?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria silabi","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Ni idadi ya silabi katika mstari wa shairi","why":"ufafanuzi"},{"q":"Ushairi hutofautianaje na riwaya?","steps":["Step 1: linganisha","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Ushairi hutumia mistari mifupi, riwaya ni hadithi ndefu","why":"tofauti"},{"q":"Kwa nini ushairi ni muhimu?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria hisia","Step 2: jamii"],"ans":"Huwasilisha hisia na kufundisha maadili","why":"umuhimu"}]'
);

-- Topic: kiswahili -> fasihi_andishi -> D — Wahusika (Characters)
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'kiswahili',
  'fasihi_andishi',
  'D — Wahusika (Characters)',
  '<h2>Wahusika (Characters)</h2>

<h3> MAELEZO / NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Wahusika</b> → ni watu au viumbe wanaoshiriki katika hadithi, riwaya, tamthilia au shairi</li>
<li><b>Mhusika Mkuu</b> → ndiye mhusika muhimu anayebeba hadithi</li>
<li><b>Wahusika Wadogo</b> → husaidia kuendeleza hadithi</li>
<li><b>Sifa za Wahusika</b> → tabia zao kama wema, ubaya, ujasiri au woga</li>
<li><b>Mabadiliko</b> → wahusika wanaweza kubadilika kutokana na matukio</li>
<li><b>Dhamira</b> → wahusika husaidia kuonyesha ujumbe wa kazi ya fasihi</li>
</ul>

<h3> WAZO KUU</h3>
<pre>
Wahusika ni watu au viumbe wanaoendesha matukio na kuwasilisha ujumbe katika fasihi.
</pre>

<h3> AINA ZA WAHUSIKA</h3>
<pre>
Mhusika Mkuu → hubeba hadithi yote

Wahusika Wadogo → husaidia mhusika mkuu

Mhusika Mbadala → huleta mabadiliko ya matukio
</pre>

<h3> MFANO WA WAHUSIKA</h3>
<pre>
Riwaya: Amina (mhusika mkuu), Juma (msaidizi)

Tamthilia: Mfalme, washauri wake

Ushairi: Mzungumzaji (shairi linaweza kuwa sauti ya mtu)
</pre>

<h3> MAKOSA YA KAWAIDA</h3>
<ul>
<li> Kudhani wahusika ni watu halisi tu</li>
<li> Kushindwa kutofautisha mhusika mkuu na mdogo</li>
<li> Kuchambua tabia bila ushahidi</li>
<li> Kupuuza mabadiliko ya wahusika</li>
</ul>

<h3> MATUMIZI HALISI</h3>
<ul>
<li> Husaidia kuelewa hadithi vizuri</li>
<li> Hukuza uwezo wa kuchambua tabia</li>
<li> Hutumika katika maigizo na filamu</li>
<li> Hufundisha maadili kupitia tabia</li>
<li> Husaidia kuelewa jamii kupitia wahusika</li>
</ul>
',
  '[{"q":"Wahusika ni nini?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria hadithi","Step 2: watu au viumbe"],"ans":"Ni watu au viumbe wanaoshiriki katika hadithi","why":"ufafanuzi"},{"q":"Taja aina moja ya mhusika","steps":["Step 1: fikiria nafasi","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Mhusika mkuu","why":"aina"},{"q":"Mhusika mkuu ni nani?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria hadithi","Step 2: muhimu"],"ans":"Ni mhusika anayebeba hadithi yote","why":"ufafanuzi"},{"q":"Wahusika wadogo hufanya nini?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria msaada","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Husaidia kuendeleza hadithi","why":"nafasi"},{"q":"Kwa nini wahusika ni muhimu?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria ujumbe","Step 2: hadithi"],"ans":"Husaidia kuwasilisha dhamira ya hadithi","why":"umuhimu"}]'
);

-- Topic: kiswahili -> fasihi_andishi -> E — Mandhari (Setting)
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'kiswahili',
  'fasihi_andishi',
  'E — Mandhari (Setting)',
  '<h2>Mandhari (Setting)</h2>

<h3> MAELEZO / NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Mandhari</b> → ni mahali na wakati ambapo matukio ya hadithi hutokea</li>
<li><b>Mahali</b> → inaweza kuwa kijijini, mjini, shuleni au msituni</li>
<li><b>Wakati</b> → inaweza kuwa zamani, sasa au baadaye</li>
<li><b>Mandhari Halisi</b> → mahali halisi kama mji au kijiji</li>
<li><b>Mandhari ya Kufikirika</b> → mahali pa kubuni kama katika hadithi za fantasia</li>
<li><b>Umuhimu</b> → husaidia kuelewa mazingira ya wahusika</li>
</ul>

<h3> WAZO KUU</h3>
<pre>
Mandhari ni mahali na wakati ambapo matukio ya fasihi hutokea.
</pre>

<h3> AINA ZA MANDHARI</h3>
<pre>
Mandhari Halisi → mji, kijiji, shule

Mandhari ya Kufikirika → maeneo ya kubuni

Mandhari ya Wakati → zamani, sasa, baadaye
</pre>

<h3> MFANO WA MANDHARI</h3>
<pre>
Kijiji → maisha ya wakulima

Mjini → biashara na maendeleo

Shuleni → elimu na mafunzo
</pre>

<h3> MAKOSA YA KAWAIDA</h3>
<ul>
<li> Kuchanganya mandhari na wahusika</li>
<li> Kushindwa kutambua wakati wa hadithi</li>
<li> Kupuuza mazingira ya tukio</li>
<li> Kudhani mandhari ni mahali pekee</li>
</ul>

<h3> MATUMIZI HALISI</h3>
<ul>
<li> Husaidia kuelewa hadithi vizuri</li>
<li> Huchangia uchambuzi wa fasihi</li>
<li> Husaidia katika uigizaji</li>
<li> Hufundisha tofauti za mazingira</li>
<li> Husaidia kuelewa maisha ya jamii tofauti</li>
</ul>
',
  '[{"q":"Mandhari ni nini?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria mahali","Step 2: wakati"],"ans":"Ni mahali na wakati wa matukio ya hadithi","why":"ufafanuzi"},{"q":"Taja aina moja ya mandhari","steps":["Step 1: fikiria mazingira","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Mandhari halisi","why":"aina"},{"q":"Tofauti kati ya mandhari halisi na ya kufikirika ni nini?","steps":["Step 1: linganisha","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Halisi ni ya kweli, ya kufikirika ni ya kubuni","why":"tofauti"},{"q":"Mandhari husaidia nini?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria hadithi","Step 2: uelewa"],"ans":"Husaidia kuelewa mazingira ya hadithi","why":"umuhimu"},{"q":"Toa mfano wa mandhari","steps":["Step 1: fikiria mahali","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Kijijini au mjini","why":"mfano"}]'
);

-- Topic: kiswahili -> fasihi_andishi -> F — Dhamira (Themes)
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'kiswahili',
  'fasihi_andishi',
  'F — Dhamira (Themes)',
  '<h2>Dhamira (Themes)</h2>

<h3> MAELEZO / NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Dhamira</b> → ni ujumbe mkuu au wazo kuu katika kazi ya fasihi</li>
<li><b>Dhamira Kuu</b> → wazo kuu linalobeba hadithi nzima</li>
<li><b>Dhamira Ndogo</b> → mawazo madogo yanayosaidia dhamira kuu</li>
<li><b>Ushahidi</b> → dhamira hupatikana kupitia matukio na mazungumzo</li>
<li><b>Wahusika</b> → husaidia kuonyesha dhamira</li>
<li><b>Mazingira</b> → pia huonyesha ujumbe wa hadithi</li>
</ul>

<h3> WAZO KUU</h3>
<pre>
Dhamira ni ujumbe mkuu unaopatikana katika kazi ya fasihi.
</pre>

<h3> MFANO WA DHAMIRA</h3>
<pre>
Ufisadi → matumizi mabaya ya madaraka

Mapenzi → hisia kati ya watu wawili

Ujasiri → kukabiliana na changamoto

Umaskini → hali ya maisha magumu
</pre>

<h3> DHAMIRA KUU NA NDOGO</h3>
<pre>
Dhamira Kuu → ujumbe mkuu wa hadithi

Dhamira Ndogo → mawazo madogo yanayosaidia kuu
</pre>

<h3> MAKOSA YA KAWAIDA</h3>
<ul>
<li> Kuchanganya dhamira na muhtasari</li>
<li> Kutoa maelezo bila ushahidi</li>
<li> Kutambua tukio badala ya ujumbe</li>
<li> Kushindwa kutofautisha dhamira kuu na ndogo</li>
</ul>

<h3> MATUMIZI HALISI</h3>
<ul>
<li> Husaidia kuelewa ujumbe wa fasihi</li>
<li> Hukuza uwezo wa uchambuzi</li>
<li> Hufundisha maadili ya jamii</li>
<li> Husaidia kuelewa matatizo ya kijamii</li>
<li> Huchangia mabadiliko chanya katika jamii</li>
</ul>
',
  '[{"q":"Dhamira ni nini?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria ujumbe","Step 2: hadithi"],"ans":"Ni ujumbe mkuu katika kazi ya fasihi","why":"ufafanuzi"},{"q":"Taja mfano wa dhamira","steps":["Step 1: fikiria jamii","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Ufisadi","why":"mfano"},{"q":"Tofauti kati ya dhamira kuu na ndogo ni nini?","steps":["Step 1: linganisha","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Kuu ni ujumbe mkuu, ndogo ni mawazo yanayosaidia","why":"tofauti"},{"q":"Dhamira hupatikana vipi?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria maandishi","Step 2: ushahidi"],"ans":"Kupitia matukio na wahusika","why":"uchambuzi"},{"q":"Kwa nini dhamira ni muhimu?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria elimu","Step 2: jamii"],"ans":"Husaidia kuelewa ujumbe wa fasihi","why":"umuhimu"}]'
);

-- Topic: kiswahili -> fasihi_andishi -> G — Mbinu za Kisanaa (Literary Devices)
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'kiswahili',
  'fasihi_andishi',
  'G — Mbinu za Kisanaa (Literary Devices)',
  '<h2>Mbinu za Kisanaa</h2>

<h3> MAELEZO / NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Mbinu za Kisanaa</b> → ni njia zinazotumiwa na waandishi kupamba na kuimarisha lugha</li>
<li><b>Tashbihi</b> → kulinganisha vitu kwa kutumia “kama” au “mfano wa”</li>
<li><b>Sitiari</b> → kulinganisha moja kwa moja bila kutumia “kama”</li>
<li><b>Jazanda</b> → matumizi ya picha au ishara kuwakilisha maana</li>
<li><b>Tanakali sauti</b> → matumizi ya sauti kuiga hali fulani</li>
<li><b>Chuku</b> → kutia chumvi katika kueleza jambo</li>
<li><b>Kinaya</b> → kusema jambo tofauti na maana halisi</li>
</ul>

<h3> WAZO KUU</h3>
<pre>
Mbinu za kisanaa ni njia zinazotumiwa kupamba na kuimarisha lugha ya fasihi.
</pre>

<h3> MIFANO YA MBINU ZA KISANAA</h3>
<pre>
Tashbihi → “Ana nguvu kama simba”

Sitiari → “Yeye ni simba wa darasa”

Chuku → “Nimekuita mara milioni”

Tanakali sauti → “Bam! mlango ukagonga”
</pre>

<h3> AINA ZA MBINU</h3>
<pre>
Tashbihi → kulinganisha kwa “kama”

Sitiari → kulinganisha moja kwa moja

Chuku → kutia chumvi

Kinaya → maana tofauti na maneno
</pre>

<h3> MAKOSA YA KAWAIDA</h3>
<ul>
<li> Kuchanganya tashbihi na sitiari</li>
<li> Kutotambua maana ya ishara</li>
<li> Kuchukua maneno kihalisi</li>
<li> Kushindwa kutoa mifano sahihi</li>
</ul>

<h3> MATUMIZI HALISI</h3>
<ul>
<li> Hupamba maandishi ya fasihi</li>
<li> Hukuza ubunifu wa lugha</li>
<li> Hutumika katika mashairi na nyimbo</li>
<li> Hufanya maandishi yawe ya kuvutia</li>
<li> Husaidia kueleza hisia kwa nguvu zaidi</li>
</ul>
',
  '[{"q":"Mbinu za kisanaa ni nini?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria lugha","Step 2: kupamba"],"ans":"Ni njia zinazotumiwa kupamba lugha ya fasihi","why":"ufafanuzi"},{"q":"Taja mbinu moja ya kisanaa","steps":["Step 1: fikiria mfano","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Tashbihi","why":"mfano"},{"q":"Tashbihi ni nini?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria kulinganisha","Step 2: ''kama''"],"ans":"Ni kulinganisha vitu kwa kutumia ''kama''","why":"ufafanuzi"},{"q":"Sitiari ni nini?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria moja kwa moja","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Ni kulinganisha moja kwa moja bila kutumia ''kama''","why":"ufafanuzi"},{"q":"Kwa nini mbinu za kisanaa ni muhimu?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria lugha","Step 2: urembo"],"ans":"Hupamba na kuifanya lugha kuvutia","why":"umuhimu"}]'
);

-- Topic: kiswahili -> fasihi_andishi -> H — Wahusika (Characters in Depth)
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'kiswahili',
  'fasihi_andishi',
  'H — Wahusika (Characters in Depth)',
  '<h2>Wahusika (Uchambuzi wa Kina)</h2>

<h3> MAELEZO / NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Mhusika Bapa</b> → ana tabia moja isiyobadilika (mfano: mnyoofu au mwovu tu)</li>
<li><b>Mhusika Duara</b> → hubadilika kulingana na matukio</li>
<li><b>Mhusika Mkuu</b> → hubeba hadithi nzima</li>
<li><b>Mhusika Msaidizi</b> → husaidia kuendeleza hadithi</li>
<li><b>Tabia</b> → hujulikana kupitia matendo na maneno</li>
<li><b>Ushahidi</b> → lazima utoe mifano kutoka kwenye maandishi</li>
</ul>

<h3> WAZO KUU</h3>
<pre>
Wahusika huonyesha tabia, migogoro na dhamira ya kazi ya fasihi.
</pre>

<h3> AINA ZA WAHUSIKA</h3>
<pre>
Mhusika Bapa → tabia moja (mwema au mwovu)

Mhusika Duara → hubadilika na matukio

Mhusika Mkuu → mhusika muhimu zaidi

Mhusika Msaidizi → husaidia hadithi
</pre>

<h3> MFANO WA UCHAMBUZI</h3>
<pre>
Amina → mnyenyekevu lakini anakuwa jasiri baada ya changamoto

Juma → msaidizi mwaminifu

Mwalimu → mwongozo wa maadili
</pre>

<h3> MAKOSA YA KAWAIDA</h3>
<ul>
<li> Kuchambua mhusika bila ushahidi</li>
<li> Kudhani wahusika wote hawabadiliki</li>
<li> Kuchanganya aina za wahusika</li>
<li> Kutotambua nafasi ya mhusika</li>
</ul>

<h3> MATUMIZI HALISI</h3>
<ul>
<li> Husaidia kuelewa hadithi kwa kina</li>
<li> Hukuza uwezo wa uchambuzi</li>
<li> Hutumika katika maigizo na filamu</li>
<li> Hufundisha tabia nzuri na mbaya</li>
<li> Husaidia kuelewa jamii kupitia wahusika</li>
</ul>
',
  '[{"q":"Mhusika bapa ni nini?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria tabia moja","Step 2: thabiti"],"ans":"Ni mhusika mwenye tabia moja isiyobadilika","why":"ufafanuzi"},{"q":"Mhusika duara ni nini?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria mabadiliko","Step 2: hadithi"],"ans":"Ni mhusika anayebadilika kulingana na matukio","why":"ufafanuzi"},{"q":"Taja aina moja ya mhusika","steps":["Step 1: fikiria daraja","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Mhusika mkuu","why":"aina"},{"q":"Kwa nini ushahidi ni muhimu?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria maandishi","Step 2: uthibitisho"],"ans":"Huthibitisha uchambuzi wa mhusika","why":"umuhimu"},{"q":"Wahusika husaidia nini?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria hadithi","Step 2: dhamira"],"ans":"Hujenga dhamira ya fasihi","why":"umuhimu"}]'
);

-- Topic: kiswahili -> fasihi_andishi -> I — Mandhari (Setting in Depth)
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'kiswahili',
  'fasihi_andishi',
  'I — Mandhari (Setting in Depth)',
  '<h2>Mandhari (Uchambuzi wa Kina)</h2>

<h3> MAELEZO / NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Mandhari</b> → ni mahali na wakati ambapo matukio ya fasihi hutokea</li>
<li><b>Mahali</b> → mfano kijijini, mjini, shuleni au msituni</li>
<li><b>Wakati</b> → inaweza kuwa zamani, sasa au baadaye</li>
<li><b>Mandhari Halisi</b> → mahali halisi katika dunia ya kweli</li>
<li><b>Mandhari ya Kubuni</b> → mahali pa kufikirika au fantasia</li>
<li><b>Mabadiliko ya Mandhari</b> → hadithi inaweza kubadilisha maeneo na nyakati</li>
<li><b>Uhusiano</b> → mandhari huathiri tabia na matendo ya wahusika</li>
</ul>

<h3> WAZO KUU</h3>
<pre>
Mandhari huweka mazingira ya matukio na kuathiri wahusika na dhamira ya hadithi.
</pre>

<h3> AINA ZA MANDHARI</h3>
<pre>
Mandhari Halisi → kijiji, mji, shule

Mandhari ya Kubuni → maeneo ya kufikirika

Mandhari ya Wakati → zamani, sasa, baadaye
</pre>

<h3> MFANO WA UCHAMBUZI</h3>
<pre>
Kijijini → maisha ya kilimo na umaskini

Mjini → maisha ya kasi na biashara

Zamani → mila na desturi za kale
</pre>

<h3> MAKOSA YA KAWAIDA</h3>
<ul>
<li> Kuchanganya mandhari na wahusika</li>
<li> Kutotambua wakati wa hadithi</li>
<li> Kudhani mandhari ni mahali tu bila wakati</li>
<li> Kushindwa kuchambua mabadiliko ya mandhari</li>
</ul>

<h3> MATUMIZI HALISI</h3>
<ul>
<li> Husaidia kuelewa hadithi kwa kina</li>
<li> Hukuza uwezo wa uchambuzi wa fasihi</li>
<li> Husaidia katika maigizo na filamu</li>
<li> Hufundisha tofauti za mazingira ya jamii</li>
<li> Husaidia kuelewa maisha ya jamii tofauti</li>
</ul>
',
  '[{"q":"Mandhari ni nini?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria mahali na wakati","Step 2: hadithi"],"ans":"Ni mahali na wakati ambapo matukio ya fasihi hutokea","why":"ufafanuzi"},{"q":"Taja aina moja ya mandhari","steps":["Step 1: fikiria mazingira","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Mandhari halisi","why":"aina"},{"q":"Mandhari ya kubuni ni nini?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria fantasia","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Ni mandhari ya kufikirika au yasiyo halisi","why":"ufafanuzi"},{"q":"Mandhari huathiri nini?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria wahusika","Step 2: tabia"],"ans":"Huathiri wahusika na matendo yao","why":"athari"},{"q":"Kwa nini mandhari ni muhimu?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria hadithi","Step 2: uelewa"],"ans":"Husaidia kuelewa mazingira ya hadithi","why":"umuhimu"}]'
);

-- Topic: kiswahili -> matumizi_ya_lugha -> A — Utangulizi wa Matumizi ya Lugha
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'kiswahili',
  'matumizi_ya_lugha',
  'A — Utangulizi wa Matumizi ya Lugha',
  '<h2>Utangulizi wa Matumizi ya Lugha</h2>

<h3> MAELEZO / NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Maana ya Matumizi ya Lugha</b> → ni namna watu wanavyotumia lugha kuwasiliana</li>
<li><b>Lugha kama Chombo cha Mawasiliano</b> → hutumika kupeleka ujumbe kutoka kwa mtu mmoja hadi mwingine</li>
<li><b>Lugha na Jamii</b> → lugha huunganisha watu na kuunda mahusiano</li>
<li><b>Lugha hubeba hisia</b> → kama furaha, huzuni, hasira au upendo</li>
<li><b>Matumizi hutegemea mazingira</b> → rasmi au yasiyo rasmi</li>
<li><b>Lugha sahihi huleta uelewano</b> → hupunguza migogoro na mkanganyiko</li>
</ul>

<h3> WAZO KUU</h3>
<pre>
Matumizi ya lugha ni msingi wa mawasiliano yote ya binadamu.
</pre>

<h3> MIFANO</h3>
<pre>
Darasani → mwanafunzi anajibu swali la mwalimu

Nyumbani → mzazi anazungumza na mtoto

Sokoni → mnunuzi na muuzaji wanajadili bei

Simu → watu wanawasiliana kwa umbali
</pre>

<h3> MAKOSA YA KAWAIDA</h3>
<ul>
<li> Kudhani lugha ni maneno tu bila maana ya ndani</li>
<li> Kutotofautisha matumizi ya lugha kulingana na mazingira</li>
<li> Kutumia lugha isiyofaa katika hali rasmi</li>
<li> Kupuuza umuhimu wa lugha katika mawasiliano</li>
</ul>

<h3> MATUMIZI HALISI</h3>
<ul>
<li> Husaidia katika masomo ya lugha na fasihi</li>
<li> Huboresha uwezo wa kufikiri na kueleza mawazo</li>
<li> Hutumika katika mawasiliano ya kila siku</li>
<li> Ni muhimu katika kazi na biashara</li>
<li> Hujenga mahusiano mazuri katika jamii</li>
</ul>
',
  '[{"q":"Matumizi ya lugha ni nini?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria mawasiliano","Step 2: matumizi ya maneno"],"ans":"Ni namna ya kutumia lugha katika mawasiliano","why":"ufafanuzi"},{"q":"Kwa nini lugha ni muhimu?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria watu","Step 2: uelewano"],"ans":"Husaidia watu kuwasiliana na kuelewana","why":"umuhimu"},{"q":"Lugha hutumika wapi?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria mazingira","Step 2: toa mifano"],"ans":"Darasani, nyumbani, sokoni na kazini","why":"matumizi"},{"q":"Lugha ina uhusiano gani na jamii?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria watu","Step 2: uhusiano"],"ans":"Huunganisha watu na kujenga mahusiano","why":"jamii"},{"q":"Kwa nini kutumia lugha sahihi ni muhimu?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria mawasiliano","Step 2: uelewano"],"ans":"Husaidia kuleta uelewano na kuepuka mkanganyiko","why":"usahihi"}]'
);

-- Topic: kiswahili -> matumizi_ya_lugha -> B — Mchakato wa Mawasiliano
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'kiswahili',
  'matumizi_ya_lugha',
  'B — Mchakato wa Mawasiliano',
  '<h2>Mchakato wa Mawasiliano</h2>

<h3> MAELEZO / NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Mzungumzaji</b> → mtu anayetoa ujumbe</li>
<li><b>Ujumbe</b> → taarifa au wazo linalotumwa</li>
<li><b>Msikilizaji</b> → mtu anayepokea ujumbe</li>
<li><b>Majibu (Feedback)</b> → mwitikio wa msikilizaji kwa ujumbe</li>
<li><b>Njia ya Mawasiliano</b> → kama kuzungumza, kuandika au kutumia simu</li>
<li><b>Vikwazo</b> → kelele, lugha ngumu au kutosikiliza vizuri</li>
<li><b>Mawasiliano mazuri</b> → hutokea pale ujumbe unaeleweka ipasavyo</li>
</ul>

<h3> WAZO KUU</h3>
<pre>
Mawasiliano ni mchakato unaohusisha mzungumzaji, ujumbe, msikilizaji na majibu.
</pre>

<h3> MCHAKATO WA MAWASILIANO</h3>
<pre>
Mzungumzaji → Ujumbe → Msikilizaji → Majibu (Feedback)
</pre>

<h3> MIFANO</h3>
<pre>
Darasani → Mwalimu anaeleza somo, wanafunzi wanasikiliza na kuuliza maswali

Simu → Mtu anapiga simu, mwingine anapokea na kujibu

Mazungumzo → Rafiki anauliza swali, mwingine anajibu
</pre>

<h3> MAKOSA YA KAWAIDA</h3>
<ul>
<li> Kutomsikiliza vizuri mzungumzaji</li>
<li> Kutumia lugha isiyoeleweka</li>
<li> Kukosa kutoa majibu (feedback)</li>
<li> Kuvurugwa na kelele au mazingira</li>
</ul>

<h3> MATUMIZI HALISI</h3>
<ul>
<li> Husaidia kuelewa mafunzo darasani</li>
<li> Huboresha mawasiliano ya kila siku</li>
<li> Ni muhimu katika kazi na mikutano</li>
<li> Hutumika katika mawasiliano ya simu na mtandao</li>
<li> Hujenga uelewano mzuri katika jamii</li>
</ul>
',
  '[{"q":"Mchakato wa mawasiliano ni nini?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria mawasiliano","Step 2: angalia hatua"],"ans":"Ni hatua zinazohusisha mzungumzaji, ujumbe, msikilizaji na majibu","why":"ufafanuzi"},{"q":"Taja sehemu kuu 3 za mawasiliano","steps":["Step 1: kumbuka mchakato","Step 2: taja vipengele"],"ans":"Mzungumzaji, ujumbe na msikilizaji","why":"vipengele"},{"q":"Majibu (feedback) ni nini?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria mwitikio","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Ni mwitikio wa msikilizaji kwa ujumbe","why":"ufafanuzi"},{"q":"Ni nini kinaweza kuharibu mawasiliano?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria vikwazo","Step 2: toa mfano"],"ans":"Kelele, lugha ngumu na kutosikiliza vizuri","why":"vikwazo"},{"q":"Kwa nini feedback ni muhimu?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria uelewa","Step 2: majibu"],"ans":"Husaidia kujua kama ujumbe umeeleweka","why":"umuhimu"}]'
);

-- Topic: kiswahili -> matumizi_ya_lugha -> C — Maongezi Rasmi
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'kiswahili',
  'matumizi_ya_lugha',
  'C — Maongezi Rasmi',
  '<h2>Maongezi Rasmi</h2>

<h3> MAELEZO / NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Maongezi Rasmi</b> → ni mawasiliano yanayofuata kanuni na heshima</li>
<li><b>Lugha Sanifu</b> → hutumia Kiswahili sahihi bila makosa</li>
<li><b>Heshima</b> → maneno ya adabu na staha hutumika</li>
<li><b>Mpangilio</b> → ujumbe hupangwa vizuri na kueleweka</li>
<li><b>Mazingira</b> → hutumika katika shule, ofisi, mikutano na taasisi</li>
<li><b>Kuepuka Lugha ya Mtaani</b> → hakuna slang au lugha ya kiholela</li>
<li><b>Lengo Maalum</b> → mara nyingi huwa na kusudi rasmi kama kutoa taarifa au maagizo</li>
</ul>

<h3> WAZO KUU</h3>
<pre>
Maongezi rasmi hutumia lugha ya heshima, mpangilio na usahihi ili kufikisha ujumbe kwa uwazi.
</pre>

<h3> MIFANO</h3>
<pre>
Darasani → "Tafadhali eleza jibu lako."

Ofisini → "Naomba kuwasilisha ripoti yangu."

Mkutano → "Ningependa kutoa hoja kuhusu ajenda hii."
</pre>

<h3> MFANO WA MAZUNGUMZO RASMI</h3>
<pre>
Mwalimu: Tafadhali simama na ujieleze.
Mwanafunzi: Asante mwalimu, jina langu ni Amina.
Mwalimu: Karibu sana, tafadhali kaa chini.
</pre>

<h3> MAKOSA YA KAWAIDA</h3>
<ul>
<li> Kutumia lugha ya mtaani katika mazingira rasmi</li>
<li> Kukosa heshima unapozungumza</li>
<li> Kutotumia Kiswahili sanifu</li>
<li> Kuongea bila mpangilio au kwa fujo</li>
</ul>

<h3> MATUMIZI HALISI</h3>
<ul>
<li> Hutumika katika elimu (darasani)</li>
<li> Ni muhimu katika kazi na ofisi</li>
<li> Hutumika katika mikutano na taasisi</li>
<li> Ni msingi wa barua rasmi na hotuba</li>
<li> Hujenga heshima na nidhamu katika jamii</li>
</ul>
',
  '[{"q":"Maongezi rasmi ni nini?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria kanuni","Step 2: heshima"],"ans":"Ni mawasiliano yanayofuata kanuni na heshima","why":"ufafanuzi"},{"q":"Taja sifa moja ya maongezi rasmi","steps":["Step 1: fikiria lugha","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Hutumika lugha ya heshima","why":"sifa"},{"q":"Maongezi rasmi hutumika wapi?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria mazingira","Step 2: toa mfano"],"ans":"Shuleni, ofisini na mikutanoni","why":"matumizi"},{"q":"Kwa nini maongezi rasmi ni muhimu?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria mawasiliano","Step 2: heshima"],"ans":"Hujenga heshima na kueleweka vizuri","why":"umuhimu"},{"q":"Ni kosa gani la kawaida katika maongezi rasmi?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria makosa","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Kutumia lugha ya mtaani","why":"makosa"}]'
);

-- Topic: kiswahili -> matumizi_ya_lugha -> D — Maongezi Yasiyo Rasmi
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'kiswahili',
  'matumizi_ya_lugha',
  'D — Maongezi Yasiyo Rasmi',
  '<h2>Maongezi Yasiyo Rasmi</h2>

<h3> MAELEZO / NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Maongezi Yasiyo Rasmi</b> → ni mawasiliano ya kawaida yasiyofuata kanuni kali</li>
<li><b>Lugha Rahisi</b> → hutumia maneno ya kawaida na ya kila siku</li>
<li><b>Uhuru wa Lugha</b> → hakuna masharti makubwa ya mpangilio</li>
<li><b>Mazingira</b> → hutumika kati ya marafiki, familia na watu wa karibu</li>
<li><b>Lugha ya Mtaani</b> → wakati mwingine hutumika slang au misemo ya kawaida</li>
<li><b>Hisia</b> → huonyesha hisia kwa uhuru zaidi</li>
<li><b>Hakuna Ukali wa Kanuni</b> → tofauti na maongezi rasmi</li>
</ul>

<h3> WAZO KUU</h3>
<pre>
Maongezi yasiyo rasmi hutumia lugha ya kawaida kwa uhuru katika mazingira ya kirafiki.
</pre>

<h3> MIFANO</h3>
<pre>
Mitaani → "Niaje? Uko aje?"

Nyumbani → "Umekula?"

Marafiki → "Leo tunatoka wapi?"
</pre>

<h3> MFANO WA MAZUNGUMZO</h3>
<pre>
A: Niaje?
B: Poa sana, wewe?
A: Niko fiti, tunaenda wapi leo?
B: Twende tukacheze mpira.
</pre>

<h3> MAKOSA YA KAWAIDA</h3>
<ul>
<li> Kutumia maongezi yasiyo rasmi katika mazingira rasmi</li>
<li> Kutumia lugha ya mtaani kupita kiasi</li>
<li> Kukosa mipaka ya heshima</li>
<li> Kuchanganya lugha bila mpangilio</li>
</ul>

<h3> MATUMIZI HALISI</h3>
<ul>
<li> Hutumika nyumbani na familia</li>
<li> Huimarisha urafiki</li>
<li> Hutumika katika mazungumzo ya kila siku</li>
<li> Husaidia kuonyesha hisia kwa uhuru</li>
<li> Ni sehemu ya utamaduni wa jamii</li>
</ul>
',
  '[{"q":"Maongezi yasiyo rasmi ni nini?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria kawaida","Step 2: hakuna kanuni kali"],"ans":"Ni mawasiliano ya kawaida yasiyofuata kanuni kali","why":"ufafanuzi"},{"q":"Hutumika wapi?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria watu wa karibu","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Kwa marafiki na familia","why":"matumizi"},{"q":"Toa mfano wa lugha isiyo rasmi","steps":["Step 1: fikiria mitaani","Step 2: toa mfano"],"ans":"Niaje?","why":"mfano"},{"q":"Tofauti na maongezi rasmi ni ipi?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria kanuni","Step 2: linganisha"],"ans":"Hayafuati kanuni kali na hutumia lugha rahisi","why":"tofauti"},{"q":"Ni kosa gani linaweza kufanywa?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria mazingira","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Kutumia lugha isiyo rasmi katika mazingira rasmi","why":"makosa"}]'
);

-- Topic: kiswahili -> matumizi_ya_lugha -> E — Barua Rasmi
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'kiswahili',
  'matumizi_ya_lugha',
  'E — Barua Rasmi',
  '<h2>Barua Rasmi</h2>

<h3> MAELEZO / NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Barua Rasmi</b> → ni barua inayoandikwa kwa ofisi, taasisi au shirika</li>
<li><b>Lugha Rasmi</b> → hutumia Kiswahili sanifu na cha heshima</li>
<li><b>Muundo Maalum</b> → lazima ifuate mpangilio rasmi</li>
<li><b>Lengo</b> → kuomba, kutoa taarifa au malalamiko</li>
<li><b>Heshima</b> → hutumia maneno ya staha kama "Tafadhali" na "Naomba"</li>
<li><b>Uwazi</b> → ujumbe lazima uwe wazi na mfupi</li>
</ul>

<h3> WAZO KUU</h3>
<pre>
Barua rasmi huandikwa kwa kufuata muundo maalum na kutumia lugha ya heshima.
</pre>

<h3> MUUNDO WA BARUA RASMI</h3>
<pre>
Anuani ya mwandishi
Tarehe

Anuani ya mpokeaji

Salamu (Mfano: Ndugu/Mheshimiwa)

Kichwa cha barua

Mwili wa barua

Hitimisho (Mfano: Wako mwaminifu)

Jina na sahihi
</pre>

<h3> MFANO</h3>
<pre>
S.L.P 123,
Nairobi,
10/04/2026

Mkuu wa Shule,
Shule ya Upili XYZ,
Nairobi

YAH: OMBI LA NAFASI YA MASOMO

Naomba nafasi ya kujiunga na shule yenu...

Wako mwaminifu,
Juma Ali
</pre>

<h3> MAKOSA YA KAWAIDA</h3>
<ul>
<li> Kutofuata muundo wa barua rasmi</li>
<li> Kutumia lugha isiyo rasmi</li>
<li> Kuandika bila mpangilio</li>
<li> Kukosa heshima katika mawasiliano</li>
</ul>

<h3> MATUMIZI HALISI</h3>
<ul>
<li> Hutumika katika maombi ya kazi</li>
<li> Hutumika kuwasiliana na shule</li>
<li> Hutumika katika taasisi na ofisi</li>
<li> Hutumika kutoa taarifa rasmi</li>
<li> Hujenga mawasiliano ya kitaaluma</li>
</ul>
',
  '[{"q":"Barua rasmi ni nini?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria ofisi","Step 2: barua"],"ans":"Ni barua inayoandikwa kwa ofisi au taasisi","why":"ufafanuzi"},{"q":"Taja sehemu moja ya barua rasmi","steps":["Step 1: kumbuka muundo","Step 2: taja"],"ans":"Anuani ya mwandishi","why":"muundo"},{"q":"Ni lugha gani hutumika?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria heshima","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Lugha rasmi ya heshima","why":"lugha"},{"q":"Kwa nini barua rasmi ni muhimu?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria mawasiliano","Step 2: taasisi"],"ans":"Husaidia mawasiliano rasmi katika taasisi","why":"umuhimu"},{"q":"Ni kosa gani linaweza kufanywa?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria makosa","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Kutumia lugha isiyo rasmi","why":"makosa"}]'
);

-- Topic: kiswahili -> matumizi_ya_lugha -> F — Barua Zisizo Rasmi
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'kiswahili',
  'matumizi_ya_lugha',
  'F — Barua Zisizo Rasmi',
  '<h2>Barua Zisizo Rasmi</h2>

<h3> MAELEZO / NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Barua Zisizo Rasmi</b> → ni barua zinazoandikwa kwa marafiki, familia au watu wa karibu</li>
<li><b>Lugha ya Kawaida</b> → hutumia lugha rahisi na ya kirafiki</li>
<li><b>Hakuna Ukali wa Muundo</b> → haifuati kanuni kali kama barua rasmi</li>
<li><b>Hisia</b> → huruhusu kuonyesha hisia kwa uhuru</li>
<li><b>Mazingira</b> → hutumika katika mawasiliano ya kijamii</li>
<li><b>Lengo</b> → kutoa habari, kusalimiana au kushiriki mawazo</li>
</ul>

<h3> WAZO KUU</h3>
<pre>
Barua zisizo rasmi hutumia lugha ya kawaida na huandikwa kwa uhuru kwa watu wa karibu.
</pre>

<h3> MUUNDO WA BARUA ISIYO RASMI</h3>
<pre>
Anuani ya mwandishi
Tarehe

Salamu (Mfano: Rafiki yangu)

Mwili wa barua

Hitimisho (Mfano: Rafiki yako)

Jina
</pre>

<h3> MFANO</h3>
<pre>
S.L.P 456,
Nairobi,
10/04/2026

Rafiki yangu John,

Habari yako? Natumai uko salama...

Rafiki yako,
Ali
</pre>

<h3> MAKOSA YA KAWAIDA</h3>
<ul>
<li> Kuchanganya lugha rasmi na isiyo rasmi</li>
<li> Kutumia lugha isiyoeleweka</li>
<li> Kukosa mpangilio wa mawazo</li>
<li> Kuandika bila kuzingatia msomaji</li>
</ul>

<h3> MATUMIZI HALISI</h3>
<ul>
<li> Mawasiliano kati ya marafiki</li>
<li> Mawasiliano ya kifamilia</li>
<li> Kutuma salamu na habari</li>
<li> Kushiriki mawazo na hisia</li>
<li> Hutumika hata katika ujumbe wa kidijitali (chat, email)</li>
</ul>
',
  '[{"q":"Barua zisizo rasmi ni nini?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria marafiki","Step 2: barua"],"ans":"Ni barua zinazoandikwa kwa marafiki au familia","why":"ufafanuzi"},{"q":"Ni lugha gani hutumika?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria kawaida","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Lugha ya kawaida na ya kirafiki","why":"lugha"},{"q":"Tofauti na barua rasmi ni ipi?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria kanuni","Step 2: linganisha"],"ans":"Haina muundo mkali na hutumia lugha rahisi","why":"tofauti"},{"q":"Hutumika wapi?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria watu wa karibu","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Kwa marafiki na familia","why":"matumizi"},{"q":"Ni kosa gani linaweza kufanywa?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria makosa","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Kuchanganya lugha rasmi na isiyo rasmi","why":"makosa"}]'
);

-- Topic: kiswahili -> matumizi_ya_lugha -> G — Hotuba
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'kiswahili',
  'matumizi_ya_lugha',
  'G — Hotuba',
  '<h2>Hotuba</h2>

<h3> MAELEZO / NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Hotuba</b> → ni mazungumzo yanayotolewa mbele ya hadhira kwa kusudi maalum</li>
<li><b>Lugha Rasmi</b> → mara nyingi hutumia lugha ya heshima na sanifu</li>
<li><b>Madhumuni</b> → kuelimisha, kuhamasisha au kuburudisha</li>
<li><b>Hadhira</b> → watu wanaosikiliza hotuba</li>
<li><b>Mpangilio</b> → hotuba huwa na mwanzo, kati na mwisho</li>
<li><b>Sauti na Mwili</b> → matumizi ya sauti na ishara huongeza uelewa</li>
<li><b>Maandalizi</b> → hotuba nzuri huandaliwa kabla</li>
</ul>

<h3> WAZO KUU</h3>
<pre>
Hotuba ni njia ya kuwasilisha ujumbe kwa hadhira kwa mpangilio na kusudi maalum.
</pre>

<h3> MUUNDO WA HOTUBA</h3>
<pre>
Utangulizi → salamu na kujitambulisha

Mwili → maelezo ya hoja kuu

Hitimisho → muhtasari na shukrani
</pre>

<h3> MIFANO YA MADHUMUNI YA HOTUBA</h3>
<pre>
Elimu → kufundisha mada fulani

Hamasa → kuhimiza watu wafanye jambo

Burudani → kuburudisha hadhira
</pre>

<h3> MFANO MFUPI WA HOTUBA</h3>
<pre>
Waheshimiwa wote, nawasalimu.

Leo ningependa kuzungumzia umuhimu wa elimu...

Asanteni kwa kunisikiliza.
</pre>

<h3> MAKOSA YA KAWAIDA</h3>
<ul>
<li> Kukosa maandalizi</li>
<li> Kuongea bila mpangilio</li>
<li> Kutotumia lugha sahihi</li>
<li> Kukosa kujiamini mbele ya hadhira</li>
</ul>

<h3> MATUMIZI HALISI</h3>
<ul>
<li> Hutumika shuleni katika mijadala na mawasilisho</li>
<li> Hutumika katika mikutano na hafla</li>
<li> Ni muhimu katika kazi na uongozi</li>
<li> Hutumika katika matangazo na mawasiliano ya umma</li>
<li> Hujenga ujasiri na uwezo wa kuzungumza hadharani</li>
</ul>
',
  '[{"q":"Hotuba ni nini?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria hadhira","Step 2: ujumbe"],"ans":"Ni mazungumzo yanayotolewa mbele ya hadhira kwa kusudi maalum","why":"ufafanuzi"},{"q":"Taja sehemu 3 za hotuba","steps":["Step 1: kumbuka muundo","Step 2: taja"],"ans":"Utangulizi, mwili na hitimisho","why":"muundo"},{"q":"Hotuba hutolewa kwa madhumuni gani?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria kusudi","Step 2: toa mfano"],"ans":"Kuleta elimu, hamasa au burudani","why":"madhumuni"},{"q":"Ni nini hufanya hotuba iwe nzuri?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria maandalizi","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Mpangilio mzuri na maandalizi","why":"sifa"},{"q":"Ni kosa gani linaweza kufanywa?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria makosa","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Kukosa maandalizi","why":"makosa"}]'
);

-- Topic: kiswahili -> matumizi_ya_lugha -> H — Mazungumzo (Dialogue)
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'kiswahili',
  'matumizi_ya_lugha',
  'H — Mazungumzo (Dialogue)',
  '<h2>Mazungumzo (Dialogue)</h2>

<h3> MAELEZO / NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Mazungumzo</b> → ni mawasiliano kati ya watu wawili au zaidi kwa kupokezana</li>
<li><b>Wahusika</b> → watu wanaoshiriki katika mazungumzo</li>
<li><b>Mpangilio</b> → kila msemaji hupewa zamu yake</li>
<li><b>Lugha</b> → hutegemea mazingira (rasmi au isiyo rasmi)</li>
<li><b>Uwazi</b> → ujumbe lazima uwe wazi na mfupi</li>
<li><b>Mtiririko</b> → mazungumzo yanapaswa kuendelea kwa mantiki</li>
</ul>

<h3> WAZO KUU</h3>
<pre>
Mazungumzo ni kubadilishana mawazo kati ya watu wawili au zaidi kwa kupokezana.
</pre>

<h3> MUUNDO WA MAZUNGUMZO</h3>
<pre>
Jina la msemaji → maneno yake

Jina la msemaji mwingine → majibu yake
</pre>

<h3> MFANO WA MAZUNGUMZO</h3>
<pre>
A: Habari yako?
B: Nzuri, na wewe?
A: Niko salama, unafanya nini?
B: Ninasoma Kiswahili.
</pre>

<h3> SIFA ZA MAZUNGUMZO</h3>
<ul>
<li> Hutumia watu wawili au zaidi</li>
<li> Kuna kupokezana kwa zamu</li>
<li> Huwa na mtiririko wa mawazo</li>
<li> Hutegemea mazingira</li>
<li> Huandikwa kwa majina ya wahusika</li>
</ul>

<h3> MAKOSA YA KAWAIDA</h3>
<ul>
<li> Kutokufuata zamu za kuzungumza</li>
<li> Mazungumzo yasiyo na mantiki</li>
<li> Kutumia lugha isiyo sahihi</li>
<li> Kuchanganya hotuba na mazungumzo</li>
</ul>

<h3> MATUMIZI HALISI</h3>
<ul>
<li> Darasani kati ya wanafunzi</li>
<li> Nyumbani kati ya familia</li>
<li> Sokoni kati ya mnunuzi na muuzaji</li>
<li> Katika simu na mitandao ya kijamii</li>
<li> Katika maisha ya kila siku</li>
</ul>
',
  '[{"q":"Mazungumzo ni nini?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria watu","Step 2: kupokezana"],"ans":"Ni mawasiliano kati ya watu wawili au zaidi kwa kupokezana","why":"ufafanuzi"},{"q":"Taja sifa moja ya mazungumzo","steps":["Step 1: fikiria tabia","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Kuna kupokezana kwa zamu","why":"sifa"},{"q":"Mazungumzo hutofautianaje na hotuba?","steps":["Step 1: linganisha","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Mazungumzo ni kati ya watu wengi, hotuba hutolewa na mtu mmoja","why":"tofauti"},{"q":"Mazungumzo hutumika wapi?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria mazingira","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Darasani, nyumbani na sokoni","why":"matumizi"},{"q":"Ni kosa gani linaweza kutokea?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria makosa","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Kutofuata zamu za kuzungumza","why":"makosa"}]'
);

-- Topic: kiswahili -> matumizi_ya_lugha -> I — Mawasiliano ya Kijamii
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'kiswahili',
  'matumizi_ya_lugha',
  'I — Mawasiliano ya Kijamii',
  '<h2>Mawasiliano ya Kijamii</h2>
<h3> MAELEZO / NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Mawasiliano ya Kijamii</b> → ni ubadilishanaji wa taarifa kati ya watu katika jamii</li>
<li><b>Njia za Mawasiliano</b> → kuzungumza, kuandika, simu, mitandao ya kijamii</li>
<li><b>Wahusika</b> → watu wote katika jamii wanaowasiliana</li>
<li><b>Lengo</b> → kuelewana, kushirikiana na kuishi kwa amani</li>
<li><b>Teknolojia</b> → imeongeza kasi ya mawasiliano (simu, WhatsApp, barua pepe)</li>
<li><b>Changamoto</b> → upotoshaji wa taarifa na matumizi mabaya ya lugha</li>
</ul>

<h3> WAZO KUU</h3>
<pre>
Mawasiliano ya kijamii ni msingi wa uhusiano na mshikamano katika jamii.
</pre>

<h3> NJIA ZA MAWASILIANO</h3>
<pre>
Kuzungumza → uso kwa uso

Kuandika → barua, ujumbe

Teknolojia → simu, mitandao ya kijamii
</pre>

<h3> MIFANO</h3>
<pre>
Familia → wazazi na watoto wanazungumza nyumbani

Shule → walimu na wanafunzi

Jamii → mikutano ya kijamii

Mtandao → WhatsApp, Facebook
</pre>

<h3> MAKOSA YA KAWAIDA</h3>
<ul>
<li> Kutumia lugha chafu kwenye jamii</li>
<li> Kusambaza taarifa zisizo sahihi</li>
<li> Kutokuheshimu mawasiliano rasmi</li>
<li> Kutegemea mitandao bila uhakiki</li>
</ul>

<h3> MATUMIZI HALISI</h3>
<ul>
<li> Kujenga uhusiano kati ya watu</li>
<li> Kuboresha elimu na maarifa</li>
<li> Kushirikiana katika shughuli za kijamii</li>
<li> Mawasiliano ya kidijitali</li>
<li> Kukuza uelewano na amani</li>
</ul>
',
  '[{"q":"Mawasiliano ya kijamii ni nini?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria jamii","Step 2: kubadilishana taarifa"],"ans":"Ni ubadilishanaji wa taarifa kati ya watu katika jamii","why":"ufafanuzi"},{"q":"Taja njia moja ya mawasiliano ya kijamii","steps":["Step 1: fikiria njia","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Simu","why":"njia"},{"q":"Kwa nini mawasiliano ya kijamii ni muhimu?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria jamii","Step 2: uelewano"],"ans":"Husaidia watu kuelewana na kuishi kwa amani","why":"umuhimu"},{"q":"Ni nini changamoto ya mawasiliano?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria matatizo","Step 2: jibu"],"ans":"Upotoshaji wa taarifa","why":"changamoto"},{"q":"Teknolojia ina nafasi gani?","steps":["Step 1: fikiria simu","Step 2: mtandao"],"ans":"Imeongeza kasi ya mawasiliano","why":"teknolojia"}]'
);

-- Topic: kiswahili -> msamiati -> Msamiati na Tajiri ya Lugha (Vocabulary Enrichment)
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'kiswahili',
  'msamiati',
  'Msamiati na Tajiri ya Lugha (Vocabulary Enrichment)',
  '<h2>Msamiati na Tajiri ya Lugha</h2>
<p>Msamiati ni jumla ya maneno yanayotumika katika lugha ya Kiswahili. Tajiri ya lugha ni uwezo wa kutumia maneno mengi kwa usahihi na ubunifu.</p>

<hr>

<h3> MWENDO WA UJIFUNZAJI WA MSAMIATI</h3>

<pre>
Neno → Maana → Matumizi → Mfano → Muktadha
</pre>

<hr>

<h3> 7 SUBTOPICS</h3>

<ul>
<li>Visawe (Synonyms)</li>
<li>Vinyume (Antonyms)</li>
<li>Nahau na misemo</li>
<li>Mikopo ya maneno</li>
<li>Matumizi ya kamusi</li>
<li>Msamiati wa kitaaluma</li>
<li>Lugha ya kila siku</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> MAELEZO NA MIFANO</h3>

<h4>1. Visawe</h4>
<p>Ni maneno yenye maana sawa.</p>
<p><b>Mfano:</b> furaha = shangwe</p>

<h4>2. Vinyume</h4>
<p>Ni maneno yenye maana tofauti.</p>
<p><b>Mfano:</b> kubwa ↔ ndogo</p>

<h4>3. Nahau</h4>
<p>Ni maneno yenye maana fiche.</p>
<p><b>Mfano:</b> kula kona = kuepuka jambo</p>

<h4>4. Mikopo ya maneno</h4>
<p>Ni maneno yaliyokopwa kutoka lugha nyingine.</p>
<p><b>Mfano:</b> shule (school), baiskeli (bicycle)</p>

<hr>

<h3> MFANO WA MATUMIZI</h3>

<pre>
Neno: Jasiri
Maana: Asiyeogopa
Sentensi: Askari jasiri alilinda nchi
</pre>

<hr>

<h3> UMUHIMU WA MSAMIATI</h3>

<ul>
<li>Huboresha uandishi</li>
<li>Huongeza ufasaha wa lugha</li>
<li>Husaidia kuelewa maandishi</li>
<li>Huongeza alama katika mitihani</li>
</ul>
',
  '[{"q":"Msamiati ni nini?","hint":"Vocabulary","ans":"Msamiati ni jumla ya maneno ya lugha","explain":"Inahusisha maneno yote yanayotumika","why":"Kuelewa lugha"},{"q":"Taja mfano wa visawe","hint":"Same meaning","ans":"Furaha na shangwe","explain":"Maneno yenye maana sawa","why":"Kuboresha lugha"},{"q":"Vinyume ni nini?","hint":"Opposites","ans":"Maneno yenye maana tofauti","explain":"Mfano kubwa na ndogo","why":"Kuelewa tofauti za maneno"},{"q":"Toa mfano wa nahau","hint":"Idioms","ans":"Kula kona","explain":"Ina maana fiche ya kuepuka jambo","why":"Lugha ya kisanaa"},{"q":"Mikopo ya maneno ni nini?","hint":"Borrowed words","ans":"Maneno kutoka lugha nyingine","explain":"Mfano shule kutoka English","why":"Kuonyesha maendeleo ya lugha"},{"q":"Taja matumizi ya kamusi","hint":"Dictionary use","ans":"Kujua maana ya maneno","explain":"Husaidia kuelewa msamiati","why":"Kuboresha uandishi"},{"q":"Eleza msamiati wa kitaaluma","hint":"Academic words","ans":"Maneno yanayotumika shuleni au taaluma","explain":"Mfano: algebra, sarufi","why":"Kujifunza masomo"},{"q":"Tofautisha visawe na vinyume","hint":"Same vs opposite","ans":"Visawe vina maana sawa, vinyume vina maana tofauti","explain":"Furaha vs huzuni","why":"Kuelewa lugha vizuri"},{"q":"Kwa nini msamiati ni muhimu?","hint":"Importance","ans":"Huboresha ufasaha wa lugha","explain":"Huongeza uwezo wa kuandika na kuzungumza","why":"Kuboresha mawasiliano"},{"q":"Andika mfano wa sentensi yenye neno jasiri","hint":"Sentence","ans":"Askari jasiri alilinda nchi","explain":"Inaonyesha matumizi ya neno","why":"Kujifunza matumizi ya msamiati"}]'
);

-- Topic: math -> numbers -> Number Systems & Basic Operations
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'math',
  'numbers',
  'Number Systems & Basic Operations',
  '<h2>Number Systems & Basic Operations</h2>
<hr>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<h4>1. Number Systems</h4>
<p>
Number systems are different categories of numbers used to represent quantity, order, and change in mathematics. Each type expands the way we understand values.
</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Natural Numbers:</b> 1, 2, 3, 4, ...  
<br> Used for counting objects in real life (no zero included).</li>
<li><b>Whole Numbers:</b> 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, ...  
<br> Natural numbers plus zero, used for counting and representing “nothing”.</li>
<li><b>Integers:</b> ... -3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3 ...  
<br> Includes negative numbers, zero, and positives. Used for temperature, debt, and elevation.</li>
<li><b>Fractions:</b> 1/2, 3/4, 5/8  
<br> Represent parts of a whole. Used when quantities are divided.</li>
<li><b>Decimals:</b> 0.5, 2.75, 10.1  
<br> Another way of showing fractions in base-10 form for precision and measurement.</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4>2. Key Idea</h4>
<p>
All numbers belong to a structured system, but their behavior changes depending on their type.  
Despite differences, they all follow the same mathematical rules when used in operations like addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division.
</p>
<p>
 Example:
<ul>
<li>Whole numbers behave predictably in counting.</li>
<li>Fractions and decimals require place-value understanding.</li>
<li>Negative numbers introduce direction and loss.</li>
</ul>
</p>
<hr>
<h4>3. BODMAS Rule</h4>
<p>
BODMAS defines the correct order in which mathematical operations must be solved to avoid confusion.
</p>
<pre>
B → Brackets
O → Orders (powers, squares, roots)
D → Division
M → Multiplication
A → Addition
S → Subtraction
</pre>
<p><b>Key Principle:</b> Operations are solved from left to right after applying priority rules.</p>
<p><b>Example:</b></p>
<pre>
8 + 2 × (5 - 3)²
</pre>
<p>Step-by-step idea:</p>
<ul>
<li>First solve Brackets: (5 - 3) = 2</li>
<li>Then Orders: 2² = 4</li>
<li>Then Multiplication: 2 × 4 = 8</li>
<li>Then Addition: 8 + 8 = 16</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h4>4. Core Insight</h4>
<p>
Mathematics is a structured language. Number systems define what we can represent, while rules like BODMAS define how we process them correctly.
</p>
',
  '[{"q":"Solve: 6 + 2 × 3","hint":"Use BODMAS","steps":["Step 1: 6 + 2 × 3","Step 2: 6 + (2 × 3) = 6 + 6","Step 3: = 12"],"ans":"12","why":"BODMAS: Multiplication before Addition → 2 × 3 = 6, then 6 + 6 = 12"},{"q":"Find: 15 ÷ 3 + 2","hint":"Division first","steps":["Step 1: 15 ÷ 3 + 2","Step 2: (15 ÷ 3) + 2 = 5 + 2","Step 3: = 7"],"ans":"7","why":"BODMAS: Division before Addition → 15 ÷ 3 = 5, then 5 + 2 = 7"},{"q":"What type of number is -5?","hint":"check sign","steps":["Step 1: -5 < 0 → negative number","Step 2: -5 ∈ {..., -3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3, ...}","Step 3: ∴ -5 ∈ ℤ (integers)"],"ans":"Integer","why":"ℤ = {..., -3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, ...} → -5 ∈ ℤ"},{"q":"Convert 3/4 into a decimal","hint":"divide","steps":["Step 1: 3/4 = 3 ÷ 4","Step 2: 3 ÷ 4 = 0.75"],"ans":"0.75","why":"3/4 = 3 ÷ 4 = 0.75"},{"q":"Solve: (5 + 3) × 2","hint":"brackets first","steps":["Step 1: (5 + 3) × 2","Step 2: (8) × 2","Step 3: = 16"],"ans":"16","why":"BODMAS: Brackets first → (5 + 3) = 8, then 8 × 2 = 16"}]'
);

-- Topic: math -> complex_numbers -> Imaginary Unit and Basic Complex Numbers
INSERT INTO content (sid, cid, topic, notes, qs) VALUES (
  'math',
  'complex_numbers',
  'Imaginary Unit and Basic Complex Numbers',
  '
<h2> Imaginary Unit and Complex Numbers</h2>
<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<p>
A complex number extends the real number system by introducing the imaginary unit i, where i² = -1.
It allows us to represent quantities that cannot be described on the real number line alone, especially in rotation, waves, and oscillations.
</p>
<pre>
z = a + bi
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> DEFINITIONS (EXPANDED)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Real part:</b> a → the measurable quantity on the real number line</li>
<li><b>Imaginary part:</b> bi → represents a perpendicular dimension to real numbers</li>
<li><b>Imaginary unit:</b> i = √(-1), a mathematical construct that enables square roots of negative numbers</li>
<li><b>Complex number:</b> a combination of real + imaginary parts forming a 2D number system</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> KEY IDEA (DEEPER UNDERSTANDING)</h3>
<pre>
Complex number = real axis + imaginary axis
z = a + bi
Think of it as a coordinate:
(a, b) in a 2D plane
</pre>
<p>
Instead of existing on a straight line like real numbers, complex numbers exist on a plane called the <b>Argand Plane</b>.
</p>
<hr>
<h3> GEOMETRIC INTERPRETATION</h3>
<div style="text-align:center;margin:1rem 0;">
<svg viewBox="0 0 280 200" width="280" height="200" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px;background:#0d0d1e;box-shadow: 0 4px 15px rgba(0,0,0,0.45);border: 1px solid #1e1e2f;">
  
  <defs>
    <pattern id="grid" width="20" height="20" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
      <path d="M 20 0 L 0 0 0 20" fill="none" stroke="#22223b" stroke-width="0.5"/>
    </pattern>
    <marker id="math-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="5" refY="5" markerWidth="4" markerHeight="4" orient="auto">
      <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="#888"/>
    </marker>
    <marker id="vector-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="6" refY="5" markerWidth="4" markerHeight="4" orient="auto">
      <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="#3498db"/>
    </marker>
  </defs>

  <rect width="280" height="200" fill="url(#grid)"/>
  <line x1="20" y1="100" x2="260" y2="100" stroke="#667" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#math-arrow)"/>
  <line x1="140" y1="180" x2="140" y2="20" stroke="#667" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#math-arrow)"/>
  <line x1="200" y1="97" x2="200" y2="103" stroke="#888" stroke-width="1"/>
  <line x1="137" y1="50" x2="143" y2="50" stroke="#888" stroke-width="1"/>
  <line x1="200" y1="100" x2="200" y2="50" stroke="#f1c40f" stroke-dasharray="3,3" stroke-width="1"/>
  <line x1="140" y1="50" x2="200" y2="50" stroke="#f1c40f" stroke-dasharray="3,3" stroke-width="1"/>
  <line x1="140" y1="100" x2="195" y2="54" stroke="#3498db" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#vector-arrow)"/>
  <circle cx="200" cy="50" r="4.5" fill="#e74c3c"/>
  <text x="275" y="103" fill="#3498db" font-size="9" font-family="sans-serif" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="end">Re</text>
  <text x="140" y="14" fill="#9b59b6" font-size="9" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle" font-family="sans-serif">Im</text>
  <text x="208" y="44" fill="#fff" font-size="10" font-family="sans-serif" font-weight="bold">z = a + bi</text>
  <text x="208" y="56" fill="#aaa" font-size="8" font-family="monospace">(a, b)</text>
  <text x="200" y="114" fill="#fff" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle" font-family="monospace">a</text>
  <text x="130" y="54" fill="#fff" font-size="9" text-anchor="end" font-family="monospace">bi</text>
  <text x="130" y="112" fill="#888" font-size="8" text-anchor="end" font-family="monospace">O</text>
</svg>
</div>
<p>
This means every complex number represents a <b>point or vector in 2D space</b>.
</p>
<hr>
<h3> OPERATIONS (EXTENDED UNDERSTANDING)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Addition:</b> combine real with real, imaginary with imaginary</li>
<li><b>Subtraction:</b> same structure as addition</li>
<li><b>Multiplication:</b> uses distributive law and i² = -1</li>
<li><b>Division:</b> involves multiplying by conjugate to remove i from denominator</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> COMMON MISTAKES (EXPLAINED)</h3>
<ul>
<li> Treating i like a variable → i is a defined constant (√-1)</li>
<li> Ignoring i² = -1 → leads to incorrect simplification</li>
<li> Mixing real/imaginary parts incorrectly during operations</li>
<li> Forgetting complex numbers are 2D, not 1D values</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3> WORKED EXAMPLE (EXPANDED)</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Simplify (3 + 2i) + (1 + 5i)</p>
<p><b>Step 1: Group real parts</b></p>
<p>3 + 1 = 4</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Group imaginary parts</b></p>
<p>2i + 5i = 7i</p>
<p><b>Step 3: Final answer</b></p>
<p><b>4 + 7i</b></p>
<p><b>Interpretation:</b> This is a point (4, 7) in the complex plane.</p>
<hr>
<h3> ADVANCED INSIGHT</h3>
<p>
Complex numbers are not just arithmetic tools—they represent <b>rotation and transformation</b>.
Multiplying by i rotates a number by 90° in the complex plane.
</p>
<pre>
1 → i → -1 → -i → 1 (rotation cycle)
</pre>
<hr>
<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION (EXPANDED)</h3>
<ul>
<li> Electrical engineering → alternating current (AC wave behavior)</li>
<li> Signal processing → encoding and filtering signals</li>
<li> Quantum mechanics → probability amplitudes</li>
<li> Physics → wave motion and oscillations</li>
<li> Computer graphics → rotations and transformations</li>
</ul>
<h3> QUIZ QUESTIONS</h3>
',
  '[{"q":"What is i defined as?","hint":"square root","steps":["Step 1: The imaginary unit i satisfies i² = -1","Step 2: Therefore i = √(-1)"],"ans":"√(-1)","why":"It extends the number system beyond real numbers"},{"q":"Find real and imaginary parts of 5 + 3i","hint":"separate terms","steps":["Step 1: Real part = 5","Step 2: Imaginary part = 3i","Step 3: Combine as 5 + 3i"],"ans":"Real = 5, Imaginary = 3i","why":"Complex number a + bi splits into real part a and imaginary part bi"},{"q":"Simplify 2i + 4i","hint":"combine like terms","steps":["Step 1: Combine like terms: 2i + 4i","Step 2: Add coefficients: 2 + 4 = 6","Step 3: Result = 6i"],"ans":"6i","why":"Imaginary terms combine by adding coefficients, yielding 6i"},{"q":"What type of number is a + bi?","hint":"name of system","steps":["Step 1: Identify the form a + bi","Step 2: Recognize that a is the real part and bi is the imaginary part","Step 3: Any number of the form a + bi belongs to the complex number system","Step 4: Therefore, a + bi is a complex number"],"ans":"Complex number","why":"A complex number combines a real part (a) and an imaginary part (bi) into a single number of the form a + bi."},{"q":"What happens when i² is calculated?","hint":"negative result","steps":["Step 1: Define the imaginary unit: i = √(-1)","Step 2: Square both sides: i² = (√(-1))²","Step 3: Simplify the square and square root: (√(-1))² = -1","Step 4: Therefore, i² = -1"],"ans":"-1","why":"The imaginary unit i is defined so that i² = -1. This is the fundamental property of complex numbers."}]'
);

